hadiths[1148]{hadithnumber,text}:
"17224","(17218) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Hāshimī narrated to us in Baghdād: ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Ḥunaynī narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn Ḥammād ibn Ṭalḥah narrated to us: Asbāṭ narrated from Simāk from Ḥumayd, the son of Ṣafwān's sister, from Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah, who said: ""I was sleeping in the mosque on a small cloak of mine worth thirty dirhams. A man came and snatched it from me. The man was seized and brought to the Prophet ﷺ, who ordered that he be cut off. I came to him and said: 'Will you cut off his hand for thirty dirhams? I will sell it to him and defer its price.' He said: 'Why did you not do this before you brought him to me?'"" Thus narrated a group from ʿAmr ibn Ḥammād. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17225","(17219) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, who said: Abū Dāwūd said: Zāʾidah narrated from Simāk from Juʿayd ibn Ḥujayr, who said: ""Ṣafwān slept."" Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Ṣafwān's cloak was secured by his lying upon it, so the Prophet ﷺ cut off the hand of the one who stole his cloak."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17226","(17220) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, who said: ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: ""The hand of a thief is not cut until he removes the goods from the house."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17227","(17221) Abū Saʿīd Sharīk ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Isfarāʾinī narrated to us therein: Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāʾinī narrated to us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from al-Zuhrī from Thaʿlabah al-Shāmī, who said: Ṭāriq, who had been appointed as governor over Madīnah, was brought a thief. He punished him, and the thief confessed to the theft. He sent to Ibn ʿUmar to ask about that. He said: ""Do not cut off his hand until he produces the stolen item."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17228","(17222) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sābūr al-Daqīqī narrated to us in Baghdād: Abū Nuʿaym, that is al-Ḥalabī, ʿUbayd ibn Hishām, narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Madanī narrated from Ḥusayn ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḍumayrah from his father from his grandfather, who said: ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""The hand of a thief is not cut until he removes the goods from the house."" This was also narrated through another chain from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, with the same meaning. Sulaymān ibn Mūsā also narrated it from ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17229","(17223) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzzakkī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān that a slave stole a date palm sapling from a man's garden and planted it in his master's garden. The owner of the sapling went out looking for it and found it. He sought the help of Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam against the slave. Marwān imprisoned the slave and intended to cut off his hand. The slave's master went to Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj and asked him about that. He informed him that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""There is no cutting off of the hand for fruit or for the kathar."" (Al-kathar refers to the heart of the palm tree.) The man said: ""Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam has taken my slave and intends to cut off his hand, and I would like you to come with me to him and inform him of what you heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" So Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj walked with him until he came to Marwān and said: ""Have you taken this man's slave?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""What are you going to do with him?"" He said: ""I intended to cut off his hand."" Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj said to him: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'There is no cutting off of the hand for fruit or for the kathar.'"" So Marwān ordered the slave to be released. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17230","(17224) Abū Aḥmad narrated to us: Abū Bakr narrated to us: Muḥammad narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Ibn Abī Ḥusayn al-Makkī that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no cutting off of the hand for hanging fruit, nor for the herding of a mountain. But when it is brought to the enclosure (al-murāḥ) or the drying place (al-jarīn), then the hand is cut off for whatever reaches the price of the shield (al-mijann)."" We have narrated this as a connected hadith from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from his grandfather.

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""Gardens are not a secure place (ḥirz) for date palms or for fruit, because most of them are accessible, entered from their sides. So whoever steals something from a garden of hanging fruit is not to have his hand cut off. But when it is brought to the drying place, the hand is cut off for it.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""The summary of what constitutes a secure place (ḥirz) is that the stolen item should be considered: if the place from which it was stolen is regarded by the general people as a secure place in such a location, then the hand is cut off when he removes it from the secure place. If the general people do not regard it as a secure place, then the hand is not cut off."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17231","(17225) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, Jarīr narrated from Manṣūr, from Mujāhid, who said: Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah was a man among those who were granted clemency (al-ṭulaqāʾ). He came to the Prophet ﷺ and made his mount kneel down and placed his cloak upon it, then moved aside to relieve himself. A man came and stole his cloak. He (Ṣafwān) caught him and brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who ordered that his hand be cut off. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, would you cut off his hand for my cloak? I grant it to him as a gift."" He said: ""Why not before you brought him to me?"" [Ṣaḥīh. It was previously mentioned near 17215]"
"17232","(17226) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Shaybān al-Ramlī narrated to us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Ṭāwūs, who said: It was said to Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah ibn Khalaf: ""There is no religion for one who does not emigrate."" He said: ""By Allah, I will not return to my house until I go to Madīnah."" So he came to Madīnah and stayed with al-ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him. While he was sleeping in the mosque with a lock of hair upon his head, a thief came and stole it. He (Ṣafwān) caught him and brought him to the Prophet ﷺ, who ordered that his hand be cut off. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, it is for him (as a gift)."" He said: ""Why not before you brought him?"""
"17233","(17227) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ibn Milḥān narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Quraysh were greatly concerned about the matter of the Makhzūmī woman who had stolen. They said: ""Who will speak to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about her?"" They said: ""Who would dare to do so except Usāmah ibn Zayd, the beloved of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" So Usāmah spoke to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Do you intercede regarding one of the prescribed punishments of Allah?"" Then he stood and addressed the people, saying: ""The people before you were destroyed because when a noble person among them committed theft, they left him, but when a weak person among them committed theft, they carried out the prescribed punishment upon him. By Allah, if Fāṭimah, the daughter of Muḥammad, were to steal, I would cut off her hand.""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it from the ḥadīth of al-Layth ibn Saʿd."
"17234","(17228) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAmr al-Ḥīrī informed me, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūnus narrated to us, Abū al-Ṭāhir informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Yūnus informed me from Ibn Shihāb, who said: ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: The Quraysh were greatly concerned about the matter of the woman who had stolen during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the expedition of the Conquest (Fatḥ). He mentioned the meaning of the ḥadīth of al-Layth, adding: Then that woman who had stolen was brought, and her hand was cut off.

Yūnus said: Ibn Shihāb said: ʿUrwah said: ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""Her repentance thereafter became excellent, and she married. She would thereafter come to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I would convey her need to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""

Muslim recorded it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Ṭāhir, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Ibn Abī Uways from Ibn Wahb.

Our companions said: If the amputation were waived by gifting the stolen property from the thief, then their intercession and their concern regarding the matter that troubled them would have been directed toward the one from whom it was stolen. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīh]"
"17235","(17229) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, and Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Shādhayākhī, among others, said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʤAbd al-Ḥakam al-Miṣrī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to me, ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Zayd narrated from Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr ibn Ḥazm, from his father, from ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Overlook the slips of those of respectable standing, except for a prescribed punishment from among the prescribed punishments of Allah."" [Ṣaḥīh]"
"17236","(17230) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us, Ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated from his father, from the jurists of the people of Madīnah, who used to say: Whoever steals a young slave or a non-Arab slave who has no recourse, his hand is cut off. And it is narrated from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, that he did not consider amputation to be required in their case. He said: ""These are deceivers (khallābūn)."" Our companions said: Its meaning regarding the slave is when he is of sound mind. And it is narrated from ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he cut off a man's hand for a slave boy he had stolen. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17237","(17231) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Bāghandī narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Mūsā al-Anṣārī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh — and he is Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUrwah — narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Prophet ﷺ was brought a man who used to steal young boys, and he ordered that his hand be cut off. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17238","(17232) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Mūsā narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUrwah narrated to me, Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam was the governor of Madīnah. A man was brought to him who used to steal young boys and then take them out to sell them in another land. Marwān consulted regarding his affair, and ʿUrwah narrated this ḥadīth to him from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he had cut off a man's hand for that. So Marwān ordered that the one who stole young boys have his hand cut off.

Abū Aḥmad said: This is not preserved from Hishām except through the narration of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā from him. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17239","(17233) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī said: Abū al-Ḥasan al-Dāraquṭnī, the ḥāfiẓ, said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUrwah is alone in narrating it from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, and he makes many errors regarding Hishām, and his ḥadīth is weak. [Ṣaḥīh]"
"17240","(17234) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Mālik narrated from Nāfiʿ, that a slave of Ibn ʿUmar stole while he was a runaway (ābiq). ʿAbd Allāh sent him to Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ, who was the governor of Madīnah, to cut off his hand. Saʿīd refused to cut off his hand and said: ""The hand of a runaway slave is not cut off when he steals."" Ibn ʿUmar said to him: ""In which book of Allah did you find this?"" Then Ibn ʿUmar ordered that his hand be cut off. [Ṣaḥīh]"
"17241","(17235) Abū Ḥāẓim al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīrūyah narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us, saying: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abī Laylā narrated from Nāfiʿ that a slave of Ibn ʿUmar ran away and committed theft during his escape. He was brought to Ibn ʿUmar, who said to him: ""Your running away will not save you from a ḥadd (prescribed punishment) of the ḥudūd of Allah."" He said: ""So he amputated his hand."" [Weak]"
"17242","(17236) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, saying: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, saying: Mālik narrated from Ruzayq ibn Ḥakīm that he seized a slave who had run away and committed theft. He wrote concerning him to ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz: ""I used to hear that when a slave who has run away commits theft, his hand is not amputated."" So ʿUmar wrote back: ""Indeed, Allah says: 'The thief, male or female, cut off their hands as a recompense for what they have earned, as a punishment from Allah. And Allah is Mighty, Wise.' [Al-Māʾidah 38] So if his theft reaches a quarter of a dīnār or more, then amputate his hand.""

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""This is the view of al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, and others. Ibn ʿAbbās used to hold the view that there is no amputation for a slave who has run away if he commits theft. We have set aside his statement in favour of the statement of other Companions, because it is more in accordance with the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic."" Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Disobedience to Allah through running away does not increase him in goodness."" The Shaykh said: ""Some of the weak narrators have raised it to Ibn ʿAbbās, but it is nothing."""
"17243","(17237) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Raffāʾ al-Baghdādī narrated to us, saying: ʿUthmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated from his father, from the jurists of the people of Madīnah, that they used to say: ""The bandit (al-ṭarrār) is subject to amputation."" And they used to say: ""There is no amputation except for that whose value reaches a quarter of a dīnār or more."" [Weak]"
"17244","(17238) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, saying: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Abī ʿImrān from al-Mushaʿith ibn Ṭarīf from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ṣāmit from Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""O Abū Dharr!"" I said: ""Here I am, and at your service!"" He said: ""How will you be when death befalls the people and the house therein is the waṣīf?"" — meaning the grave. He said: I said: ""Allah and His Messenger know best, or whatever Allah and His Messenger have chosen."" He said: ""Then adhere to patience."" [Authentic]"
"17245","(17239) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, saying: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn al-Musāwir narrated to us, saying: Sahl ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us, saying: Sharīk narrated from al-Shaybānī from al-Shaʿbī, who said: ""The grave-robber (al-nabbāsh) is a thief."" [Weak]"
"17246","(17240) He said: And Sharīk narrated from Mughīrah from Ibrāhīm the like of it. And from Ismāʿīl from al-Ḥasan the like of it. [Weak]"
"17247","(17241) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us, saying: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us, saying: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: I heard Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrating from ʿUmar ibn Ayyūb from ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī that he said: ""Amputation is carried out in the case of our dead just as it is carried out in the case of our living."" [Weak]"
"17248","(17242) He said: And Ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: Ḥarmalah ibn ʿImrān al-Tujībī narrated to us, saying: Ayyūb ibn Sharḥabīl wrote to ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz asking him about the grave-robbers of tombs. So ʿUmar wrote to him: ""By my life, it is only befitting that the thief of the dead be punished with the same punishment with which the thief of the living is punished."" [Good]"
"17249","(17243) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Shaybānī narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn al-Wahhāb narrated to us, saying: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us, saying: Ḥajjāj narrated from ʿAṭāʾ, who said: ""The grave-robber is subject to amputation."" And it has been narrated to us from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab.

Al-Bukhārī said in al-Tārīkh: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Suhayl narrated to us, saying: ""I witnessed Ibn al-Zubayr amputate a grave-robber."" Abū Bakr al-Fārisī narrated it to us, saying: Abū Isḥāq al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī narrated it, and he mentioned it.

Al-Bukhārī said: And ʿAbbād ibn al-ʿAwwām said: ""We used to suspect him of lying"" — meaning Suhayl — and he is Suhayl ibn Dhakwān, Abū al-Sindī al-Makkī. [Weak]"
"17250","(17244) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, saying: Mālik narrated from Abī al-Rijāl from his mother, ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that the Prophet ﷺ cursed the one who secretly takes land (al-mukhtafī) and the one who is secretly taken from (al-mukhtafā). This is a mursal narration. [Weak]"
"17251","(17245) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Sulaymān al-Burulsī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Abū al-Rijāl from ʿAmrah from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) cursed the male and the female who steal by deception (al-mukhtafī and al-mukhtafīyah).

And Abū Qutaybah similarly narrated it from Mālik. [Munkar]"
"17252","(17246) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Azharī informed us, Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs ibn Kāmil narrated to us, Mūsā ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us, Abū Qutaybah narrated to us, Mālik ibn Anas narrated to us, Abū al-Rijāl narrated it with a connected chain, but the correct version is mursal. [Munkar]"
"17253","(17247) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Isfarāʾinī, Ibn al-Saqqāʾ, informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Baṭṭah narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Zakariyyā al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Umawī narrated to us, Muslim ibn Khālid narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid regarding the recitation of Ibn Masʿūd: ""As-sāriq wa as-sāriqah faqṭaʾū aymānahumā"" (The male thief and the female thief, cut off their hands). And similarly Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated it from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, and this chain is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected).

And Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī said the same, except that he said: ""In our recitation it is: As-sāriqūn wa as-sāriqāt tuqṭaʾu aymānahum"" (The male thieves and the female thieves, their hands shall be cut off). [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17254","(17248) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us, Ibn Ṣāʿid narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Rajāʾ narrated to us, Wakīʿ narrated to us, Masarrat ibn Maʿbad narrated to us, saying: I heard Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī al-Muhājir narrating from Rajāʾ ibn Ḥaywah from ʿAdī that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) cut off the hand of a thief from the joint."
"17255","(17249) He said: And Wakīʿ narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir a similar narration. [Ḥasan]"
"17256","(17250) Abū Saʿid al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā al-Washshāʾ al-Ṣūfī in Tinnīs narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Salm al-Baṣrī narrated to us, Khālid ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Marwazī al-Khurāsānī narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Layth from Mujāhid from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) cut off a thief from the joint.

Abū Aḥmad said: I do not know this ḥadīth from Mālik ibn Mighwal except through the narration of Khālid from him. [Ḥasan]"
"17257","(17251) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīruwayh informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) used to cut off the hand of the thief from the joint, and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) used to cut it from the middle of the foot. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17258","(17252) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, Salm ibn Junādah narrated to us, Wakīʿ narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abjar narrated from his father from Salamah ibn Kuhayl from Ḥujayyah ibn ʿAdī that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) cut off their hands from the joint and cauterized them, and it is as though I am looking at their hands as if they were the testicles of donkeys (i.e., red from cauterization). [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17259","(17253) He said: And Wakīʿ narrated to us, Qays narrated from Mughīrah from al-Shaʿbī that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) used to cut off the foot while leaving the heel upon which the person leans. Thus ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) used to differentiate between the hand and the foot: he would cut off the hand from the joint and cut off the foot from the middle of the foot.

And we say in accordance with the statement of other Companions regarding equating the two, and that is the position of the majority. And success is through Allah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17260","(17254) Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū ʿUbayd al-Qāsim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad al-Dārawardī narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Khuṣayfah narrated to us from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Thawbān from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was brought a thief who had stolen a cloak. They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, this one has stolen."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""I do not think he has stolen."" The thief said: ""Indeed, O Messenger of Allah!"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Go with him, cut off his hand, then cauterize it, then bring him to me."" So his hand was cut off and he was brought to him. He said: ""Repent to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."" He said: ""I repent to Allah."" He said: ""Allah has accepted your repentance.""

Yaʿqūb connected it from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, and others followed him upon it. [Munkar]"
"17261","(17255) And it was narrated from him by ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī — he informed us — Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh informed us — Bisr ibn Aḥmad informed us — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr al-Ḥaddāʾ narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us — ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad al-Dārawardī narrated to us. And he mentioned it with its meaning as a mursal narration, without mentioning Abū Hurayrah in it, except that he said: ""So they amputated it, then they cauterized it, then they brought it to him.""

[Weak]"
"17262","(17256) He said: And ʿAlī narrated to us, he said: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated it to me — Yazīd ibn Khuṣayfah narrated to me from Ibn Thawbān.

(Ḥ) And he said: And ʿAlī narrated to us — Sufyān narrated to us — Ibn Khuṣayfah narrated to us from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Thawbān, and he mentioned it as a mursal narration.

ʿAlī said: Neither of them narrated it with a chain going above Ibn Thawbān to anyone. And it has reached me that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated it from Yazīd ibn Khuṣayfah from Ibn Thawbān from Abū Hurayrah, but I do not think he preserved it.

Imam Aḥmad said: And it has also been narrated from him as a mursal narration. [Weak]"
"17263","(17257) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad — Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf informed us — Bisr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāʾinī informed us — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Ḥaddāʾ informed us — ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abjar informed us — Salamah ibn Kuhayl informed us — Ḥujayyah ibn ʿAdī informed us, he said: ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, used to amputate, cauterize, and imprison. When they recovered, he would send for them and release them. Then he would say: ""Raise your hands toward Allah."" He said: So they would raise them, and he would say: ""Who amputated you?"" They would say: ""ʿAlī."" He would say: ""Why?"" They would say: ""We stole."" He would say: ""O Allah, bear witness! O Allah, bear witness!""

The wording is that of the Ḥaddāʾ's narration. In his narration, ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī added: ""And ʿAmmār ibn Ruzayq al-Dabbī narrated this hadith from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, but he contradicted Ibn Abjar in its chain of narration."" [Weak]"
"17264","(17258) The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us — they both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Abū al-Jawwāb narrated to us — ʿAmmār narrated to us — from Salamah ibn Kuhayl — from Abī al-Zaʿrāʾ — from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, that when he caught a thief, he would amputate him, then cauterize him, then throw him into prison. When they recovered and he wished to release them, he would say: ""Raise your hands toward Allah."" He said: ""It was as though I were looking at them, as though they were the testicles of donkeys."" Then he would say: ""Who amputated you?"" They would say: ""ʿAlī."" He would say: ""O Allah, they have spoken the truth — in You I amputated them, and in You I sent them forth.""

ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī said regarding the first chain: ""And the hadith, according to me, is the hadith of Ibn Abjar.""

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""It is as though he used to command tending to them until they recovered, unless he imprisoned them as a disciplinary punishment (taʿzīr).""

(T) Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — from Abī Jaʿfar that ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""The imprisonment of the Imam after the establishment of the ḥadd punishment is an injustice."" [Weak]"
"17265","(17259) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us — Ibn Nājiyah narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Bakkār narrated to us — Abū Maʿshar narrated to us — from Muṣʿab ibn Thābit.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us — Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed me — Khalīl ibn Abī Rāfiʿ narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUbayd ibn ʿAqīl narrated to us — my grandfather narrated to us — Muṣʿab narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUbayd ibn ʿAqīl al-Hilālī narrated to us — my grandfather narrated to us — from Muṣʿab ibn Thābit ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr — from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir — from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: A thief was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: ""Kill him."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, he only stole."" He said: ""Amputate him."" So his hand was amputated. Then he was brought a second time, and he said: ""Kill him."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, he only stole."" He said: ""Amputate him."" He said: So his foot was amputated. Then he was brought a third time, and he said: ""Kill him."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, he only stole."" He said: ""Amputate him."" Then he was brought a fourth time, and he said: ""Kill him."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, he only stole."" He said: ""Amputate him."" Then he was brought a fifth time, and he said: ""Kill him."" Jābir said: ""So we took him and killed him, then dragged him and threw him into a well, and pelted stones upon him.""

The wording is that of Abū Dāwūd's narration. In the narration of Abū Maʿshar, regarding the first time, it says: He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, he has stolen."" He said: ""Amputate his hand."" And regarding the second time, after this statement, he said: ""Amputate his foot."" And regarding the third time: ""Amputate his hand."" And regarding the fourth time: ""Amputate his foot."" And regarding the fifth time, he said: ""Did I not tell you to kill him? Kill him?"" He said: ""So we passed by him to a camel pen, and we set the camels upon him, and he flailed with his hands and feet until the camels bolted from him. Then we pelted him with stones until we killed him."" [Weak]"
"17266","(17260) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh informed us — Ṣāliḥ ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥabīb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū Mūsā Isḥāq ibn Mūsā al-Anṣārī narrated to us — ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīl al-Ashjaʿī narrated to us — from Muṣʿab ibn Thābit — from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir — from Jābir, who said: The Prophet ﷺ was brought a thief, and he ordered the amputation of his hand. Then he was brought having stolen, and he ordered it, so his foot was amputated. Then he was brought again having stolen, and he ordered the amputation of his left hand. Then he was brought having stolen, and he ordered the amputation of his right foot. Then he was brought having stolen, and he ordered his killing.

This hadith has also been narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah and Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥumayd from Ibn al-Munkadir. [Weak]"
"17267","(17261) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me by way of ijāzah regarding what was not dictated from the book al-Mustadrak — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh narrated to us — Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī narrated to us — ʿAffān ibn Muslim narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us — Yūsuf ibn Saʿd narrated to us — from al-Ḥārith ibn Ḥāṭib that a man stole during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Kill him."" They said: ""He only stole."" He said: ""Then amputate him."" Then he stole again and was amputated. Then he stole during the time of Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and was amputated. Then he stole and was amputated, until his limbs were amputated. Then he stole a fifth time, and Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was most knowledgeable about this when he ordered his killing. Take him and kill him."" So he was given to a group of Quraysh youth, among whom was ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr. ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr said: ""Put me in charge over you."" So they put him in charge, and whenever he struck him, they struck him until they killed him.

Isḥāq al-Ḥanẓalī followed him in this, from al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl, from Ḥammād ibn Salamah, from Yūsuf ibn Saʿd. [Weak]"
"17268","(17262) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us — al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʬAṭāʾ informed me — Ibn Jurayj informed me — from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Umayyah — from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Rabīʿah, who said: A thief was brought, and they said: ""O Messenger of Allah, this is a slave boy of some orphans among the Anṣār. By Allah, we know of no wealth for them other than this."" So he let him go. Then he was brought a second time, and he let him go. Then he was brought a third time, and he let him go. Then he was brought a fourth time, and he let him go. Then he was brought a fifth time, and he amputated his hand. Then he was brought a sixth time, and he amputated his foot. Then he was brought a seventh time, and he amputated his hand. Then he was brought an eighth time, and he amputated his foot. Thus I found it in my book.

And Ḥammād ibn Masʿadah said, from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Umayyah, from al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Rabīʿah, and this is more correct, and it is a ḥasan mursal narration with a ṣaḥīḥ chain.

Abū Dāwūd narrated it in al-Marāsīl from Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Anbārī from Ḥammād ibn Masʿadah.

And Isḥāq al-Ḥanẓalī narrated it from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAbd Rabbihi ibn Abī Umayyah, that al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Rabīʿah and Ibn Sābiṭ al-Aḥwal both narrated to him that the Prophet ﷺ was brought a slave, and he mentioned its meaning. It is as though he did not consider the theft in the four instances to have reached the threshold requiring amputation, or he did not regard the theft as having reached that level. Then he saw that it necessitated amputation in the subsequent instances, and so he ordered the amputation. And this mursal narration strengthens the connected narration before it and strengthens the statement of those Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, who agreed with it. [Weak]"
"17269","(17263) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — Mālik informed us — from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim — from his father that a man from the people of Yemen, whose hand and foot had been amputated, came to Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, and complained to him that the governor of Yemen had wronged him. The man used to pray at night, and Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, would say: ""By your father, your night is not the night of a thief!"" Then they missed some jewelry belonging to Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays, may Allah be pleased with her, the wife of Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. The man began to go around with them, saying: ""O Allah, upon You is the one who raided the people of this righteous house by night."" They found the jewelry with a goldsmith, and it was the one with the amputated limbs who had brought it. The one with the amputated limbs confessed, or testimony was given against him, and Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, ordered that his left hand be amputated. Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""By Allah, his supplication against himself is more severe in my eyes than his theft."" [Weak]"
"17270","(17264) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us — they both said: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Khushaysh narrated to us — Salm ibn Junādah narrated to us — Wakīʿ narrated to us — Sufyān narrated to us — from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim — from his father that Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, intended to amputate a man after the hand and the foot, but ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""The Sunnah is the hand."" The statement of ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him — ""The Sunnah is the hand"" — suggests that he knew the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in this matter. [Weak]"
"17271","(17265) Abū Ḥāẓim al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah al-Anṣārī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīrūyah narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us, saying: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated to us from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Nāfiʿ, from Ṣafiyyah bint Abī ʿUbayd, that a man committed theft during the caliphate of Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and his hand and foot had already been amputated. Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) intended to amputate his other foot and leave his hand so that he could use it for purification and cleanliness and benefit from it. ʿUmar then said: ""No, by the One in whose Hand is my soul, his other hand shall certainly be cut off."" So Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) ordered it, and his hand was cut off. [Weak]"
"17272","(17266) Abū Ḥāẓim narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīrūyah narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad narrated to us, saying: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Khālid narrated to us, saying: ʿIkrimah narrated to us from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""I witnessed ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) amputate a hand after a hand and a foot."" [Authentic]"
"17273","(17267) He said: And Saʿīd narrated to us, saying: Khālid narrated to us from Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) amputated a hand after a hand and a foot. [Its chain of narration is authentic]"
"17274","(17268) Abū Ḥāẓim and Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Faḍl al-Karābīsī narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us, saying: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us, saying: Simāk ibn Ḥarb narrated to us from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀʾidh, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought a man whose hand and foot had already been amputated, and he had committed theft. ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) ordered that his foot be cut off. ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: 'The only reward of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger…' [Al-Māʾidah 33] to the end of the verse. You have already cut off this man's hand and foot; it is not appropriate that you cut off his other foot, leaving him with no limb upon which to walk. Either you punish him with a discretionary punishment, or you commit him to prison."" So he committed him to prison.

The first narration from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) is more deserving of being authentic. How can this narration be authentic from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) when in the first narration he disapproved of amputating the foot after the hand and foot had been cut, and he indicated toward the hand?

The narration of Ibn ʿAbbās is connected and testifies to the authenticity of the first narration, as does the narration of Ṣafiyyah bint Abī ʿUbayd, which contains what is in the narration of al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr. [Weak]"
"17275","(17269) As for what has been narrated from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding this matter, it has been narrated from him through another route. Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq and ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb and Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, saying: Shuʿbah narrated to us from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salamah, that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought a thief, so he amputated his hand. Then he was brought again, so he amputated his foot. Then he was brought again, and he said: ""Shall I cut off his hand? With what shall he wipe himself and with what shall he eat?"" Then he said: ""Shall I cut off his foot? On what shall I make him walk? Indeed, I am shy before Allah."" Then he struck him and consigned him to prison.

As for the killing in the fifth instance, which is transmitted in the elevated report, al-Shāfiʿī said: ""The killing of one upon whom the ḥadd punishment has been carried out four times and is brought a fifth time is abrogated."" He cited as evidence what is transmitted in the chapters of the ḥadd of the drinker. And by Allah is the success granted. [Weak]"
"17276","(17270) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Yūsuf al-Qāḍī narrated to us, saying: Naṣr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, saying: ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī narrated to us from Ḥajjāj, from Makḥūl, from Ibn Muḥayrīz, who said: ""I said to Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd: What is your opinion regarding hanging the hand of the thief from his neck — is it from the Sunnah?"" He said: ""Yes. I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) amputate a thief and then order that his hand be hung from his neck."" [Weak]"
"17277","(17271) Abū al-Ḥasan narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥasan narrated to us, saying: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us, saying: ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, saying: Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh narrated to us from Makḥūl, from Ibn Muḥayrīz, who said: ""I said to Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd — and he was among those who gave allegiance under the tree —"" then he mentioned something similar. [Weak]"
"17278","(17272) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl narrated to us in Baghdad, saying: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us, saying: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Muqātil narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us, saying: Ḥamdān ibn ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Nuʿaym — who is Ibn Ḥammād — narrated to us, saying: Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us from Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh, from Makḥūl, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥayrīz, who said: ""I asked Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd about hanging the hand of the thief from his neck, and he said: 'It is a Sunnah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) amputated the hand of a thief and hung his hand from his neck.'""

Nuʿaym said: ""I heard it from Abū Bakr ibn ʿAlī.""

The wording is that of Nuʿaym's ḥadīth. In the narration of Muḥammad ibn Muqātil, he said: ""From Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd, who said: 'It is the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) that his hand be hung from his neck' — meaning the thief — when it is cut off."" [Weak]"
"17279","(17273) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn Bālūyah narrated to me, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me, saying: My father narrated to me, saying: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us, saying: Shuʿbah narrated to us from al-Masʿūdī, from al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from his father, that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) amputated a thief, and they passed by him with his hand hanging from his neck. [Authentic]"
"17280","(17274) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Khusrūjirdī narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us, saying: Ibn Zaydān narrated to us, saying: Abū Kurayb narrated to us, saying: Ḥafṣ narrated to us from al-Aʿmash, from al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from his father, who said: ""I saw ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) when a thief confessed before him twice, so he amputated his hand and hung it from his neck. It is as though I am looking at his hand striking his chest."" [Authentic]"
"17281","(17275) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; Yaʿqūb al-Dawraqī narrated to us; al-Dāwardī narrated to us, from Yazīd ibn Khuṣayfah, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Thawbān, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: A thief who had stolen a cloak was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They said, ""Indeed, this one has stolen."" He said, ""I do not think he has stolen."" The man said, ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah, I have indeed stolen."" He said, ""Take him, cut off his hand, then cauterize it, then bring him to me."" So he was brought to him, and he said, ""Repent to Allah."" He said, ""I repent to Allah."" The Prophet ﷺ said, ""Allah has accepted your repentance."" [Munkar]"
"17282","(17276) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Ibn Rajāʾ narrated to us; Hammām narrated to us, from Isḥāq — meaning Ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah — from Abī al-Mundhir al-Barrād, from Abī Umayyah, a man from the Anṣār, that a thief stole some goods and was apprehended with the goods in his possession. He confessed, and was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, who said to him, ""I do not think you have stolen."" He said, ""Yes."" He said it three times, and the Prophet ﷺ ordered that his hand be cut off. When it was cut off, he said, ""Repent to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."" He said, ""I repent to Allah."" The Prophet ﷺ said, ""O Allah, accept his repentance.""

Hammād ibn Salamah also narrated it from Isḥāq, and he said: from Abī Umayyah al-Makhzūmī, and he said in its text: ""No goods were found with him.""

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"17283","(17277) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Hammād narrated to us, from Thābit, from Anas, that ʿUmar was brought a thief who said, ""By Allah, I have never stolen before this."" ʿUmar said, ""You have lied. It is not befitting of Allah that He should expose a servant's sin for the first time."" Then he ordered that his hand be cut off. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17284","(17278) Abū Ḥāziṃ al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah both said: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīruwayh informed us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us; al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah narrated to us, from Yazīd ibn Abī Kabshah al-Anmārī, from Abī al-Dardāʾ, that a black slave-girl who had stolen was brought to him. He said to her, ""Have you stolen? Say no."" She said, ""No,"" and so he let her go. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17285","(17279) Abū Bakr al-Ardistānī informed us; Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī informed us; Sufyān al-Jawharī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us, from Ḥammād, from Ibrāhīm, who said: Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī was brought a woman who had stolen a camel. He said, ""Have you stolen? Say no.""

And from Sufyān, from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""Drive away those who confess"" — meaning those who confess to ḥudūd offences. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17286","(17280) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Mazzakī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated to us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr, from ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who said: ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) set out for Makkah accompanied by two slave-girls and a slave-boy of Banī ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr al-Ṣiddīq. She sent with the two slave-girls a wrap of thick cloth upon which a green patch had been sewn. The boy took the wrap, tore it open, extracted the thread, and replaced it with felt or fur, sewing it up again. When the two slave-girls arrived in Madinah, they delivered it to their family. When they opened it, they found the felt but did not find the wrap. They spoke to the two slave-girls, who then spoke to ʿĀʾishah or wrote to her, and they accused the slave-boy. The slave-boy was questioned about it, and he confessed. ʿĀʾishah ordered that his hand be cut off. ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said, ""Amputation is for a quarter of a dīnār and above."""
"17287","(17281) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿUrubah narrated to us, from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""The hand is responsible for what it has taken until it returns it."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17288","(17282) ʿAlī informed us; Aḥmad informed us; ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar al-Dabbī narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Yaḥyā narrated to us, from Ibn Abī ʿUrubah, and he mentioned it similarly, except that he said: ""From the Prophet ﷺ."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17289","(17283) As for the ḥadīth: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Jaʿfar Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ḥāfiẓ in Hamadhān informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Kathīr ibn ʿUfayr said: al-Mufaḍḍal narrated to me.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Ṭayyib Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us; Bishr ibn Sahl al-Labbād narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Faḍālah narrated to us, from Yūnus, from Saʿīd ibn Ibrāhīm. My brother al-Miswar ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to me, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said…

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yaḥyā al-Khallāl narrated to us; al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Faḍālah, the judge of Egypt, narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Yazīd al-Aylī narrated to us, from Saʿīd ibn Ibrāhīm, from al-Miswar, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""The thief is not required to pay compensation when the ḥadd is carried out upon him.""

In the narration of Abū ʿAbd Allāh: ""The one from whom the theft was made is not required to pay compensation."" This is a ḥadīth in which there is disagreement concerning al-Mufaḍḍal. It has been narrated from him in this manner, and it has also been narrated from him from Yūnus from al-Zuhrī from Saʿīd, and it has been narrated from him from Yūnus from Saʿīd ibn Ibrāhīm from his brother al-Miswar. If this Saʿīd is Saʿīd ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, then we do not know of any brother of his known in narration who is called al-Miswar. And it is not established that al-Miswar, who is attributed to him — Saʿīd ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Miswar ibn Ibrāhīm — heard from his grandfather ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him), nor did he see him; thus the chain is broken. And Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān has not been confirmed to have heard from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him); it is only said that he saw him. His father died during the time of ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him), so his children only came of age after the death of their father ʿAbd al-Raḥmān; thus it is not established that they narrated from him, nor that they saw him, so the chain is broken. And if it is someone other than him, then we do not know him, nor do we know his brother. And it is not permissible for anyone to take from the wealth of his brother except that with which his soul is content. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17290","(17284) Abū Ḥāziṃ al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl al-Karābīsī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us; one of our companions narrated to us, from al-Ḥasan, that he used to say: ""He is liable for the theft in addition to the amputation of his hand."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17291","(17285) He said: And Hushaym narrated to us, Abu Hanifah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that he used to say: The thief is held liable for the stolen property whether he consumed it or did not consume it, and the amputation is incumbent upon him. [Da'if]"
"17292","(17286) Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — and Abu 'Abd al-Rahman — Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Sulami — informed us, both saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; 'Amr ibn al-Harith and Hisham ibn Sa'd informed us, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As, that a man from Muzaynah came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what is your opinion regarding the livestock pastured on the mountain?"" He said: ""It is [liable for] its equivalent and a punitive punishment. There is no amputation for anything of the livestock except that which is kept in the fold and reaches the value of a shield — then there is amputation of the hand. And whatever does not reach the value of a shield, then there is a fine of double its value and punitive lashes."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, then what is your opinion regarding hanging fruit?"" He said: ""It is [liable for] its equivalent along with it and a punitive punishment. There is no amputation for anything of the hanging fruit except that which is stored in the barn. So whatever is taken from the barn and reaches the value of a shield, then there is amputation. And whatever does not reach the value of a shield, then there is a fine of double its value and punitive lashes."" [Da'if]"
"17293","(17287) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, both saying: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Shaybani narrated to us; Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us; Ja'far ibn 'Awn narrated to us; Hisham ibn 'Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, who said: The slaves of Hatib ibn Abi Balta'ah in al-'Aliyah stole a she-camel belonging to a man of Muzaynah and slaughtered it, and they confessed to it. So 'Umar sent to him and mentioned that to him, saying: ""These are your slaves — they have stolen and slaughtered a she-camel of a man from Muzaynah and have confessed to it. So command Kathir ibn al-Salt to cut off their hands."" Then he sent for him after he had gone and called him and said: ""Were it not that I think you starve them until one of them has done what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has forbidden, I would have cut off their hands. But, by Allah, if you leave them, I shall impose upon you a fine that will pain you."" He said: ""What was its price for the Muzani?"" He said: ""I would not have let it go for four hundred."" He said: ""Then give him eight hundred."" [Sahih]"
"17294","(17288) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us; Abu al-'Abbas al-Asamm informed us; al-Rabi' informed us; al-Shafi'i said: ""The fine is not doubled upon anyone in anything. Punishment is only in the bodies, not in wealth. We only abandoned the doubling of the fine because the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ruled regarding the she-camel of Bara' ibn 'Azib that the owners of properties are responsible for guarding them during the day, and whatever the livestock destroy at night is guaranteed by their owners."" He said: ""They guarantee it at its value, not at double its value."" He said: ""And the claim of the claimant is not accepted regarding the amount of the value, because the Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'The proof is upon the claimant, and the oath is upon the one against whom the claim is made.'"" [Sahih]"
"17295","(17289) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajan informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far al-Muzakkī informed us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Haram ibn Sa'd ibn Muhayyisah, that a she-camel of Bara' ibn 'Azib entered a man's garden and damaged it. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ruled that the owners of gardens are responsible for guarding them during the day, and that whatever the livestock destroy at night is guaranteed by their owners. And we have mentioned its corroborating narrations in their appropriate place. [Sahih]"
"17296","(17290) Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Rudhbari al-Faqih, Abu 'Abd Allah al-Husayn ibn 'Umar ibn Burhan al-Ghazzal, Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan, and Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukri informed us, all saying: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us; al-Hasan ibn 'Arafah informed us; 'Isa ibn Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq al-Suba'i narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ansari, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no amputation for the snatcher, nor for the plunderer, nor for the traitor."" [Sahih]"
"17297","(17291) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us, who said: Abu Dawud — that is, al-Sijistani — said: ""This hadith was not heard by Ibn Jurayj from Abu al-Zubayr. And it has reached me from Ahmad ibn Hanbal that he said: 'Ibn Jurayj only heard it from Yasin al-Zayyat.'"" Abu Dawud said: ""And al-Mughirah ibn Muslim narrated it from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (ﷺ)."" [Sahih]"
"17298","(17292) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar informed us in Baghdad; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us; Sa'dan ibn Nasr informed us; Shabbabah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah ibn Muslim, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no amputation for the snatcher, nor for the plunderer, nor for the traitor."" [Sahih]"
"17299","(17293) Abu Hazim al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Khamirawayh informed us; Ahmad ibn Najdah informed us; Sa'id ibn Mansur informed us; Hushaym informed us; Abu Mu'adh Fudayl informed us, from Abu Hariz, from al-Sha'bi, that a man called Ayyub ibn Burayqah snatched a necklace from a person and it was brought to 'Ammar ibn Yasir. So 'Ammar wrote regarding him to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), and he wrote to him: ""That is daytime snatching. So punish him with a punishment, then release him and do not cut off his hand.""

(T) And in the narration of al-Thawri from Humayd al-Tawil, he said: ""'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz (may Allah have mercy on him) was brought a man who had snatched a necklace from a slave girl, and he did not see amputation in his case. He said: 'That is daytime snatching.'"" [Da'if]"
"17300","(17294) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Amr ibn Matar informed us; Yahya ibn Muhammad informed us; 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh informed us; my father narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us, from Simak, from a son of 'Ubayd ibn al-Abras, who said: I witnessed 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) being brought a man who had snatched another man's garment. The snatcher said: ""I would have recognized him."" So 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) did not cut off his hand. [Da'if]"
"17301","(17295) Abū Manṣūr — ʿAbd al-Qāhir bin Ṭāhir, and Abū Naṣr — ʿUmar bin ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz bin Qatādah, and Abū al-Qāsim — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān bin ʿAlī bin Ḥamdān al-Fārisī, all of them said: Abū ʿAmr Ismāʿīl bin Nujayd informed us, Abū Muslim informed us, al-Anṣārī narrated to us, from ʿAwf, from Khilās, that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) would not amputate the hand in the case of snatching (al-daghrah), but he would amputate the hand in the case of theft committed secretly. [Weak]"
"17302","(17296) Abū Saʿīd bin Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad bin Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ bin Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Mālik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, that Marwān bin al-Ḥakam was brought a man who had committed a pilfering theft (ikhtalasa matāʿan), and he intended to amputate his hand. So he sent to Zayd bin Thābit to ask him about that. Zayd said: ""There is no amputation in the case of pilfering (al-khulsah).""

Mālik said: ""The ruling according to us is that there is no amputation in the case of pilfering.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Likewise, whoever borrows an item and then denies it, or a trust (wadīʿah) was in his possession and he denied it — there is no amputation upon him in either case."" [Authentic]"
"17303","(17297) The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: As for the ḥadīth narrated regarding the borrowed item (al-ʿāriyah), it is what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Aḥmad bin Jaʿfar informed us, ʿAbd Allāh bin Aḥmad narrated to us, my father narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""A woman from Banū Makhzūm used to borrow items and then deny them. So the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) ordered that her hand be amputated."" And she mentioned the ḥadīth regarding the intercession of Usāmah bin Zayd and the disapproval of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and at the end of it she said: ""So the hand of the Makhzūmī woman was amputated.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd bin Ḥumayd from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. Thus said Maʿmar from al-Zuhrī. [Authentic]"
"17304","(17298) Likewise, Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr bin Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Muḥammad bin Yaḥyā bin Fāris narrated to us, Abū Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, from al-Layth, Yūnus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: ʿUrwah used to narrate that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""A woman — meaning she borrowed jewellery on the tongues of people who were known, while she herself was not known — then she sold it and was taken. She was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he ordered that her hand be amputated. She is the one regarding whom Usāmah bin Zayd interceded, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said about her what he said.""

However, ʿAbd Allāh bin Wahb contradicted him from Yūnus, saying: from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her): ""The affair of the woman who stole during the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) during the Conquest of Makkah greatly distressed Quraysh."" Then he mentioned the ḥadīth, and its mention has passed earlier. [Authentic]"
"17305","(17299) Likewise, ʿAbd Allāh bin al-Mubārak narrated it from Yūnus from al-Zuhrī. He said: ʿUrwah bin al-Zubayr informed me: ""A woman stole during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)"" — and he mentioned the ḥadīth up to his words: ""Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) ordered regarding that woman, so her hand was amputated. Her repentance thereafter became excellent, and she married."" ʿĀʾishah said: ""She used to come to me after that, and I would convey her need to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him).""

Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us of it, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me, al-Ḥasan bin Sufyān informed us, Ḥibbān narrated to us, from Ibn al-Mubārak, with that.

And with its meaning, Shabīb narrated it from Yūnus, except that he attributed the end of it through al-Zuhrī from al-Qāsim bin Muḥammad from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) regarding the repentance.

And al-Layth bin Saʿd narrated it from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her): ""The affair of the Makhzūmī woman who stole greatly distressed Quraysh."" Then he mentioned the ḥadīth up to his words: ""By Allah, if Fāṭimah, the daughter of Muḥammad, were to steal, I would amputate her hand."" And its mention has passed earlier. [Authentic]"
"17306","(17300) And Abū al-Zubayr narrated it from Jābir: ""A woman from Banū Makhzūm stole, and she was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). She sought refuge with Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'By Allah, if Fāṭimah were to steal, I would amputate her hand.' So her hand was amputated.""

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad bin Ṣāliḥ bin Hānīʾ informed us, Ibrāhīm bin Muḥammad al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us, Salamah bin Shabīb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan bin Muḥammad bin Aʿyan narrated to us, Maʿqil narrated to us, from Abī al-Zubayr, from Jābir, and he mentioned it. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Salamah bin Shabīb. [Authentic]"
"17307","(17301) And Masʿūd bin al-Aswad narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he said in it: ""A velvet cloth (qaṭīfah) was stolen from the house of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him).""

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad bin Yaʿqūb informed us, Abū Zurʿah informed us, Aḥmad bin Khālid informed us, Muḥammad bin Isḥāq narrated to us, from Muḥammad bin Ṭalḥah bin Yazīd bin Rukānah, from his mother, from ʿĀʾishah bint Masʿūd bin al-Aswad, from her father Masʿūd, who said: ""When that woman stole the velvet cloth from the house of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), we considered it grave, and she was a woman from Quraysh. We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and spoke to him"" — and he mentioned the ḥadīth regarding the offer of ransom, the intercession, and the amputation.

As for the narration of al-Layth from Yūnus from al-Zuhrī regarding the borrowed item (al-ʿāriyah), it was only narrated by Abū Ṣāliḥ from al-Layth. However, Ibn Wahb and Ibn al-Mubārak contradicted him, and their narration is more deserving of authenticity than the narration of Abū Ṣāliḥ. As for the narration of Maʿmar from al-Zuhrī, it is singular (munfaridah), and the majority (al-ʿadd) is more deserving of preservation than the singular narrator.

And Maʿmar narrated it from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar: ""A Makhzūmī woman used to borrow items and then deny them, so the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) ordered regarding her, and her hand was amputated.""

Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us of it, Abū Bakr bin Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, al-Ḥasan bin ʿAlī and Makhlad bin Khālid al-Maʿnī both said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar narrated to us, and he mentioned it.

Abū Dāwūd said: Juwayriyah narrated it from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, or from Ṣafiyyah bint Abī ʿUbayd. And Ibn ʿAnaj narrated it from Nāfiʿ, from Ṣafiyyah bint Abī ʿUbayd.

The learned Shaykh Aḥmad (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""The ḥadīth differs over Nāfiʿ in its chain of narration. It is possible that the narration of the one who narrated the borrowed item (al-ʿāriyah) is based on his identification of it, and the amputation was the cause of her theft which is reported in the other narrations, so they are not contradictory. The meaning of the report would be: A Makhzūmī woman used to borrow items and then deny them — as Maʿmar narrated — and she stole — as others narrated — so her hand was amputated, meaning due to the theft. And Allah knows best."" [Weak]"
"17308","(17302) Abū Naṣr bin Qatādah informed us, Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawī informed us, Aḥmad bin Najdah informed us, Saʿīd bin Manṣūr narrated to us, Ḥammād bin Zayd narrated to us, from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm.

(Ḥ) And Saʿīd narrated to us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm, from Hammām, from ʿAmr bin Sharḥbīl, that Maʿqil bin Muqarrin asked Ibn Masʿūd and said: ""My slave stole a cloak (qabāʾ) from me."" He said: ""Your wealth has stolen from one another; there is no amputation upon him."" And this was the view of Ibn ʿAbbās. [Authentic]"
"17309","(17303) Abū Zakariyyā bin Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad bin Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ bin Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrānjānī informed us, Abū Bakr bin Jaʿfar informed us, Muḥammad bin Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mālik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, from al-Sāʾib bin Yazīd, that ʿAbd Allāh bin ʿAmr bin al-Ḥaḍramī brought a slave of his to ʿUmar bin al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) and said to him: ""Amputate the hand of this one, for he has stolen."" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: ""What did he steal?"" He said: ""He stole a mirror belonging to my wife, the price of which was sixty dirhams."" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Release him, for there is no amputation upon him. Your servant has stolen your belongings."" [Authentic]"
"17310","(17304) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl bin Khamīruwayh informed us, Aḥmad bin Najdah informed us, Saʿīd bin Manṣūr narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Mughīrah narrated to us, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to say: ""There is no amputation upon one who steals from the public treasury (bayt al-māl)."" [Authentic]"
"17311","(17305) Abū Ḥāzim informed us, Abū al-Faḍl informed us, Aḥmad informed us, Saʿīd narrated to us, Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us, Simāk ibn Ḥarb narrated from Ibn ʿAbīd ibn al-Abraṣ, who said: I witnessed ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) in al-Raḥbah while he was distributing the khums among the people. A man from Ḥaḍramawt stole an iron shield from the goods, and he was brought to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: ""There is no amputation for him; he is a traitor, and he has a share [in the khums].""

And al-Thawrī narrated it from Simāk from Dithār ibn Yazīd ibn ʿAbīd ibn al-Abraṣ, who said: ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought a man, and he mentioned it. [Weak]"
"17312","(17306) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī said: Abū Yūsuf informed us, one of our shaykhs informed us from Maymūn ibn Mihrān from the Prophet (ﷺ) that a slave from the slaves of the khums stole from the khums, and he did not amputate his hand, and he said: ""The property of Allah — some of it is in some."" [Weak]"
"17313","(17307) And it has been narrated as a connected narration with a chain containing weakness. Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith the jurist informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us, Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us, Jubārah narrated to us, Ḥajjāj ibn Tamīm narrated from Maymūn ibn Mihrān from Ibn ʿAbbās that a slave from the slaves of the khums stole from the khums, and the matter was raised to the Prophet (ﷺ), and he did not amputate his hand, and he said: ""The property of Allah — some of it stole from some."""
"17314","(17308) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Aṭāʾ informed us, Saʿīd — that is Ibn Abī ʿArūbah — narrated from Qatādah from Anas ibn Mālik that a group from ʿUkl and ʿUraynah came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we are people of livestock and not people of farmland, and the city has become disagreeable to us."" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ordered a herd of camels and provisions for them, and commanded them to go out and drink from their milk and urine. They set out until, when they were in the vicinity of al-Ḥarrah, they killed the shepherd of the Prophet (ﷺ) and drove away the herd, and they apostatized after their Islam. The Prophet (ﷺ) sent in pursuit of them, and he commanded regarding them, so their hands and feet were cut off, their eyes were gouged out with fire, and they were left in the vicinity of al-Ḥarrah until they died in that state.

Qatādah said: It was mentioned to us that this verse was revealed about them, meaning: ""The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger and strive upon earth to cause corruption is that they be killed or crucified"" (al-Māʾidah 33), the verse.

Qatādah said: And it reached us that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to urge charity in his sermons after that and forbid mutilation.

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī ʢArūbah. [Authentic]"
"17315","(17309) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed me, ʿAmr narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl from Abū al-Zinād from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUbayd Allāh — Aḥmad meaning Ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that some people raided the camels of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and drove them away, apostatized from Islam, and killed the shepherd of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He sent in their pursuit, and they were captured, so he cut off their hands and feet and gouged out their eyes. He said: And the verse of al-Muḥārabah was revealed concerning them, and they are those about whom Anas ibn Mālik informed al-Ḥajjāj when he asked him. [Weak]"
"17316","(17310) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Sarḥ narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed me, al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān from Abū al-Zinād that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) cut off the hands of those who stole his milking camels and gouged out their eyes with fire, Allah reproached him for that, so Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: ""The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger and strive upon earth to cause corruption is that they be killed or crucified"" (al-Māʾidah 33), the verse. The statement of Qatādah and Abū al-Zinād and others regarding the revelation of the verse concerning them is a mursal narration. [Weak]"
"17317","(17311) And Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us, al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us, ʿAffān narrated to us, Hammām narrated from Qatādah, who said: Ibn Sīrīn narrated to me that this was before the ḥudūd were revealed, meaning what was done to the ʿUraynīs. [Weak]"
"17318","(17312) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmrūyah al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Sābiq narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr from ʿĀʾishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""It is not permissible to kill a Muslim who testifies that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah, except in one of three cases: a married person who commits adultery, a person who kills and is killed in retaliation, and a person who goes out waging war against Allah and His Messenger — he is killed or crucified or exiled from the land."" [Authentic]"
"17319","(17313) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Ibrāhīm narrated from Ṣāliḥ, the mawlā of al-Tawʾamah, from Ibn ʿAbbās regarding highway robbers: ""If they kill and take the property, they are killed and crucified. If they kill and do not take the property, they are killed and not crucified. If they take the property and do not kill, their hands and feet are cut off from opposite sides. If they terrorize the road and do not take any property, they are exiled from the land."" And Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Yaḥyā has another chain for this. [Weak]"
"17320","(17314) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith the jurist both said: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Fārisī narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from Ibrāhīm from Dāwūd from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: This verse was revealed regarding the muḥārib: ""The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger"" (al-Māʾidah 33). When one commits highway robbery, kills, and takes the property, he is crucified. If he kills and does not take property, he is killed. If he takes the property and does not kill, he is cut off from opposite sides. If he flees and evades them, that is his exile. [Weak]"
"17321","(17315) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Kāmil al-Qāḍī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Saʿd ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Aṭiyyah narrated to us — my father narrated to me — my paternal uncle narrated to me — my father narrated to me — from his father — from Ibn ʿAbbās, regarding His statement {إِنَّمَا جَزَاءُ الَّذِينَ يُحَارِبُونَ اللَّهَ وَرَسُولَهُ} [Al-Māʾidah: 33], the verse: He said: If he wages battle and kills, then upon him is execution, if he is overpowered before his repentance. And if he wages battle, takes wealth, and kills, then upon him is crucifixion, if he is overpowered before his repentance. And if he wages battle and takes wealth but does not kill, then upon him is the amputation of the hand and foot from opposite sides, if he is overpowered before his repentance. And if he wages battle and terrorizes the road, then upon him is banishment, and his banishment is that he be sought.

And ʿUthmān ibn Aṭāʾ narrated from his father from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: If he is found having taken wealth and having shed blood, he is killed and crucified. [Very weak (ḍaʿīf jiddan)]"
"17322","(17316) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us — ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Aṭāʾ narrated to us — from Saʿīd — from Qatādah, that he said regarding this verse {إِنَّمَا جَزَاءُ الَّذِينَ يُحَارِبُونَ اللَّهَ وَرَسُولَهُ وَيَسْعَوْنَ فِي الْأَرْضِ فَسَادًا} [Al-Māʾidah: 33], the verse: He said: There are four ḥudūd which Allah has revealed. As for one who wages battle, sheds blood, and takes wealth, then upon him is crucifixion. As for one who wages battle and sheds blood but does not take wealth, then upon him is execution. As for one who wages battle and takes wealth but does not shed blood, then upon him is banishment.

And that was narrated from Qatādah from Muwariq. And we received it from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr and Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: The difference in their ḥudūd corresponds to the difference in their actions, according to what Ibn ʿAbbās said, if Allah wills. [Authentic (ṣaḥīḥ)]"
"17323","(17317) As an evidentiary basis, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us — Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭūsī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ḥammād narrated to us — Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us — from al-Aʿmash — from ʿAbdullāh ibn Murrah — from Masrūq — from ʿAbdullāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The blood of a person who bears witness that there is no deity but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah is not lawful to shed except for three reasons: the married adulterer (al-thayyib al-zānī), a life for a life (al-nafs bi-l-nafs), and the one who abandons his religion and separates from the community (al-tārik li-dīnihī al-mufāriq li-l-jamāʿah).""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, from Abū Muʿāwiyah, and al-Bukhārī transmitted it from another chain from al-Aʿmash. [Authentic (ṣaḥīḥ)]"
"17324","(17318) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Mālik narrated to us — from Abū al-Zinād, that a governor under ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz captured some brigands who had not killed anyone. He wished to execute or amputate them, so he wrote to ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz concerning that. ʿUmar wrote back to him: ""If only you had taken the lighter course regarding them.""

And Ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated from his father, saying regarding this story: He had killed one of them, and in his response he said: ""Why, when you interpreted this verse regarding them and considered them its people, did you not take the lighter course and reject the killing?"" [Weak (ḍaʿīf)]"
"17325","(17319) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — from Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was informed from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, who said: Whoever wages battle is a muḥārib (combatant against Allah and His Messenger).

Saʿīd said: If he sheds blood, he is killed. If he sheds blood and takes wealth, he is crucified, for crucifixion is more severe. If he takes wealth but does not shed blood, his hand and foot are amputated from opposite sides, due to His statement {"
"17326","(17320) He said: And Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us — ʿAbadah ibn Sulaymān narrated to us — from Hishām — from his father, regarding a man who has incurred the ḥadd penalties and then comes as a repentant: He said: The ḥadd penalties are carried out upon him. [Authentic (ṣaḥīḥ)]"
"17327","(17321) He said: And Abū Bakr narrated to us — Jarīr narrated to us — from Mughīrah — from Ḥammād — from Ibrāhīm, regarding a man who blocks the road, raids, and then returns as a repentant: The ḥadd is carried out upon him, and his repentance is between him and his Lord.

And it was narrated from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding the acceptance of the repentance of the muḥārib, in opposition to the statement of these scholars. And Allah knows best. [Authentic (ṣaḥīḥ)]"
"17328","(17322) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me by way of authorization (ijāzah) — Abū al-Walīd narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad, meaning Abū ʿAmr al-Ḥīrī, narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us — from Sufyān — from Ashʿath ibn Sawwār — from al-Shaʿbī, that ʿUthmān appointed Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī (may Allah be pleased with him). When he prayed Fajr, a man from Murād came and said: ""This is the place of the one who seeks refuge and repents. I am so-and-so son of so-and-so, one of those who waged war against Allah and His Messenger. I have come in repentance before you are able to overpower me."" Abū Mūsā said: ""He has come in repentance before you are able to overpower him, so let him be treated with nothing but goodness."" And he mentioned the hadith. [Weak (ḍaʿīf)]"
"17329","(17323) And as an evidentiary basis, Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Abī Hāshim al-ʿAlawī and ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Najjār al-Muqriʾ in Kūfah informed us — they both said: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Gharzah narrated to us — ʿAmr ibn Ḥammād narrated to us — from Asbāṭ ibn Naṣr — from Simāk — from ʿAlqamah ibn Wāʾil — from his father Wāʾil ibn Ḥujr, who claimed that a man assaulted a woman in the darkness of early morning while she was heading toward the mosque. She called for help from a man who passed by her, and her companion fled. Then a group of people passed by her, and she called them for help. They caught the one who had helped her, while the other man escaped. They went and brought him to her, saying: ""I am the one who helped you; the other man has gone."" She said: ""He is the one who assaulted me."" They brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She informed him that he had assaulted her, and the people informed him that they had caught him while he was fleeing, saying: ""I was only trying to help her against her companion, but these people caught me."" She said: ""He is lying; he is the one who assaulted me."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Take him away and stone him."" A man from among the people stood and said: ""Do not stone him; stone me, for I am the one who did that to her."" He confessed. So three people gathered before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: the one who had assaulted her, the one who had helped her, and the woman. He said to her: ""As for you, Allah has forgiven you."" And he spoke kindly to the one who had helped her. ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Shall I stone the one who confessed to zinā?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""No, for he has repented to Allah — I reckon it to be a repentance that, if the people of Madīnah or the people of Yathrib were to offer it, it would be accepted from them."" And he released them.

And Isrāʾīl narrated from Simāk, and he said in it: They brought the Prophet ﷺ, and when he commanded regarding him, her companion who had assaulted her stood up. And he mentioned the hadith.

Based on this narration, it is possible that he only ordered his taʿzīr (discretionary punishment), and it is possible that they testified against him regarding zinā but erred in that, until her companion stood up and confessed to zinā. His confession was like the confession of Māʿiz, al-Juhaynīyah, and al-Ghāmidīyah, and their ḥadd was not dropped, and their hadiths are more numerous and well-known. And Allah knows best. [Weak (ḍaʿīf)]"
"17330","(17324) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Mihrān ibn Khālid narrated to us — ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā informed us — Isrāʾīl — (ḥ) and Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — ʿAbbād ibn Mūsā al-Khuttalī narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — from Isrāʾīl — from Abī Isḥāq — from ʿAmr — from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When the prohibition of wine was revealed, ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Allah, make clear to us regarding wine a clear exposition."" So the verse in al-Baqarah was revealed: {"
"17331","(17325) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Marwazī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn narrated to us, from his father, from Yazīd al-Naḥwī, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The verses {O you who believe, do not approach prayer while you are intoxicated} [al-Nisāʾ 43] and {They ask you about wine and gambling. Say, ""In them is great sin and [yet, some] benefit for people""} [al-Baqarah 219] were abrogated by what was revealed in al-Māʾidah: {Indeed, wine, gambling, [sacrificing on] stone altars [to other than Allah], and divining arrows are but defilement from the work of Satan, so avoid it} [al-Māʾidah 90]. [Weak]"
"17332","(17326) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Munādī narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Simāk, from Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd, from Saʿd, who said: ""Four verses were revealed concerning me,"" and he mentioned the hadith. He said: ""A man from the Anṣār prepared food and invited us, and we drank wine before it was prohibited, until we became intoxicated. Then we boasted among ourselves. The Anṣār said, 'We are superior,' and Quraysh said, 'We are superior.' A man from the Anṣār took the jawbone of a camel and struck Saʿd's nose with it, splitting it. Thus Saʿd's nose remained split. Then the verse of wine was revealed: {Indeed, wine, gambling, [sacrificing on] stone altars [to other than Allah], and divining arrows are but defilement from the work of Satan, so avoid it, that you may be successful. Satan only wants to cause between you animosity and hatred through wine and gambling and to avert you from the remembrance of Allah and from prayer. So will you not desist?} [al-Māʾidah 90–91].""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Shuʿbah. [Authentic]"
"17333","(17327) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū ʿAlī al-Raffāʾ informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl narrated to us; Rabiʿah ibn Kulthūm narrated to us; my father narrated to me, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""The prohibition of wine was revealed concerning two tribes of the Anṣār. They drank, and when the people became intoxicated, some of them mocked others. When they sobered up, a man would see marks on his face, his head, and his beard, and would say, 'My brother so-and-so did this to me.' They were brothers, and there was no rancour in their hearts. By Allah, had there been a merciful king among them, he would not have done this to me. Thus rancour fell into their hearts. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed this verse: {O you who believe, indeed, wine, gambling, [sacrificing on] stone altars [to other than Allah], and divining arrows are but defilement from the work of Satan, so avoid it, that you may be successful. Satan only wants to cause between you animosity and hatred through wine and gambling and to avert you from the remembrance of Allah and from prayer. So will you not desist?} [al-Māʾidah 90–91].""

Some of the overly meticulous ones said, ""It is an abomination,"" and it was in the belly of so-and-so who was killed on the day of Uḥud. So Allah, glorified is He, revealed this verse: {There is no blame upon those who believe and do righteous deeds for what they have eaten [in the past], if they [now] fear Allah and believe and do righteous deeds} [al-Māʾidah 93], up to His words: {then fear Allah and do good, and Allah loves the doers of good} [al-Māʾidah 93]. [Fair]"
"17334","(17328) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī al-Ḥāfiẓ in Baghdad informed me; it was read to Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī: Abū Yaʿlā informed us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us; Thābit narrated to us, from Anas, who said: ""I was serving the people drinks on the day wine was prohibited, in the house of Abū Ṭalḥah, and their drink was nothing but faḍīkh [a beverage of fresh and dried dates]. Then a caller called out. He said, 'Go out and look.' So I went out and there was a caller calling out: 'Indeed, wine has been prohibited.' He said: 'It flowed through the streets of Madīnah.' Abū Ṭalḥah said to me, 'Go out and pour it out.' So I poured it out. They said, or some of them said, 'So-and-so was killed and so-and-so was killed, while it was in their bellies.' I do not know whether this was in the hadith of Anas. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {There is no blame upon those who believe and do righteous deeds for what they have eaten [in the past], if they [now] fear Allah and believe and do righteous deeds} [al-Māʾidah 93].""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Rabīʿ, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from another chain from Ḥammād. [Authentic]"
"17335","(17329) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Mālik ibn Anas informed me, from Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: ""I was serving Abū ʿUbaydah, Abū Ṭalḥah, and Ubayy ibn Kaʿb a drink of faḍīkh and dates. Then a person came to them and said: 'Indeed, wine has been prohibited.' Abū Ṭalḥah said, 'O Anas, get up to these jars and break them.' So I got up to a stone mortar of ours and struck them with its base until they broke."" [Authentic]"
"17336","(17330) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad al-Shaʿrānī narrated to us; my grandfather narrated to us; Ibn Abī Uways narrated to me; Mālik narrated it with its chain, similarly, except that he said, ""Then a person came to them.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways, and Muslim narrated it from Abū al-Ṭāhir from Ibn Wahb. [Authentic]"
"17337","(17331) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdad informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham al-Dayrāqūlī narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān al-Ḥakam ibn Nāfiʿ informed me; Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah informed me, from al-Zuhrī, who said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib informed me that he heard Abū Hurayrah say: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was brought, on the night of his Night Journey to Jerusalem, two vessels, one of wine and one of milk. He looked at them and then took the milk. Jibrīl, peace be upon him, said: 'Praise be to Allah, who guided you to the natural disposition (fiṭrah). Had you taken the wine, your nation would have gone astray.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān. [Authentic]"
"17338","(17332) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān informed us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""It reached ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man sold wine. He said: 'May Allah fight so-and-so who sold wine! Did he not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""May Allah fight the Jews. Fats were prohibited to them, so they melted them down and sold them.""'""

Both [al-Bukhārī and Muslim] narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah. Other reports concerning the prohibition of its sale have already been mentioned in the book of sales. [Authentic]"
"17339","(17333) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Mālik informed me, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that men from the people of Iraq said to him: ""We purchase fruit of date palms and grapes and press them into wine, and then we sell it."" ʿAbd Allāh said: ""I call Allah as witness over you, and His angels, and those among the jinn and mankind who hear, that I do not command you to sell it, nor to buy it, nor to press it, nor to serve it, for it is an abomination from the work of Satan."" [Authentic]"
"17340","(17334) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sharīḥ and Ibn Lahīʿah and al-Layth ibn Saʿd informed me, from Khālid ibn Yazīd, from Thābit ibn Yazīd al-Khawlānī, who informed him that he had an uncle who sold wine and gave charity from its proceeds. He forbade him from it, but he did not desist. So he came to Madīnah and met Ibn ʿAbbās and asked him about wine and its price. He said: ""It is forbidden, and its price is forbidden."" Then he said: ""O community of the nation of Muhammad ﷺ! If there were a book after your book and a prophet after your prophet, it would have been revealed to you as it was revealed to those before you, and this would not have been deferred from your affair until the Day of Resurrection. And by my life, it is most severe upon you.""

Thābit said: ""Then I met ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar and asked him about the price of wine. He said: 'I will inform you about wine. I was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the mosque. While he was sitting, he wrapped his garment around his knees, then he said: ""Whoever has anything of this wine, let him bring it."" So they began to bring it. One of them would say, ""I have a raqīq [large water-skin],"" and another would say, ""I have a ziqq [leather vessel],"" or whatever Allah willed that he had. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Gather it at such-and-such place in al-Baqīʿ, then inform me."" They did so, then they came to him. He stood up, and I stood with him. I walked on his right side, leaning on him. Then Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, caught up with us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ moved me to his left side and placed Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, in my place. Then ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, caught up with us. He moved me again and placed him on his left side. He walked between the two of them until he stood over the wine. He said to the people: 'Do you recognize this?' They said, 'Yes, O Messenger of Allah, this is wine.' He said: 'You have spoken truly.' He said: 'Indeed, Allah has cursed wine, its presser, the one for whom it is pressed, its drinker, its server, its carrier, the one to whom it is carried, its seller, its buyer, and the one who consumes its price.' Then he called for a knife and said, 'Sharpen it.' They did so. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took it and tore open the leather vessels with it. The people said, 'There is benefit in these vessels.' He said: 'Yes, but I do this only out of anger for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, because of His wrath that is in it.' ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said, 'I will suffice you, O Messenger of Allah.' He said, 'No.'""

Ibn Wahb said: ""Some of them add to others in the narrative of the hadith.""

He said: Ibn Lahīʿah informed me that Abū Ṭuʿmah narrated to him that he heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrating this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. [Weak]"
"17341","(17335) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Sharīk reported from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĪsā, from Abī Ṭuʿmah, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Wine has been cursed, and so has its drinker, its server, its presser, its one for whom it is pressed, its carrier, its one for whom it is carried, its seller, and the one who consumes its price."" [Ḥasan li-ghayrihi]"
"17342","(17336) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik reported from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever drinks wine in this world and then does not repent from it, it shall be forbidden to him in the Hereafter."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17343","(17337) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā said: ""I read it before Mālik, and he mentioned it similarly, except that he did not mention repentance (al-tawbah)."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17344","(17338) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us by dictation, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan by recitation; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me that ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb narrated to him from his father, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: ""Whoever misses one prayer due to intoxication, it is as though the entire world and all that is upon it were taken from him, and it is stripped away from him. And whoever misses four prayers due to intoxication, it becomes a right upon Allah to give him to drink from Ṭīnat al-Khabāl."" It was said: ""And what is Ṭīnat al-Khabāl?"" He said: ""The pus of the people of Hellfire."" [Ḥasan]"
"17345","(17339) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Yūnus ibn Yazīd reported from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām narrated to me that his father said: I heard ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: ""Avoid wine, for it is the mother of all abominations. There was a man from among those who came before you who used to worship and seclude himself from people. A wayward woman became infatuated with him, so she sent her maidservant to him, saying: 'We invite you to give testimony.' So he entered with her, and she began, every time he entered a door, to close it behind him, until he reached a beautiful woman who had a young boy and a vessel of wine with her. She said: 'By Allah, I did not invite you to give testimony; rather, I invited you so that you may lie with me, or kill this young boy, or drink this wine.' So she gave him a cup to drink, and he said: 'Give me more,' and he did not cease until he lay with her and killed the soul. So avoid wine, for it and faith never coexist together except that one of them is about to expel its companion."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17346","(17340) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Sufyān reported from ʿAmr, from Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿdah, who said: ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Beware of wine, for it is the key to every evil. A man was brought and it was said to him: 'Either tear up this book, or kill this boy, or lie with this woman, or drink this cup, or prostrate to the cross.' He did not see anything in these matters easier than drinking the cup. So when he drank it, he prostrated to the cross, killed the boy, lay with the woman, and tore up the book."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17347","(17341) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah al-Anṣārī informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us; Ayyūb reported from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Every intoxicant is wine (khamr), and every intoxicant is forbidden. Whoever drinks wine in this world and dies while being addicted to it, without having repented from it, he shall not drink it in the Hereafter."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Rabīʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17348","(17342) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad reported from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yasār, that he heard Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Three persons — Allah shall not look at them on the Day of Resurrection: the one who is disobedient to his parents, the habitual drinker of wine, and the one who reminds others of his favours."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17349","(17343) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us; Shuʿbah reported from Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād, from Mujāhid, from Abī Saʿīd, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""The one who reminds others of his favours, the one who is disobedient to his parents, and the habitual drinker shall not enter Paradise."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17350","(17344) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUmar al-Ṣanʿānī said: I heard al-Nuʿmān saying, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Every fermented substance is wine (khamr), and every intoxicant is forbidden. Whoever drinks an intoxicant, his prayer shall be diminished for forty mornings. If he repents, Allah shall accept his repentance. But if he returns to it a fourth time, it becomes a right upon Allah to give him to drink from Ṭīnat al-Khabāl."" It was said: ""And what is Ṭīnat al-Khabāl, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""The discharge (ṣadīd) of the people of the Fire. And whoever gives it to a minor who does not know its permissibility from its prohibition, it becomes a right upon Allah to give him to drink from Ṭīnat al-Khabāl."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17351","(17345) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: al-Thawrī narrated from Abū Ḥayyān, from al-Shaʿbī, from Ibn ʿUmar, from ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: ""The prohibition of khamr was revealed, and it is from five things."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17352","(17346) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Musaddad informed us: Yaḥyā narrated from Abū Ḥayyān al-Taymī, who said: ʿĀmir narrated to us from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — stood as a speaker upon the minbar of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: 'To proceed: Indeed, the prohibition of khamr was revealed on the day it was revealed, and it is from five things: from grapes, dates, honey, wheat, and barley. And khamr is that which intoxicates the mind.'""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān. In the narration of al-Thawrī, ""al-zabīb"" (raisins) is mentioned in place of ""al-ʿinab"" (grapes). And Ḥammād likewise reported it from Abū Ḥayyān. And Ibn Abī al-Safar likewise reported it from al-Shaʿbī.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and he alluded to the narration of Ḥammād and mentioned the narration of Ibn Abī al-Safar. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17353","(17347) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb al-Bisṭāmī informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Abū Yaʿlā informed us: Mūsā ibn Ḥayyān narrated —

(Ḥ) And al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed me: Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā and Muḥammad ibn Khallād said: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Abū Ḥayyān al-Taymī narrated — and this is the ḥadīth of Abū Yaʿlā — ʿĀmir narrated to us from Ibn ʿUmar. And al-Ḥasan said: al-Shaʿbī narrated to us from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him. And Abū Yaʿlā said from ʿUmar: ""He stood as a speaker upon the minbar of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: 'To proceed: Indeed, the prohibition of khamr was revealed on the day it was revealed, and it is from five things: from grapes, dates, wheat, barley, and honey. And khamr is that which intoxicates the mind. And there are three matters — O people — about which I wish the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had not departed from us until he had given us a definitive injunction regarding them to which we could refer: al-jadd (the grandfather), al-kalālah (one who leaves neither parents nor children), and certain types of ribā (usury).'""

It was said: ""What do you think about the sixth (intoxicant) that is made in al-Sind, which is called al-jāhil, from which a man drinks a single draught and it overcomes him — it is made from rice?"" He said: ""This did not exist in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Had it existed, he would have forbidden it. Do you not see that it encompasses all intoxicants?"" So he said: ""Khamr is that which intoxicates the mind.""

Abū Bakr said: In this there is evidence that his statement ""and khamr is that which intoxicates the mind"" is from the statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Abī Rajāʾ from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, except that he did not mention his words ""Had it existed, he would have forbidden it"" to the end, for that was said to al-Shaʿbī, and he is the one who responded with it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17354","(17348) Abū al-Fatḥ Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Ḥaffār informed us in Baghdād: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAyyāsh al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam informed us: Isrāʾīl narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Muhājir, from al-Shaʿbī, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Indeed, from dates there is khamr; and from raisins there is khamr; and from wheat there is khamr; and from barley there is khamr; and from honey there is khamr."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17355","(17349) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Mālik ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid informed us: Muʿtamir said: ""I read to al-Fuḍayl, from Abū Ḥurayz, that ʿĀmir narrated to him that al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Indeed, khamr is from juice (al-ʿaṣīr), raisins, dates, wheat, barley, and corn (al-dhurah). And I forbid you from every intoxicant (muskir).""'""

And likewise al-Sarī ibn Ismāʿīl narrated it from ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17356","(17350) And this does not contradict the ḥadīth that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd informed us: My father informed me: al-Awzāʿī informed me: Abū Kathīr said: I heard Abū Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Khamr is from these two trees: the date-palm and the grapevine."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17357","(17351) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Mahrūyah ibn ʿAbbās al-Rāzī informed us: Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī informed us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us: al-Awzāʿī narrated it with its like, except that he said ""from"" (ʿan) twice. Muslim extracted it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Awzāʿī and others. For he affirmed khamr from these two in this ḥadīth, and he affirmed it from these two and from others in what has passed. So all that has been established from him ﷺ is to be accepted whenever reconciliation between all of it is possible. And upon Allah is the granting of success. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17358","(17352) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Sulaymān narrated from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: ""I was standing serving drinks to my paternal uncles while they were drinking a beverage of theirs, when a man entered upon them and said: 'Have you not learned that khamr has been prohibited?' They said: 'O Anas, pour it out!' So I poured it out. By Allah, they never returned to it until they met Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."" He said: ""I asked: 'What was their drink?' He said: 'Fresh dates (al-busr) and dried dates (al-tamr).'""

Abū Bakr ibn Anas said — and Anas was present in the gathering: ""It was their khamr on that day,"" and Anas did not deny that. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17359","(17353) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Musaddad informed us: al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān said: ""I heard my father say: I heard Anas ibn Mālik say: 'I was standing serving the tribe, my paternal uncles, while I was the youngest among them in age, a drink of faḍīkh (unripe dates) of theirs.' A man came and said: 'Indeed, khamr has been prohibited.' They said: 'Pour it out, O Anas.' So I poured it out."" It was said to Anas: ""What was their drink?"" He said: ""Fresh dates (ruṭab) and unripe dates (busr).""

Abū Bakr ibn Anas said — and Anas was a witness: ""It was their khamr on that day,"" and Anas did not deny that. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17360","(17354) He said: ""And one of my companions narrated to me that he heard Anas ibn Mālik say: 'It was their khamr on that day.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā from Muʿtamir. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17361","(17355) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī informed me in Baghdad. I read to him, and he said: I read to Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Ḥamdān, who told you: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī told us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm told us: Hishām told us: Qatādah narrated from Anas, who said: ""I was giving Abū Ṭalḥah, Abū Dujānah, and Suhayl ibn Bayḍāʾ a drink made from a mixture of fresh dates and dried dates when wine was prohibited. I removed it, and I was their cupbearer that day, and I was the youngest among them. We considered it to be wine that day.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm, and Muslim also narrated it through another chain from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17362","(17356) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Razjāhī al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: al-Manīʿī informed me: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr, Muḥammad ibn Isḥkāb, and al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad said: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus told us: Abū Shihāb narrated from Yūnus, from Thābit, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: ""Wine was prohibited upon us at the time it was prohibited, and we hardly found any grape wine at all. The predominant wine of the people was made from dried dates and fresh dates.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17363","(17357) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār told us: Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Bāghandī told us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā told us: Mālik ibn Mighwal narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Wine was prohibited, and there was none of it in Madīnah""—meaning there was no grape wine in Madīnah when it was prohibited.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ through another chain from Mālik ibn Mighwal. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17364","(17358) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū Ṣāliḥ—that is, Khalaf al-Khayyām—told us: Ibrāhīm ibn Maʿqil told us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl told us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bishr told us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed me: Nāfiʿ narrated from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""The prohibition of wine was revealed, and in Madīnah on that day there were five kinds of drinks, among which there was no grape wine.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ thus. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17365","(17359) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Mālik and Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed me.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā told us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī, and Mūsā ibn Muḥammad said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā told us. He said: I read to Mālik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihāb, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said—and in the narration of Ibn Wahb, he heard ʿĀʾishah say: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about al-bitʿ (honey wine), and he said: 'Every drink that intoxicates is forbidden.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and from Ḥarmalah from Ibn Wahb from Yūnus. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17366","(17360) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us orally: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī told us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about al-bitʿ, and he said: 'Every drink that intoxicates is forbidden.'"" Al-bitʿ is nabīdh (fermented drink) of honey.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and ʿAbd from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17367","(17361) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us—I read to him—Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Sharqī told us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hāshim ibn Ḥayyān al-Ṭūsī told us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān told us: Qurrah narrated from Sayyār, Abū al-Ḥakam, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsā, who said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we have drinks—this al-bitʿ and al-mizr made from wheat and barley. What do you command us regarding them?' He said: 'I forbid you from every intoxicant.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17368","(17362) And Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar told us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb told us: Abū Dāwūd told us: Shuʿbah narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī Burdah, from his father, from Abū Mūsā, who said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, a drink is made among us from honey called al-bitʿ, and a drink from barley called al-mizr, and both of them intoxicate.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Every intoxicant is forbidden.'""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah, and al-Bukhārī used the narration of Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī as a supporting witness. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17369","(17363) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār told us: Ismāʿīl ibn al-Faḍl told me: ʿAmr ibn Qusayṭ told us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAmr narrated from Zayd ibn Abī Unaysah, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Burdah. Abū Burdah informed us from Abū Mūsā, who said: ""The Prophet ﷺ sent me and Muʿādh to Yemen and said: 'Go and invite the people to Islam, make things easy and do not make them difficult, give glad tidings and do not repel people.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, give us a ruling regarding two drinks that we used to make in Yemen: al-bitʿ, which is made from honey and left until it becomes strong, and al-mizr, which is made from wheat, barley, and sorghum and left until it becomes strong.' The Prophet ﷺ, who had been given the comprehensive and conclusive words, said: 'I forbid every intoxicant for the purpose of prayer.' Then we departed.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAmr. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17370","(17364) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb told us: Muḥammad ibn Shādhān told us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd told us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad told us: ʿUmārah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, that a man came from Jayshān—and Jayshān is in Yemen—and asked the Prophet ﷺ about a drink that they consumed in their land made from sorghum, called al-mizr. The Prophet ﷺ asked: ""Does it intoxicate?"" They said: ""Yes."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Every intoxicant is forbidden. Indeed, Allah has promised that whoever drinks an intoxicant, He will give him to drink from the ṭīnat al-khabāl."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what is ṭīnat al-khabāl?"" He said: ""The sweat of the people of the Fire, or the juice (extract) of the people of the Fire.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17371","(17365) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us: Saʿdān narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us from Ṭāwūs, from his father, who said: The Prophet ﷺ recited while upon the minbar a verse in which wine was mentioned. Thereupon Abū Wahb al-Jayshānī stood up and asked him about al-mizr. He ﷺ said: ""What is al-mizr?"" He replied: ""A drink made from grain."" The Prophet ﷺ then said: ""Every intoxicant is ḥarām."" Thus it came as a mursal [hadith]. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17372","(17366) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Muqriʾ ibn al-Ḥammāmī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us in Baghdad: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī al-Muthannā narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṭanāfisī narrated to me: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from Mardās ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Yazanī, from Daylam al-Ḥimyarī, who said: ""I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying: 'O Messenger of Allah, we live in a cold land where we perform strenuous labor, and we make a drink from this wheat to strengthen ourselves for our work and against the cold of our land.' He ﷺ asked: 'Does it intoxicate?' I said: 'Yes.' He ﷺ said: 'Then avoid it.' Then I came before him again and said the same thing. He ﷺ asked: 'Does it intoxicate?' I said: 'Yes.' He ﷺ said: 'Then avoid it.' I then said: 'Indeed, the people will not abandon it.' He ﷺ said: 'If they do not abandon it, then kill them.'"" And ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Jaʿfar similarly narrated it from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb. [Ḥasan]"
"17373","(17367) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq both informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Ibn Lahīʿah informed us from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb and ʿAyyāsh ibn ʿAbbās, from Abī al-Khayr, who is Mardās, from Daylam al-Jayshānī, who said: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we live in a very cold land, and we make a drink from wheat. Is it permissible for us, O Prophet of Allah?' He ﷺ said: 'Is it not an intoxicant?' They said: 'Indeed it is.' He ﷺ said: 'Then it is ḥarām.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17374","(17368) Abū Bakr and Abū Zakariyyā both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: Muḥammad informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us that Darrāj Abū al-Samḥ narrated to him that ʿUmar ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to him from Umm Ḥabībah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, that a group of people from Yemen came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he ﷺ taught them the ṣalāh, the sunnahs, and the obligations. Then they said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we have a drink that we make from wheat and barley."" He ﷺ said: ""Al-ghubayrāʾ?"" They said: ""Yes."" He ﷺ said: ""Do not consume it."" Then, after two days, they mentioned it to him again. He ﷺ said: ""Al-ghubayrāʾ?"" They said: ""Yes."" He ﷺ said: ""Do not consume it."" Then, when they were about to depart, they asked him about it. He ﷺ said: ""Al-ghubayrāʾ?"" They said: ""Yes."" He ﷺ said: ""Do not consume it."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17375","(17369) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Khushaysh al-Muqriʾ in Kūfah informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā informed us: Isrāʾīl informed us from Ismāʿīl ibn Sumayʿ, from Mālik ibn ʿUmayr, from Ṣaʿṣaʿah ibn Ṣūḥān, who said: ""I said to ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him—

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Ziyād ibn al-Khalīl narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Sumayʿ narrated to us: Mālik ibn ʿUmayr narrated to us: Ṣaʿṣaʿah ibn Ṣūḥān came to ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: 'Prohibit us from what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prohibited you from.' He ﷺ said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prohibited me from al-dubbāʾ, al-ḥantam, al-naqīr, al-jiʿah, a gold ring, wearing silk, al-qassī, and the red al-mītharah.' The narration of Ibn Khushaysh does not contain al-naqīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17376","(17370) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Zuhayr narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq narrated to us from Hubayrah and from the companions of ʿAlī, from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prohibited al-jiʿah, and al-jiʿah is a drink made from barley until it intoxicates."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17377","(17371) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī in Makkah narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Ayyūb al-Mukharramī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us—

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us in another narration, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us from al-Zuhrī, from Abī Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Every drink that intoxicates is ḥarām."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him. And in the narration of al-Mukharramī, he said: from ʿĀʾishah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Every intoxicant is ḥarām.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn al-Madīnī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, both from Sufyān, upon the wording that al-Shāfiʿī narrated. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17378","(17372) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed me: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā ibn al-Ṭabʿā and Abū al-Rabīʿ al-Zahrānī—

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Naḍr informed me: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Layth al-Rāzī narrated to us: Abū Kāmil narrated to us, they said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Every intoxicant is wine (khamr), and every intoxicant is ḥarām. Whoever drinks wine in the world and dies persisting in it without repenting, will not drink it in the Hereafter.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Rabīʿ and Abī Kāmil. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17379","(17373) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us: Ibn Jurayj narrated to me: Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah informed me from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Every intoxicant is wine (khamr), and every intoxicant is ḥarām.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and al-Ṣaghānī, both from Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17380","(17374) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl informed us—

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ al-Isfarāʾīnī in Isfarāyin informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, both saying: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us from ʿUbaydullāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar—and I do not know except that he attributed it to the Prophet ﷺ—who said: ""Every intoxicant is wine (khamr), and every wine (khamr) is ḥarām.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā, from Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17381","(17375) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Dūlabī narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us: Mālik ibn Anas narrated from Nāʿiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Every intoxicant is khamr (wine), and every intoxicant is ḥarām (forbidden).""

Aḥmad said: Thus Rūḥ narrated it to us, marfūʿ (raised to the Prophet). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17382","(17376) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik narrated from Nāʿiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that he said: ""Every intoxicant is khamr, and every intoxicant is ḥarām.""

Thus narrated all the companions of Mālik from Mālik, mawqūf (stopped at the Companion), except Rūḥ, for he raised it (marfūʿ) in the narration of al-Dūlabī from him. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17383","(17377) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr, who heard it from Saʿīd ibn Abī Burdah, from his father, from his grandfather: that the Prophet (ﷺ) sent him and Muʿādh to Yemen and said to them: ""Give glad tidings and make things easy, and teach, and do not repel (people)."" And I think he said: ""And cooperate with one another."" He said: When Abū Mūsā turned to leave, he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, they have a drink made from honey which they cook, and al-mizr is made from barley."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Everything that intoxicates one from (performing) the ṣalāh is ḥarām.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17384","(17378) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Mūsā informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr informed us: Sufyān narrated from Abū al-Juwayriyyah, who said: I asked Ibn ʿAbbās about al-bādhq (a fermented drink). He said: ""Muḥammad (ﷺ) preceded (in judgment regarding) al-bādhq — whatever intoxicates is ḥarām."" He said: ""The permissible drink is the pure one, not the impure ḥarām one.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Kathīr, except that he said: ""He said: 'The permissible drink is the pure one.' He said: 'There is nothing after the permissible and pure except the ḥarām and impure.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17385","(17379) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram informed us: Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us: Abū Khaythamah narrated to us: Abū al-Juwayriyyah narrated, who said: I said to Ibn ʿAbbās: ""Give me a fatwā, may Allah have mercy on you, regarding al-bādhq."" He said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) preceded (in judgment regarding) al-bādhq — whatever intoxicates is ḥarām."" He said: I said: ""Give me a fatwā, may Allah have mercy on you, regarding al-bādhq, for indeed we drink it."" He said: ""Muḥammad (ﷺ) preceded (in judgment regarding) al-bādhq — whatever intoxicates is ḥarām."" A man from the people said: ""We take grapes and press them, then cook them until they become permissible and pure.""

He said: ""Subḥān Allāh! Subḥān Allāh! Drink the permissible and pure, for there is nothing after the permissible and pure except the ḥarām and impure."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17386","(17380) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Marwān al-Nasāʾī narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAmr al-Raqqī narrated from Zayd ibn Abī Unaysah from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUbayd al-Nakhaʿī from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: A group of people came to him and asked about the sale of khamr, its purchase, and trading in it. Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""Are you Muslims?"" They said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then its sale, its purchase, and trading in it are not permissible for a Muslim. The likeness of one among you who does that is the likeness of the Children of Israel: fats were forbidden upon them, so they did not eat them; rather, they sold them and consumed their prices."" Then they asked about al-ṭilāʾ (cooked grape juice). Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""What is this al-ṭilāʾ of yours, since you ask me about it? Explain to me that which you are asking me about."" They said: ""It is grapes that are pressed, then cooked, then placed in the dīnān (large jars)."" He said: ""What are the dīnān?"" They said: ""Coated jars."" He said: ""Are they muqayyara (pitch-coated)?"" They said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Does it intoxicate?"" They said: ""If a lot of it is consumed, it intoxicates."" He said: ""Every intoxicant is ḥarām."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17387","(17381) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Jarīr narrated from al-Aʿmash from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUbayd, Abū ʿUmar al-Baḥrānī, who said: Ibn ʿAbbās was asked about al-ṭilāʾ. He said: ""Fire does not make anything ḥalāl, nor does it make anything ḥarām."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17388","(17382) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Nashīṭ al-Waʿlānī and ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh that Abū Muslim al-Khawlānī performed Ḥajj and visited ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ). She began asking him about al-Shām and its cold. He kept informing her. She said: ""How do they endure its cold?"" He said: ""O Mother of the Believers, they drink a beverage which is called al-ṭilāʾ."" She said: ""Allah spoke the truth, and my beloved conveyed. I heard my beloved, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), say: 'Indeed, some people from my Ummah will drink khamr while calling it by other than its name.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17389","(17383) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ informed me from Ḥātim ibn Ḥurayth from Mālik ibn Abī Maryam from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ghanm al-Ashʿarī from Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that he said: ""Some people from my Ummah will surely drink khamr while calling it by other than its name. Musical instruments will be played over their heads, and Allah will cause the earth to swallow them and will transform among them into apes and pigs."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17390","(17384) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik narrated from Ibn Shihāb from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd that he informed him that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāḍ (may Allah be pleased with him) came out to them and said: ""I have detected from so-and-so the smell of a drink. He claims that he drank al-ṭilāʾ. I am asking about what he drank. If it intoxicates, I will flog him."" So ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) flogged him with the full ḥadd (prescribed punishment). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17391","(17385) Abū ʿUbayd reported: Abū ʿAbd al-Rahmān al-Sulamī informed us; Abū al-Hasan Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Kārizī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz reported, saying: Abū ʿUbayd said: Numerous narrations have come regarding beverages, with varying names, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and his Companions, and each has an explanation. The first of them is *al-khamr*, which is the juice of grapes that has fermented. There is no difference of opinion among the Muslims regarding its prohibition; the difference of opinion concerns other beverages. Among them is *al-sakar*, which is the infusion of dates upon which fire has not been applied. Regarding it, it is narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: ""Al-sakar is khamr."" Among them is *al-bitʿ*, which is the nabīdh of honey. Among them is *al-jiʿah*, which is the nabīdh of barley. Among them is *al-mizr*, which is made from corn (maize). [Weak]"
"17392","(17386) Abū ʿUbayd said: Abū al-Mundhir Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUmar al-Wāsitī narrated it to me, from Mālik ibn Mihwal, from Umayl, the mu'adhdhin of Ibrāhīm, from al-Shaʿbī, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he explained these four beverages and added: ""Al-khamr is from grapes, and al-sakar is from dates."" Abū ʿUbayd said: Among them is also *al-sukurkah*, and an explanation has been narrated from al-Ashʿarī, who said: ""It is made from corn (maize)."" [Weak]"
"17393","(17387) Abū ʿUbayd said: Hajjāj and Muhammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us, from Hammād ibn Salamah, from ʿAlī ibn Zayd ibn Judʿān, from Safwān ibn Muhriz, who said: I heard Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī delivering a sermon and saying: ""The khamr of the people of Madīnah is made from fresh dates and dried dates; the khamr of the people of Persia is made from grapes; the khamr of the people of Yemen is al-bitʿ, which is made from honey; and the khamr of the people of Abyssinia is al-sukurkah."" Abū ʿUbayd said: Among the beverages is also *al-fadīkh*, which is the juice extracted from fresh dates without fire being applied to it. Regarding it, it is narrated from Ibn ʿUmar: ""It is not called al-fadīkh, but rather al-fadūkh."" [Weak]"
"17394","(17388) It is narrated from Anas that he said: ""The prohibition of khamr was revealed, and there was nothing other than your fadīkh."" Abū ʿUbayd said: It was narrated to me by Ibn ʿUlayyah, from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Suhayb, from Anas. Abū ʿUbayd said: If dates are mixed with fresh dates, that which is called *al-khalīṭayn* (the two mixed) is produced; likewise, if raisins are mixed with dates, it is the same. Among the beverages is *al-munassaf*, which is grape juice that is cooked before it ferments, until half of it evaporates. I have been informed that it causes intoxication; if it causes intoxication, then it is unlawful. If it is cooked until two-thirds of it evaporate and one-third remains, that is *al-tilāʾ* (cooked juice). It is called that because it resembles the *tilāʾ* (tanning liquid) of camels in its thickness and darkness. Some of the Arabs use the term *al-tilāʾ* to refer to khamr itself. It is narrated that ʿUbayd ibn al-Abraṣ said in one of his similitudes:

""It is khamr, called by the name of *al-tilāʾ*,
Just as the wolf is called by the name of Abū Jaʿdah.""

Likewise, *al-bādhq* is also called khamr, as is *al-matabboch* (cooked juice). The hadīth narrated regarding it is from Ibn ʿAbbās, who was asked about *al-bādhq* and said: ""Muhammad preceded *al-bādhq*, and whatever causes intoxication is unlawful."" Ibn ʿAbbās said that only because *al-bādhq* is a Persian word that was Arabized, and he did not recognize it. Abū ʿUbayd mentioned other names and then said: ""These beverages, in my view, are all allusions to the name of khamr, and I do not think they are anything other than included in the hadīth of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him): 'Indeed, a people from my Ummah will drink khamr, calling it by another name.'"" He said: What clarifies this is the statement of ʿUmar ibn al-Khattāb (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Khamr is that which overwhelms the intellect."" [Authentic]"
"17395","(17389) Abū al-Husayn ʿAlī ibn Muhammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād; Abū al-Hasan ʿAlī ibn Muhammad al-Misrī informed us; Yahyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to us; Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; al-Dahhāk ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ashajj, from ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""I forbid you from a small amount of that which a large amount of it causes intoxication."" [Authentic]"
"17396","(17390) Abū al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū Hāmid ibn al-Sharqī informed us; Abū al-Azhar and Muhammad ibn al-Munkhala both said: Abū Damrah narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn Bakr ibn Abī al-Furāt narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whatever a large amount of it causes intoxication, a small amount of it is unlawful."""
"17397","(17391) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Husayn ibn ʿUmar ibn Burhān, Abū al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattān, and Abū Muhammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yahyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkari informed us in Baghdād, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muhammad al-Saffār informed us; al-Hasan ibn ʿArafah narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Muhammad al-Mu'addib narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to me, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whatever a large amount of it causes intoxication, a small amount of it is unlawful."""
"17398","(17392) Abū al-Husayn ibn Bishrān informed us; ʿAlī ibn Muhammad al-Misrī informed us; Rūh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us; Yahyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated, from Abī Maʿshar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whatever a large amount of it causes intoxication, a small amount of it is unlawful."""
"17399","(17393) Abū Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qādī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muhammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muhammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Abī Maʿshar informed me, from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Sālim ibn ʿAbdullāh, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Every intoxicant is khamr, and whatever a large amount of it causes intoxication, a small amount of it is unlawful."""
"17400","(17394) Abū al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us; Abū Hāmid Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Hāfiz informed us; ʿAbd al-Rahmān ibn Bishr narrated to us; Yahyā ibn Saʿīd narrated, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar; ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whatever a large amount of it causes intoxication, a small amount of it is unlawful."" And similarly, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated it from ʿAmr."
"17401","(17395) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us, saying: Abu al-Abbas — al-Asamm — informed us. Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah informed us. Ibn Wahb informed us. 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar informed us from 'Amr ibn Shu'aib, from his father, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and he mentioned it."
"17402","(17396) He said: And Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Shammar ibn Numayr narrated to me from Husayn ibn 'Abd Allah — he being Ibn Dumayrah — from his father, from his grandfather, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), a similar narration. [Da'if]"
"17403","(17397) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri informed us. Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Isfarayini informed us. Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us. 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma' — the brother of Juwayriyah, and he was a righteous man — narrated to us. Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us. Abu 'Uthman al-Ansari narrated to us from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), that he said: ""Every intoxicant is forbidden (haram), and whatever intoxicates from it in the measure of a farq, then a handful of it is forbidden (haram)."" [Sahih]"
"17404","(17398) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Husayn ibn 'Umran ibn Burhan, Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan, and 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar in Baghdad informed us, saying: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us. Al-Hasan ibn 'Arafah narrated to us. Isma'il ibn Ibrahim Ibn 'Ulayyah and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi narrated to us from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Abu 'Uthman, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from 'A'ishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""Every intoxicant is forbidden (haram), and whatever intoxicates in the measure of a farq, then a sip of it is forbidden (haram)."" [Da'if]"
"17405","(17399) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us. Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us. Abu Dawud narrated to us. Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us. Abu Shihab 'Abd Rabbih ibn Nafi' narrated to us from al-Hasan ibn 'Amr al-Fuqaymi, from al-Hakam ibn 'Utaybah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Umm Salamah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade every intoxicant and every substance that induces languor."
"17406","(17400) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me. Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih informed me. Mu'adh ibn Najdah al-Qurashi narrated to us. Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us. Sufyan narrated to us from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from 'Amr ibn Sufyan, from Ibn 'Abbas, that he was asked about this verse: ""And from the fruits of the palm trees and grapevines you take intoxicant and good provision"" [An-Nahl 67]. He said: ""The intoxicant (sakar) is what is forbidden from its fruit, and the good provision (rizq hasan) is what is lawful from its fruit."" [Sahih]"
"17407","(17401) And Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us. Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdus informed us. 'Uthman ibn Sa'id narrated to us. 'Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talhah, from Ibn 'Abbas, regarding His statement: ""You take intoxicant from it"" [An-Nahl 67]: Allah subsequently forbade the intoxicant along with the prohibition of wine (khamr), because it is from it. He said: ""And good provision"" [An-Nahl 67] — that is what is lawful from vinegar (khall), condiments (rubb), nabidh, and similar things, which Allah affirmed and made lawful for the Muslims. And it has been narrated to us from Abu 'Ubayd that he said: ""The intoxicant (sakar) is nabidh of dates (naqi' al-tamr)."" And upon this is the indication of the narration of Ibn Abi Talhah from Ibn 'Abbas, along with the evidence of its inclusion in the prohibition when wine was forbidden, because it is from it. [Da'if]"
"17408","(17402) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us. 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us. Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us. Adam narrated to us. Warqa' narrated to us from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, regarding this verse: He said: ""The intoxicant (sakar) is wine (khamr) before its prohibition, and the good provision (rizq hasan) is its food."" [Hasan]"
"17409","(17403) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr said: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us. Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani informed us. Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us from Shu'bah, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, al-Sha'bi, and Abu Razin, who said regarding this verse: ""And from the fruits of the palm trees and grapevines you take intoxicant and good provision"" [An-Nahl 67]: It is abrogated (mansukhah). [Sahih]"
"17410","(17404) As for the hadith: Abu Zakariyya Yahya ibn Ibrahim al-Muzaki informed us. Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us. Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us. Ja'far ibn 'Awn informed us. Mis'ar informed us from Abu 'Awn.

(And) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us. Abu 'Uthman 'Amr ibn 'Abd Allah al-Basri informed us. Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us. Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us. Sufyan narrated to us from Abu 'Awn, from 'Abd Allah ibn Shaddad, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: ""Wine (khamr) was forbidden in its specific essence — the small amount of it and the large amount — and the intoxicant (sakar) from every drink."" What is meant by the intoxicant (sakar) mentioned therein is that which intoxicates (muskir)."
"17411","(17405) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Sa'id — Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Sufi — 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal — my father — Muhammad ibn Ja'far — Shu'bah — Mis'ab — Abu 'Awn — 'Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had — Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Khamr (intoxicant) was forbidden in its entirety, whether in small or large quantity, and that which intoxicates of every beverage. [Sahih]"
"17412","(17406) And Abu 'Abdillah informed us: the teacher Abu al-Walid — Hassan ibn Muhammad — dictated to us — 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Baghawi — Ahmad ibn Hanbal, who mentioned it with his chain of narration, except that he did not say ""whether in small or large quantity.""

(T) And similarly it was narrated from Ahmad ibn Hanbal by Musa ibn Harun. And similarly it was narrated from 'Ayyash al-'Amiri from 'Abdullah ibn Shaddad from Ibn 'Abbas: ""and that which intoxicates of every beverage."" And upon this the rest of the narrations from Ibn 'Abbas indicate."
"17413","(17407) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz — 'Ali ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mubashshir — Ahmad ibn Sinan — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi — Abu 'Awanah — Layth — 'Ata', Tawus, and Mujahid — from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The small quantity of a substance whose large quantity causes intoxication is haram (forbidden). [Da'if]"
"17414","(17408) As for the hadith which Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us: 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far — Yunus ibn Habib — Abu Dawud — Salama — Simak ibn al-Harith — al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman — his father — Abu Burdah (and he is not Ibn Abi Musa): that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Drink, but do not become intoxicated."" Thus did Abu al-Ahwas Salaym ibn Sulaym narrate it. And it has reached me from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Nasa'i that he said: This is a munkar (objectionable) hadith; Abu al-Ahwas Salaym ibn Sulaym erred in it. We do not know of anyone among the companions of Simak who corroborated him. Abu 'Abd al-Rahman said: Ahmad ibn Hanbal said: ""Abu al-Ahwas used to make errors in this hadith."" Abu 'Abd al-Rahman said: And Abu 'Awanah narrated it from Simak from Qirsafah, a woman among them, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""Drink, but do not become intoxicated."" And this is also not established. And this Qirsafah — it is not known who she is. And the well-known narration from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, contradicts that. [Munkar]"
"17415","(17409) And Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Darqutni al-Hafiz said: ""Abu al-Ahwas was mistaken in its chain of narration and its text."" And other than him narrated from Simak from al-Qasim from Ibn Buraydah from his father: ""And do not drink that which intoxicates."" The Sheikh said: And similarly it was narrated by Muharib ibn Dithar from Ibn Buraydah from his father. [Sahih — as per al-Darqutni]"
"17416","(17410) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far — 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad — Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna — Muhammad ibn Fudayl — Dirar ibn Murrah — Muharib ibn Dithar — Ibn Buraydah — his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I have forbidden you from nabidh (fermented drink) except in a waterskin; so drink from all vessels, but do not drink that which intoxicates."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna. [Sahih]"
"17417","(17411) As for the hadith which Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Isbahani informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz — Abu Sa'id Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ibrahim ibn Miskan al-Marwazi — 'Abdullah ibn Mahmud — al-'Abbas ibn Zurarah — Jarir — al-Hajjaj ibn Artah — Hammad — Ibrahim — Ibn Mas'ud, who said: ""Every intoxicant is haram — it is the drink which intoxicates you."" [Da'if]"
"17418","(17412) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Jarhi in Marw — Yahya ibn Sasawayh — 'Abd al-Karim al-Sukkari — Wahb ibn Zam'ah informed us — Sufyan ibn 'Abd al-Malik, who said: I asked 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak about the hadith of Jarir from Ibn Mas'ud: ""The drink which intoxicates you is haram."" He said: ""This is false (batil)."" [Sahih]"
"17419","(17413) And Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami and Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith informed us, they both said: Abu al-Hasan al-Darqutni said: ""Al-Hajjaj ibn Artah is da'if (weak), and it is only the statement of Ibrahim al-Nakha'i."" And he narrated it with his chain of narration from Mis'ar from Hammad from Ibrahim, as his own statement, with the same meaning. The Sheikh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: And the opposite of that was narrated from Ibrahim, and that is what was narrated by Hasan ibn 'Amr from Fudayl ibn 'Amr from Ibrahim, who said: ""They used to hold the view that whoever drank a beverage and became intoxicated from it, it was not permissible for him to return to it."" [Sahih]"
"17420","(17414) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: the Imam Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq — al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn Ziyad — Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Bukhari, who said: Zakariyya ibn 'Adi said: ""When Ibn al-Mubarak arrived in Kufah, he was afflicted with an illness. So Waqi' and our companions and the Kufans came to him and discussed matters in his presence until the topic of beverages came up. Ibn al-Mubarak began to cite the ahadith of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the companions of the Prophet ﷺ, the Muhajirun and the Ansar, the people of Madinah. They said: 'No, but rather from our hadith.' Ibn al-Mubarak said: 'Al-Hasan ibn 'Amr al-Fuqaymi informed us — Fudayl ibn 'Amr — Ibrahim, who said: ""They used to say: If one becomes intoxicated from a beverage, it is never permissible for him to return to it.""' So they hung their heads. Then Ibn al-Mubarak said to the one beside him: 'Have you seen anything more remarkable than these people? I narrate to them from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, his companions, and the Tabi'un, and they do not care. And I mention the statement of Ibrahim, and they hang their heads.'"" [Sahih]"
"17421","As for the hadith of Anas: Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Birtī al-Qāḍī informed us, ʿAffān informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn al-Muthannā al-ʿAnbarī informed us, ʿAffān informed us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Thābit from Anas who said: ""I used to give the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) to drink from this very cup all kinds of drinks—honey, nabīth, water, and milk.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from ʿAffān. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17422","As for the narration concerning ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him): Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram informed us, Abū al-Naḍr informed us, Abū Khaythamah informed us, Abū Isḥāq narrated from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn who said: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""We used to drink a nabīth that would cut through the flesh of camels in our bellies lest it harm us."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain]"
"17423","As for the description (of preparation): Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, al-Qāsim ibn al-Faḍl informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, ʿImrān ibn Mūsā informed us, Shaybān ibn Farūkh informed us, al-Qāsim informed us, Thumāmah ibn Ḥazn al-Qushayrī informed us, who said: ""I met ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) and asked her about nabīth. ʿĀʾishah called for an Ethiopian slave-girl and said: 'Ask her, for she used to prepare nabīth for the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him).' The Ethiopian girl said: 'I used to prepare nabīth for him in a waterskin at night, tie it up, and hang it. When morning came, he would drink from it.'"" The wording is from the hadith of Shaybān. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān ibn Farūkh. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17424","Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Shādhān and Muḥammad ibn al-Naḍr informed us. Ibn al-Naḍr said: ""He informed us,"" and Ibn Shādhān said: ""He narrated to us."" Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAbd al-Majīd narrated from Yūnus from al-Ḥasan from his mother from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""We used to prepare nabīth for the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) in a waterskin, the top of which was tied, and it had a spout. We would prepare it in the morning, and he would drink from it in the evening. And we would prepare it in the evening, and he would drink from it in the morning.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā."
"17425","Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Musaddad informed us, al-Muʿtamir informed us. He said: ""I heard Shabīb ibn ʿAbd al-Malik narrating from Muqātil ibn Ḥayyān, who said: ʿAmrah narrated to me from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that she used to prepare nabīth for the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) in the morning. When evening came and he had his evening meal, he would drink from it after his supper. If anything remained, I would pour it out or empty it. Then she would prepare nabīth for him at night, and when morning came and he had his morning meal, he would drink from it after his breakfast."" She said: ""We would wash the waterskin in the morning and in the evening."" Her father said to her: ""Twice in one day?"" She said: ""Yes."""
"17426","Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī informed us, Yūsuf ibn Marwān al-Nasāʾī informed us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAmr al-Raqqī narrated from Zayd ibn Abī Unaysah from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUbayd al-Nakhaʿī from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""A group of people came to him""—and he mentioned the hadith. He said: ""Then they asked him about nabīth, and he said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) went on a journey and returned from his journey, and some of his Companions had prepared nabīth for themselves in gourds, green clay jars, and hollowed pumpkin vessels. He ordered them to be poured out.' He then ordered for a waterskin to be brought, and raisins and water were placed in it. Nabīth would be prepared for him at night, and he would drink from it during that day, the following night, and the next day until evening. When evening came, he would drink from it and give it to the servants to drink. If anything remained by morning, he ordered it to be poured out.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Khalaf from Zakariyyā ibn ʿAdī from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAmr. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17427","Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Jarīr narrated from al-Aʿmash from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUbayd, Abū ʿUmar al-Baḥrānī, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""Raisins would be prepared as nabīth for the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) at night in a waterskin. When morning came, he would drink from it during that day, that night, and the following day. When the evening of the third day arrived, he would drink from it or give it to the servants. If anything remained, he would pour it out.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm."
"17428","Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī al-Ṭūsī informed us there, Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us, Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam informed us, Abū Ghassān informed us, Abū Ḥāzim narrated from Sahl ibn Saʿd that when Abū Usayd held his wedding feast, he invited the Prophet (peace be upon him) and his Companions. No food was prepared for them nor presented to them except by his wife, Umm Usayd, who had soaked dates in water overnight in a stone vessel. When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) finished eating, she mashed it and gave it to him to drink.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Sahl ibn ʿAskar from Ibn Abī Maryam. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17429","Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, ʿĪsā ibn Muḥammad informed us, Ḍamrah narrated from al-Shaybānī from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Daylamī from his father, who said: ""We came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, You know who we are and where we are from. To whom do we belong?' He said: 'To Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and to His Messenger.' We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we have grapes—what should we do with them?' He said: 'Dry them into raisins (zabīb).' We said: 'What should we do with the raisins?' He said: 'Prepare nabīth from them for your morning meal and drink it at your evening meal. Prepare nabīth for your evening meal and drink it at your morning meal. Prepare it in small water-skins (shinan) and do not prepare it in large jugs (qullah), for if it is left beyond its proper time, it will turn into vinegar (i.e., become intoxicating).'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17430","Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Abū Ḥuṣayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us, ʿAlī ibn Ḥakīm al-Awdī informed us, Sharīk narrated from Misʿar from Mūsā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Anṣārī from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""When the nabīth of the Prophet (peace be upon him) became too strong, I would put raisins in it to counteract its sourness.""

The Sheikh said: ""And the nabīth of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and other Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) was of a similar nature. Do you not see that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) only permitted al-ṭilāʾ (cooked grape juice) when its intoxication, its harm, and the share of Satan in it had departed?"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17431","(17425) And this is what Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us — Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us — al-Shafi'i informed us — Malik informed us from Dawud ibn al-Husayn from Waqid ibn 'Amr ibn Sa'd ibn Mu'adh and from Salamah ibn 'Awf ibn Salamah — they both informed him from Mahmud ibn Labid al-Ansari: that when 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) arrived in Syria, the people of Syria complained to him about the land's diseases and heaviness, and they said, ""Nothing will set us right except this beverage."" 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""Drink honey."" They said, ""Honey will not set us right."" Then some of the people of the land said, ""Would you like us to make for you from this beverage something that does not intoxicate?"" He said, ""Yes."" So they cooked it until two-thirds of it had evaporated and one-third remained, and they brought it to 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him). 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) dipped his finger into it, then raised his hand, and it stretched along with his finger, and he said, ""This is thick, like the thick residue of camels."" 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) then commanded them to drink it. 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit said to him, ""You have permitted it, by Allah!"" 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""No, by Allah! O Allah, I shall not permit for them anything You have made unlawful for them, nor shall I make unlawful for them anything You have made lawful for them."" [Sahih]"
"17432","(17426) Abu Hazim al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-Fadl ibn Khumayruwayh informed us — Ahmad ibn Najdah informed us — Sa'id ibn Mansur informed us — Isma'il ibn Ibrahim informed us — Hisham ibn Hassan informed us from Muhammad ibn Sirin from 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote: ""Cook your beverage until the share of Satan departs from it, for Satan has two portions and you have one."" [Sahih]"
"17433","(17427) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us — Abu al-Husayn al-Jawzi informed us — Ibn Abi al-Dunya informed us — Abu Kaythama informed us — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi informed us from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar from Zayd ibn Aslam from his father, who said: The nabidh that 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) would drink was prepared for him by steeping raisins in the morning, and he would drink it in the evening; and it was steeped for him in the evening, and he would drink it in the morning, and no durdī (sediment/residue) was placed in it. [Sahih]"
"17434","(17428) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq and al-Hasan ibn Mukram both informed us — 'Umar ibn 'Umar informed us — Shu'bah informed us from Abu Hamzah, their neighbor, who said: I heard Hilal al-Mazini narrating from Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) with a jar containing nabidh, and he forbade me from it, so I broke it. Suwayd said: Steep your nabidh at the beginning of the night and drink it at the end of the night, and steep it at the beginning of the day and drink it at the end of the day. [This is the wording of the hadith of al-Saghani, and in the narration of al-Hasan he said: from Hilal al-Mazini.] [Da'if]"
"17435","(17429) Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Darastawayh informed us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed me — 'Umar ibn al-Haytham al-Mu'adh-dhin informed me — 'Awf ibn Abi Jamilah informed me from Abu al-Qamus Zayd ibn 'Ali, from one of the delegation who came to the Prophet of Allah (peace be upon him) from the delegation of 'Abd al-Qays — if it was not Qays ibn al-Nu'man, for I have forgotten his name — he said: A man among us said, ""O Messenger of Allah, our land is a land of pestilence, and nothing agrees with it except beverages. So what of the vessels is lawful for us, and what is unlawful?"" He said, ""Do not drink in the dubba' (gourd vessel), nor the naqir (hollowed-out wooden vessel), nor the muzaffat (pitch-coated vessel). Drink in the jalal (large leather vessel), or he said, the jild muka' 'alayh (leather vessel sealed at its mouth). If its strength becomes intense, then dilute it with water. If it overcomes you, then pour it out."" The shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: The established narrations in the story of the delegation of 'Abd al-Qays are devoid of this wording, and in this chain of narration there are those whose status is unknown, and Allah knows best.

And it has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) in this story that he said: ""And if you fear its viciousness"" — or he said ""its intensity"" — ""then pour water upon it."" [Da'if]"
"17436","(17430) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Isfahani informed him — 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, Ibn Sa'id, and al-Husayn ibn Isma'il all informed us — Abu al-Ash'ath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam informed us — Nuh ibn Qays informed us from Ibn 'Awn from Muhammad ibn Sirin from Abu Hurayrah from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) that he said to the delegation of 'Abd al-Qays: ""Do not drink in the naqir, nor the muqayyar (pitch-lined vessel), nor the dubba', nor the hantam (sealed clay vessel), nor the mazadah (water-skin). Rather, drink in your water-skins (siqa'), provided it is not intoxicating. And if you fear its viciousness, then pour water upon it."" [This is the wording of Ibn Mani'. And a group narrated it from Nuh ibn Qays without mentioning this wording, so it is as though it is from the statement of some of the narrators. And the breaking with water has been narrated from another route from Abu Hurayrah, and its chain of narration is weak.] [Hasan]"
"17437","(17431) And 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us — 'Uthman ibn 'Umar informed us — Ibn Rajā' informed us — Isra'il informed us from 'Ali ibn Badhimah from Qays ibn Habtar from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, who said: The first to ask the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) about nabidh was 'Abd al-Qays. They came to him and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, we are in a fertile land, and we obtain produce. So command us regarding a beverage."" He said, ""Drink in the vessels (al-asqiya, plural of siqāyah), and do not drink in the jar (al-jar), nor in the dubba', nor the muzaffat, nor the naqir. And I have been forbidden from wine (al-khamr), gambling (al-maysir), and the kūbah (which is the drum), and every intoxicant is unlawful."" They said, ""O Messenger of Allah, what if it becomes intense?"" He said, ""Pour water upon it."" He said: ""What if it becomes intense?"" He said: ""Pour water upon it."" He said on the third or fourth occasion: ""If it becomes intense, then pour it out."" [Hasan]"
"17438","(17432) Abu Hamzah contradicted him regarding Ibn 'Abbas, mentioning the breaking with water as the statement of Ibn 'Abbas. Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed him — Abu 'Umar ibn Matar and Abu al-Hasan al-Sarraj both informed us — Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Sulayman informed us — 'Asim ibn 'Ali informed us — Shu'bah informed me — Abu Hamzah said: Ibn 'Abbas used to seat me on his bed. Then he mentioned the hadith and said: I said, ""Did 'Abd al-Qays used to steep nabidh in a mazadah (water-skin) that was very strong?"" He said, ""If you fear its intensity, then break it with water."" Then he said: ""When 'Abd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)..."" and he mentioned the hadith, but it did not contain the command to break it with water, and that will be addressed, Allah willing. And what is meant by breaking with water in this and other narrations, when one fears its intensity before it reaches the point of intoxication, is indicated by his statement: ""And every intoxant is unlawful."" And the unlawful is not rendered lawful by the introduction of water into it. [Sahih]"
"17439","(17433) And regarding what reaches the point of intoxication, there came what Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — Hisham ibn 'Ammar informed us — Sadaqah ibn Khalid informed us — Zayd ibn Waqid informed us — Khalid ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Husayn informed us from Abu Hurayrah, who said: I knew that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was fasting, so I sought to prepare his fast-breaking with nabidh that I had made in a dubba' (gourd vessel). Then I brought it to him, and it was bubbling. He said, ""Strike this with the wall, for this is the drink of one who does not believe in Allah and the Last Day."" [Sahih]"
"17440","(17434) And Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us — al-Hulwani (meaning Ahmad ibn Yahya) informed us — al-Haytham ibn Kharijah informed us — 'Uthman ibn 'Allaq informed us — Zayd ibn Waqid said: Khalid ibn Husayn, the freedman of 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him), informed me. He said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying — and he mentioned its meaning. [Sahih]"
"17441","(17435) Abū ʿAbdullāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us that al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazyad informed me that my father narrated to me that al-Awzāʿī narrated to me that Muḥammad ibn Abī Mūsā narrated to me that he heard al-Qāsim ibn Mukhaymira reporting that Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, may Allah be pleased with him, came to the Prophet ﷺ with nabīdh in a jar that was fizzing. He said: ""Throw it against the wall, for this is not the drink of one who believes in Allah and the Last Day."" The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""If there were a way to render it lawful by pouring water over it, he would not have commanded its destruction. And Allah knows best.""

I also saw in the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from Thumāma ibn Kilāb from Abī Salamah from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, a marfūʿ narration: ""Do not prepare nabīdh in dubbāʾ, muzaffat, naqīr, or ḥantamah. And do not combine fresh dates and dried dates, or dates and raisins. Whatever else becomes strong for you, dilute it with water."" However, Thumāma ibn Kilāb is majhūl (unknown). The established narration from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr is from Abī Salamah from Abī Qatādah from the Prophet ﷺ, concerning the prohibition of mixing two things (al-khalīṭayn), without this wording. And Allah knows best.

I also saw it in the ḥadīth of ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār from Abī Kathīr al-Suḥaymī from Abī Hurayrah, marfūʿ, except that he said: ""When doubt arises in your drink, pour water over it; cast away its unlawful portion and drink its lawful portion."" This is also ḍaʿīf. ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār became confused in his later years and his memory deteriorated, so he narrated what was not corroborated. ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated it from ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār, and his statement ""when doubt arises in your drink"" was said by Abī Hurayrah, as mentioned by Isḥāq al-Ḥanẓalī in his Musnad. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17442","(17436) As for the ḥadīth that Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us — both saying that ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā al-Bazzāz narrated to us that ʿUmar ibn Shabba narrated to us that ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī al-Muqaddamī narrated from al-Kalbī from Abī Ṣāliḥ from al-Muṭṭalib ibn Abī Wadaʿah al-Sahmī, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ṭawāf around the House on an intensely hot day and asked a group of Quraysh for something to drink, saying: 'Does anyone among you have a drink that he could send for?' So one of them sent to his house, and a slave girl came with a vessel containing nabīdh of raisins. When the Prophet ﷺ saw her, he said: 'Why did she not cover it, even with a stick placed across it?'

When it was brought near him, he detected a strong odor, so he frowned and returned the vessel. The man said: 'O Messenger of Allah, if it is unlawful, we shall not drink it.' He called for a bucket of Zamzam water and poured it over the vessel, saying: 'When your drink becomes strong, do this with it.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17443","(17437) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Tamtām narrated to us that Abī Ḥudhayfah narrated to us that Sufyān narrated from al-Kalbī from Abī Ṣāliḥ from al-Muṭṭalib ibn Abī Wadaʿah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ṭawāf on a hot day and asked for a drink. A vessel of nabīdh was brought to him. When he raised it to his mouth, he frowned and set it aside. The man said: 'O Messenger of Allah, this is the drink of the people of Makkah — is it unlawful?' He remained silent. Then it was brought to him a second time, and he frowned and pushed it away. The man said the same thing. So he called for a large bucket or pail of water and poured it over it, then gave drink to the one beside him and the one to his right, saying: 'Do this with it when it overcomes you.'""

This was narrated only by al-Kalbī, and al-Kalbī is matrūk (abandoned), and Abī Ṣāliḥ Bādhān is ḍaʿīf — their reports are not used as evidence. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17444","(17438) Yaḥyā ibn Yamān narrated it from Sufyān, but he erred in its isnād. Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us that Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Abū Maʿmar narrated to us that Ibn Yamān narrated —

And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us that ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAlī Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān and Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Baḥr al-ʿAṭṭār, both in Baṣrah, said that Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥabīb ibn al-Shahīd narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Yamān narrated from Sufyān from Manṣūr from Khālid ibn Saʿd from Abī Masʿūd al-Anṣārī, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ felt thirsty near the Kaʿbah and asked for a drink. Nabīdh from the siqāyah was brought to him. He smelled it and frowned. He said: 'Bring me a large bucket of Zamzam.' It was poured over it, then he drank. A man said: 'Is it unlawful, O Messenger of Allah?' He said: 'No.'"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Shahīdī. The ḥadīth of Abī Maʿmar is abridged: ""The Prophet ﷺ was asked while in ṭawāf: 'Is it lawful or unlawful?' He said: 'Lawful,'"" meaning the nabīdh. ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar said: ""This ḥadīth is well-known through Yaḥyā ibn Yamān, and it is said that the isnād was reversed for him and became confused with the ḥadīth of al-Kalbī from Abī Ṣāliḥ. Al-Kalbī is matrūk and Abī Ṣāliḥ is ḍaʿīf."" [Munkar]"
"17445","(17439) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us that Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: ""I heard ʿAbdān say: 'I heard Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr say: Ibn Yamān is quick to forget, and his ḥadīth from Sufyān from Manṣūr from Khālid ibn Saʿd from Abī Masʿūd is an error. It is in fact from al-Kalbī from Abī Ṣāliḥ from al-Muṭṭalib ibn Abī Wadaʿah.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17446","(17440) Abū Saʿd informed us that Abī Aḥmad narrated to us that al-Junaydī said: ""Al-Bukhārī said regarding this ḥadīth of Yaḥyā ibn al-Yamān: 'This is not authentic from the Prophet ﷺ.'"" And al-Ashjaʿī and others said from Sufyān from al-Kalbī from Abī Ṣāliḥ from al-Muṭṭalib. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"17447","(17441) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us that Abū al-Ḥasan al-Maḥmūdī narrated to us that Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Abū Mūsā said: ""I mentioned the ḥadīth of Sufyān from Manṣūr regarding nabīdh to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī, and he said: 'Do not narrate this.'"" The Shaykh said: ""ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abān stole it and narrated it from Sufyān, and al-Yasaʿ ibn Ismāʿīl stole it and narrated it from Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb from Sufyān. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abān is matrūk, and al-Yasaʿ ibn Ismāʿīl is ḍaʿīf in ḥadīth."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — from Ibn Mahdī]"
"17448","(17442) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us from Abū al-Ḥasan al-Dāraquṭnī regarding it. And Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated it from Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, in the story of the Prophet's ﷺ ṭawāf and his request for a drink. He said: ""A drink was brought; he drank from it, then he called for water and poured it over it and drank. Then it became strong upon him, so he called for water and poured it over it, then drank two or three times, and said: 'When it becomes strong for you, kill it with water.'"" Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād is ḍaʿīf and is not used as evidence due to his poor memory.

Khālid al-Ḥadhdāʾ narrated from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās the story of the Prophet's ﷺ ṭawāf and his drinking, but he did not mention what Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād mentioned. This addition is known only from the narration of al-Kalbī, as previously stated. Yazīd added that he drank from it before mixing it with water, which contradicts all other narrations. How could it be supposed that the Prophet ﷺ would drink an intoxicating substance — if it were intoxicating, as they claim — before mixing it with water? This indicates that it has no basis. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17449","(17443) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us that Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Sarrāj narrated to us that Mūsā ibn Hārūn narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated to us that Dārim — meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥanafī — said: ""I was present with ʿAṭāʾ when he was asked about nabīdh. He said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Every intoxicant is unlawful.""' I said: 'O Ibn Abī Rabāḥ, these people give us to drink in the mosque.' He said: 'By Allah, I have witnessed it — a man would drink from it and his lips would stick together from its sweetness. But freedom has passed away, and slavery has taken its place, and they have been negligent with it.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17450","(17444) As for the ḥadīth that ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us of — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us that ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar al-Ḍabbī narrated to us that Musaddad narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us that Sulaymān al-Shaybānī narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Malik, the brother of al-Qaʿqāʿ, narrated from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ detected the smell of nabīdh from a man and said: 'What is this smell?'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17451","(17445) 'Alī informed us; Aḥmad informed us; Tamtām narrated to us; 'Abd al-Ṣamad narrated to us; Warqā' narrated from Sulaymān al-Shaybānī, from 'Abd al-Mālik ibn Nāfi', the brother of al-Qa'qā', from Ibn 'Umar, who said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and noticed a smell from him. He said: ""What is this smell?"" He said: ""It is nabīdh (fermented drink)."" He said: ""Send some of it to me."" So he sent it to him, and he found it to be strong. He then called for water and poured it over it, then drank from it and said: ""When your drinks become strong (intoxicating), then dilute them with water.""

It was also narrated by Ismā'īl ibn Abī Khāld from Qurrah al-'Ijli from 'Abd al-Mālik, and he said: ""Then cut their bodies (strength) with water."""
"17452","(17446) 'Alī informed us; Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us; Ja'far ibn Kadhāl narrated to us; 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; Ibn Abī Zā'idah narrated from Ismā'īl ibn Abī Khāld; Qurrah al-'Ijli narrated to me from 'Abd al-Mālik, the brother of al-Qa'qā' ibn Shawr, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: We were with the Prophet ﷺ when a drink was mentioned to him. A cup of it was brought to him, and when he brought it close to his mouth, he disliked its smell and returned it. Some of the people said: ""Is it forbidden, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Return it."" Then he took from it, then called for water and poured it over it, then said: ""Look at these vessels — when their contents become strong, then cut their bodies (strength) with water.""

This is a hadith known through 'Abd al-Mālik ibn Nāfi', and he is an unknown man (majhūl). They differed regarding his name and his father's name. It was said thus, and it was said 'Abd al-Mālik ibn al-Qa'qā', and it was said Ibn Abī al-Qa'qā', and it was said Mālik ibn al-Qa'qā'. [Weak]"
"17453","(17447) Abū Sa'd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn 'Adī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us. He said: I said to Yaḥyā ibn Ma'īn: ""What do you think of the hadith of 'Abd al-Mālik ibn Nāfi' which Ismā'īl ibn Abī Khāld narrates regarding nabīdh?"" He said: ""They consider him weak (yuḍa''ifūnahu).""

He said: And Abū Aḥmad informed us, he said: I heard Ibn Ḥammād say: Al-Bukhārī said: ""'Abd al-Mālik ibn Nāfi', the brother of al-Qa'qā' ibn Shawr, from Ibn 'Umar regarding nabīdh — he was not corroborated in it (lam yutāba' 'alayhi).""

And Abū 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Nasā'ī said: ""'Abd al-Mālik ibn Nāfi' is not well-known (mashhūr), and his hadith is not used as evidence. And the well-known narration from Ibn 'Umar is contrary to his report."" [Authentic as a statement of Ibn Ma'īn]"
"17454","(17448) As for the athar: Abū 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī both informed us, they said: Abū al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn 'Umar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; 'Abd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Abd al-'Azīz narrated to us; Khalaf ibn Hishām narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd, from Sa'īd ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Thaqīf presented 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) with nabīdh, and he found it to be strong. So he called for water and poured it over it two or three times. [Authentic]"
"17455","(17449) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us; 'Abd Allāh ibn Ja'far informed us; Ya'qūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān informed me; Shu'ayb narrated; and al-Ḥajjāj narrated; my grandfather narrated — all from al-Zuhrī. Mu'ādh ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Taymī informed me that his father, 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Uthmān, said: I accompanied 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) to Makkah, and a caravan from Thaqīf presented him with two saṭīḥahs of nabīdh. (A saṭīḥah is larger than an idāwah but smaller than a mizādah.) 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Uthmān said: 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) drank one of them. Al-Ḥajjāj said: It was pleasant. Then milk was presented to him, so he turned away from drinking the second one until what was in it became strong. 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) went to drink from it and found that it had become strong. He said: ""Dilute it with water."" Its strength was — and Allah knows best — due to sourness or sweetness.

It has also been narrated from Nāfī', the freedman of Ibn 'Umar, that 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said to Yarfā': ""Go to our brothers and seek a drink for us from them."" So he went to them and they said: ""We have nothing except this idāwah, and it has changed."" So 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) called for it, tasted it, and his face contracted. Then he called for water, poured it over it, and then drank. Nāfī' said: By Allah, his face did not contract except because it had become sour (takhallala). [Good]"
"17456","(17450) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us; Abū al-Ḥusayn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ja'far al-Jawzī informed us; Ibn Abī al-Dunyā narrated to me; Ibrāhīm ibn Sa'īd narrated to us; Maḥbūb ibn Mūsā informed us; 'Abd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Usāmah ibn Zayd, from Nāfī', who said: By Allah, 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) did not contract his face from the idāwah when he tasted it except because it had become sour (takhallala).

And it has been narrated from Sa'īd ibn al-Musayyib from 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) something similar to the narration of Nāfī'. And it is mentioned from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim from 'Utbah ibn Farqad, who said: The nabīdh that 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) drank had become sour. And it is mentioned from Zayd ibn Aslam that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when the nabīdh became sour for them, they would dilute it with water. [Weak]"
"17457","(17451) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn 'Abdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us; 'Abd Allāh ibn Aḥmad narrated to us; Yaḥyā — that is, Ibn Ma'īn — narrated to us; al-Mu'tamir — that is, Ibn Sulaymān — narrated to me; my father said: ""You narrated to me from 'Ubayd Allāh ibn 'Umar, who said: 'Umar only diluted the nabīdh because of its excessive sweetness.'"" [Weak]"
"17458","(17452) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr al-Jarrāḥī informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Sāsūyah narrated to us; 'Abd al-Karīm al-Sukkari narrated to us; Wahb ibn Zam'ah narrated to me; 'Alī al-Bāshānī narrated to me; he said: 'Abd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak said: 'Ubayd Allāh ibn 'Umar said to Abū Ḥanīfah regarding nabīdh. Abū Ḥanīfah said: ""We took it from your father."" He said: ""And what did my father say?"" He said: ""When you have doubt about it, then dilute it with water."" 'Ubayd Allāh al-'Umarī said: ""When you are certain about it and have no doubt, what do you do?"" Abū Ḥanīfah fell silent. [Authentic]"
"17459","(17453) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān in Baghdad informed us; Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Jawzī informed us; Ibn Abī al-Dunyā narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Samīnah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; he said: I heard Sulaymān al-Taymī say: ""There is no drink of nabīdh by which a man would risk his religion."" [Authentic]"
"17460","(17454) And I heard Abū al-Qāsim 'Abd al-Khāliq ibn 'Alī al-Mu'adhdhin say: I heard Abū 'Alī Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥmūd al-Muzakkī in Bukhārā say: I heard Abū 'Abd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Marwazī the Imām in Samarqand say: I heard Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥanẓalī say: I heard 'Abd Allāh ibn Idrīs al-Kūfī say: I said to the people of Kūfah: ""O people of Kūfah, the hadith that you narrate regarding the permissibility of nabīdh is only from the blind, the one-eyed, and the weak-sighted. Where are you from the children of the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār?""

Muḥammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Alqamah ibn Wāqqāṣ al-Laythī narrated to me from Abū Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Awf, from 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Every intoxicant is khamr (wine), and every intoxicant is forbidden (ḥarām)."""
"17461","(17455) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, and Layth ibn Saʿd and Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, and Layth narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he forbade soaking raisins and dates together, and he forbade soaking unripe dates (busr) and ripe dates (ruṭb) together.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, and from Shaybān from Jarīr. Al-Bukhārī also recorded it from the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj from ʿAṭāʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17462","(17456) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Abū Muslim narrated to us, and Muslim narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Hishām narrated to us, and Yaḥyā narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Qatādah from his father, that the Prophet ﷺ forbade combining dates (tamr) with unripe dates (zahw), and dates with raisins (zabīb), and he commanded that each one of them be soaked separately.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17463","(17457) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us, Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim narrated to us, and Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abī Qatādah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Do not soak ripe dates (ruṭb) and unripe dates (zahw) together, nor dates (tamr) and raisins (zabīb) together; rather, soak each one of them separately."" Yaḥyā said: ""I asked ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Qatādah about that, and he informed me of it from his father.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī from Rūḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17464","(17458) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī narrated to us, ʿAffān narrated to us, Abān narrated to us, and Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Qatādah from his father, that the Prophet ﷺ forbade the mixture (khalīṭ) of unripe dates (busr) and dates (tamr), the mixture of raisins (zabīb) and dates (tamr), and the mixture of unripe dates (zahw) and ripe dates (ruṭb), and he said: ""Soak each one separately."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17465","(17459) He said: ""And Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to me from Abī Qatādah from the Prophet ﷺ the same.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq from ʿAffān. He also recorded it additionally from the ḥadīth of Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, Abū Hurayrah, Ibn ʿAbbās, and Ibn ʿUmar from the Prophet ﷺ — may Allah be pleased with them all. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17466","(17460) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated to us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Aḥmasī narrated to us, and ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated from al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ from Khālid ibn al-Fizr from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Beware, indeed al-muzzāʿ is forbidden! Beware, indeed al-muzzāʿ is forbidden!"" — meaning the soaking of unripe dates (burs) and dates (tamr) together, and dates (tamr) and raisins (zabīb) together. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17467","(17461) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated from Thābit ibn ʿUmārah, who said: Rayṭah narrated to me from Kabshah ibn Abī Maryam, who said: ""I asked Umm Salamah what the Prophet ﷺ used to forbid."" She said: ""He used to forbid us from cooking the date-stones thoroughly or mixing raisins (zabīb) and dates (tamr).""

The Shaykh — may Allah have mercy on him — said: ""It appears that he forbade the excessive ripening of the date-stones because it spoils the taste of the dates, or because it is fodder for domestic animals, and its nutritional value is lost when it ripens."" Abū Sulaymān al-Khaṭṭābī — may Allah have mercy on him — said this. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17468","(17462) Abū Zakariyyā and Abū Bakr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to me, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Salmān narrated from ʿUqayl ibn Khālid from Maʿbad ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik from his brother ʿAbd Allāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik, from a woman who heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Do not soak dates (tamr) and raisins (zabīb) together; rather, soak each one of them on its own.""

The Shaykh — may Allah have mercy on him — said: ""The Prophet's ﷺ prohibition of the two mixtures may carry two possibilities. The first is that he forbade it due to the combination itself, regardless of whether it reaches the point of intoxication or not, and he permitted drinking it when soaked separately. The second is that he forbade it because it is more prone to rapid fermentation and intensification, whereas when soaked separately it is less likely to intensify. So long as it does not reach the state of intensification in either case, it is not forbidden."" [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrih]"
"17469","(17463) In accordance with this second meaning, what Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us supports this: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dāwūd narrated from Misʿar from Mūsā ibn ʿAbd Allāh from a woman of Banī Asad from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to have raisins (zabīb) soaked for him, and dates would be thrown into it, or dates would be soaked and raisins would be thrown into it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17470","(17464) Abū ʿAlī narrated to us, Abū Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Ziyād ibn Yaḥyā al-Ḥassānī narrated to us, Abū Baḥr narrated to us, ʿAttāb ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīl al-Ḥimmānī narrated to me, and Ṣafiyyah ibn ʿAṭiyyah narrated to me, saying: ""I entered upon ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — along with some women from ʿAbd al-Qays, and we asked her about dates (tamr) and raisins (zabīb)."" She said: ""I used to take a handful of dates and a handful of raisins, throw them into a vessel, stir it, and then give it to the Prophet ﷺ to drink."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17471","(17465) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me that Qatādah ibn Dīʿamah narrated to him, that he heard Anas ibn Mālik saying: ""Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade mixing unripe dates (al-tamr) and ripe dates (al-zahw) together and then drinking the infusion, and that was the common drink (khamr) of the people on the day intoxicants were prohibited.""

Al-Bukhārī said: And ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith mentioned it.

Muslim narrated it from Abū al-Ṭāhir, from Ibn Wahb. In this hadith is evidence that the prohibition was because it constituted khamr (intoxicant), and khamr is that which overwhelms the intellect. And we hold it preferable to avoid mixing the two (types of dates) even if it does not produce intoxication, due to the established narrations prohibiting it absolutely, and those narrations are more firmly established than what has been narrated regarding permissibility. And with Allah lies success. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17472","(17466) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated from Sufyān: Sulaymān narrated to me from Ibrāhīm al-Taymī from al-Ḥārith ibn Suwayd from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade (the use of) al-dubbāʾ and al-muzaffat (vessels).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Jarīr and others, from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17473","(17467) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā said: I read to Mālik, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) addressed the people during one of his military expeditions. Ibn ʿUmar said: ""I went towards him, but he departed before I could reach him, so I asked: 'What did he say?' They said: 'He forbade that nabīdh be prepared in al-dubbāʾ and al-muzaffat.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17474","(17468) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī in Kūfah narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghazrah narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated from Manṣūr ibn Ḥayyān from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿUmar and Ibn ʿAbbās, that they both testified that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade al-dubbāʾ, al-ḥantam, al-naqīr, and al-muzaffat.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others, from Marwān. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17475","(17469) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl narrated to us: Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Shaybān narrated to us: Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated to us: Yaʿlā ibn Ḥakīm narrated to us, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, who said: ""I asked Ibn ʿUmar about nabīdh prepared in al-jar (earthenware vessels), and he said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) declared nabīdh of al-jar to be ḥarām.'""

He said: ""So I went to Ibn ʿAbbās and said: 'Do you not hear what Ibn ʿUmar is saying?' He said: 'What does he say?' I said: 'He says: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) declared nabīdh of al-jar to be ḥarām.' He said: 'Ibn ʿUmar has spoken the truth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) declared nabīdh of al-jar to be ḥarām.' I said: 'And what is nabīdh of al-jar?' He said: 'Everything that is made from clay (al-madar).'""

The wording is that of the hadith of Shaybān. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān ibn Farrūkh. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17476","(17470) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us. Abū ʿAbdillāh said: He informed me, and Abū Saʿīd said: He narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Muzanī informed us: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us: Abū al-Yamān informed me: Shuʿayb informed me, from al-Zuhrī, who said: Anas ibn Mālik informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Do not prepare nabīdh in al-dubbāʾ or al-muzaffat."" And Abū Hurayrah used to add alongside them al-ḥantam and al-naqīr.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17477","(17471) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, dictating: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī in Makkah narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Ayyūb al-Mukharramī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Anas ibn Mālik, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade that nabīdh be prepared in al-dubbāʾ and al-muzaffat."
"17478","(17472) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān said: I heard al-Zuhrī saying: I heard Anas saying: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade al-dubbāʾ and al-muzaffat, that nabīdh be prepared in them."""
"17479","(17473) He said: And Sufyān informed us from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Do not prepare nabīdh in al-dubbāʾ and al-muzaffat."" Then Abū Hurayrah would say: ""And avoid al-ḥanātim and al-naqīr.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid, from Sufyān."
"17480","(17474) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym and Aḥmad ibn Sahl narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. They all said: Naṣr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Nūḥ ibn Qays narrated to us, from Ibn ʿAwn, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), that he said to the delegation of ʿAbd al-Qays: ""I forbid you from al-naqīr, al-muqayyar, al-ḥantam, al-dubbāʾ, and al-muzādah al-majbūbah (a water-skin with its top cut off). But drink in your water-skins, and seal them.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Naṣr ibn ʿAlī. And in the hadith of Abū Ṣāliḥ, it was said to Abū Hurayrah: ""What is al-ḥantam?"" He said: ""The green earthenware jar (al-jar al-akhḍar)."""
"17481","(17475) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: Ḥāmid ibn ʿUmar informed us: ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād informed us: Sulaymān al-Shaybānī informed us. He said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Awfá say: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade nabīdh prepared in the green jar. I said: May I drink from white jars? He said: No.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl from ʿAbd al-Wāḥid. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17482","(17476) And Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan — and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān narrated from Abī Isḥāq from Ibn Abī Awfá, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade nabīdh prepared in the green jar, the white jar, and the red jar. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17483","(17477) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Abū Khaythamah narrated from Abī al-Zubayr.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus informed us: Zuhayr informed us: Abī al-Zubayr narrated from Jābir and Ibn ʿUmar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade al-Naqīr, al-Muzaffat, and al-Dubbāʾ.

And from Jābir, who said: Nabīdh would be prepared for the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) in a waterskin (siqāʾ). When they could not find a waterskin for him, it would be prepared for him in a small stone vessel (tawr). A member of the people said — and I heard it directly from Abī al-Zubayr — ""From birām?"" He said: ""From birām.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and Aḥmad ibn Yūnus. And in this chapter there are narrations from ʿĀʾishah, Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī, and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ, Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"17484","(17478) And Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak, may Allah have mercy on him — informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn Fāris informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī informed us: Shuʿbah informed me: ʿAmr ibn Murrah informed me: I heard Zādhan say: I said to Ibn ʿUmar: Inform us of what the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade regarding vessels — inform us in your language and explain it to us in our language. He said: He forbade al-Ḥantam, which is the jar (jarrah); and he forbade al-Muzaffat, which is al-Muqayyar (pitch-coated); and he forbade al-Dubbāʾ, which is the gourd (qarʿ); and he forbade al-Naqīr, which is the base of a palm tree, hollowed out by carving and woven; and he commanded that nabīdh be prepared in waterskins (asqiyah).

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā and Bundār from Abī Dāwūd. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17485","(17479) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: ʿUyyaynah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jawshan informed me: My father said: Abū Bakrah used to have nabīdh prepared for him in a jar. Abū Barzah arrived from a journey he had been on and stopped at the house of Abū Bakrah before going to his own house. He mentioned the hadith regarding the disapproval of what had been prepared for him in a jar, and his statement to his wife: ""I wish you had put it in a waterskin."" When Abū Bakrah came, he said: ""We know what we were forbidden — we were forbidden al-Dubbāʾ, al-Naqīr, al-Ḥantam, and al-Muzaffat. As for al-Dubbāʾ, we the people of Thaqīf in al-Ṭāʾif used to take al-Dubbāʾ, carve out bunches of grapes inside it, bury it, and leave it until it fermented vigorously and then died (i.e., the fermentation subsided). As for al-Naqīr, the people of al-Yamāmah used to hollow out the base of a palm tree, crush fresh dates and unripe dates inside it, and leave it until it fermented vigorously and then died. As for al-Ḥantam, they were jars in which wine was brought to us. And al-Muzaffat refers to these pitch-coated vessels.""

(Q) The Shaykh said: This is how it was narrated from Abū Bakrah. A group of the scholars of knowledge have said that the meaning of the prohibition against preparing nabīdh in these vessels is that nabīdh in them turns to corruption and intoxication more rapidly, until it becomes an intoxicant, whereas in waterskins it is less likely to do so. Then a concession (rukhṣah) came permitting all vessels, provided that one does not drink what intoxicates. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17486","(17480) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed me: My father informed me: Sufyān narrated from Sulaymān al-Aḥwal from Mujāhid from Abī ʿIyāḍ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: When the Prophet (peace be upon him) forbade the vessels, they said: ""Not everyone can find a waterskin."" So he granted a concession regarding the jar (jarrah) other than al-Muzaffat. This is the wording of Aḥmad's hadith. In al-Shāfiʿī's narration it reads: ""So he permitted them the jar other than al-Muzaffat."" And Abī ʿIyāḍ is dropped from the isnād of his hadith, though he is in it. Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from a group of narrators from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17487","(17481) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Ziyād informed us: Sharīk narrated from Ziyād ibn Fayyāḍ from Abī ʿIyāḍ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) mentioned the vessels — al-Dubbāʾ, al-Ḥantam, al-Muzaffat, and al-Naqīr — and forbade them. A Bedouin said: ""We have no vessels (other than these)."" He said: ""Then drink what is lawful."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17488","(17482) He said: And Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam informed us: Sharīk narrated with his isnād, saying: ""Avoid what intoxicates."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17489","(17483) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā informed us: Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī informed us: Sufyān narrated from Manṣūr from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade vessels (al-ẓurūf). The Anṣār said: ""We cannot do without them."" He said: ""Then there is no harm.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yūsuf ibn Mūsā from Abī Aḥmad. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17490","(17484) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us: Ibn Abī Maryam informed us: Nāfiʿ ibn Yazīd informed us: Abū Ḥazrah — Yaʿqūb ibn Mujāhid — informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated from his father Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""I had forbidden you from preparing nabīdh in al-Dubbāʾ, al-Ḥantam, and al-Muzaffat. So prepare nabīdh (in vessels), but I do not permit what intoxicates."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17491","(17485) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Salamah al-ʿAnazī informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus informed us: Muʿarrif ibn Wāṣil informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Walīd informed me: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: Wakīʿ narrated from Muʿarrif ibn Wāṣil, from Muḥārib ibn Dithār, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I had forbidden you from drinking in the vessels of leather (adum), so drink from every vessel, but do not drink that which intoxicates.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17492","(17486) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdūs informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr informed us: Sufyān informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bashshār informed us: Abū ʿĀṣim informed us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAlqamah ibn Murthad, from Sulaymān ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I had forbidden you from visiting graves, but permission has been given to Muḥammad to visit the grave of his mother, so visit them, for they remind of the Hereafter. And I had forbidden you from storing the meat of sacrificial animals beyond three days, so that those of means might be generous toward those who have none; so now eat as you wish, feed others, and store. And I had forbidden you from using leather vessels (al-ẓurūf), but leather vessels do not render anything forbidden nor do they render anything lawful. Every intoxicant is forbidden.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿĀṣim. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥajjāj ibn al-Shāʿir from Abū ʿĀṣim. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17493","(17487) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Usāmah ibn Zayd al-Laythī informed me that Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān informed him that Wāsiʿ ibn Ḥabbān informed him that Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī informed him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I had forbidden you from nabīdh (fermented drinks); so now make nabīdh, but I do not permit that which intoxicates."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17494","(17488) And Abū Bakr and Abū Zakariyyā informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: Muḥammad informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Ibn Jurayj narrated from Ayyūb ibn Hāniʾ, from Masrūq ibn al-Ajdaʿ, from ʿAbdillāh ibn Masʿūd, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I had forbidden you from nabīdh in vessels (al-awʿiyah); but no vessel renders anything forbidden. Every intoxicant is forbidden."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17495","(17489) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān narrated from Abū Ḥayyān, who is Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Taymī, from his father, from Maryam bint Ṭāriq, who said: I entered upon ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) along with some women from the people of the provincial towns, and they began asking her about leather vessels (al-ẓurūf). She said: ""You are asking about vessels that were not in use during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). I forbid you every intoxicant, even if the water of one of you's leather water-skin were to become intoxicating."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17496","(17490) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl informed us: Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Makkī informed us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUbaydillāh, from Abū Saʿīd, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade drinking from the mouths of water-skins (al-asqiyah).

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid from Sufyān."
"17497","(17491) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Naṣr al-Dārabardī in Marw informed us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Rūḥ al-Madāʾinī informed us: Shabābah informed us: Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUbaydillāh ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUtbah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, from the Prophet (ﷺ), that he forbade drinking from the mouths of water-skins (al-asqiyah).

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb. The entirety of this chapter has already been covered in the Book of Walīmah."
"17498","(17492) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us: Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk informed us: Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed us: Ismāʿīl, who is Ibn ʿUlayyah, narrated from Ayyūb, from ʿIkrimah, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), that he forbade a man to drink from the mouth of a water-skin (al-siqāʾ). Ayyūb said: ""I was told that a man drank from the mouth of a water-skin and a snake came out of it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17499","(17493) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us: Wuhaib ibn Khālid informed us: Ayyūb narrated from ʿAbdillāh ibn Abī Mulaykah, from ʿUqbah ibn al-Ḥārith, that al-Nuʿaymān or the son of al-Nuʿaymān was brought to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was intoxicated. This caused the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) great distress. He then commanded those who were in the house to strike him. So they struck him with sandals and palm stalks. He (ʿUqbah) said: ""I was among those who struck him.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17500","(17494) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Adīb al-Bisṭāmī informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr al-Ḥadhāʾ informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī informed us: Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ informed us: Ibn al-Hād narrated from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Taymī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: A man who had been drinking was brought to the Prophet (ﷺ). The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Strike him."" So among us were those who struck with their hands, those who struck with their sandals, and those who struck with their garments. When the man departed, some of the people said: ""May Allah disgrace you!"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not say thus, and do not assist the Shayṭān against him. Rather, say: 'May Allah have mercy on you.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17501","(17495) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to us, Ibn al-Hād narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to me that Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed him from Abū Hurayrah: that the Prophet ﷺ was brought a drunkard, so the Prophet ﷺ ordered his Companions to strike him. Among them were those who struck him with his sandal, and among them were those who struck him with his hand, and among them were those who struck him with his garment. Then he said, ""Leave him."" Then he ordered them, and they rebuked him. They said, ""Have you no shame in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that you do this?"" Then he sent him away. When he departed, the people began to invoke curses upon him and revile him, one of them saying, ""O Allah, humiliate him! O Allah, curse him!"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""Do not say this, but rather say: O Allah, forgive him! O Allah, have mercy on him!""

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17502","(17496) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me, al-Layth narrated to me.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me, Abū al-Ḥusayn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Rāzī narrated to us, Abū Zurʿah narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to me, al-Layth narrated to me, Khālid ibn Yazīd narrated to me from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl from Zayd ibn Aslam from his father from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābb, may Allah be pleased with him: that during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ there was a man whose name was ʿAbd Allāh, who was nicknamed ""Ḥimār"" (the donkey), and he used to make the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laugh. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had previously flogged him for drinking. One day he was brought before the Prophet ﷺ, and he ordered that he be flogged. A man from the people said, ""O Allah, curse him! How often he is brought!"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""Do not curse him, for by Allah, I know that he loves Allah and His Messenger.""

The wording of both their narrations is the same. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Bukayr.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17503","(17497) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, who heard al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd say: I heard ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, say: It was mentioned to me that ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar and some of his companions had drunk an intoxicating beverage, and I am inquiring about it. If it intoxicates, I will carry out the ḥadd punishment on them. Sufyān said, from Maʿmar, from al-Zuhrī, from al-Sāʾib: I saw that he carried out the ḥadd on them.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17504","(17498) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū Muḥammad al-Muzanī informed us, ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us, Abū al-Yamān informed me, Shuʿayb informed me from al-Zuhrī, Sālim informed me that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar said: My brother ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUmar drank, and Abū Sirwaʿah ʿUqbah ibn al-Ḥārith drank with him, while we were in Egypt during the caliphate of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābb, may Allah be pleased with him. They became intoxicated, and when they sobered up, they went to ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, who was the governor of Egypt, and said, ""Purify us, for we have become intoxicated from a drink we consumed."" ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar said: I did not realize that they had gone to ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ. He said: My brother mentioned to me that he had become intoxicated. I said to him, ""Enter the house so that I may purify you."" He said, ""The governor has already been informed."" ʿAbd Allāh said: I said, ""By Allah, you shall not be shaved today in front of the people. Enter, and I will shave you."" At that time, they would shave the head along with the ḥadd punishment. So he entered the house with me. ʿAbd Allāh said: I shaved my brother with my own hand. Then ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ flogged them both. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābb, may Allah be pleased with him, heard of this and wrote to ʿAmr: ""Send ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUmar to me on a camel saddle."" So ʿAmr did that. When ʿAbd al-Raḥmān arrived before ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, he flogged him and punished him on account of his status in relation to him. Then he sent him away. He remained healthy for several months, then his appointed fate came upon him. The common people think that he died from ʿUmar's flogging, but he did not die from his flogging.

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: What is apparent is that he flogged him as a taʿzīr (discretionary punishment), for the ḥadd punishment is not repeated. And Allah knows best.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17505","(17499) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Yaḥyā narrated to us from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: No man is brought to me who has consumed khamr or an intoxicating nabīdh except that I will carry out the ḥadd punishment on him.

[Ṣaḥīḥ in its meaning]"
"17506","(17500) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Saʿīd al-Dimashqī narrated to us, Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us, al-Walīd narrated to us, Ibn Lahīʿah narrated to us from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from ʿUrwah that he narrated to ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Flog for a small amount of khamr and for a large amount, for its beginning and its end are both forbidden.""

[Very weak (Ḍaʿīf jiddan)]"
"17507","(17501) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Abān narrated from ʿĀṣim from Abī Ṣāliḥ from Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When they drink khamr, flog them. Then if they drink, flog them. Then if they drink, flog them. Then if they drink, flog them. Then if they drink, kill them.""

[Ḥasan]"
"17508","(17502) And Abū ʿAlī informed us, Abū Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Ḥammād narrated from Ḥumayd ibn Yazīd from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said with this meaning, and I think he said regarding the fifth time: ""If he drinks it, kill him.""

[Weak (Ḍaʿīf)]"
"17509","(17503) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dawrī narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When he becomes intoxicated, flog him. Then if he becomes intoxicated, flog him. Then if he becomes intoxicated, flog him. Then if he repeats a fourth time, strike his neck (i.e., execute him).""

The wording is that of Yazīd's narration. And in the narration of al-Ṭayālisī: ""Whoever drinks khamr, flog him. Then if he repeats, flog him. Then if he repeats a fourth time, kill him.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17510","(17504) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, and Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī said: Similarly is the narration of ʿUmar ibn Abī Salamah from his father from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet ﷺ: ""When he drinks khamr, flog him. Then if he repeats a fourth time, kill him."" And similarly the narration of Suhayl from Abī Ṣāliḥ from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet ﷺ: ""If they drink a fourth time, kill them."" And similarly the narration of Ibn Abī Nuʿm from Ibn ʿUmar from the Prophet ﷺ, and likewise the narration of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr from the Prophet ﷺ, and the narration of al-Sharīd from the Prophet ﷺ. And in the narration of al-Jadalī from Muʿāwiyah from the Prophet ﷺ: ""Then if he repeats a third or fourth time, kill him.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17511","(17505) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; both said: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī from Qabīṣah ibn Dhuʾayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever drinks wine, flog him; then if he drinks, flog him; then if he drinks, flog him; then if he drinks a fourth time, kill him."" A man who had drunk wine was brought, and he flogged him; then he was brought again, and he flogged him; then he was brought again, and he flogged him; then he was brought a fourth time, and he flogged him. Thus the execution was lifted from the people, and it became a dispensation that remained established. [Ṣaḥīh in other than this chain]"
"17512","(17506) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī from Qabīṣah ibn Dhuʾayb, and he mentioned this ḥadīth, except that he said: ""Then if he drinks, kill him."" Al-Zuhrī did not know whether this was after the third or the fourth time. And he said at the end: ""The execution was waived and it became a dispensation."" Sufyān said: Al-Zuhrī said to Manṣūr ibn al-Muʿtamir and Mukhāwil: ""Be the two envoys of Iraq with this ḥadīth."""
"17513","(17507) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us; Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Jahm al-Simmārī narrated to us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq from al-Zuhrī from Qabīṣah ibn Dhuʾayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""If he drinks wine, flog him; if he repeats it, flog him; if he repeats it, flog him; if he repeats it, kill him."" A man from the Anṣār called Nuʿaymān was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he struck him four times. The Muslims then perceived that the execution had been deferred and that the flogging had become obligatory. This ḥadīth has also been narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yasār from Ibn al-Munkadir from Jābir. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17514","(17508) The Shaykh al-Imām Abū al-Ṭayyib Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated to us; the Imām, my father, narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Ḥarashī narrated to us; Ziyād ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever drinks wine, flog him; if he repeats it, flog him; if he repeats it, flog him; if he repeats it a fourth time, kill him."" He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ flogged al-Nuʿaymān four times. He said: The Muslims then perceived that the ḥadd had been established when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ flogged four times. Maʿmar narrated it from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir and from Zayd ibn Aslam, that both of them said the same. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17515","(17509) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us; Ibn Abī ʀāṣim informed us by dictation; Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā and al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī both narrated to us; Abū ʀāṣim narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Rukānah informed me; ʀikramah narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not fix a specific punishment for wine. Ibn ʿAbbās said: A man drank and became intoxicated. He was found staggering in the pathway. He was taken to the Prophet ﷺ, but when he reached near the house of al-ʿAbbās, he slipped away and entered upon al-ʿAbbās and clung to him. This was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ, and he laughed and said: ""Did he really do that?"" Then he did not command anything regarding him. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17516","(17510) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī and Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated it with something similar to it, except that he said: ""He did not fix a specific punishment."" Abū Dāwūd said: This ḥadīth is among those narrated exclusively by the people of Madīnah."
"17517","(17511) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Isfarāyinī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Barāʾ narrated to us. He said: ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī was asked about Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Rukānah, who narrated this ḥadīth from ʀikramah, and he said: ""He is unknown (majhūl)."" The Shaykh said: The meaning of part of this ḥadīth has also been narrated by Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yasār from Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥah ibn Yazīd ibn Rukānah. [Ṣaḥīh according to Ibn al-Madīnī]"
"17518","(17512) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī all informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq; Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥah ibn Yazīd ibn Rukānah narrated to me from ʀikramah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not flog for wine except in the latter period. He had gone on the expedition of Tabūk, and Abū ʀalamah ibn al-Aʿwar al-Sulamī came to his chamber at night while he was drunk, to the extent that he cut some of the ropes of the chamber. He said: ""Who is this?"" It was said: ""Abū ʀalamah, drunk."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Let one of you go to him and take his hand until he returns him to his mount."" (Q) And if this is authentic, then the statement of Ibn ʿAbbās, ""He did not fix a specific punishment for wine,"" means he did not fix it verbally, though he did fix it in practice, and this does not contradict the earlier ḥadīth. A man was seen staggering in the pathway, and they began to bring him to the Prophet ﷺ, but he fled to al-ʿAbbās; because it was suspected that he was intoxicated, and the intoxication had not yet subsided, so he left him. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17519","(17513) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, went out and prayed over a funeral. Al-Sāʾib heard him saying: ""I have found from ʿUbaydullāh and his companions the smell of a drink. I am asking about what they drank; if it was intoxicating, I will punish them with the ḥadd."" Sufyān said: Maʿmar informed me from al-Zuhrī from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd that he was present when he punished them with the ḥadd."
"17520","(17514) Abū Zakariyyā narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Muslim ibn Khālid narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to ʀāʾiq: ""Do you flog on the basis of the smell of a drink?"" ʀāʾiq said: ""The smell may come from a drink that is not harmful. But if they all gathered together upon a single drink and one of them became intoxicated, they should all be flogged the full ḥadd."" Al-Shāfiʿī said: The statement of ʀāʾiq is like the statement of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him. [Ṣaḥīh in other than this chain]"
"17521","(17515) Abū Muḥammad — al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal — narrated to us: Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated from Ibrāhīm from ʿAlqamah, who said: ʿAbd Allāh said: ""I was sitting in Ḥimṣ when they said to me, 'Recite.' So I recited Sūrah Yūsuf. A man from the people then said, 'By Allāh, this is not how Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, revealed it.' He said: I said, 'Woe to you! I recited it to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), and he said, ""You have done well."" And you say to me what you say?' He said: While I was speaking to him, I detected the smell of wine from him. So I said, 'You deny the Book of Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, and you drink wine? By Allāh, you shall not return to your family until I have flogged you the ḥadd punishment.'""

It was narrated in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Aʿmash. It is possible that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd did not flog him until it was established to him that he had consumed an intoxicating amount, by way of evidence or confession. And Allāh knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17522","(17516) Abū Muḥammad — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkārī — in Baghdād narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from Maʿmar from al-Zuhrī, who informed me: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah — and his father had witnessed Badr — informed me that ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) appointed Qudāmah ibn Maẓʿūn as governor over al-Baḥrayn, and he was the maternal uncle of Ḥafṣah and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar. Then al-Jārūd, the chief of ʿAbd al-Qays, came to ʿUmar and said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, Qudāmah drank and became intoxicated, and I have seen one of the ḥudūd of Allāh that it is incumbent upon me to bring it to you."" ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""Who witnessed with you?"" He said: ""Abū Hurayrah."" So he called Abū Hurayrah and said: ""What do you testify to?"" He said: ""I did not see him drink, but I saw him intoxicated, vomiting."" ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""You have been excessive in your testimony.""

He said: Then ʿUmar wrote to Qudāmah, commanding him to come to him from al-Baḥrayn. So he came, and al-Jārūd stood before him and said: ""Establish upon this one the Book of Allāh."" ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""Are you an accuser or a witness?"" He said: ""Rather, a witness."" He said: ""Then you have delivered your testimony."" So al-Jārūd fell silent. Then the next day he came to ʿUmar and said: ""Establish upon this one the ḥadd of Allāh."" ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""I see you only as an accuser, and no one witnessed with you except one man."" Al-Jārūd said: ""I adjure you by Allāh."" ʿUmar said: ""You must hold your tongue, or I shall do you harm."" Abū Hurayrah said: ""If you doubt our testimony, send to the daughter of al-Walīd and ask her, for she is the wife of Qudāmah."" So ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) sent to Hind bint al-Walīd, adjuring her, and she bore witness against her husband. ʿUmar said to Qudāmah: ""I am going to flog you."" He said: ""Even if I drank as they say, you would not have the right to flog me."" ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""Why?"" Qudāmah said: ""Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, said: *'There is no blame upon those who believe and do righteous deeds for what they have eaten'* [al-Māʾidah: 93] — the verse."" ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""You have erred in your interpretation. If you feared Allāh, you would have avoided what Allāh has forbidden you.""

He said: Then ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) turned to the people and said: ""What do you think about flogging Qudāmah?"" They said: ""We do not think you should flog him so long as he is ill."" So he remained silent about that for several days. Then one morning he resolved to flog him and said to his companions: ""What do you think about flogging Qudāmah?"" The people said: ""We do not think you should flog him so long as he is in pain."" ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""That I meet Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, under the lashes is more beloved to me than that I meet Him while it is upon my neck. Bring me a complete whip."" So ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) ordered Qudāmah to be flogged. Qudāmah then became angry with ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) and boycotted him. They performed Ḥajj together, with Qudāmah angry with him. When they returned from their Ḥajj and ʿUmar stopped at al-Suqiyā, he awoke from his sleep and said: ""Bring me Qudāmah quickly, for I see that someone came to me and said: 'Reconcile with Qudāmah, for he is your brother.' Bring him to me quickly."" When they brought him, he refused to come. So ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) ordered that he be dragged to him until he spoke to him and sought forgiveness for him. And that was the first reconciliation between them.

(Qāl) At the beginning of this narration there is evidence that ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) hesitated in accepting their testimony when they did not both agree upon his drinking, and when he did carry out the ḥadd, it is possible that his drinking was established to him by his own confession or by the testimony of another witness alongside al-Jārūd. And Allāh knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17523","(17517) Abū Manṣūr ʿAbd al-Qāhir ibn Ṭāhir al-Baghdādī al-Imām, Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah, and Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥamdān al-Fārisī narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAmr ibn Nujayd narrated to us: Abū Muslim narrated to us: al-Anṣārī narrated to us: Ibn ʿAwn narrated from Muḥammad — that is, Ibn Sīrīn — that when al-Jārūd came to ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him), he mentioned the ḥadīth and said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, you appointed over us one who drinks wine."" He said: ""Who are your witnesses?"" He said: ""Abū Hurayrah.""

He said: ""Your in-law! Your in-law! Your in-law!"" al-Anṣārī said: The sister of al-Jārūd was married to Abū Hurayrah. He said: ""By Allāh, I shall surely strike his back with the whip."" He said: He said to him: ""That is not just — that your in-law drinks and you flog my in-law."" He said: ""Who?"" He said: ""ʿAlqamah."" So they bore witness before him, and he ordered him to be flogged. And he said: ""I have not shown favouritism to anyone in my governorship since I was appointed other than him, and no blessing was placed in it. Take him and flog him."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17524","(17518) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Musaddad and Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl — with the same meaning — both said: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn al-Mukhtār narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh al-Dānāj narrated to us: Ḥuḍayn ibn al-Mundhir al-Raqāshī — and he is Abū Sāsān — said: I witnessed ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allāh be pleased with him) when al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah was brought to him. Ḥumrān and another man bore witness against him. One of them testified that he saw him drink it — meaning wine — and the other testified that he saw him vomiting it. ʿUthmān (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""He did not vomit it until he drank it."" Then he said to ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him): ""Carry out the ḥadd upon him."" ʿAlī said to al-Ḥasan (may Allāh be pleased with them both): ""Carry out the ḥadd upon him."" He said: ""Let the one who assumed authority bear its heat."" So ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him) said to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar: ""Carry out the ḥadd upon him."" He said: So he took the whip and flogged him, and ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him) was counting. When he reached forty, he said: ""That is enough. The Prophet (ﷺ) flogged forty — I think he said — and Abū Bakr (may Allāh be pleased with him) flogged forty, and ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) flogged eighty. Each is a Sunnah, and this is more beloved to me.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. And I do not know of any valid interpretation for this other than that he accepted the testimony against him as such. Whoever disagrees with him says: Their testimony did not concur upon his drinking, and it is possible that he was compelled to drink and then vomited it. Al-Shāfiʿī said regarding a similar issue: ""A hidden meaning for which no one is punished and no one is held accountable — people are only punished on the basis of certainty."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17525","(17519) Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh al-Dānāj from Ḥuḍayn Abī Sāsān, who said: A group among them set out and came to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allāh be pleased with him) and informed him of what al-Walīd had done. ʿUthmān said to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allāh be pleased with them both): ""Here is your cousin — flog him."" Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ narrated to us: Saʿīd narrated it.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Saʿīd."
"17526","(17520) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — dictating — Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Wahb narrated to us: Ayyūb narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Mulaykah from ʿUqbah ibn al-Ḥārith, who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) was brought al-Nuʿaymān or the son of al-Nuʿaymān while he was intoxicated. This caused the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) great distress. Then he ordered those who were in the house to strike him. So they struck him with sandals and palm stalks. He said: ""And I was among those who struck him.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb — thus Wahb narrated it from Ayyūb."
"17527","(17521) ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated it from Ayyūb, and said: al-Nuʿaymān or the son of al-Nuʿaymān was brought as a drinker. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said to those in the house: ""Strike him."" Abū ʿAmr al-Bisṭāmī narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: Bundār narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAbd al-Majīd narrated from Ayyūb, and he mentioned it.

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah from ʿAbd al-Wahhāb. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17528","(17522) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Hudbah narrated to us: Hammām narrated to us: Qatādah narrated from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: A man who had become intoxicated was brought to the Prophet (ﷺ). He ordered approximately twenty men, and they flogged him with palm stalks and sandals. And he mentioned the ḥadīth.

It is possible that he was brought to him after his intoxication had subsided. And Allāh knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17529","(17523) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Abū al-Tayyāḥ from Abū al-Wadāk from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: ""I do not drink nabīdh of jars after the time when the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) was brought a man called Nashwān."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, I did not drink wine; I only drank nabīdh of raisins and dates in a dubbāʾ vessel."" He said: The Prophet (ﷺ) ordered him to be struck, and he was pushed with hands and beaten with sandals. He said: And he forbade the mixing of raisins and dates, and he forbade the dubbāʾ vessel. [Ḥasan]"
"17530","(17524) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Isḥāq, who said: I heard a man from the people of Najrān narrate from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) was brought a man who was intoxicated. He said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, I did not drink wine; I only drank raisins and dates."" So he was ordered to be flogged the ḥadd punishment, and he forbade the two from being mixed together in this manner.

This is the narration of the group from Shuʿbah, then from Abū Isḥāq. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17531","(17525) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hamdawayh informed me; Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; 'Ali ibn Hujr al-Sa'di narrated to us; Dawud ibn al-Zibriqan narrated from Shu'bah from Abu Ishaq, who said: A jurist from among the people of Najran narrated to me from Ibn 'Umar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was brought a man who was intoxicated—or he said, inebriated. When his intoxication subsided, he ordered that he be flogged. The man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I did not drink wine; I only drank a mixture of unripe dates and ripe dates."" So he ordered that he be flogged, and then he prohibited the two from being mixed together. [Da'if]"
"17532","(17526) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed us; Abu al-Hasan al-Karizi informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz informed us from Abu 'Ubayd, who said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us from Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah from Thabit from Abu Rafi' from 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that a drinker was brought to him. He said: ""I shall send you to a man who will show you no partiality."" So he sent him to Muṭi' ibn al-Aswad al-'Adawi and said: ""When you wake up tomorrow, flog him the prescribed punishment."" The next day, 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) came while he was flogging him with severe blows. He said: ""You have killed the man! How many blows did you strike him?"" He said: ""Sixty."" He said: ""Then deduct twenty from him."" Abu 'Ubayd said: ""He said 'deduct twenty from him,' meaning: make the severity of the blows you struck as recompense for the twenty that remain."" Among the jurisprudential points in this hadith is that the flogging of the drinker is a light flogging. Also, he did not flog him while he was intoxicated; rather, he waited until his intoxication had subsided. Did you not hear his words: ""When you wake up tomorrow, flog him the prescribed punishment""?

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: And in it is the point that exceeding forty is a discretionary punishment (ta'zir) and not part of the prescribed hadd. [Sahih]"
"17533","(17527) Abu Muhammad Janah ibn Nadhir ibn Janah al-Qadi in Kufa informed us; Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Duḥaym narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Hazim narrated to us; 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa informed us; Isra'il narrated from Yahya al-Jabir from Abu Majid, who said: A man from among the Muslims came with his nephew while he was intoxicated. He said: ""O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, my nephew is intoxicated."" He said: ""Shake him, rouse him, and smell his breath."" They did so, and then he was taken to prison. The next morning he was summoned, and he mentioned the hadith regarding the manner of his flogging. Abu 'Ubayd said: It means that he is to be shaken, agitated, and his breath smelled until the odour is detected from him, so that it may be known what he drank. This is al-taltalah, al-tartarah, and al-mazmazah, all having the same meaning. Abu 'Ubayd said: Some of the scholars reject this hadith.

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Due to the weakness of Yahya al-Jabir and the obscurity of Abu Majid. [Very Da'if]"
"17534","(17528) Abu al-Hasan al-Raffa' informed us; 'Uthman ibn Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us; Isma'il al-Qadi narrated to us; Ibn Abi Uways and 'Isa ibn Mina both narrated to us; Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated from his father from the jurists among the people of Madinah, who used to say: The intoxicated person is not to be flogged until he regains sobriety."
"17535","(17529) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Ahmad ibn Faris narrated to us; Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn Fayruz from Hudhayn Abu Sasani al-Raqashi, who said: I was present with 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) when al-Walid ibn 'Uqbah was brought, having drunk wine. Humran ibn Aban and another man testified against him. 'Uthman said to 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with both of them): ""Carry out the prescribed punishment on him."" So 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) ordered 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far Dhu al-Janahayn (may Allah be pleased with both of them) to flog him. He began flogging him while 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) counted, until he had struck forty blows. Then he said: ""Stop. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) flogged forty, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) flogged forty, and 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) flogged eighty. Each is a Sunnah, and this is dearer to me.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from the hadith of 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar."
"17536","(17530) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us; Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us; 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' narrated from Sa'id from 'Abd Allah al-Danaj from Hudhayn Abu Sasani, who said: A group of them set out and came to 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) and informed him of what al-Walid had done. 'Uthman said to 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with both of them): ""Take charge of your cousin and flog him."" 'Ali said to al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with both of them): ""Get up and flog him.""

Al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""What has this to do with you? Appoint someone other than yourself for this."" He said: ""Rather, you have become incapable, weak, and feeble. O 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far, get up and flog him."" So he began flogging him while 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) counted, until he reached forty. He said: ""Stop. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) flogged forty, and Abu Bakr flogged forty, and 'Umar flogged eighty. Each is a Sunnah."""
"17537","(17531) And Abu Muhammad informed us; Abu Sa'id informed us; al-Za'farani narrated to us; Yazid ibn Harun informed us; Sa'id narrated from 'Abd Allah al-Danaj from Hudhayn ibn al-Mundhir ibn al-Harith ibn Wa'lah that al-Walid ibn 'Uqbah led the people in the Fajr prayer performing four rak'ats, then turned to them and said: ""Shall I add more for you?"" This was reported to 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned something similar to the previous narration, except that in the hadith of Yazid it states: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) struck forty, and Abu Bakr and 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) struck forty at the beginning of his caliphate, then 'Umar completed it to eighty. Each is a Sunnah.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from the hadith of Ibn 'Ulayyah from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah, in an abridged form."
"17538","(17532) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Sakhtawayh narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us; Muslim and Abu 'Umar both narrated to us; Hisham narrated from Qatadah from Anas that the Prophet (ﷺ) flogged for wine with palm fronds. Abu 'Umar said: ""He struck for wine with palm fronds and sandals."" Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) struck forty. When 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) assumed authority, he said: ""The people have drawn near to the countryside (and wine has become more accessible). What do you think about the punishment for wine?"" 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: ""I think you should make it the lightest of the prescribed punishments."" So he flogged eighty.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Abu 'Umar Hafs ibn 'Umar, in an abridged form. [Sahih]"
"17539","(17533) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu 'Amr al-Hiri informed us; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Waki' narrated from Hisham from Qatadah from Anas that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to strike for wine with sandals and palm fronds, forty blows. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) struck forty. When 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) assumed authority, he was asked about that, so 'Umar consulted them. Ibn 'Awf (may Allah be pleased with both of them) said: ""I think you should strike him eighty."" So he struck him eighty.

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah."
"17540","(17534) Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Rudhbari narrated to us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Mahmawayh al-'Askari in Basrah narrated to us; Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Qalanisi narrated to us; Adam narrated; Shu'bah narrated; Qatadah narrated from Anas ibn Malik that the Prophet (ﷺ) was brought a man who had drunk wine. He struck him with palm fronds approximately forty times. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) did likewise. When 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) assumed authority, he consulted the people about it. 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: ""The lightest of the prescribed punishments is eighty."" So he did that.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Adam ibn Abi Iyas, in an abridged form.

And Ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated from Qatadah, saying from the Prophet (ﷺ): ""He flogged with palm fronds and sandals, forty."""
"17541","(17535) It was narrated by Hammām from Qatādah, who said: He (the Prophet ﷺ) ordered approximately twenty men, and each man struck him twice with palm-fiber sandals and shoes. Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, Khalaf narrated to us, Bahz narrated to us, Hammām narrated to us, Qatādah narrated from Anas: that a man who was intoxicated was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he mentioned it (the hadith)."
"17542","(17536) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdān al-Ṣayrafī in Marw narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn al-Faḍl al-Balkhī narrated to us, Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, al-Juʿayd narrated from Yazīd ibn Khuṣayfah from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd, who said: We used to be brought the drinker during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the time of Abū Bakr, and the early part of the caliphate of ʿUmar — meaning — so we would strike them with our hands, our sandals, and our garments. This continued until the early part of the caliphate of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), so he (ʿUmar) flogged forty (lashes). Then when they became excessive in it and acted immorally, he flogged eighty.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17543","(17537) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan — and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāāq al-Muzakkī informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, al-Shāfiʿī said: Maʿmar informed us, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Azhar, who said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Ḥunayn asking about the mount of Khālid ibn al-Walīd. I ran before him asking about Khālid's mount until he (Khālid) came as a young man. Then a drinker was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: ""Strike him."" So they struck him with their hands, their sandals, and the ends of their garments, and they threw dust upon him. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Rebuke him."" So they rebuked him, then he released him. He said: When Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was in authority, he asked those who had been present about the one who had been struck, and he assessed it at forty (lashes). So Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) flogged for wine-drinking forty lashes throughout his life. Then ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) did likewise until the people became excessive in wine-drinking, so he consulted (the Companions), and he flogged eighty.

Hishām ibn Yūṣuf al-Ṣanʿānī likewise narrated it from Maʿmar. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17544","(17538) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us, Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Azhar, who said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ on the day of the Conquest, while I was a young boy, asking about the residence of Khālid ibn al-Walīd. Then a drinker was brought, and he ordered them, so they struck him with whatever was in their hands — among them were those who struck with a whip and among them those who struck with a stick. And the Prophet ﷺ threw dust upon him. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17545","(17539) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī al-Faqīh — informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us, al-Qāḍī al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dawraqī narrated to us, Ṣafwān ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us, Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated from al-Zuhrī, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Azhar informed me, who said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Ḥunayn, moving through the people asking about the residence of Khālid ibn al-Walīd. Then a drunkard was brought. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to those around him: ""Strike him."" So they struck him with whatever was in their hands. He said: And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ threw dust (upon him). He said: Then Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought a drunkard, so he estimated what their striking had been on that day, and he flogged forty (lashes).

Al-Zuhrī said: Then Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me from Ibn Wabarah al-Kalbī, who said: Khālid ibn al-Walīd sent me to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him). I came to him while ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with them both), ʿAlī, Ṭalḥah, and al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with them) were with him, and they were reclining with him in the mosque. I said: Khālid ibn al-Walīd has sent me to you, sending you greetings, and saying: The people have plunged into wine-drinking and have belittled the punishment for it. ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""These people are with you — ask them."" ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I think that when he drinks, he becomes intoxicated; when he becomes intoxicated, he raves; and when he raves, he slanders. And upon the slanderer is eighty lashes."" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Convey to your companion what he said."" He said: So Khālid (may Allah be pleased with him) flogged eighty, and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) flogged eighty. He said: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), when a weak man was brought to him who had committed the offence, would flog him forty. He said: And ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) also flogged both eighty and forty. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17546","(17540) He (al-Ḥusayn) said: And Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Rūḥ narrated to us, Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated to us, Ibn Shihāb narrated to me: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Azhar informed me from the Prophet ﷺ the like of that. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17547","(17541) He (al-Ḥusayn) said: And Yaʿqūb narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Azhar, from the Prophet ﷺ, and he mentioned the like of that. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17548","(17542) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Ibn al-Sarḥ narrated to us, saying: I found in the book of my paternal uncle ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd, from ʿUqayl, that Ibn Shihāb informed him that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Azhar informed him from his father: A drinker was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at Ḥunayn. He threw dust in his face, then he ordered his Companions, and they struck him with their sandals and whatever was in their hands, until he said to them: ""Stop."" So they stopped. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away. Then Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) flogged for wine-drinking forty lashes. Then ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) flogged forty lashes at the beginning of his caliphate, then flogged eighty at the end of his caliphate. Then ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) flogged both — eighty and forty. Then Muʿāwiyah (may Allah have mercy on him) established the punishment at eighty. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17549","(17543) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī informed me, al-Walīd ibn Abān narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Kathīr ibn ʿUfayr narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Fulayḥ — the brother of Muḥammad ibn Fulayḥ — narrated to us, Thawr ibn Zayd narrated from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās: The drinkers used to be struck during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — meaning with the hands, sandals, and sticks. He said: And they were more numerous during the caliphate of Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) than they had been during the time of the Prophet ﷺ. So Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""If we were to assign a fixed punishment for them, approximating what they used to be struck with during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ..."" So Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) used to flog them forty lashes until he passed away. Then ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) after them flogged them likewise — forty lashes — until a man from the early Muhājirūn was brought who had drunk wine, and he was ordered to be flogged. He said: ""Why do you flog me? Between me and you is the Book of Allah."" He said: ""In which Book of Allah do you find that I should not flog you?"" He said: ""Indeed, Allah, the Most High, says in His Book: {There is no blame upon those who believe and do righteous deeds for what they have eaten} [al-Māʾidah:90] — the verse. I witnessed Badr, Uḥud, the Trench, and the (other) battles with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Will you not respond to what he says?"" Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""Indeed, these verses were revealed as an excuse for those who had passed and as a proof against those who remained. An excuse for those who had passed because they met Allah (Mighty and Majestic) before wine was made unlawful for them, and a proof against those who remained because Allah, the Most High, says: {O you who believe, indeed wine, gambling, idols, and divination arrows are an abomination} [al-Māʾidah:90] — the verse. So if he is among those who believed and did righteous deeds, then feared Allah and did good, then indeed Allah has forbidden wine to be drunk."" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""What do you all think?"" ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""We think that when he drinks, he becomes intoxicated; when he becomes intoxicated, he raves; and when he raves, he slanders. And upon the slanderer are eighty lashes."" So ʿUmar ordered, and he was flogged eighty. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17550","(17544) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed it to us through a higher chain: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Fārisī narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Kathīr ibn ʿUfayr narrated to me, Yaḥyā ibn Fulayḥ narrated to me, from Thawr ibn Zayd, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās: The drinkers used to be struck during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with the hands, sandals, and sticks, until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away. He said: Then he mentioned the hadith in its entirety."
"17551","(17545) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Ardastani — informed us; Abu Nasr al-Iraqi — informed us; Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari — narrated to us; Ali ibn al-Hasan — narrated to us; Abdullah ibn al-Walid — narrated to us; Sufyan — from Abu Sinan al-Shaybani — from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, who said: 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought an old man who had consumed wine in the month of Ramadan. He flogged him eighty lashes and exiled him to Syria, and he kept saying to those around him: ""In the month of Ramadan, while our children are fasting?"" [Hasan]"
"17552","(17546) He said, and Sufyan narrated to us — narrated to us 'Ata' ibn Abi Marwan — from his father, who said: 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought a man of the Negus (al-Najashi) who had consumed wine in Ramadan and had broken his fast. He flogged him eighty lashes. Then he brought him out the following day and flogged him twenty more, and said: ""I only struck you these twenty lashes for your audacity against Allah and for breaking your fast in the month of Ramadan."" [Sahih]"
"17553","(17547) Abu Muhammad — Abdullah ibn Yusuf — informed us; Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi — informed us; Sa'dan ibn Nasr — narrated to us; Sufyan — from 'Amr — from Muhammad ibn 'Ali, that 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) flogged a man for wine with forty lashes using a whip that had two ends, as though he intended to make it forty with the two ends. He mentioned this in another place as we have narrated in the hadith of Sa'dan. We have narrated in the connected hadith from him that he ordered that he be flogged forty lashes, and he cited as evidence the precedent of those before him. This narration is mursal (disconnected). And Allah knows best. [Da'if]"
"17554","(17548) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajani — informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim — narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr — narrated to us; Malik — from Ibn Shihab, that he was asked about the flogging of a slave for wine. He said: ""It has reached us that upon him is half the punishment of a free man. Indeed, 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, and Abdullah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) used to flog their slaves half the punishment of a free man for wine."" [Sahih — from al-Zuhri]"
"17555","(17549) Abu 'Amr — Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Bistami — informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili — informed us; al-Qasim, who is ibn Zakariyya — narrated to us; Bundar, Ahmad ibn Sinan, and Ya'qub — they said: narrated to us Ibn Mahdi — narrated to us Sufyan — from Abu Husayn — from 'Umayr ibn Sa'id al-Nakha'i — from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""There is no man upon whom I have carried out a prescribed punishment and who then dies, but that I feel regret in my soul — except for wine. If he dies from it, I will pay his blood-money, for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not establish it (as a fixed punishment).""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Muthanna from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi, and al-Bukhari narrated it through another chain from Sufyan. He (the narrator) intended — and Allah knows best — that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not establish it beyond forty, or that he did not establish it specifically with whips, for he did establish it with sandals and the ends of garments to the extent of forty. And Allah knows best. [Sahih]"
"17556","(17550) And in what Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz permitted me to narrate from him — from al-'Abbas, Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman — informed us; al-Shafi'i — informed us; Ibrahim ibn Muhammad — from Ali ibn Yahya — from al-Hasan, that 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""There is no one who dies under any of the prescribed punishments but that I feel something in my soul concerning it, except the one who dies under the punishment for wine. For it is something we innovated after the Prophet (ﷺ). So whoever dies from it, I will pay his blood-money"" — either he said, ""from the public treasury,"" or he said, ""from the Imam's clan"" — ""I am uncertain,"" meaning al-Shafi'i.

Al-Shafi'i (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""And it has reached us that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) sent for a woman, and she was alarmed and miscarried what was in her womb. He consulted 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), and he advised him to pay the blood-money. So 'Umar commanded 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both), and he said: 'I adjure you that you distribute it among your people.'"" [Sahih]"
"17557","(17551) Abu al-Qasim — 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn Mu'min ibn Shabban al-'Attar in Baghdad — narrated to us; al-Baqi ibn Qani' — narrated to us; Hamid ibn Muhammad — narrated to us; Shurayh — narrated to us; Hushaym — from al-Ash'ath — from al-Fudayl — from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil, that 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) flogged a man a prescribed punishment, and the executioner added two extra lashes. So 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) exacted retribution from the executioner for those two lashes. [Da'if]"
"17558","(17552) The Imam Abu al-Tayyib — Sahl ibn Muhammad ibn Sulayman (may Allah have mercy on him) — narrated to us by dictation; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman — narrated to us; Ayyub ibn Suwayd — from Ibn Shawdhab, meaning Abdullah ibn Shawdhab — from 'Amir ibn 'Abd al-Wahid — from Abdullah ibn Buraydah al-Aslami — from Abdullah ibn 'Amr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""When the Prophet (ﷺ) obtained a war-spoil, he would command Bilal, and he would make the announcement three times. The people would then bring what they had obtained. Then he would have it divided, and the fifth was taken from it. Then a man came to him with a rein made of hair after the war-spoil had already been divided. He (the Prophet ﷺ) said: 'Did you hear Bilal making the announcement three times?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'What prevented you from bringing it?' The man offered an apology to him. He (the Prophet ﷺ) said to him: 'You will be the one who presents it on the Day of Resurrection, for I will not accept it from you.'""

And likewise Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated it from Abdullah ibn Shawdhab. [Da'if]"
"17559","(17553) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz — informed me; Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah — informed us; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan — narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah — narrated to us; Jarir — from Sulayman al-Taymi — from Abu 'Uthman — from Abdullah, who said: ""A man committed an act with a woman short of intercourse. He came to 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), and he considered it grave. Then he came to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and he also considered it grave. Then he came to the Prophet (ﷺ), and I do not know whether he considered it grave or not. Then Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, revealed: 'Establish prayer at the two ends of the day and in the early hours of the night. Indeed, good deeds remove evil deeds.' [Hud: 114] The man said: 'Is this for me specifically, O Messenger of Allah?' He said: 'It is for those who act upon it from my Ummah.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah, and al-Bukhari narrated it through another chain from al-Taymi. [Sahih]"
"17560","(17554) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran — informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar — narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Mansur — narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq — informed us; Ibn Jurayj and Ibn Abi Sabrah — both said: ""Two men exchanged insults in the presence of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said nothing to them. Then they exchanged insults in the presence of 'Umar, and he disciplined them."" [Da'if]"
"17561","(17555) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Mu'mmal informed us. Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us. Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād. Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us. Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Saghānī narrated to us. Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us. Al-Aʿmash narrated from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said:

ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) came out with his hands placed over his ears, saying: ""Yā labbaykāh, yā labbaykāh."" The people said: ""What is the matter with him?"" He said: ""A messenger came to me from one of my governors informing me that a river had come between them and crossing over, and they could not find any boats. Their commander said: 'Find for us a man who knows the depth of the water.' So an old man was brought, but he said: 'I fear the cold, and that is in the cold season.' So he compelled him and put him into the water. The cold did not spare him for long, and he began calling out: 'Yā ʿUmarāh, yā ʿUmarāh!' and he drowned. So I wrote to him and he came. I turned away from him for several days. Whenever he found any anger in me towards any of them, he would do the same to him. Then he said: 'What happened to the man whom you killed?' He said: 'O Commander of the Faithful, I did not intend to kill him. We found nothing by which to cross, and we wanted to know the depth of the water, so we opened such and such and we struck such and such.' ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'A single Muslim man is more beloved to me than everything you have brought. Were it not that it would become a precedent, I would strike your neck. Go, pay his family his blood-money, and leave, for I do not wish to see you.'"""
"17562","(17556) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us. Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us. Ibn Wahb informed us.

(Ḥ) And Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: It was read to Ibn Wahb: Yūnus informed you from Ibn Shihāb, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), that he said:

""The fitrah (natural disposition) consists of five things: circumcision, shaving the pubic hair, trimming the moustache, clipping the nails, and plucking the armpit hair.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Ṭāhir and Ḥarmalah, from Ibn Wahb. Al-Bukhārī also narrated it from another route from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17563","(17557) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us. Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Hārūn ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ghazzī narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Ḥammā al-Ẓihrānī narrated to us. ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us. Ibn Jurayj informed us. I was informed from ʿUthaym ibn Kulaib, from his father, from his grandfather, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and accepted Islam. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Remove from yourself the hair of disbelief and be circumcised.""

Abū Aḥmad said: What Ibn Jurayj said in this chain of narration — ""I was informed from ʿUthaym ibn Kulaib"" — was in fact narrated to him by Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Yaḥyā, and he used a kunya in place of his name. [Very weak]"
"17564","(17558) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Bundār al-Qazwīnī informed us in Makkah. Abū Muḥammad Sahl ibn Aḥmad al-Dībājī narrated to us. Abū ʿAlī Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ashʿath narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd ibn Sulaymān al-Ṣūfī informed us. It was read to Abū ʿAlī Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ashʿath al-Kūfī. Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Mūsā ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us. My father narrated to me from his father, from his grandfather Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from his grandfather ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, from his father, from his father ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

""We found in the scabbard of the sword of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), in the scroll: 'The uncircumcised person shall not be left in Islam until he is circumcised, even if he reaches eighty years of age.'""

This is a hadith narrated exclusively by the Ahl al-Bayt (peace be upon them) through this chain of narration. [Weak; fabricated]"
"17565","(17559) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us. Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us. Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿĀṣim al-Dimashqī narrated to us. Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us. Marwān narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Ḥassān narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from Umm ʿAṭiyyah al-Anṣāriyyah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) instructed a woman who performed circumcision and said: ""When you circumcise, do not go to excess, for that is better for the woman and more beloved to the husband."" [Weak]"
"17566","(17560) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us. Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us. Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm al-Ashjaʿī both said: Marwān narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Ḥassān narrated — ʿAbd al-Wahhāb said al-Kūfī — from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from Umm ʿAṭiyyah al-Anṣāriyyah, that there was a woman in Madīnah who performed circumcision. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to her: ""Do not go to excess, for that is better for the woman and more beloved to the husband.""

Abū Dāwūd said: Muḥammad ibn Ḥassān is unknown (majhūl), and this hadith is weak."
"17567","(17561) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkārī informed us in Baghdād. Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī informed us. Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Azhar narrated to us. al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Ghassān al-Ghulābī narrated to us. He said: I asked Abū Zakariyyā about a hadith that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, who said: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAmr narrated to us. A man from the people of Kūfah narrated to me from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from al-Ḍaḥḥāq ibn Qays, who said:

There was a woman in Madīnah called Umm ʿAṭiyyah who circumcised slave girls. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to her: ""O Umm ʿAṭiyyah, circumcise and do not go to excess, for it is more radiant for the face and more pleasing to the husband.""

Al-Ghulābī said: Abū Zakariyyā — meaning Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn — said: ""This al-Ḍaḥḥāq ibn Qays is not al-Fihrī."""
"17568","(17562) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us. Abū Bakr ibn Abī Dārim informed us. Aḥmad ibn Mūsā ibn Isḥāq narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us. Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū Khalīfah informed us. Muḥammad ibn Salām al-Jumaḥī narrated to us. Zāʾidah ibn Abī al-Ruqād narrated to us. Thābit narrated from Anas that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to Umm ʿAṭiyyah:

""When you circumcise, take a small amount and do not go to excess, for it is more radiant for the face and more pleasing to the husband.""

Abū Aḥmad said: This is narrated from Thābit by Zāʾidah ibn Abī al-Ruqād; I do not know anyone other than him who narrates it from him."
"17569","(17563) And Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us. Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us. Muḥammad ibn al-Mutawakkil narrated to us. al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated from Zuhayr ibn Muḥammad al-Makkī, from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jābir, who said:

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) performed the ʿaqiqah for al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn and circumcised them on the seventh day. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains]"
"17570","(17564) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us. Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us. al-Asfaṭī — meaning al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl — and Tamtām both said: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us. Umm al-Aswad narrated to us. She said: I heard Munayyah bint ʿUbayd ibn Abī Barzah narrate from her grandfather Abū Barzah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), regarding an uncircumcised man performing Ḥajj of the House of Allah. He said: ""Not until he is circumcised.""

This is the wording of Tamtām's hadith. In the narration of al-Asfaṭī, he said: ""I heard Munayyah say: I heard Abū Barzah say: 'We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) about an uncircumcised man performing Ḥajj of the House of Allah.' He said: 'Not until he is circumcised.'"" [Weak]"
"17571","(17565) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us; Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan informed us; 'Abdan narrated to us; Ayyub al-Wazzan narrated to us; al-Walid ibn al-Walid narrated to us; Ibn Thawban narrated from Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abbas from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Circumcision is a Sunnah for men and an honour for women."" This chain of narration is weak. [Very weak]"
"17572","(17566) The preserved version is mawquf. It was narrated by Hilal ibn Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Haffar; al-Husayn ibn Yahya ibn 'Ayyash al-Qattan narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Mushajjar narrated to us; Waki' ibn al-Jarrah narrated from Sa'id ibn Bashir from Qatadah from Jabir ibn Zayd from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: ""Circumcision is a Sunnah for men and an honour for women."" [Weak]"
"17573","(17567) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Muhammad ibn Abi Hamid al-Muqri' informed us, both saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Sulayman al-Burlusi narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us; Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated from al-Hajjaj from Abu Malih ibn Usamah from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Circumcision is a Sunnah for men and an honour for women."" Al-Hajjaj ibn Arta'ah — his narration is not used as a proof. [Weak]"
"17574","(17568) It was also narrated from him from Makhul from Abu Ayyub, though it is munqati' (discontinuous). It was narrated by 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri'; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us; 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us; al-Hajjaj narrated from Makhul from Abu Ayyub, who said: The Prophet ﷺ said: ""Circumcision is a Sunnah for men and an honour for women."" [Weak]"
"17575","(17569) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abd Allah al-San'ani narrated to us; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated from Ma'mar from Qatadah from a man from Ibn 'Abbas, that he disliked the slaughtering of the uncircumcised person and said: ""His prayer is not accepted and his testimony is not valid."" [Weak]"
"17576","(17570) He said: And 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us from Ibn Abi Yahya from Dawud ibn al-Husayn from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: ""The prayer of a man who has not been circumcised is not accepted."" This indicates that he considered it obligatory, and that his statement ""Circumcision is a Sunnah"" was intended to mean the obligatory Sunnah of the Prophet ﷺ. [Very weak]"
"17577","(17571) The strongest evidence that may be cited on this matter is what Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us of; Abu Muhammad Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Nusayr ibn al-Qasim al-Khawwas narrated to us; Musa ibn Harun narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us; al-Mughirah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated from Abu al-Zinad from al-A'raj from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Ibrahim, the Prophet, upon him peace, was circumcised at the age of eighty with an adze."" Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Qutaybah ibn Sa'id. Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said: ""Then We revealed to you that you should follow the religion of Ibrahim, a man of true faith"" [al-Nahl 123]. And we have narrated in the Book of Purification from Ibn 'Abbas regarding His words: ""And when his Lord tried Ibrahim with certain words and he fulfilled them"" [al-Baqarah 124], that he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, tested him with five matters pertaining to the head and five pertaining to the body. Those pertaining to the head are: trimming the moustache, rinsing the mouth, sniffing water into the nose, using the toothstick, and parting the hair. Those pertaining to the body are: clipping the nails, shaving the pubic area, circumcision, plucking the armpit hair, and washing the place of excrement and urine with water. Our companions said: Testing (ibtila') generally occurs with that which is obligatory. [Sahih]"
"17578","(17572) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu Tahir al-Muhammadabadi informed us; Abu Qilabah narrated to us; Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us; Abu Shahab 'Abd Rabbah narrated from Hamzah al-Jazari from 'Abd al-Karim from Ibrahim from 'Alqamah, that 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, did not accept the testimony of the uncircumcised person. Hamzah al-Jazari was abandoned by the scholars; his narration is not used as a proof. [Very weak]"
"17579","(17573) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, both saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd Allah narrated to us; Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri' narrated to us; Musa ibn 'Ali said: I heard my father say: Indeed Ibrahim, the Intimate Friend of the Most Merciful, was commanded to be circumcised when he was eighty years old. He hastened and was circumcised with an adze. The pain became severe upon him, so he supplicated to his Lord, and Allah revealed to him: ""You hastened before We commanded you with the instrument."" He said: ""O Lord, I disliked delaying Your command."" He said: And Isma'il, upon him peace, was circumcised at the age of thirteen, and Ishaq, upon him peace, was circumcised at the age of seven days. [Sahih — attribution to 'Ali ibn Rabbah]"
"17580","(17574) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Malik narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam that a man confessed to adultery, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called for a whip. A broken whip was brought, so he said: ""Something above this."" Then a new whip was brought whose end had not been cut, so he said: ""Something between these two."" Then a whip was brought that had been used for riding, and he ordered that he be flogged with it. Then he said: ""O people, it is time for you to refrain from the prohibitions of Allah. Whoever among you commits any of these foul acts should conceal himself with the veil of Allah, for whoever exposes his offence to us, we shall enforce upon him the decree of Allah, Mighty and Majestic."" Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, said: This is a discontinuous hadith that does not stand as proof in itself. However, I have seen among the scholars of our land those who know it and act upon it, so we act upon it. [Weak]"
"17581","(17575) Abu Bakr al-Ardastani informed us — Abu Nasr al-Iraqi informed us in Bukhari — Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari narrated to us — Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us — Abdullah ibn al-Walid narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us — Asim al-Ahwal narrated from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought a man for a prescribed punishment, and a whip of severe intensity was brought. He said: ""I want one softer than this."" Then a whip of gentle flexibility was brought. He said: ""I want one more severe than this."" Then a whip of intermediate character was brought between the two. He said: ""Strike, and let not your armpits be seen, and give each limb its due."" [Hasan]"
"17582","(17576) He said, and Sufyan narrated to us — Abu Hasin informed us — one who informed me narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: A man was brought to him for drinking wine. He said: ""Leave his hands free that he may protect himself with them."" [Da'if]"
"17583","(17577) He said, and Sufyan narrated to us — Juwaybir narrated from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim — from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, who said: ""Nakedness, stretching at length, deception, and binding with ropes are not permissible in this Ummah."" [Da'if]"
"17584","(17578) Abu Muhammad Janah ibn Nudhayr ibn Janah, the judge in Kufa, informed us — Abu Ja'far ibn Duham narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Hazim informed us — Ubayd Allah ibn Musa informed us — Isra'il narrated from Yahya al-Jabir — from Abu Majid, who said: A Muslim man came with his brother who was intoxicated. He said: ""O Abu Abd al-Rahman, my brother is intoxicated."" He said: ""Make him rinse, make him sniff, and make him vomit."" They did so, and he was taken to prison. The following day he summoned him and called for a whip. Then he ordered that his punishment be administered, so it was crushed between two stones until it became a fine paste. Ubayd Allah indicated with both of his fingers, joining them together, and then said to the executioner: ""Flog, and withdraw your hand, and give each limb its due."" I said: ""What does 'withdraw your hand' mean?"" He said: ""The whiteness of his armpit should not be visible."" So he was struck with a non-disfiguring blow. I said: ""What is a non-disfiguring blow?"" He said: ""A strike that is neither severe nor feeble."" And he was struck while wearing a shirt and a lower garment, or a shirt and trousers. And he mentioned the hadith."
"17585","(17579) Abu Hazim al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-Fadl ibn Khumayruwayh informed us — Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us — Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us — Sufyan said: I heard Sa'd ibn Ibrahim narrating from al-Zuhri, who said: ""The people of Iraq say that the one guilty of false accusation (qadhf) is not to be flogged with a severe lashing."" Sa'd said: ""I bear witness on the authority of my father, who narrated to me that when Abu Bakrah was flogged, his mother ordered that a sheep be slaughtered, then skinned, and she clothed him in its skin. Is that anything but evidence of a severe lashing?"" [Da'if]"
"17586","(17580) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Shayban narrated to us — Sufyan narrated from Abu al-Zinad — from al-A'raj — from Abu Hurayrah that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: ""When any one of you strikes, let him avoid the face.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Amr al-Naqidh and Zuhayr, from Sufyan. [Sahih]"
"17587","(17581) Abu Hazim informed us — Abu al-Fadl ibn Khumayruwayh informed us — Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us — Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us — Hushaym narrated to us — Ibn Abi Layla narrated — from Adiyy ibn Thabit, who said: Hunaydah ibn Khalid informed me that he witnessed Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, carrying out a prescribed punishment upon a man. He said to the executioner: ""Strike, and give each limb its due, and avoid his face and his private parts."" [Da'if]"
"17588","(17582) Abu Hazim informed us — Abu al-Fadl ibn Khumayruwayh informed us — Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us — Sa'id narrated to us — Hushaym informed us — one of our companions informed us — from al-Hakam — from Yahya ibn al-Jazar that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: ""The man is to be flogged standing, and the woman seated."""
"17589","(17583) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki informed us — Abu Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab informed us — Ja'far ibn Awn informed us — Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah narrated — from Wasil — from al-Ma'rur, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought a woman who had committed adultery. He said: ""Woe to the one who has corrupted her beauty! Take her and flog her, but do not rupture her skin."" (Tirmidhi) And we have previously narrated in the hadith of Imran ibn Husayn, regarding the story of the woman of Juhaynah who confessed adultery: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered that her garments be fastened upon her, and in another narration, that she be bound, and then he ordered that she be stoned. [Sahih]"
"17590","(17584) Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami dictated to us, and Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah said — Ali ibn al-Fadl ibn Muhammad ibn Uqayl narrated to us — Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Najjah narrated to us. (H) And Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Harith al-Isbahani, the jurist, informed us — Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us — Ibn Najjah narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Husayn al-Asbahi narrated to us — Umar ibn Ali al-Muqaddami narrated to us — Mis'ar narrated from his maternal uncle, al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman — from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir — thus he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: ""Whoever strikes…"" And in the narration of al-Isbahani: ""Whoever inflicts a prescribed punishment beyond its proper limit is among the transgressors."" [Da'if]"
"17591","(17585) The preserved version of this hadith is mursal. Al-Sharīf Abū al-Fatḥ al-ʿUmarī informed us; Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Saqaṭī narrated to us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Miʿsar narrated from al-Walīd, from al-Ḍaḥḥāk, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever reaches a prescribed punishment (ḥadd) in a matter that is not a prescribed punishment, then he is among the transgressors."""
"17592","(17586) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīrūyah informed us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Hushaym informed us; Mughīrah said: ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz wrote that the minimum of the prescribed punishments should not be reached in discretionary punishment (taʿzīr), namely forty lashes. Differing narrations have been reported from the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) regarding the amount of that. The best to which one may resort in this matter is what is established from the Prophet (ﷺ)."
"17593","(17587) And that is what Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Abū Shuʿayb al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā al-Miṣrī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr al-Razjāhī informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Manīʿī and al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān said: Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to me; ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated from Bukayr ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ashajj, who said: While we were with Sulaymān ibn Yasār, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jābir entered and narrated to Sulaymān ibn Yasār. Then Sulaymān turned towards us and said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jābir narrated to me that his father narrated to him from Abū Burdah al-Anṣārī that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""No one shall be flogged above ten lashes except in a prescribed punishment from among the prescribed punishments of Allah.""

The wording is from the hadith of Abū ʿAmr. In the narration of Ibn ʿAbdān, it is narrated from him. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān from Ibn Wahb, and Muslim narrated it from Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā. Similarly, ʿUmar ibn al-Ḥārith narrated it from Bukayr, and likewise it was narrated from Usāmah ibn Zayd from Bukayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17594","(17588) Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrated it without mentioning Jābir in his isnād. ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us of it; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Milḥān narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated from Ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from Bukayr ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulaymān ibn Yasār, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, from Abū Burdah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to say: ""No one shall be flogged above ten lashes except in a prescribed punishment from among the prescribed punishments of Allah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf from al-Layth. And similarly, Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb narrated it from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb."
"17595","(17589) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Marwazī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb narrated to us, from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from Bukayr ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulaymān ibn Yasār, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Burdah ibn Niyār, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""No one shall be struck above ten lashes except in a prescribed punishment from among the prescribed punishments of Allah."""
"17596","(17590) It has a mursal witness. Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Hishām narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from al-Muhājir ibn ʿIkrimah, that ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr narrated to him that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""It is not permissible for a man who believes in Allah and the Last Day to flog above ten lashes except in a prescribed punishment.""

Yaʿqūb said: And it was narrated by someone whose narration is not trusted, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated to him. However, it is actually ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17597","(17591) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Tamtām narrated to me; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr al-Muqaddamī narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī ibn Muqaddam narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Muhājir narrated from Zufar ibn Wathīmah, from Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade that retaliation (qiṣāṣ) be carried out in the mosques, that poetry be recited in them, or that the prescribed punishments (ḥudūd) be executed in them. [Ḥasan]"
"17598","(17592) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Abī Idrīs, from ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in a gathering when he said: ""Pledge to me that you will not associate anything with Allah."" And he recited the verse to them and said: ""Whoever among you fulfills it, his reward is upon Allah. And whoever commits something of that and is punished, it shall be an expiation for him. And whoever commits something of that and Allah conceals it for him, then it is for Allah: if He wills, He will forgive him, and if He wills, He will punish him.""

The wording is from the hadith of al-Shāfiʿī. Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from a group from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah. Al-Shāfiʿī said in the narration of Abū Saʿīd: ""I did not hear in the matter of the prescribed punishments a hadith clearer than this."" And it has been narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said: ""And how do you know? Perhaps the prescribed punishments were revealed as expiation for sins."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17599","(17593) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris al-ʿAṭṭār said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us by dictation; al-Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated from Abī Isḥāq, from Abī Juḥayfah, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever commits a sin in this world and is punished for it, then Allah is more just than to double His punishment upon His servant. And whoever commits a sin in this world and Allah conceals it and pardons him, then Allah is more generous than to return to something He has already pardoned."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17600","(17594) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Ibn Manīʿ narrated to us; my grandfather and Ziyād ibn Ayyūb and ʿAlī ibn Muslim said: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us; Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Ibn Khuzaymah ibn Thābit, from his father, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Whoever commits a sin and the prescribed punishment for that sin is carried out upon him, then it shall be his expiation."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17601","(17595) As for the hadith which Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar informed us, from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I do not know whether Tubbaʿ was accursed or not, and I do not know whether Dhū al-Qarnayn was a prophet or not, and I do not know whether the prescribed punishments are expiations for their people or not.""

Thus ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated it from Maʿmar. And Hishām al-Ṣanʿānī narrated it from Maʿmar, from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, from al-Zuhrī, from the Prophet ﷺ, as a mursal narration. Al-Bukhārī said: ""And it is more authentic, and this is not established from the Prophet ﷺ, because the Prophet ﷺ said: 'The prescribed punishments are expiations.'""

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""We have recorded it through another chain from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb as a connected narration."" [Munkar]"
"17602","(17596) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī in Hamadhān narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dīzīl narrated to us, Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us, Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from al-Maqburī, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said… and he mentioned it with a similar meaning. If it is authentic, then it may be that he ﷺ said it at a time when knowledge had not yet come to him from Allah, the Most High, and then when it came to him, he said what we have narrated in the hadith of ʿUbādah and others. This is similar to what we have narrated in the hadith of Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh regarding the story of Māʿiz ibn Mālik, that the Prophet ﷺ ordered his stoning and did not pray over him. Then we have narrated from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn regarding the story of the Juhaniyyah woman, that the Prophet ﷺ ordered that she be stoned, and she was stoned, and he prayed over her. ʿUmar said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, do you pray over her when she committed adultery?"" He said: ""She has repented a repentance that, if it were divided among seventy of the people of Madīnah, it would suffice them. Can you find anything better than that she gave her life for the sake of Allah?""

And we have narrated in the hadith of Sulaymān ibn Buraydah from his father regarding the story of Māʿiz, about the hesitation concerning his case for two or three days, then the command to seek forgiveness for Māʿiz — which is similar to what we have mentioned. And Allah knows best. It is not possible to use the narration of Abū Hurayrah as evidence that it came after the hadith of ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, because the Companions used to take from one another. So it is possible that Abū Hurayrah — if the narration from him is authentic — took it from one of the Companions whose Islam came earlier. And Allah knows best."
"17603","(17597) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, who said: When ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, stoned Shurāḥah, I said: ""She died at the worst of times."" He then grabbed my garment and said: ""Whoever commits a sin carrying a prescribed punishment and the punishment is carried out upon him, it is his expiation."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17604","(17598) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Abū Yaḥyā al-Ḥimmānī narrated from al-Masʿūdī, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, that ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, carried out a prescribed punishment upon a man, and the people began to curse and revile him. So ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""As for this sin of his, he will not be questioned about it."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17605","(17599) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, both saying: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Kāmil al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Saʿd al-ʿAwfī narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us, the nephew of Ibn Shihāb narrated from his uncle, who said: Sālim said: I heard Abū Hurayrah say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""All of my Ummah will be pardoned except those who commit sins openly. And from open sin is that a man does a deed at night, and his Lord has concealed it, and then in the morning he says: 'O so-and-so, I did such-and-such last night,' while his Lord had covered him during the night. He sleeps under the cover of his Lord and rises to expose the cover of Allah over him.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim and ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd from Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from the nephew of Ibn Shihāb. [Ṣaḥīḥ]

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""A well-known hadith has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, though to my knowledge it is not connected in its chain, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Whoever among you is afflicted by any of these foul sins, let him seek the concealment of Allah, for whoever exposes his sin to us, we shall carry out upon him the decree of Allah.'"""
"17606","(17600) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from the Prophet ﷺ, and he mentioned it as a mursal narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrih]"
"17607","(17601) Abū al-Fatḥ Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Ḥaffār in Baghdād informed us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAyyāsh al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn ʿAmr al-Rabālī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated to us, and he said: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī say: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār narrated to me from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, after stoning the man from the tribe of Aslam, said: ""Beware of these foul sins which Allah has forbidden. Whoever is afflicted by any of them, let him seek the concealment of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17608","(17602) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Hārūn ibn Mūsā al-Farwī narrated to us, Abū Ḍamrah narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār, and he mentioned it with the same meaning, adding: ""And let him repent to Allah, for whoever exposes his sin to us, we shall carry out upon him the decree of Allah.""

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""And it is narrated that Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, ordered a man who had committed a prescribed punishment to conceal himself, and that ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, ordered him to do so."" The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""The chain of this has already been given in the chapter on confession of adultery."""
"17609","(17603) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated from Wāṣil, from al-Maʿrūr, who said: ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought a woman who had committed adultery… and he mentioned the hadith. Then ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Allah has made the four witnesses a screen that conceals you from your indecencies. Let no one tear the screen of Allah. Indeed, if Allah had willed, He could have made it one witness, truthful or lying.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""And we love for the one who has committed a prescribed punishment to conceal himself, to fear Allah, and not to return to the disobedience of Allah, for Allah accepts the repentance of His servants."""
"17610","(17604) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated to me, al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, that Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed him that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""A Muslim is the brother of a Muslim; he does not wrong him nor forsake him. Whoever fulfills the need of his brother, Allah will fulfill his need. Whoever relieves a Muslim of a hardship, Allah will relieve him thereby of a hardship from the hardships of the Day of Resurrection. And whoever conceals the faults of a Muslim, Allah will conceal his faults on the Day of Resurrection.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17611","(17605) And 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭabarānī informed us, Ibn Kaysān informed us, Abū Ḥudhayfah informed us, Sufyān narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam from Yazīd ibn Nu'aym from his father, from the Prophet ﷺ, in the story of Mā'iz ibn Mālik, he said regarding him: ""O Hazzāl, had you covered him with your garment, it would have been better for you than what you did."""
"17612","(17606) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad 'Abd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī both informed us, saying: Abū Muḥammad 'Abd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Fākihī informed us in Makkah, Abū Yaḥyā ibn Abī Masarrat informed us, Abū Jābir informed us, Shu'bah narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir from Ibn Hazzāl from his father Hazzāl, a man from Aslam, that he mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ the ḥadīth of Mā'iz, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: ""Had you covered him with your garment, it would have been better for you.""

Thus narrated by a group from Shu'bah."
"17613","(17607) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-'Anbarī informed us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us, Muḥammad ibn 'Amr Kashmurd informed us, al-Qa'nabī informed us, Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to a man from Aslam called Hazzāl: ""Had you covered him with your garment, it would have been better for you."" Yaḥyā said: ""I narrated this ḥadīth in a gathering in which Yazīd ibn Nu'aym ibn Hazzāl al-Aslamī was present, and he said: 'Hazzāl is my grandfather, and this ḥadīth is true.' This is more authentic than what precedes it."""
"17614","(17608) And Abū 'Alī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Muḥammad ibn 'Ubayd informed us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd informed us, Yaḥyā narrated from Ibn al-Munkadir that Hazzāl commanded Mā'iz to come to the Prophet ﷺ and inform him.

And al-Layth narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd from Yazīd ibn Nu'aym from his grandfather Hazzāl. And likewise 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammār narrated it from Yazīd ibn Nu'aym ibn Hazzāl from his grandfather Hazzāl."
"17615","(17609) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us, 'Abd Allāh ibn Ja'far informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Nashīṭ from Ka'b ibn 'Alqamah from Abū al-Haytham, who said: It was said to 'Uqbah ibn 'Āmir: ""We have neighbours who drink wine and do such and such."" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whoever sees a fault (of another) and conceals it is as though he revived a buried infant girl from her grave.'"" [Weak]"
"17616","(17610) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, 'Abd Allāh ibn Ja'far informed us, Ya'qūb ibn Sufyān informed us, Abū al-Walīd Hishām informed us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd informed me, Ibrāhīm ibn Nashīṭ al-Wa'lānī narrated from Ka'b ibn 'Alqamamah from Dukhayn, Abū al-Haytham, the scribe of 'Uqbah, who said: I said to 'Uqbah ibn 'Āmir: ""We have neighbours who drink wine, and I am going to call the authorities upon them so they will seize them."" He said: ""Do not do that; rather, admonish them and warn them."" He (Dukhayn) said: ""So I did that, but they did not desist. So Dukhayn came to 'Uqbah and said: 'I forbade them, but they did not desist, and I am going to call the authorities upon them.' 'Uqbah said: 'Woe to you, do not do that! For I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Whoever conceals the fault of a believer is as though he revived a buried infant girl from her grave.'"""
"17617","(17611) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-Mahrī informed us, Ibn Wahb informed us, and he said: I heard Ibn Jurayj narrating from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb from his father, from 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Amr ibn al-'Āṣ, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Pardon (waive) the prescribed punishments amongst yourselves, for whatever (case of) a prescribed punishment reaches me, it becomes obligatory (to carry out)."" [Weak]"
"17618","(17612) Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ al-Muḥāribī informed us in Kūfah, Abū Ja'far Muḥammad ibn 'Alī ibn Duḥaym informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us, 'Ubayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us, Isrā'īl narrated from Yaḥyā al-Jābir from Abū Mājid, who said: A Muslim man brought his brother, who was intoxicated, meaning to 'Abd Allāh ibn Mas'ūd. He mentioned the ḥadīth regarding the manner of his flogging. Then he said to his uncle: ""Evil, by the life of Allah, is the guardian of the orphan that you are! You did not discipline him well, nor did you conceal his fault."" He said: ""O Abū 'Abd al-Raḥmān, by Allah, he is my brother's son, and I have no children of my own, and I feel for him the same anguish I feel for my own child. But I have not neglected what is good."" 'Abd Allāh said: ""Allah is Pardoning and loves pardon. But it is not befitting for a person in authority that a prescribed punishment is brought to him except that he carries it out."" Then he began to narrate to us from the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, saying: ""The first man from the Muslims whose hand was cut off was a man from the Anṣār. He was brought to the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, having stolen. He said: 'Go with your companion and cut off his hand.' It was as though the face of the Prophet of Allah ﷺ turned ashen. Then he gestured with his hand, concealing it. Some of the people said: 'It seems as though this is difficult for you?' He said: 'It is not befitting that you be helpers of Satan or Iblīs. For it is not befitting for a person in authority that a prescribed punishment is brought to him except that he carries it out. And Allah is Pardoning and loves pardon.' Then he recited: {And let them pardon and overlook} [al-Nūr, 22:22] — the verse."""
"17619","(17613) He said: And Aḥmad informed us, Abū Nu'aym informed us, Sufyān narrated from Yaḥyā al-Jābir from Abū Mājid, from 'Abd Allāh, from the Prophet ﷺ, something similar to it."
"17620","(17614) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, Abū 'Utbah informed us, Baqiyyah narrated from Warqā' ibn 'Umar from Jābir ibn Yazīd from Yazīd ibn Murrah from Abū Majza'ah, who said: ""Whoever commits a sin, let us come to us so we may purify him."" A people came and struck him. Then Salmān al-Fārṣī, may Allah be pleased with him, came in a state of anger and said: ""Has Allah made repentance anything?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Then throw down the whip and do not tear open a veil that Allah has covered.""

(T) And it has been narrated to us from 'Ikrimah that 'Ammār ibn Yāsir, may Allah be pleased with him, had a cloak stolen from him, and the one who stole it was identified, but he left him alone. And from 'Ikrimah, who said: Ibn 'Abbās was brought a thief who had stolen from a freedwoman of his, so he gave him provisions and sent him away. [Weak]"
"17621","(17615) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us; Ali ibn Umar al-Hafiz informed us; Sa'id ibn Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Hannat informed us; Abu Hisham al-Rifa'i informed us; Ibn Fudayl informed us; 'Atā' ibn al-Sā'ib informed us from Maythara, who said: A man and his mother came to 'Alī (may Allah be pleased with him), and she said: ""This son of mine has killed my husband."" The son said: ""My slave fell upon my mother."" 'Alī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""You are both ruined and at a loss! If you are truthful, we shall kill your son; and if your son is truthful, we shall stone you."" Then 'Alī (may Allah be pleased with him) stood up for Salah. The boy said to his mother: ""Do you not see that he will either kill me or stone you?"" So they departed. When he finished the Salah, he inquired about them and was told: ""They have gone."" [Da'if]"
"17622","(17616) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih informed me; Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us; Abu al-Walid informed us; Layth ibn Sa'd informed us.

(Cont.) He said: And Abu al-Nadr Ibrahim ibn Isma'il al-'Anbari informed me; Muhammad ibn Rumh informed us; Layth ibn Sa'd informed us from Ibn Shihab from 'Urwah from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Quraysh were concerned about the case of the Makhzumi woman who had committed theft. They said: ""Who will speak to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) about her?"" They said: ""Who would dare to do so except Usamah ibn Zayd, the beloved of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)?"" So Usamah spoke to him. He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""O Usamah, do you intercede regarding one of the prescribed punishments of Allah?"" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) stood up and delivered a sermon, saying: ""Indeed, those who came before you were destroyed because when a person of nobility among them committed theft, they left him alone; but when a person of low standing among them committed theft, they carried out the prescribed punishment upon him. By Allah, were Fatimah, the daughter of Muhammad, to commit theft, I would certainly cut off her hand.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu al-Walid, and Muslim narrated it from Muhammad ibn Rumh. [Sahih]"
"17623","(17617) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri informed us; Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr informed us; Zuhayr informed us; 'Umarah ibn Ghazi'ah informed us; Yahya ibn Rashid al-Dimashqi informed us: that they sat with Ibn 'Umar. He said: I did not see him willing to sit with us until we said: ""Come to the assembly, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman!"" He said: I saw him show reluctance. Then he sat, and we remained silent; none of us spoke. He said: ""Why do you not speak? Why do you not say 'SubhanAllahi wa bihamdihi'? For one utterance is worth ten, ten is worth a hundred, and a hundred is worth a thousand. And whatever you increase, Allah will increase for you."" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: 'Whoever's intercession prevents one of the prescribed punishments of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has indeed opposed Allah in His command. And whoever dies while owing a debt, it is not to be repaid in dinars and dirhams, but rather in good deeds and bad deeds. And whoever argues for falsehood while knowing it remains in the displeasure of Allah until he desists. And whoever says about a believer that which is not in him, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, will place him in the mire of perdition until he comes out of what he said.'"" [Sahih]"
"17624","(17618) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us; Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Sharqi informed us; Abu Hatim Muhammad ibn Idris al-Hanzhali informed us; Safwan ibn Salih al-Mu'adhdhin informed us; Marwan ibn Muhammad informed us; Sa'id ibn Bashir informed us; Matar al-Warraq informed us; Nafi' informed us from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to his Companions while they were sitting: ""Why do you not speak? Whoever says 'SubhanAllahi wa bihamdihi,' Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, records for him ten good deeds. Whoever says it ten times, Allah records for him a hundred good deeds. Whoever says it a hundred times, Allah records for him a thousand good deeds. And whoever increases, Allah increases for him. And whoever seeks forgiveness, Allah forgives him. And whoever's intercession prevents one of the prescribed punishments of Allah has indeed opposed Allah in His judgment. And whoever accuses an innocent person, Allah will cast him into the mire of perdition until he produces an exit from what he said. And whoever disowns his child, thereby disgracing him in this world, Allah will disgrace him before all of creation on the Day of Resurrection."" [Da'if]"
"17625","(17619) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-'Abbas al-Dawri informed us; Abu Nu'aim al-Fadl ibn Dukayn informed us; Isra'il informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Jahm informed us; 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed us from his father, al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Intercede regarding the prescribed punishments so long as the matter has not reached the Sultan (ruling authority). But when it reaches the Sultan, do not intercede."""
"17626","(17620) And Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us; Abu 'Abdillah ibn Ya'qub informed us; Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us; Ja'far ibn 'Awn informed us; Hisham ibn 'Urwah informed us; 'Abdullah ibn 'Urwah informed us from al-Farafisah al-Hanafi, who said: Al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by us while we had apprehended a thief. He began interceding on his behalf, saying: ""Release him."" We said: ""O Abu 'Abdillah, do you command us to release him?"" He said: ""That may be done before the matter reaches the Sultan. But when it reaches the Sultan, then may Allah not pardon him if he is pardoned."" [Sahih]"
"17627","(17621) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed me; Muhammad ibn Salih ibn Hani' informed us; Ja'far ibn Ahmad al-Shamati informed us; 'Abd al-Quddus ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Kabir informed us; 'Amr ibn 'Asim informed us; Hammam informed us; Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah informed us from Anas, who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) when a man came to him and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have committed a prescribed punishment, so enforce it upon me."" He (peace and blessings be upon him) did not ask him about it. Then the time for Salah came, so he prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). When the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) finished the Salah, the man stood before him and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have indeed committed a prescribed punishment, so enforce upon me the Book of Allah."" He said: ""Did you not pray with us?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Indeed, Allah has forgiven your sin.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abd al-Quddus ibn Muhammad, and Muslim narrated it from al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Hulwani from 'Amr ibn 'Asim. Abu Umamah also narrated something to that effect from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). [Sahih]"
"17628","(17622) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdillah ibn Ya'qub informed us; Ja'far ibn Muhammad and Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Salam informed us; Yahya ibn Yahya informed us. He said: I recited to Malik from Abu al-Zinad from al-A'raj from Abu Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Beware of suspicion, for suspicion is the most false of speech. Do not spy upon one another, do not search for one another's faults, do not compete with one another, do not envy one another, do not hate one another, and do not turn your backs on one another. And be, O servants of Allah, brothers.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and al-Bukhari narrated it through another chain from al-A'raj. [Sahih]"
"17629","(17623) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan informed us; Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami informed us; Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi informed us; Sufyan informed us; Thawr informed us; Rashid ibn Sa'd informed us; Mu'awiyah informed us: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: ""If you follow the hidden faults of the people, you will corrupt them, or nearly corrupt them."" Abu al-Darda' used to say: ""A word that Mu'awiyah heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and Allah benefited him thereby."" [Sahih]"
"17630","(17624) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dassah informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; Sa'id ibn 'Amr al-Hadrami informed us; Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash informed us; Damdam ibn Zur'ah informed us; Shurayh ibn 'Ubayd informed us; Jubayr ibn Nufayr, Kathir ibn Murrah, 'Amr ibn al-Aswad, al-Maqdad ibn Ma'dikarib, and Abu Umamah informed us from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""Indeed, when a ruler seeks out the faults of the people, he corrupts them."" [Da'if]"
"17631","(17625) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar reported, from al-Zuhrī, from Zurārah ibn Muṣʿab ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, that he kept watch with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with both of them, one night in Madīnah. While they were walking, a lamp flared up in a house, so they set out towards it. When they drew near it, they found a door closed upon people who had raised voices and clamorous speech therein. ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, took ʿAbd al-Raḥmān by the hand and said: ""Do you know whose house this is?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""This is the house of Rabīʿah ibn Umayyah ibn Khalaf. They are now drinking. What do you think?"" ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: ""I think we have come to that which Allah has forbidden us,"" and he recited: {And do not spy} [al-Ḥujurāt 12]. ""We have indeed spied."" So ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, turned away from them and left them. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17632","(17626) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; al-Aʿmash reported, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: It was said to ʿAbdullāh: ""What do you say about so-and-so whose beard drips with wine?"" He said: ""Indeed, Allah has forbidden us to spy. If something becomes manifest to us, we shall take hold of him."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17633","(17627) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh al-Muzakkī both said: Ḥassān ibn Muḥammad al-Qurashī, the Imam Abū al-Walīd, narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Nāfiʿ al-Madanī reported, from Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm, who said: ʿAmrah said: ʿĀʾishah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Overlook the slips of those of reputable standing."" [Referred to previously at no. 17249]"
"17634","(17628) Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Mihrānī al-Muzakkī, Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Shādhayākhī, and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl reported, from Ibn Abī Fudayk; ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Zayd narrated to me, from Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr ibn Ḥazm, from his father, from ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿĀʾishah, that she said: The Prophet ﷺ said: ""Overlook the stumbles of those of reputable standing, except in the case of a prescribed punishment (ḥadd) from among the prescribed punishments of Allah.""

Duhaym and Abū al-Ṭāhir ibn al-Sarḥ also narrated it from Ibn Abī Fudayk. And a group narrated it from Ibn Abī Fudayk without mentioning his father in the chain. And Allah knows best. [Referred to previously at no. 17249]"
"17635","(17629) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ reported that al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Those of reputable standing (dhawī al-hayʾāt) whose stumbles are to be overlooked are those who are not known for evil, yet one of them commits a lapse."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — from al-Shāfiʿī]"
"17636","(17630) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus reported, from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: When Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, dispatched Khālid ibn al-Walīd to the people of apostasy (al-Riddah), he mobilized the people with him, and Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, went out with him until they stopped at Dhū al-Qaṣṣah from Madīnah, two postal stages away. There he marshaled his armies and gave him his instructions. He appointed Thābit ibn Qays ibn al-Shammās over the Anṣār, placing him under Khālid's command, and he appointed Khālid over the entire body of people from among the Muhājirūn and the Arab tribes. Then he ordered him to march against Ṭulayḥah ibn Khuwaylid al-Asadī, and when he was finished with him, to press on to the territory of Banī Tamīm until he had dealt with what was there. He confided this to him privately, but gave the outward impression that he would meet Khālid with the remaining people in the region of Khaybar, though he did not intend that — he only made this outward show as a stratagem, having already mobilized the people fully with Khālid. So Khālid proceeded until he met Ṭulayḥah on the day of Buzākhah at a watering place of Banī Asad called Quṭan. With Ṭulayḥah was ʿUyaynah ibn Badr with seven hundred men from Fazārah. When the battle stirred, ʿUyaynah came to Ṭulayḥah and said: ""May I be your ransom! Has Jibrīl come to you yet?"" He said: ""No, by Allah."" He said: ""What is keeping him? He has indeed exhausted us."" Then he came to him again and asked, and he said: ""Yes, he has come to me."" He said: ""He said: 'You have a mill that you will turn and a discourse you will not forget.'"" He said: ""I think Allah knew that there would be for you a discourse you will not forget. By Allah, O Banī Fazārah, he is a liar! Go about your business."" The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: We have narrated in the book on the fighting of the people of rebellion (Kitāb Qiṭāl Ahl al-Baghy) from al-Zuhrī the account of the killing of Ṭulayḥah by ʿUkāshah ibn Miḥṣan and Thābit ibn Aqram during this campaign, and then his Islam when the truth prevailed, and his entering iḥrām for ʿUmrah, and his passing by Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, in Madīnah. And it has not reached us that Abū Bakr exacted retaliation from him or held him liable for blood-money. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17637","(17631) In my book, from Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — and I believe it is from what I heard from him, otherwise it is from what he authorized to me — that Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Aṣbahānī informed them: al-Ḥasan ibn al-Jahm narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faraj narrated to us; al-Wāqidī narrated to me; Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī reported, from his father, who said: When the rout occurred in Ṭulayḥah's army, he fled among the people until he reached Shām. Then he came to Makkah during the caliphate of ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him. When ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, saw him, he said: ""O Ṭulayḥah, I will not love you after you killed the two righteous men, ʿUkāshah ibn Miḥṣan and Thābit ibn Aqram."" He said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, Allah honored them at my hands and did not disgrace me at their hands. Not all households are built upon love, but rather upon a gracious pardon, for people extend the hand of reconciliation despite mutual rancor."" And Ṭulayḥah embraced Islam with a sound Islam. [Very weak — Ḍaʿīf jiddan]"
"17638","(17632) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated to us; Sufyān reported, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb, who said: The delegation of Buzākhah — from Asad and Ghaṭafān — came to Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, asking him for a truce. So Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, gave them the choice between a war of expulsion or a humiliating peace. They said: ""This war of expulsion — we know it. What is the humiliating peace?"" Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""You surrender the armor and the weapons, and you leave behind a people who follow the tails of camels, until Allah shows the Khalīfah of His Prophet and the Muslims a matter by which they may excuse you. And you pay the blood-money for our slain, and we will not pay the blood-money for your slain. Our slain are in Paradise, and your slain are in the Fire. And you return what you have taken from us, and we take as spoils what we have taken from you."" ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I have formed an opinion, and I shall consult you regarding it. As for their surrendering the armor and the weapons — what a fine opinion you have formed! As for leaving behind a people who follow the tails of camels until Allah shows the Khalīfah of His Prophet and the Muslims a matter by which they may excuse them — what a fine opinion you have formed! As for our taking as spoils what we have taken from them and their returning what they have taken from us — what a fine opinion you have formed! As for their slain being in the Fire and our slain being in Paradise — what a fine opinion you have formed! But as for their paying the blood-money for our slain — no. Our slain were killed in the cause of Allah, so there is no blood-money for them."" The people then fell in agreement upon that. The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: The statement of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding property does not contradict his statement regarding blood, for he intended thereby — and Allah knows best — the specific properties of the Muslims that were in their possession, not liability for what they had destroyed. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17639","(17633) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Asfāṭī narrated to us; Abū al-Walīd narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to me; my father narrated to me, from Abī ʿUbaydah ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAwf, from Saʿīd ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever is killed in defense of his property is a martyr; whoever is killed in defense of his family is a martyr; and whoever is killed in defense of his religion is a martyr.""

Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī and Abū Ayyūb al-Hāshimī narrated it from Ibrāhīm, and he said: ""And whoever is killed in defense of his family, or in defense of his blood, or in defense of his religion, he is a martyr."" And its mention has passed. [Ḥasan]"
"17640","(17634) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Shuʿayb al-Kaysānī narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Muqriʾ narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Tarqafī informed us; Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Muqriʾ narrated to me; Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb narrated to me; Abū al-Aswad reported, from ʿIkrimah, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Whoever is killed in defense of his property, being wronged, then for him is Paradise."" The wording of both narrations is the same. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17641","(17635) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Anbarī informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr informed us. Isḥāq said: ""informed us,"" and Ibn Rāfiʿ said: ""narrated to us."" ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed me; Sulaymān al-Aḥwali informed me that Thābit, the freedman of ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, informed him: that when there occurred what took place between ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr and ʿAnbasah ibn Abī Sufyān, and they prepared for battle, Khālid ibn al-ʿĀṣ rode to ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr and admonished him. ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr said: ""Are you not aware that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Whoever is killed in defence of his property is a martyr.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr and Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ."
"17642","(17636) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm, who said: I heard a man from Banī Makhzūm narrating from his uncle: that Muʿāwiyah intended to take the Waht property from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, so he ordered his freedmen to arm themselves. He was told about that, and he said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: 'Whoever is killed in defence of his property is a martyr.'"""
"17643","(17637) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed us; Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us; al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from his father, from Abū Hurayrah: that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you think if a man comes to me wanting to take my property?"" He said: ""Do not give him your property."" He said: ""What if he fights me?"" He said: ""Then fight him."" He said: ""What if he kills me?"" He said: ""Then you are a martyr."" He said: ""What if I kill him?"" He said: ""He is in the Fire.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ through another chain from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17644","(17638) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-ʿAlawī and Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Muḥammad al-Najjār al-Muqriʾ in Kūfah both said: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Ḥammād narrated from Asbāṭ, from Simāk, from Qābūs ibn Mukhāriq, from his father, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Prophet of Allah, a man comes to me wanting to seize my property — what should I do with him?"" He said: ""Appeal to Allah against him."" He said: ""What if I appeal to him and he refuses to desist?"" He said: ""Seek the help of the Muslims."" He said: ""O Prophet of Allah, what if there is no Muslim I can seek help from against him?"" He said: ""Then seek help from the ruler."" He said: ""O Prophet of Allah, what if there is no ruler available to me to seek help from?"" He said: ""Then fight him. If he kills you, you will be among the martyrs of the Hereafter, and if you kill him, you will have protected your property."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17645","(17639) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Ṣibghī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us; Ibn Abī Uways narrated to me; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn al-Muṭṭalib narrated from his brother al-Ḥakam, from his father al-Muṭṭalib ibn Ḥanṭab, from Quhayd al-Ghifārī, who said: A questioner asked the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, if an aggressor transgresses against me?"" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him: ""Remind him of Allah."" He commanded him to remind him three times. ""If he refuses, then fight him. If he kills you, you will be in Paradise, and if you kill him, he will be in the Fire."" Thus he said. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17646","(17640) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; my father and Shuʿayb both said: al-Layth narrated from Ibn al-Hād, from Quhayd ibn Muṭarrif al-Ghifārī, from Abū Hurayrah: that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what if my property is transgressed upon?"" He said: ""Then appeal to Allah."" He said: ""What if they refuse?"" He said: ""Then appeal to Allah."" He said: ""What if they refuse?"" He said: ""Then appeal to Allah."" He said: ""What if they persist against me?"" He said: ""Then fight. If you are killed, you will be in Paradise, and if he is killed, he will be in the Fire."" Thus I found it, and the correct version is from Ibn al-Hād, from ʿAmr ibn Abī ʿAmr, from Quhayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17647","(17641) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Ibn Jurayj narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ that Ṣafwān ibn Yaʿlā ibn Umayyah narrated to him from Yaʿlā ibn Umayyah, who said: I went on the campaign of al-ʿUsrah with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and it was the most burdensome of my deeds in my own estimation. I had a hired man who fought with a man, and one of them bit the other. He pulled his hand away from his mouth, and his front tooth fell out. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he waived his right to the tooth. ʿAṭāʾ said: I think Ṣafwān said that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Would he leave his hand in your mouth so that you might chew it as a stallion chews?""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥs through various chains from Ibn Jurayj. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17648","(17642) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; Baḥr narrated to us; Ibn Wahb said: And I heard Ibn Jurayj narrating from Ibn Abī Mulaykah, from his father: that a man fought with another man and bit him. The man pulled his hand from his mouth, and his tooth was extracted. They both came to Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him), and he waived the right to it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17649","(17643) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhabārī al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmūyah al-ʿAskarī narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us; Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us; Qatādah narrated. He said: I heard Zurārah ibn Awfāʾ narrating from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn: that a man bit the hand of another man. The man pulled his hand from his mouth, and his two front teeth fell out. They brought their dispute to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he said: ""One of you bites his brother as a stallion bites — there is no blood-money for you.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar from Shuʿbah."
"17650","(17644) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik narrated from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah: that Saʿd said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you think if I find a man with my wife — should I leave him until I bring four witnesses?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Yes.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Mālik as previously mentioned. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17651","(17645) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Thaqafī narrated to us, that Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, that ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from Suhayl from his father from Abū Hurayrah: that Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah al-Anṣārī said, ""O Messenger of Allah, if a man finds another man with his wife, should he kill him?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""No."" Saʿd said, ""Yes, indeed, by the One Who has honoured you with the truth."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""Listen to what your master is saying.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd."
"17652","(17646) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar and Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us that ʿAbdah ibn Sulaymān narrated from Sulaymān al-Aʿmash from Ibrāhīm from ʿAlqamah from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: ""While we were in the mosque on the night of Jumuʿah, a man said, 'If a man were to find another man with his wife and kill him, would you kill him? And if he were to speak of it, would you flog him?' He said, 'I shall mention this to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.'"" He said, ""So he mentioned it to the Prophet ﷺ, and Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed the verses of al-Liʿān (the oath of mutual cursing). Then the man came and accused his wife of adultery, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ carried out the liʿān between them and said, 'Perhaps she will bring forth a black, curly-haired child.' And she did bring forth a black, curly-haired child.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17653","(17647) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrānjānī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us that Ibn Bukayr narrated to us that Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib: that a man from the people of Shām, known as Ibn Khaybarī, found another man with his wife and killed him—or killed both of them. The matter became difficult for Muʿāwiyah to judge, so Muʿāwiyah wrote to Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī asking him to inquire of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālliib, may Allah be pleased with him, about that. So Abū Mūsā asked ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib about it, and ʿAlī said, ""This is a matter that has not occurred in my land. I adjure you, you must inform me."" Abū Mūsā said, ""Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān wrote to me about this."" ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said, ""I am Abū al-Ḥasan: if he does not bring four witnesses, let him be given over entirely [to retribution]."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17654","(17648) As for the narration that Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us of in Baghdād: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us that Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us that ʿAffān ibn Muslim narrated to us that Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us that Thābit and Ḥumayd and Maṭar and ʿAbbād ibn Manṣūr narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUbayy ibn ʿUmayr: that a man from among the Arabs had a group of people as his guests. He slaughtered a sheep for them. He had two daughters. He said to one of them, ""Go and gather firewood."" She went, and when she had gone some distance, one of the guests followed her and tried to force her to commit indecency. She said, ""Fear Allah!"" and adjured him, but he persisted. She said, ""Wait a moment until I make myself ready for you."" She left him and he fell asleep. She then came with a rock and split his head open, killing him. She came to her father and told him the news. He said, ""Be silent and do not tell anyone."" He then prepared food and placed it before his companions and said, ""Eat."" They said, ""Not until our companion comes."" He said, ""Eat, for he will come to you."" When they had eaten, he praised Allah and extolled Him and said, ""Such and such happened."" They said, ""Enemy of Allah, you have killed our companion! By Allah, we shall kill you for it."" They went to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him. He said, ""What was the name of your companion?"" They said, ""Ghufal."" He said, ""He is indeed as his name suggests,"" and he declared his blood to be forfeit (i.e., not subject to retribution). This is a mursal narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17655","(17649) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us in Baghdād that Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz and Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, saying: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us that Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr: that a man hosted a group from Hudhayl. A slave-girl of theirs went out to gather firewood, and one of the men tried to force her to commit indecency. She struck him with a stone and killed him. The matter was raised to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him. He said, ""That is a slain one of Allah; Allah will never pay his blood-money."""
"17656","(17650) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us that Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us that al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī said: ""This [ruling] from ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, is [held] by us [as authoritative] because proof was established before him against the slain person, or because the guardian of the slain person acknowledged before him that which necessitates the killing of the slain person."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — from al-Shāfiʿī]"
"17657","(17651) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hāshim narrated to us that Sufyān—

(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us that Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us in Makkah that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us that Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, who heard Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī say: ""A man peered through a hole into the chamber of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ had a comb (midayyah) with which he was scratching his head. He said, 'Had I known that you were looking, I would have struck it into your eye. Permission to enter (al-isti'dhān) was only made on account of sight.'"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Zaʿfarānī. In the narration of Ibn Hāshim it reads: ""Had I known that you were looking at me…""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17658","(17652) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us in Baghdād that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us that Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī from Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī: that a man peered at the Prophet ﷺ through the curtain of his chamber, and in the hand of the Prophet ﷺ was a comb (midayyah). He said, ""Had I known that this one was looking at me until I reached him, I would have struck the comb into his eye. Permission to enter (al-isti'dhān) was only made on account of sight (al-baṣar).""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ through another chain from Maʿmar ibn Rāshid."
"17659","(17653) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us that ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us that al-Ḥajjāj ibn al-Minhāl and Abū al-Nuʿmān narrated to us, saying: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr from Anas ibn Mālik: that a man peered into one of the chambers of the Prophet ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came toward him with a sharp-pointed arrowhead (mishqaṣ) or arrowheads, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went toward the man, stalking him to strike him with it. Al-Ḥajjāj said: ""It is as though I am looking at the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stalking him to strike him.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Nuʿmān, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others from Ḥammād. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17660","(17654) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Muqriʾ, Ibn al-Ḥammāmī, informed us in Baghdād that Abū Muḥammad Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAlī ibn Ismāʿīl al-Khaṭabī narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥarbī narrated to us that Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us that Abān ibn Yazīd narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah from Anas ibn Mālik: that a Bedouin came to the door of the Prophet ﷺ and pressed his eye to the crevice of the door to look inside. The Prophet ﷺ noticed him and took up a sharpened stick, and he struck at the eye of the Bedouin, who then withdrew. The Prophet ﷺ said, ""Had he remained, I would have gouged out his eye."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17661","(17655) It was narrated to us by 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bishran in Baghdad: it was narrated to us by Abu Ja'far 'Amr ibn Muhammad ar-Razzaz: it was narrated to us by Sa'dan ibn Nasr: it was narrated to us by Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from Abu az-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, that he conveyed from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: ""If a person were to look into your house without your permission and you were to strike him with a pebble, thereby destroying his eye, there would be no sin upon you.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ali, and Muslim narrated it from Ibn Abi 'Umar, both of them from Sufyan."
"17662","(17656) It was narrated to us by Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz: it was narrated to us by Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim: it was narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Salamah: it was narrated to us by Ishaq ibn Ibrahim: it was narrated to us by Jarir, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), that he said: ""Whoever looks into the house of a people without their permission, it is permissible for them to gouge out his eye.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Zuhayr ibn Harb, from Jarir."
"17663","(17657) It was narrated to us by Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan: it was narrated to us by Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd as-Saffar: it was narrated to us by Abu Muslim: it was narrated to us by al-Hajjaj: it was narrated to us by Hammad ibn Salamah: it was narrated to us by Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, who said: I was with my father when one of his companions had looked into the house of some people and seen a woman. He mentioned the hadith, then he said: Abu Hurayrah informed us that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever looks into the house of a people without their permission and they gouge out his eye, his eye is thereby rendered of no legal consequence."""
"17664","(17658) And it was narrated to us by Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan: it was narrated to us by Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd: it was narrated to us by Tamtam: it was narrated to us by Sulayman ibn Dawud: it was narrated to us by Mu'adh ibn Hisham: my father informed me, from Qatadah, from an-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nahik, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), that he said: ""Whoever looks upon a people without their permission and they strike him, hitting his eye, there shall be no blood-money and no retribution upon them."""
"17665","(17659) It was narrated to us by Abu al-Qasim 'Abd al-Khaliq ibn 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Khaliq al-Mu'adhdhin: it was narrated to us by Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khanb: it was narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Isma'il as-Sulami: it was narrated to us by Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways informed me: Sulayman ibn Bilal informed me, from 'Abd ar-Rahman ibn Abi 'Atiq, from Nafi', that Ibn 'Umar informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""If a man were to look into the house of another man and gouge out his eye, there would be no liability upon him in that regard."""
"17666","(17660) It was narrated to us by Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr, who both said: it was narrated to us by Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub: it was narrated to us by ar-Rabi' ibn Sulayman: it was narrated to us by Ibn Wahb: it was narrated to us by Sulayman, meaning Ibn Bilal, from Kathir ibn Zayd, from al-Walid ibn Rabbah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When the gaze enters, there is no need for permission."" [Da'if]"
"17667","(17661) It was narrated to us by Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri': it was narrated to us by al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq: it was narrated to us by Yusuf ibn Ya'qub: it was narrated to us by Abu ar-Rabi': it was narrated to us by Jarir, from al-A'mash, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, who said: Sa'd ibn Mu'adh came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and sought permission while facing the door. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) gestured with his hand, saying: ""Like this, O Sa'd, for seeking permission is only on account of the gaze."" [Sahih]"
"17668","(17662) And it was narrated to us by Abu Tahir al-Faqih: it was narrated to us by Abu Hamid ibn Bilal: it was narrated to us by 'Abd ar-Rahman ibn Bishr: it was narrated to us by Sufyan, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, that Sa'd sought permission from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) while facing the door, and he said to him: ""When you seek permission, do not face the door."" Both of these are mursal. [Da'if]"
"17669","(17663) It was narrated to us by Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan: it was narrated to us by 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far: it was narrated to us by Ya'qub ibn Sufyan: it was narrated to us by Adam: it was narrated to us by Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid: it was narrated to us by Muhammad ibn 'Abd ar-Rahman al-Yahsubi, who said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn Busr say:

(And in another chain) It was narrated to us by Abu 'Ali ar-Rudhbari: it was narrated to us by Abu Bakr ibn Dassah: it was narrated to us by Abu Dawud: it was narrated to us by Mu'mil ibn al-Fadl al-Harani among others, who said: it was narrated to us by Baqiyyah: it was narrated to us by Muhammad ibn 'Abd ar-Rahman, from 'Abdullah ibn Busr, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) came to the door of a people, he would walk alongside the wall and would not face the door; rather he would stand to the right or to the left and then seek permission. If permission was granted to him, he would enter; otherwise he would leave. That was because the people did not have curtains over their doors. This is the wording of the hadith of Adam. In the narration of al-Harani it adds: ""He would not face the door directly in front of him, but rather from its right corner or its left corner, and he would say: 'As-salamu 'alaykum.' That was because the houses did not have curtains upon them at that time."" [Da'if]"
"17670","(17664) It was narrated to us by Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz: it was narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Ya'qub, that is ash-Shaybani: it was narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Shadhan: it was narrated to us by Qutaybah ibn Sa'id:

(And in another chain) He said: and it was narrated to us by 'Ali ibn 'Isa: it was narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Abi Talib: it was narrated to us by Ibn Abi 'Umar, who both said: it was narrated to us by Sufyan: Yazid ibn Khusayfah informed me, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: Abu Musa sought permission from 'Umayr (i.e., 'Umar), may Allah be pleased with them both, but permission was not granted to him, so he left. 'Umar said to him: ""Why did you not come to me?"" He said: ""I came and sought permission three times, but permission was not granted to me, so I left. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Whoever seeks permission three times and is not granted permission, let him go back.'"" 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Produce evidence for this, or I shall punish you."" Abu Sa'id said: Abu Musa came to us alarmed, or frightened, and said: ""I have come to call you as witnesses."" Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Sit, for none shall stand with you except the youngest of the people."" Abu Sa'id said: ""I was the youngest among them, so I stood and testified before 'Umar that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Whoever seeks permission three times and is not granted permission, let him go back.'""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ali ibn 'Abdullah, from Sufyan, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah and Ibn Abi 'Umar. [Sahih]"
"17671","(17665) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us: Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us: Ibn Jurayj narrated from ʿAmr ibn Abī Sufyān from ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Ṣafwān from Kaladah ibn al-Ḥanbal: that Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah sent him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with milk, young goats, and cucumbers. He entered without offering salām, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: ""Go back and offer salām."" [Ḥasan]"
"17672","(17666) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Maymūnī narrated to us: Rūḥ narrated to us: Ibn Jurayj informed us: ʿAmr ibn Abī Sufyān informed him that ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Ṣafwān informed him that Kaladah ibn al-Ḥanbal informed him: that Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah sent him during the Conquest (of Makkah) with milk, young goats, and cucumbers, while the Prophet ﷺ was in the valley. He said: ""I entered upon him without offering salām and without seeking permission. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Go back and say: al-Salāmu ʿalaykum, may I enter?'"" — after Ṣafwān had embraced Islam. ʿAmr said: ""Umayyah ibn Ṣafwān also informed me this report, but he did not say: 'I heard it from Kaladah.'"""
"17673","(17667) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated from Manṣūr from Ribʿī: ""A man from Banī ʿĀmir narrated to me: He sought permission to enter upon the Prophet ﷺ while he was in a house, and said: 'Shall I enter?' The Prophet ﷺ said to his servant: 'Go out to this one and teach him how to seek permission. Tell him to say: al-Salāmu ʿalaykum, may I enter?'"" The man heard this and said: ""al-Salāmu ʿalaykum, may I enter?"" The Prophet ﷺ then gave him permission, and he entered. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17674","(17668) Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Hannād ibn al-Sarī narrated from Abū al-Aḥwaṣ from Manṣūr from Ribʿī ibn Ḥirāsh, who said: ""I was told that a man from Banī ʿĀmir sought permission to enter upon the Prophet ﷺ"" — with the same meaning. Abū Dāwūd said: ""Likewise, Musaddad narrated to us: Abū ʿĀwānah narrated from Manṣūr, but he did not mention 'from a man of Banī ʿĀmir.'"""
"17675","(17669) He said: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Manṣūr from Ribʿī ibn Ḥirāsh from a man of Banī ʿĀmir that he sought permission to enter upon the Prophet ﷺ — with the same meaning. He said: ""I heard him say: 'al-Salāmu ʿalaykum, may I enter?'"" And it has been narrated to us from Ibn ʿAbbās from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) that he came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was in his upper room and said: ""al-Salāmu ʿalayka, O Messenger of Allah! al-Salāmu ʿalayka — shall ʿUmar enter?"""
"17676","(17670) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated — (ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: al-Faḍl ibn al-Ḥubāb narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, who said: ""I heard Jābir say: 'I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarding a debt owed by my father. I knocked on the door and he said: ""Who is it?"" I said: ""It is me (anā)."" He said: ""Me, me (anā, anā)"" — twice, as though he disliked it.'""

The wording is from the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAmr. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd, and Muslim narrated it through various chains from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17677","(17671) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿAlī and Tamāmām said: ʿAlī ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us: Ḥammād narrated to us: Ayyūb narrated — (ḥ) And ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us: Abū ʿAlī Ḥāmid ibn Muḥammad al-Harawī narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Ayyūb and Ḥabīb ibn al-Shahīd from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'A man's messenger to another man is his permission (to enter).'""

And Abū al-Khayr al-Muḥammadbādhī informed us: Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadbādhī narrated to us: ʿUthmān al-Dārimī narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Ḥammād narrated from Ḥabīb and Hishām from Muḥammad, and he mentioned it with the same wording. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17678","(17672) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us: Saʿīd narrated from Qatādah from Abū Rāfiʿ from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When one of you is invited and comes with the messenger, that constitutes his permission (to enter)."" (Q) The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""This, in my view — and Allah knows best — applies when there is no ḥurmah (private family space) in the house. If there is a ḥurmah in it, then seeking permission is essential after the revelation of the āyah of Ḥijāb."""
"17679","(17673) Abū ʿAbdillāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Baghdādī informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: ʿUmar ibn Dharr narrated to us: Mujāhid narrated that Abū Hurayrah used to narrate — and he mentioned the ḥadīth of the Aṣḥāb al-Ṣuffah — in which the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Go."" So he went, and I followed him. He entered, and I sought permission, and I was given permission. I entered and found milk in a vessel. He said: ""Where did this milk come from?"" They said: ""So-and-so or so-and-so gifted it to you."" He said: ""Abā Hirr!"" I said: ""Labbayka, O Messenger of Allah!"" He said: ""Go to the Aṣḥāb al-Ṣuffah and invite them for me."" And he mentioned the ḥadīth until he said: ""So I came to them and invited them. They came forward until they sought permission, and they were given permission, and they took their places in the house.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17680","(17674) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Bāshānī al-Muzakkī — who came to us in Bayhaq as a pilgrim — informed us: Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥasnūyah narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Idrīs al-Anṣārī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us: Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd narrated — (ḥ) And Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: al-Manjanīqī Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Khalaf narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr al-Sulamī narrated from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn from ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: ""A house is a ḥaram (sanctuary). So whoever enters upon you in your ḥaram, kill him.""

Abū Aḥmad said: ""Muḥammad ibn Kathīr al-Sulamī al-Baṣrī, narrating from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, is munkar al-ḥadīth (one whose narrations are rejected). I heard Ibn Ḥammād mention this from al-Bukhārī."" The Shaykh said: ""It has also been narrated through another weak chain from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd. And if it is authentic, then what is intended — and Allah knows best — is that he is commanded to leave; if he does not leave, then he may be struck; and if the striking leads to his death, so be it."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17681","(17675) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik ibn Anas narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from Ḥarām ibn Saʿd ibn Muḥayṣa, that a she-camel belonging to al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib entered a garden of a people and caused damage therein. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) ruled that the owners of properties are responsible for guarding them during the day, and whatever livestock destroy at night, their owners are liable for it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17682","(17676) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAwf narrated to us: Abū al-Mughīra narrated to us: al-Awzāʿī narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥarām ibn Muḥayṣa al-Anṣārī, that he informed him: al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib had a vicious she-camel that entered a garden and caused damage therein. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was consulted regarding it, and he ruled that the guarding of gardens during the day is the responsibility of their owners, and the guarding of livestock at night is the responsibility of their owners, and upon the owners of livestock is whatever their livestock destroy at night."
"17683","(17677) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ayyūb ibn Suwayd narrated to us: al-Awzāʿī narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥarām ibn Muḥayṣa, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, that a she-camel belonging to al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib entered the garden of a man from the Anṣār and caused damage therein. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) ruled that the guarding of gardens during the day is the responsibility of their owners, and upon the owners of livestock is whatever their livestock destroy at night."
"17684","(17678) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsa narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Maḥmūd ibn Khālid narrated to us: al-Firyābī narrated from al-Awzāʿī, from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥarām ibn Muḥayṣa al-Anṣārī, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, who said: He had a vicious she-camel, and he mentioned something similar to the ḥadīth of Abū al-Mughīra, except that he narrated it from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, whereas Abū al-Mughīra did not state it directly from him."
"17685","(17679) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: al-Ramādī and others narrated, saying: Muḥammad ibn Muṣʿab narrated to us: al-Awzāʿī narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥarām ibn Muḥayṣa, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, that he had a vicious she-camel that caused damage, and he mentioned it. Thus Ayyūb ibn Suwayd followed him in narrating from al-Awzāʿī the statement ""from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib."""
"17686","(17680) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan, Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr, and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris all informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Muʿāwiya, meaning ibn Hishām, narrated from Sufyān, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĪsā, from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥarām ibn Muḥayṣa, from al-Barāʾ, that a she-camel of the family of al-Barāʾ caused damage to something. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) ruled that the guarding of fruits is the responsibility of their owners during the day, and he held the owners of livestock liable for whatever their livestock destroy at night."
"17687","(17681) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Ḥājib ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: Muʾammal narrated to us: Sufyān narrated with his chain similarly, and he said: from Ḥarām, from al-Barāʾ, that a she-camel belonging to them..."
"17688","(17682) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsa narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Thābit al-Marwazī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥarām ibn Muḥayṣa, from his father, that a she-camel belonging to al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib entered a man's garden and caused damage. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) ruled that the owners of properties are responsible for guarding them during the day, and the owners of livestock are responsible for guarding them at night. A group narrated it from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. (T) However, Wahb and Abū Masʿūd al-Zajjāj contradicted him from Maʿmar, and they did not say ""from his father."""
"17689","(17683) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatāda informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīruwayh informed us: Aḥmad ibn Najda narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib and Ḥarām ibn Saʿd ibn Muḥayṣa, that a she-camel belonging to al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib entered a garden of a people from the Anṣār and caused damage. So they brought their dispute to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and he ruled that the guarding of gardens is the responsibility of their owners during the day, and upon the owners of livestock is whatever the livestock destroy at night.

And it has been narrated to us from al-Shaʿbī, from Sharīḥ, that he would hold liable for what sheep destroyed at night and would not hold liable for what they destroyed during the day, and he interpreted the verse: ""And David and Solomon, when they judged concerning the field, when the sheep of the people had strayed into it"" [al-Anbiyāʾ 78]. He used to say: al-Nafsh (straying) occurs at night.

Abū Naṣr ibn Qatāda informed us: Abū ʿAlī al-Raffāʾ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Azhar narrated to us: Ibn ʿAwn narrated from al-Shaʿbī, from Sharīḥ, regarding the verse: ""when the sheep of the people had strayed into it"" [al-Anbiyāʾ 78]. He said: al-Nafsh was at night."
"17690","(17684) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatāda informed us: Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Najda narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, from al-Shaʿbī, who said: Sharīḥ was brought a sheep that had eaten dough. He asked: ""During the day or at night?"" They said: ""During the day."" So he invalidated the claim and recited: ""when the sheep of the people had strayed into it"" [al-Anbiyāʾ 78], and said: al-Nafsh is only at night. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17691","In the narration of Qatādah from al-Sha'bī: that a sheep was brought before Shurayḥ which had damaged some wool. Al-Sha'bī said: ""Examine the matter, for he will ask them whether it was during the night or during the day."" So he asked them, and he said: ""If it was during the night, then you are liable; and if it was during the day, then there is no liability upon you."" He also said: ""Grazing (unsupervised) is at night, and trampling is during the day."" And he narrated once from Masrūq regarding the verse {when the sheep of the people grazed therein} [al-Anbiyā' 78], saying: ""It was a vineyard, and they entered it at night and left nothing green in it."""
"17692","Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed me, Abū al-Walīd informed me, Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Marwazī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab and Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The wound caused by an animal is void (no liability); a well is void (no liability); a mine is void (no liability); and in the buried treasure (rikāz) there is one-fifth (khums).""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ, and al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17693","Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Shaybān narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd and Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""The animal — its wound is void; the mine is void; the well is void; and in the buried treasure (rikāz) there is one-fifth (khums).""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others from Ibn ʿUyaynah."
"17694","As for the narration that Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Khāliq al-Mu'adhdhin informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Mu'ammal informed us, al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, al-Nufaylī narrated to us, ʿAbbād ibn al-ʿAwwām narrated from Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn, from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: ""The foot (causing injury) is void (no liability)."" Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: As for what has been narrated from the Prophet ﷺ regarding ""the foot is void,"" it is an error — and Allah knows best — because the ḥuffāẓ (master narrators) did not preserve it thus. The Shaykh said: This additional wording is narrated uniquely by Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn from al-Zuhrī, whereas Mālik ibn Anas, al-Layth ibn Saʿd, Ibn Jurayj, Maʿmar, ʿUqayl, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, and others narrated it from al-Zuhrī, and none of them mentioned ""the foot"" in it."
"17695","Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, saying: Abū al-Ḥasan al-Dāraquṭnī al-Ḥāfiẓ said: No one followed up Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn on his statement ""the foot is void,"" and it is an error, because the trustworthy narrators contradicted him and did not mention that. [Ṣaḥīḥ — from al-Dāraquṭnī]"
"17696","Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ashnānī, and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us, saying: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs narrated to us, saying: I heard ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī say: I asked Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn about Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn, and he said: ""He is trustworthy (thiqah), but he is weak (ḍaʿīf) in ḥadīth from al-Zuhrī."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — from Ibn Maʿīn]"
"17697","As for the narration that Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Fārisī narrated to us, Jaʿfar al-Qalānisī narrated to us, Ādam narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Ziyād, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The beast — its wound is void; the foot is void; the well is void; the mine is void; and in the buried treasure (rikāz) there is one-fifth (khums)."" Abū al-Ḥasan al-Dāraquṭnī said: ""This is what he said, and it is an error, and no one followed him in it from Shuʿbah."" The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: This ḥadīth was narrated from Shuʿbah by Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar Ghundar — and he is the authority (al-ḥakam) regarding the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah — and Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh al-ʿAnbarī, Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm, Abū ʿUmar al-Ḥawḍī, and others, without this additional wording. Likewise, al-Rabīʿ ibn Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Ziyād without this additional wording. [Ṣaḥīḥ] — except for ""the foot is void."""
"17698","As for the narration that Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Sayyārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Bāshānī narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Shaqīq narrated to us, Abū Ḥamzah narrated from al-Aʿmash, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Tharwān.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh al-Aṣbahānī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Aḥmad al-Zayyāt narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn ʿAmr narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Abū Qays, from Huzayl ibn Sharḥbīl, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The mine is void; the well is void; the freely grazing animal is void; the foot is void; and in the buried treasure (rikāz) there is one-fifth (khums).""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Thawrī. In the narration of al-Aʿmash it is: ""The animal is void; the well is void; the mine is void; the foot is void; and in the buried treasure (rikāz) there is one-fifth (khums)."" This is a mursal (disconnected) narration, and no proof can be established by it. And Qays ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated it as a connected (mawṣūl) narration with the mention of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd in it. However, Qays is not used as a basis for proof (lā yaḥtājj bihi)."
"17699","Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Zakariyyā narrated to us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us, Abū Naṣr al-Tammār narrated to us, Abū Jazī Naṣr ibn Ṭarīf narrated from al-Sarī ibn Ismāʿīl, from al-Shaʿbī, from Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever leaves an animal loose on one of the roads of the Muslims or in their markets, and it strikes with a hand or a foot, then he is liable (ḍāmin)."" Abū Jazī and al-Sarī ibn Ismāʿīl are both weak (ḍaʿīfān). [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17700","Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar narrated from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""The animal — its wound is void; the mine is void; fire is void; and in the buried treasure (rikāz) there is one-fifth (khums)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17701","(17695) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated this hadith in abridged form regarding al-Nār. Al-Ramādī said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq said: Maʿmar said: ""I consider it to be nothing but an error (wahm)."""
"17702","(17696) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād: Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us: Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: I heard Abū ʿAbd Allāh Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal say regarding the hadith of Abū Hurayrah — the hadith of ʿAbd al-Razzāq in which he narrates: ""Al-Nār is jabbār"" — ""It is nothing; it was not in the written copies. It is baseless and not authentic."""
"17703","(17697) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Makhlad narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us, saying: I heard Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal say: ""The people of Yemen used to write al-Nār as al-Nīr, and they used to write al-Bīr — meaning similarly — and this is a scribal error (taṣḥīf)."" [Authenticated. As per Aḥmad]"
"17704","(17698) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Iyād ibn Laqīṭ narrated to me: Iyād ibn Laqīṭ narrated to me from Abū Rimthah, who said: ""I set out with my father towards the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). My father greeted him, and we sat for a while and spoke. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to my father: 'Is this your son?' He said: 'Yes, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah.' He said: 'Truly?' He said: 'I bear witness to that.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) smiled, laughing at the firmness of my resemblance to my father and at my father's swearing an oath upon that. Then he said: 'Indeed, this son of yours shall not bear your sin, nor shall you bear his.' And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recited: 'No bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another' [al-Najm 38] until His words: 'This is a warner from among the first warners' [al-Najm 56]."" [Authentic]"
"17705","(17699) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Qabīṣah narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us from Ashʿath ibn Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ from al-Aswad ibn Hilāl from Thaʿlabah ibn Zahdam al-Ḥanẓalī, who said: ""A group of us from Banū Taym came to the Prophet (ﷺ). We approached him while he was saying: 'The hand of the giver is the upper hand. Begin with those whom you support: your mother, your father, your sister, your brother, then your next closest, then your next closest.' A man from the Anṣār said: 'O Messenger of Allah, these are Banū Thaʿlabah ibn Yarbuʿ, who killed so-and-so during the Jāhiliyyah.' The Prophet (ﷺ) called out: 'Indeed, no soul shall bear the sin of another.'"" [Authentic in other chains]"
"17706","(17700) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān narrated to us from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from ʿAmr ibn Aws, who said: ""A person used to be held accountable for the sin of another, until Ibrāhīm (peace be upon him) came. Then Allah, the Most High, said: 'And Ibrāhīm, who fulfilled — no bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another' [al-Najm 37–38]."" Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""And what I have heard — and Allah knows best — regarding the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: 'No bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another' [al-Najm 38], is that no one shall be held accountable for the sin of another, because Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, recompenses the servants for the deeds of their own selves, and likewise their wealth — no one bears the burden of another in matters of wealth — except where the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) specifically designated that the blood-money for accidental killing of a free human being falls upon the ʿāqilah (kinship group)."" [Authentic]"
"17707","(17701) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī informed us: Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Shaybān narrated to us from al-Aʿmash from Jāmiʿ ibn Shaddād from Ṣafwān ibn Muḥriz from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, who said: ""I was sitting with the Prophet (ﷺ) when a group from Banū Taym came to him. He said: 'Accept the glad tidings, O Banū Taym!' They said: 'You have given us glad tidings; now give us something, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: Then some people from the people of Yemen came to him, and he said: 'Accept the glad tidings, O people of Yemen, since Banū Taym did not accept it.' They said: 'We have accepted, O Messenger of Allah. We have come to seek understanding in the religion and to ask you about the beginning of this matter — what was it?' He said: 'Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, existed, and nothing existed before Him. His Throne was upon the water. Then He created the heavens and the earth, and He wrote in the Remembrance (al-Dhikr) everything.' He said: 'Then a man came to him and said: 'O ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, your mount — find your she-camel, for it has gone!' So I set out in search of it, and lo, the mirage cut it off from me. By Allah, I wished it had gone and that I had not stood up.'"" [Authentic. Bukhārī 3190, 3192, 4360, 4386]"
"17708","(17702) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us: My father narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated to us: Jāmiʿ ibn Shaddād narrated to us from Ṣafwān ibn Muḥriz, who narrated to him from ʿImrān ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, who said: ""I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)…"" and he mentioned the hadith, and in it he said: They said: ""We have come to ask you about this matter."" He said: ""Allah existed, and nothing existed besides Him. His Throne was upon the water, and He wrote in the Remembrance everything, and He created the heavens and the earth.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth. And what is meant by it — and Allah knows best — is: ""Then He created the water, and He created the Throne upon the water, and He created the Pen and commanded it, so it wrote in the Remembrance everything."" [Authentic. As previously stated]"
"17709","(17703) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Abī Hāshim al-ʿAlawī informed us in al-Kūfah: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-ʿAbsī informed us: Wakīʿ ibn al-Jarrāḥ narrated to us from al-Aʿmash from Abī Ẓabyān from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""Indeed, the first thing that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, created of anything was the Pen. He said: 'Write.' It said: 'O my Lord, what shall I write?' He said: 'Write al-Qadar (the Decree).' He said: 'So it wrote everything that would be from that day until the establishment of the Hour.' He said: 'Then He created the Nūn (the great fish/whale), and He spread the earth upon it. Then the vapor of the water rose, and from it the heavens were split apart. And the Nūn became agitated, and the earth trembled, so it was anchored with the mountains. Indeed, the mountains will continue to take pride over the earth until the Day of Resurrection.'"" [Authentic]"
"17710","(17704) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb al-Rāzī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Jamīl al-Marwazī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us from Rabāḥ ibn Zayd from ʿUmar ibn Ḥabīb from al-Qāsim ibn Abī Bazza from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās, who used to narrate that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, the first thing that Allah — exalted be His praise — created was the Pen, and He commanded it, so it wrote everything that would come to be."" And this was also narrated in a marfūʿ hadith of ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit. [Authentic]"
"17711","(17705) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Faqīh informed us, he said: it was recited to Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zibriqān while I was listening, al-Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad al-Aʿwar informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah informed me from Ayyūb ibn Khālid from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rāfīʿ, the freedman of Umm Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) took hold of my hand and said: ""Allah created the earth on Saturday, and created the mountains therein on Sunday, and created the trees on Monday, and created the detested things on Tuesday, and created the light on Wednesday, and spread the beasts therein on Thursday, and created Ādam after ʿAṣr on Friday — the last of creation — in the last hour of the hours of Friday, between ʿAṣr and nightfall.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Surayj ibn Yūnus and Hārūn ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from al-Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"17712","(17706) Abū Manṣūr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Dāmghānī informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Azhar al-Ṭūsī informed me in Baghdad, Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, Khālid narrated from al-Shaybānī from ʿAwn ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah — I think he said — from his brother ʿUbayd Allāh, who said: Abū Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) say: ""Indeed, on Friday there is an hour in which no servant asks Allah for anything except that He grants it to him.""

He said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām said: ""Indeed, Allah, the Most High, began creation and created the earth on Sunday and Monday, and created the heavens on Tuesday and Wednesday, and created the sustenance and whatever is in the earth on Thursday and Friday, and He finished that at the time of ʿAṣr prayer. That hour is between ʿAṣr and sunset."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"17713","(17707) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Ṣanʿānī informed me in Makkah, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar informed me, ʿAwf informed me from Qāsim ibn Zuhayr from Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said: ""Allah created Ādam from the surface of the entire earth, and his progeny came forth accordingly — among them the white, the black, the brown, and the red, and among them what is between that, and among them the easy-going, the wicked, and the good."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17714","(17708) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār and Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us, they said: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Isḥāq al-Azraq narrated from ʿAwf al-Aʿrābī from Qāsim ibn Zuhayr from Abū Mūsā, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Ādam was created from a handful that was taken from the entirety of the earth, and the children of Ādam came forth in proportion to the earth — among them the red, the black, the easy-going, the harsh, and between that, and among them the wicked and the good."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17715","(17709) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā, Abū al-Azhar, and Ḥamdān al-Sulamī narrated to us, they said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar informed from al-Zuhrī from ʿUrwah from ʿĀʾishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""The angels were created from light, the jinn were created from a smokeless flame of fire, and Ādam was created from that which has been described to you.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]

Imām al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) said: Allah — exalted be His praise — said: {And I did not create the jinn and mankind except to worship Me} [al-Dhāriyāt: 56]. Al-Shāfiʿī said: Allah created creation for His worship — meaning, whomever He willed among His servants — or to command whomever He willed among them with His worship, and He guides whomever He wills to a straight path."
"17716","(17710) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd informed us, my father informed me, he said: I heard al-Awzāʿī — Rabīʿah ibn Yazīd and Yaḥyā ibn Abī ʿAmr al-Saybānī narrated to me, they said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Fayrūz al-Daylamī narrated to me, he said: I entered upon ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and he mentioned the hadith until he said: ʿAbd Allāh said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) say: ""Indeed, Allah created His creation in darkness, then He cast upon them of His light. Whoever was touched by anything of that light on that day was guided, and whoever missed it went astray. That is why I say: 'The pen has dried according to the knowledge of Allah.'""

Al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) said: Then Allah — exalted be His praise — made clear that the best of His creation are His Prophets, for He said: {Mankind was one community, and Allah sent Prophets as bearers of glad tidings and warners} [al-Baqarah: 213]. He made our Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) among His chosen ones above His servants, by entrusting him with His revelation and by his establishing His proof among them. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Daylamī]"
"17717","(17711) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl ibn Idrīs al-Sāmirī narrated to us in Baghdad, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿArafah al-ʿAbdī narrated to me, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Saʿīdī al-Baṣrī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Jurayj narrated from ʿAṭāʾ from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr from Abū Dharr, who said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) while he was in the mosque, and he mentioned the hadith until he said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, how many Prophets are there?"" He said: ""One hundred and twenty-four thousand Prophets."" I said: ""How many Messengers are among them?"" He said: ""Three hundred and thirteen."" Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Saʿīdī is alone in narrating it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17718","(17712) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Shādhān and Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, they said: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, al-Layth narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd from his father from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""There is not a single Prophet among the Prophets except that he was given signs the like of which would cause people to believe in him. And what I have been given is revelation that Allah revealed to me, and I hope that I will have the most followers on the Day of Resurrection.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf and others from al-Layth, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]

Al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) said: Then He mentioned the special distinction of His chosen ones, for He said: {Indeed, Allah chose Ādam and Nūḥ and the family of Ibrāhīm and the family of ʿImrān above all the worlds} [Āl ʿImrān: 33]. Al-Shāfiʿī continued his discourse on it until he said: Then He chose Muḥammad (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) from the best of the family of Ibrāhīm, and He sent down His books before sending down the Furqān upon Muḥammad (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), describing his excellence and the excellence of those who follow him, for He said: {Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah; and those with him are harsh against the disbelievers, merciful among themselves. You see them bowing and prostrating, seeking bounty from Allah and His pleasure. Their mark is on their faces from the trace of prostration. That is their description in the Torah, and their description in the Gospel: like a plant that sends forth its shoot and strengthens it} [al-Fatḥ: 29]."
"17719","(17713) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān al-Murādī and Saʿīd ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us, they said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated from al-Awzāʿī, Abū ʿAmmār narrated to me from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Farrūkh from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""I am the master of the children of Ādam on the Day of Resurrection, and the first for whom the earth will split open, and the first intercessor, and the first whose intercession will be accepted.""

Muslim extracted it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of al-Awzāʿī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 2278]"
"17720","(17714) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī, Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Burhān, Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān, and others informed us, they said: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿArafah narrated to us, al-Qāsim ibn Mālik al-Muzanī narrated from al-Mukhtār ibn Fulful from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""I am the first intercessor on the Day of Resurrection, and I am the one with the most followers among the Prophets on the Day of Resurrection. Indeed, among the Prophets there are those who will come on the Day of Resurrection with no one to confirm their truth except a single person.""

Muslim extracted it through various chains from al-Mukhtār. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 196]"
"17721","(17715) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Hushaym informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Hushaym informed us: Sayyār narrated to us: Yazīd al-Faqīr informed us: Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""I have been given five things which no one before me was given: I was aided with terror (cast into the hearts of enemies) at a distance of a month's journey; the spoils of war were made lawful for me, though they were not lawful for anyone before me; the earth has been made for me a place of prayer and a means of purification — so whenever the time of prayer comes upon anyone of my Ummah, let him pray wherever he is; and I was given the right of intercession; and every prophet was sent only to his own people specifically, whereas I have been sent to all of mankind generally."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū al-Rabīʿ. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Sinān from Hushaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
"17722","(17716) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Zakariyyā al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us: Isḥāq informed us: Jarīr narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Khaythamah, who said: A man recited Sūrat al-Fatḥ before ʿAbdillāh (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), and when he reached the verse: {"
"17723","(17717) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Bahz ibn Ḥakīm ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Qushayrī narrated from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) say: ""You will be made to complete seventy nations, and you are the best of them and the most honoured in the sight of Allah, Mighty and Majestic."" [Ḥasan]

Al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) said: Then He, Exalted is His praise, informed that He made him the opener of His mercy after a cessation of His messengers, for He said: {"
"17724","(17718) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""I have been favoured over the prophets with six things: I was given comprehensive speech (jawāmiʿ al-kalām); I was aided with terror (cast into the hearts of enemies); the spoils of war were made lawful for me; the earth was made for me a means of purification and a place of prayer; I was sent to all of creation generally; and through me the prophets were sealed."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb and others from Ismāʿīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 523]"
"17725","(17719) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī (raḥimahullāh) informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī narrated to us: ʿAffān ibn Muslim narrated to us: Sulaym ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us. He said: I heard Saʿīd ibn Mīnāʾ say: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) say: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said — (Ḥ) and Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us: Sulaym ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us. He said: I heard Saʿīd ibn Mīnāʾ say: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) say: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""My example and the example of the prophets before me is like that of a man who built a house."" And Yazīd said: ""He built a house and beautified it and completed it, except for the place of one brick. So the people began entering it and admiring it and saying: 'If only the place of this brick were filled!'"" The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""I am the place of that brick; I came and sealed the prophets."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Sinān from Sulaym, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and Abū Kurayb from ʿAffān.

Al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) said: And He decreed that He would make His religion prevail over all religions, for He said: {"
"17726","(17720) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥām al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid narrated from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim, from Khabbāb (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: We complained to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) — while he was reclining on his cloak in the shade of the Kaʿbah — and we said: ""Will you not supplicate to Allah for us? Will you not seek Allah's help for us?"" He sat up, his face reddened, and he said: ""By Allah, those before you used to take a man and dig a pit for him, then place a saw upon his head and split him in two — and that would not turn him away from his religion. Or he would be combed with iron combs between his flesh and his sinews — and that would not turn him away from his religion. And Allah will surely bring this matter to completion until a rider among you will travel from Ṣanʿāʾ to Ḥaḍramawt, fearing none but Allah — or the wolf concerning his sheep — but you are hasty.""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥs from the ḥadīth of Ismāʿīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"17727","(17721) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAmr al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū al-Ṭāhir Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Yūnus informed us from Ibn Shihāb. He said: ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhā), the wife of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), informed him. She said: ""The first thing that began to come to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) of revelation was true dreams in sleep. He never saw a vision except that it came like the breaking of dawn. Then seclusion became beloved to him, and he would retreat to the Cave of Ḥirāʾ, where he would devote himself to worship — that is, worship through prayer — for several nights before returning to his family, and he would take provisions for that. Then he would return to Khadījah, and she would provide him with similar provisions, until the Truth came to him while he was in the Cave of Ḥirāʾ. The angel came to him and said: 'Read!' He said: 'I am not one who reads.' He said: 'So he seized me and pressed me until I reached the limit of endurance, then he released me and said: ""Read!"" I said: ""I am not one who reads."" So he seized me and pressed me a second time until I reached the limit of endurance, then he released me and said: ""Read!"" I said: ""I am not one who reads."" So he seized me and pressed me a third time until I reached the limit of endurance, then he released me and said: {"
"17728","(17722) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us: ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Mukram narrated to us: ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb. He said: I heard Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān say: Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) informed me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) say: ""Revelation ceased from me for a period. While I was walking, I heard a voice from the sky. I raised my gaze toward the sky, and there was the angel who had come to me at Ḥirāʾ, seated upon a chair between the sky and the earth. I was overcome with terror on account of him, until I fell to the ground. I came to my family and said to them: 'Cover me! Cover me!' So they covered me. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {"
"17729","(17723) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū Sahl Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAqīl — al-Khusrujardī — narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us: My father informed me, from my grandfather, who informed me: ʿUqayl ibn Khālid informed me from Ibn Shihāb. He said: I heard Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān say: Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) informed me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) say: ""Then revelation ceased from me for a period."" Then he mentioned the ḥadīth with the same meaning as the one above. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"17730","(17724) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī narrated to us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us by dictation: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhā), who said: ""The first thing that was revealed of the Qurʾān was: {"
"17731","(17725) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Abū Kurayb narrated to us; Abū Usāmah narrated from al-Aʿmash, from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbāy (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said:

When this verse was revealed: **""And warn your closest kinsfolk""** (al-Shuʿarāʾ 214), along with the instruction regarding his devoted group among them, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) went out until he ascended al-Ṣafā and cried out: **""Yā ṣabāḥāh!""** (a cry of alarm). The people said: ""Who is this one calling out?"" They said: ""Muḥammad."" He said: ""So they gathered around him, and he said: 'O Banū so-and-so! O Banū so-and-so! O Banū ʿAbd Manāf! O Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib! What would you say if I told you that cavalry are emerging from the base of this mountain — would you believe me?' They said: 'We have never known you to lie.' He said: 'Then indeed I am a warner to you of a severe punishment to come.'""

Abū Lahab then said: ""May you perish! Is it only for this that you gathered us?"" Then he stood up, and this verse was revealed: **""May the hands of Abū Lahab perish, and may he perish""** (al-Masad 1). Al-Aʿmash recited it this way to the end of the sūrah.

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yūsuf ibn Mūsā from Abū Usāmah, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Kurayb. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]

---"
"17732","(17726) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, who said: ""One who heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Nawfal narrated to me, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

When this verse was revealed upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): **""And warn your closest kinsfolk, and lower your wing to those who follow you among the believers""** (al-Shuʿarāʾ 214–215), the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'I knew that if I were to begin with my people regarding it, I would see from them that which I detest.' So he remained silent concerning it. Then Jibrīl (peace be upon him) came to him and said: 'O Muḥammad, if you do not do what your Lord has commanded you, your Lord will punish you.' Then he mentioned the story of their gathering and his warning them."" [Ḍaʿīf]

---"
"17733","(17727) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ ibn al-Ḥammāmī informed us in Baghdād; Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Nijād narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī narrated to me; Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAlqamah narrated from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Rabiʿah ibn ʿAbād al-Duʾalī, who said:

""I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) at Dhī al-Majāz, following the people to their dwellings, calling them to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. Behind him was a man who was saying: 'O people, let not this man deceive you away from your religion and the religion of your forefathers!' I said: 'Who is this?' They said: 'His uncle, Abū Lahab.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other chains]

---"
"17734","(17728) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbāb ibn al-Walīd informed us; my father informed me; I heard al-Awzāʿī say: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Taymī narrated to me; ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, who said: ""I asked ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ. I said: 'Tell me of the severest thing the polytheists did to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family).' He said:

""ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ came while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was praying near the Kaʿbah. He wrapped his garment around his neck and choked him severely. Then Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) came, took him by his shoulders, and pushed him away from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), saying: **'Would you kill a man for saying ""my Lord is Allah,"" when he has come to you with clear proofs from your Lord?'**""

Al-Bukhārī extracted it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Awzāʿī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by al-Bukhārī.]

---"
"17735","(17729) Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ al-Qāḍī informed us in al-Kūfah; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Gharazah informed us; ʿUbayd Allāh, i.e. ibn Mūsā, informed us; Isrāʾīl narrated from Abī Isḥāq, from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn, from ʿAbd Allāh, i.e. ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

""While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was standing in prayer near the Kaʿbah, and the gathering of Quraysh was in their assemblies watching, one of them said: 'Do you not look at this ostentatious one? Which of you will go to the camel of the family of so-and-so, collect its dung, blood, and intestines, and bring it — then wait until he prostrates and place it between his shoulders?' The most wretched among them got up and brought it. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prostrated, he placed it between his shoulders. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) remained in prostration, and they laughed until some of them leaned over one another from laughter. Someone went to Fāṭimah (may Allah be pleased with her) — she was a young girl at the time — and she came running. She cast it off from him and began to curse them. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) finished the prayer, he said: 'O Allah, seize Quraysh' — three times. Then he named them: 'O Allah, seize ʿAmr ibn Hishām, ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah, Shaybah ibn Rabīʿah, al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah, Umayyah ibn Khalaf, ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ, and ʿUmarāh ibn al-Walīd.'""

ʿAbd Allāh said: ""By Allah, I saw them struck down on the day of Badr, being dragged to the well of Badr."" Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""And the companions of the well were followed by a curse.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā, and he and Muslim extracted it through another chain from Abī Isḥāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]

---"
"17736","(17730) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us by dictation; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hilālī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us, saying: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; al-Ḥārith ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Saʿīd al-Jurayrī narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shiqīq, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said:

""The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) used to be guarded until this verse was revealed: **'O Messenger, convey what has been revealed to you from your Lord, and if you do not, then you have not conveyed His message. And Allah will protect you from the people'** (al-Māʾidah 67). The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) then put his head out from the tent and said: 'O people, go your way, for Allah has protected me.'""

In the narration of al-Hilālī, he said to them: ""O people."" [Ḍaʿīf]

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""The meaning of 'Allah will protect you' is that He will protect you from their killing you, until you convey what has been revealed to you. So he conveyed what he was commanded to, and a people mocked him, whereupon this verse was revealed: **'Proclaim what you are commanded and turn away from the polytheists. Indeed, We are sufficient for you against the mockers'** (al-Ḥijr 94–95).""

---"
"17737","(17731) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Razīn narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Iyās, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), regarding the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: **""Indeed, We are sufficient for you against the mockers""** (al-Ḥijr 95). He said:

""The mockers were: al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah, al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth al-Zuhrī, al-Aswad ibn al-Muṭṭalib Abū Zamʿah from Banī Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAṭal al-Sahmī, and al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil.""

Jibrīl (peace be upon him) came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) when he complained about them. The Prophet showed him al-Walīd, Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Mughīrah, and Jibrīl gestured toward his tendon (in the hand). The Prophet said: ""What did you do?"" He said: ""You have been spared from him."" Then the Prophet showed him al-Aswad ibn al-Muṭṭalib, and Jibrīl gestured toward his eyes. The Prophet said: ""What did you do?"" He said: ""You have been spared from him."" Then the Prophet showed him al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth al-Zuhrī, and Jibrīl gestured toward his head. The Prophet said: ""What did you do?"" He said: ""You have been spared from him."" Then the Prophet showed him al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAṭal al-Sahmī, and Jibrīl gestured toward his head. The Prophet said: ""What did you do?"" He said: ""You have been spared from him."" Then al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil passed by, and Jibrīl gestured toward his heel. The Prophet said: ""What did you do?"" He said: ""You have been spared from him.""

As for al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah, he passed by a man from Khuzāʿah who was fletching his arrows. An arrow struck his tendon and severed it. As for al-Aswad ibn al-Muṭṭalib, he went blind. Some say he went blind in this manner; others say he sat beneath a samurah tree and began to say: ""O my sons, will you not defend me? I have been killed!"" They said: ""We see nothing."" He kept saying: ""O my sons, will you not protect me? I am being destroyed! Here I am — I am being pricked with thorns in my eyes!"" They said: ""We see nothing."" He continued in this state until both his eyes went blind.

As for al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth al-Zuhrī, sores appeared on his head and he died from them. As for al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAṭal, yellow water accumulated in his belly until his excrement came out of his mouth, and he died from it. As for al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil, a thorn entered his head and it filled with them, and he died from it. Others say he rode a donkey to al-Ṭāʾif, and it knelt upon a thorny plant. A thorn entered the hollow of his foot and killed him.

---"
"17738","(17732) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Hārūn ibn Sulaymān al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from ʿImrān Abī al-Ḥakam al-Sulamī, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said:

""Quraysh said to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): 'Call upon your Lord to make al-Ṣafā gold for us, and we will believe in you.' He said: 'Will you really do so?' They said: 'Yes.' So he supplicated, and Jibrīl (peace be upon him) came to him and said: 'Allah sends you peace and says: If you wish, al-Ṣafā will become gold, and whoever disbelieves after that I will punish with a punishment I will not punish anyone among the worlds with. Or if you wish, I will open for them the door of repentance and mercy.' He said: 'Rather, the door of repentance and mercy.'"" [Ḥasan]

---"
"17739","(17733) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from ʿĪsā ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Tamīmī, from al-Rabīʿ ibn Anas, from Abī al-ʿĀliyah, regarding the verse: **""Then be patient, as were those of determination among the messengers""** (al-Aḥqāf 35). He said:

""Those of determination were Nūḥ, Hūd, and Ibrāhīm. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was commanded to be patient as these three were patient. They were three, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was the fourth of them.

Nūḥ said: **'If my staying among you and my reminding you of the signs of Allah has become burdensome upon you, then upon Allah I rely. So resolve your affair and your partners, then let not your affair be a confusion for you. Then carry out your judgment upon me and do not delay me'** (Yūnus 71). Thus he made clear to them the point of separation.

And Hūd, when they said: **'We only say that some of our gods have afflicted you with evil'** (Hūd 54), likewise made clear to them the point of separation.

And Ibrāhīm said: **'There has been an excellent example in Ibrāhīm'** (al-Mumtaḥanah 4) to the end of the verse, thus making clear to them the point of separation.

And Muḥammad said: **'I have been forbidden to worship those whom you call upon besides Allah'** (al-Anʿām 56). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) stood near the Kaʿbah, reciting it to the polytheists, and thus made clear to them the point of separation."" [Ḍaʿīf]

---"
"17740","(17734) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq; al-Zuhrī narrated to me from Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām, from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said:

""When Makkah became constricted for us, and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) were harmed and put to trial, and they saw the affliction and tribulation befalling them in their religion — and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was unable to ward it off from them, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was under the protection of his people and his uncle, and nothing of what he detested could reach his Companions — the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to them: 'In the land of Abyssinia there is a king under whom no one is wronged. So go to his country until Allah grants you relief and a way out of what you are in.'

So we departed for it in groups until we gathered there. We settled in the best of homes, with the best of neighbors. We felt secure in our religion and feared no injustice from him.""

Then she mentioned the ḥadīth at length. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17741","(17735) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Asfaṭī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us, from Abū al-Zubayr Muslim ibn Muslim, that he narrated to him that Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allāh be pleased with him) narrated to him: that the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) remained for ten years, following the pilgrims to their encampments during the seasons at Majanna and ʿUkāẓ, and to their dwellings at Minā, saying: ""Who will shelter me and support me so that I may convey the messages of my Lord, and for him is Paradise?"" But he found no one who would shelter him or support him, to the point that when a man arrived from the territory of Muḍar or Yemen, his own people or his kinsmen would say to him: ""Beware of the young man of Quraysh! Let him not harm you as he walks among their dwellings, calling them to Allāh,"" pointing at him with their fingers. Until Allāh sent us from Yathrib, and a man from among us would come to him, believe in him, and he would recite the Qurʾān to him, whereupon he would return to his family and they would accept Islam because of his Islam, until there remained no household in Yathrib except that among them was a group of Muslims openly professing Islam. Then Allāh sent us, and we consulted one another and gathered — seventy men from among us — and we said: ""How long shall the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) be driven away in the mountains of Makkah, and be in fear"" — or he said, ""and be feared""?

So we set out until we came to him during the season, and he appointed for us the pass of ʿAqabah. We gathered there, coming in ones and twos, until we assembled before him in full number, and we said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, upon what shall we pledge allegiance to you?"" He said: ""You pledge allegiance to me upon hearing and obedience in times of ease and hardship; upon spending in times of difficulty and ease; upon enjoining good and forbidding evil; and upon speaking for Allāh without fearing, for the sake of Allāh, the reproach of any reproacher; and upon supporting me if I come to you in Yathrib, and protecting me from that from which you protect yourselves, your wives, and your sons — and for you is Paradise.""

We said: ""We pledge allegiance to you."" Then Asʿad ibn Zurārah — who was the youngest of the seventy men, apart from me — took his hand and said: ""Slowly, O people of Yathrib! We have not driven the livers of our mounts toward him except while knowing that he is the Messenger of Allāh, and that his being taken forth today means the departure from all the Arabs, the slaying of the best among you, and that the swords will bite you. So if you are a people who will endure the biting of swords, the slaying of your best, and the departure from all the Arabs, then take him, and your reward is with Allāh. But if you fear for yourselves a fear, then leave him, for that is more excusable for you before Allāh.""

They said: ""Withdraw your hand from us, O Asʿad ibn Zurārah! By Allāh, we shall not abandon this pledge of allegiance nor shall we rescind it."" So we rose to him one by one, and he took from us the covenant and promised us Paradise in return. [Ḥasan. Ḥadīth of Ḥākim.]"
"17742","(17736) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Jarīr narrated to us, from Qābūs ibn Abī Ẓabyān, from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) was in Makkah, and he was commanded with migration, and the following was revealed upon him: {And say: ""My Lord, cause me to enter a truthful entrance and cause me to exit a truthful exit, and grant me from Yourself a supporting authority.""} [al-Isrāʾ, 80]. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
"17743","(17737) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: al-Ḥajjāj ibn Abī Manīʿ informed us: my grandfather narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allāh be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said to the Muslims while he was in Makkah: ""I have been shown the place of your migration. I have been shown a tract of land with date palms, situated between two lava fields, and they are the two Ḥarras."" So whoever migrated, migrated toward Madīnah when the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) mentioned that. And some of the Muslims who had migrated to the land of Abyssinia returned to Madīnah. And Abū Bakr (may Allāh be pleased with him) prepared to migrate, so the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said to him: ""Take your time, for I hope that permission will be given to me."" Abū Bakr (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""And do you hope for that? May my father and mother be your ransom!"" He said: ""Yes."" So Abū Bakr (may Allāh be pleased with him) held himself back to accompany the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), and he fed two she-camels of his with the leaves of the samur tree for four months. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ in full from the ḥadīth of ʿUqayl and Yūnus, from al-Zuḥrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2298.]"
"17744","(17738) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: Abū al-Walīd Hishām ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Bāhilī and Abū ʿUmar Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar al-Namarī both said: Shuʿbah narrated to us. He said: Abū Isḥāq informed us. He said: I heard al-Barāʾ (may Allāh be pleased with him) say: The first of the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) to come to us were Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr and Ibn Umm Maktūm, and they both used to recite the Qurʾān. Then ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, Bilāl, and Saʿd came. Then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allāh be pleased with him) came — meaning, the twentieth of the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). Then the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) came, and I never saw the people of Madīnah rejoice over anything as they rejoiced over him, to the point that I saw the young girls and the boys running in the streets, saying: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) has come! The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) has come!"" He said: He had not arrived in Madīnah before I had learned {Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High} [al-Aʿlā, 1] and similar sūrahs from the Mufaṣṣal. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Walīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī.]"
"17745","(17739) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Abū al-Yamān al-Ḥakam ibn Nāfiʿ informed us: Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah informed us, from al-Zuḥrī. ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me that Usāmah ibn Zayd (may Allāh be pleased with him) informed him: that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) rode a donkey upon which was a saddle blanket, over which was a velvet cloth of Fadk, and he seated Usāmah ibn Zayd behind him, going to visit Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah among Banī al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, before the battle of Badr. He journeyed until he passed by a gathering in which was ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl — and that was before ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy had accepted Islam. In the gathering were men from among the Muslims, the polytheists who worshipped idols, and the Jews. Among the Muslims was ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah. When the dust of the animal enveloped the gathering, Ibn Ubayy covered his nose with his cloak and said: ""Do not raise dust upon us."" The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) greeted them with salām, then stopped, dismounted, and called them to Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, and recited the Qurʾān to them. ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl said: ""O man, there is nothing better than what you say, if it is true. So do not harm us with it in your gathering. Return to your mount, and whoever comes to you, narrate it to him."" ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah said: ""On the contrary, O Messenger of Allāh! Visit us in our gatherings, for we love that."" Then the Muslims, the polytheists, and the Jews reviled one another until they nearly came to blows. The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) continued to calm them until they fell silent.

Then the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) mounted his animal and journeyed until he entered upon Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah (may Allāh be pleased with him). The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said to him: ""O Saʿd, have you not heard what Abū Ḥubāb said?"" — meaning ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy — ""He said such and such."" Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, pardon him and overlook. By the One Who sent down the Book, Allāh has come with the truth that He revealed to you. The people of this settlement had agreed to crown him and place a turban upon his head, but when Allāh repelled that through the truth that He gave you, he choked upon it, and that is what caused him to do what you saw."" So the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) pardoned him. He and his companions used to pardon the polytheists and the People of the Book, as Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, commanded them, and they bore the harm. Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, said: {And you will certainly hear from those who were given the Scripture before you and from those who associate partners with Allāh much abuse. But if you are patient and fear Allāh, indeed that is of the matters of great resolve.} [Āl ʿImrān, 186] And Allāh said: {Many of the People of the Book wish they could turn you back to disbelief after you have believed, out of envy from themselves, after the truth has become clear to them. So pardon and overlook until Allāh brings His command. Indeed, Allāh is over all things competent.} [al-Baqarah, 109] The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) used to act upon pardon in accordance with what Allāh commanded him, until permission was given to him regarding them. When the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) fought at Badr, and Allāh caused the slaying of the chiefs of the disbelievers of Quraysh, ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl and those with him from among the idol worshippers said: ""This is a matter that has gained the upper hand."" So they pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) upon Islam. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Yamān, and he also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Maʿmar and ʿUqayl, from al-Zuḥrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh.]"
"17746","(17740) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Sinān al-Qazzāz narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf al-Azraq narrated to us: Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash, from Muslim al-Baṭīn, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with them both), who said: The people of Makkah expelled the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""Indeed, to Allāh we belong, and to Him we shall return. They have expelled their Prophet — they will surely perish.""

He said: Then the following was revealed: {Permission to fight has been given to those who are fought against, because they have been wronged, and indeed Allāh is capable of granting them victory.} [al-Ḥajj, 39] Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with him) used to recite it: {ʾudhunun} [al-Tawbah, 61]. Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""Then I knew that it meant fighting."" Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""And it was the first āyah to be revealed regarding fighting."" [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
"17747","(17741) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Qāsim ibn al-Qāsim al-Sayyārī informed us in Marw: Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn Ḥātim al-Bāshānī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Shaqīq narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Wāqid narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās: that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf and some of his companions (may Allāh be pleased with them) came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and said: ""O Prophet of Allāh, we were in a state of honor when we were polytheists, but when we believed, we became humiliated."" He said: ""Indeed, I have been commanded to pardon, so do not fight the people."" When Allāh transferred him to Madīnah, He commanded him to fight, so they held back. Then Allāh revealed: {Have you not considered those to whom it was said: 'Restrain your hands and establish prayer and give zakāh'? But when fighting was prescribed for them, at once a group of them feared people.} [al-Nisāʾ, 77]. [Ḥasan.]"
"17748","(17742) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣāliḥ informed us, from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with them both), regarding His statement: {So kill the polytheists wherever you find them} [al-Tawbah, 5] and His statement: {Fight those who do not believe in Allāh nor in the Last Day} [al-Tawbah, 29]. He said: ""This abrogated the pardon regarding the polytheists, and His statement: 'O Prophet, strive against the disbelievers and the hypocrites, and be harsh upon them' [al-Tawbah, 73] — so Allāh commanded him to strive against the disbelievers with the sword and against the hypocrites with the tongue, and He removed gentleness from them."" [Ḍaʿīf.]"
"17749","(17743) Through this chain of narration, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with them both), who said: His statement: {And turn away from the polytheists} [al-Anʿām, 106] and {You are not over them a controller} [al-Ghāshiyah, 22] — he said: ""You are not over them a tyrant"" — and {So pardon them and overlook} [al-Māʾidah, 13] and {And if you pardon and overlook} [al-Taghābun, 14] and {So pardon and overlook until Allāh brings His command} [al-Baqarah, 109] and {Say to those who believe to forgive those who do not hope for the days of Allāh} [al-Jāthiyah, 14] and the like of this in the Qurʾān — Allāh commanded pardon regarding the polytheists, and He abrogated all of that with His statement: {Kill the polytheists wherever you find them} [al-Tawbah, 5] and His statement: {Fight those who do not believe in Allāh nor in the Last Day} [al-Tawbah, 29] up to His statement: {while they are humbled} [al-Tawbah, 29]. So this abrogated the pardon regarding the polytheists. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
"17750","(17744) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated to us, from Abī Isḥāq al-Fazārī, from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAṭāʾ, from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with them both), who said: Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, said: {So if they turn away, then seize them and kill them wherever you find them, and do not take from among them any ally or helper — except for those who join a people between whom and you there is a treaty} [al-Nisāʾ, 89–90]. And He said: {Allāh does not forbid you regarding those who did not fight you because of your religion and did not expel you from your homes} [al-Mumtaḥanah, 8]. Then these āyāt were abrogated, and Allāh revealed: {A declaration of disassociation from Allāh and His Messenger to those polytheists with whom you had made a treaty} [al-Tawbah, 1] up to His statement: {Then when the sacred months have passed, kill the polytheists wherever you find them} [al-Tawbah, 5]. And He revealed: {Fight the polytheists entirely, as they fight you entirely} [al-Tawbah, 36]. He said: {And if they incline toward peace, then incline toward it} [al-Anfāl, 61]. Then this āyah was abrogated by this āyah: {Fight those who do not believe in Allāh nor in the Last Day, nor do what Allāh and His Messenger have forbidden} [al-Tawbah, 29]. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
"17751","(17745) Abu al-Qasim 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad al-'Attar informed us in Baghdad — Abu 'Amr 'Uthman ibn Ahmad al-Daqqaq informed us — 'Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad al-Raqashi informed us — my father informed us — al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman informed us, he said: I heard my father narrating from al-Hadrami, from Abu al-Sawwar, from Jundub ibn 'Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, dispatched a contingent and appointed 'Ubaydah ibn al-Harith as their commander. When he set out to depart, he wept out of longing for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent in his place a man named 'Abd Allah ibn Jahsh and wrote him a letter, commanding him not to read it except at such and such a place, and said: ""Do not compel any of your companions to march with you.""

When he reached that place, he read the letter and recited: ""We hear and we obey, for the sake of Allah and His Messenger."" Two of his companions turned back, and the rest went on with him. They encountered Ibn al-Hadrami and killed him, though it was not known whether this occurred in Rajab or in Jumada al-Akhirah. The polytheists said: ""You have killed during the sacred month."" So Allah revealed: *""They ask you concerning the sacred month — fighting therein. Say: Fighting therein is a grave sin""* [al-Baqarah 217] — until His words: *""And fitnah is graver than killing""* [al-Baqarah 217]. Some of the Muslims said: ""If they have done good, they shall have no reward."" So Allah revealed: *""Indeed, those who believed and those who emigrated and strove in the cause of Allah — these are the ones who hope for the mercy of Allah. And Allah is Forgiving, Merciful""* [al-Baqarah 218]. [Hasan]"
"17752","(17746) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us — Abu Muhammad al-Muzani informed us — 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Isa informed us — Abu al-Yaman informed me — Shu'ayb informed me, from al-Zuhri, who informed me that 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, dispatched a raiding party of the Muslims and placed 'Abd Allah ibn Jahsh al-Asadi in command. They proceeded until they descended upon Nakhlah, where they found 'Amr ibn al-Hadrami in a caravan of merchandise belonging to Quraysh. He mentioned the hadith concerning the killing of Ibn al-Hadrami and the revelation of the words: *""They ask you concerning the sacred month""* [al-Baqarah 217]. He said: ""It has reached us that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, paid the blood-wit of Ibn al-Hadrami and sanctified the sacred month as it had been sanctified, until Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, revealed: *""A declaration of immunity from Allah and His Messenger""* [al-Tawbah 1].""

The Sheikh, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: ""It is as though he intended the words of Allah, the Almighty and Majestic: *""And fight the polytheists all together""* [al-Tawbah 36], and the verse mentioned by al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy upon him, is more general regarding abrogation. And Allah knows best."" [Da'if]"
"17753","(17747) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Bahr ibn Nasr informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed me — Makhrah ibn Bukayr informed me, from his father, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, who was asked: ""Is it permissible for the Muslims to fight the disbelievers during the sacred month?"" Sa'id said: ""Yes."" And Sulayman ibn Yasar said the same. [Sahih]"
"17754","(17748) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan informed us — they both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us — Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr informed us, from Abu Ishaq, who said: I asked Sufyan about the words of Allah: *""They ask you concerning the sacred month — fighting therein. Say: Fighting therein is a grave sin""* [al-Baqarah 217]. He said: ""This is something that has been abrogated and has passed. There is no harm in fighting during the sacred month or otherwise."" [Sahih]"
"17755","(17749) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Shafi'i informed us — Muhammad ibn Maslamah al-Wasiti informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Mu'qri informed us — Haywah and another man informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal al-Asadi informed us, who said: A military expedition was imposed upon the people of Madinah, and my name was written therein. I met 'Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, and he emphatically forbade me. Then he said: ""Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed me that there were certain Muslims who had joined the ranks of the polytheists, increasing their numbers against the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. An arrow would be shot and strike one of them, killing him, or he would be struck and killed. So Allah, the Most High, mentioned them: *'Indeed, those whom the angels take in death while they are wronging themselves, they will say: In what [condition] were you? They will say: We were oppressed in the land. They will you: Was not the earth of Allah spacious enough for you to emigrate therein? For those, their refuge is Hell — and evil is the destination'* [al-Nisa 97]."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Mu'qri. [Sahih — al-Bukhari]"
"17756","(17750) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us — Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah, Abu Muslim, informed us — Hajjaj informed us — Hammad informed us, from al-Hajjaj, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Jarir ibn 'Abd Allah al-Bajali, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Whoever resides with the polytheists, his protection is dissolved."" [Da'if]"
"17757","(17751) Abu al-Hasan al-Mu'qri informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub informed us — Abu al-Rabi' informed us — Jarir informed us, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Abu Nukhaylah, from Jarir ibn 'Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I came to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, while he was accepting the people's pledge. I said: ""O Prophet of Allah, extend your hand so that I may pledge allegiance to you, and impose conditions upon me, for you know better what conditions are fitting for me than I do."" He said: ""I accept your pledge upon that you worship Allah, establish the prayer, give the zakah, advise the believer, and separate from the polytheist."" [Sahih]"
"17758","(17752) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us — they both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar informed us — Yunus ibn Bukayr informed us — Qurrah ibn Khalid informed us — Yazid ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir informed us, who said: ""While we were at this enclosure, there came to us a Bedouin, disheveled of head, with a piece of leather or a piece of skin. We said: 'It seems this man is not from the people of this town.' He said: 'Indeed, no. I have a letter that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, wrote for me.' The people said: 'Bring it forth!' So I took it and read it, and therein was: 'In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. This is a letter from Muhammad, the Prophet, the Messenger of Allah, to Banu Zuhayr ibn Uqaysh' — and Abu al-'Ala' said: 'They are a tribe of 'Ukl: ""If you bear witness that there is no deity but Allah, establish the prayer, give the zakah, separate from the polytheists, and give one-fifth of the war spoils, and the share of the Prophet and his chosen portion"" — and he might have said: ""and his chosen portion"" — ""then you are secure under the protection of Allah and the protection of His Messenger.""'"" [Da'if]"
"17759","(17753) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us — Abu al-Hasan al-Tara'ifi informed us — 'Uthman ibn Sa'id informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Salih informed us — Mu'awiyah ibn Salih informed us — 'Ali ibn Abi Talhah informed us, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: ""Every occurrence of 'asā (perhaps) in the Qur'an denotes an obligation (wājib)."" [Da'if]"
"17760","(17754) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdus informed us — 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi informed us — Sulayman ibn Harb informed us — Hammad ibn Zayd informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, that Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, recited this verse: *""Except the oppressed among men, women, and children""* [al-Nisa 98], and said: ""I and my mother were among those for whom Allah granted excuse."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Sulayman ibn Harb. [Sahih — al-Bukhari 1357]"
"17761","(17755) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bashran informed us in Baghdad: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us: Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, who said: I heard Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) saying: I and my mother were among the musta'afin (the weak and oppressed). My mother was among the women, and I was among the children. [Narrated by al-Bukhari from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad from Sufyan.]

[Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"17762","(17756) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Ishaq: Nafi' narrated to me from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, from his father 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When we gathered for the Hijrah, 'Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah, Hisham ibn al-'As ibn Wa'il, and I agreed upon a rendezvous at Tan'ud of Banu Ghifar. Whoever among you did not arrive there in the morning would be considered detained, and his two companions should proceed. So 'Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah and I arrived at the appointed place, while Hisham was detained from us. He was subjected to tribulations and succumbed to them. When we arrived in Madinah, we used to say that Allah would never accept the repentance of those people who had known Allah, believed in Him, and believed in His Messenger, then turned back from that because of the afflictions that befell them in the world. They used to say this about themselves. So Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, revealed concerning them: **""Say: O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, do not despair of the mercy of Allah""** until His words: **""a home for the arrogant""** [al-Zumar 59:60]. 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I wrote it down with my own hand as a letter, then sent it to Hisham. Hisham ibn al-'As said: When it reached me, I took it out to Dhu Tuwa, raising it and lowering it so that I might understand it. I said: O Allah, grant me understanding of it. Then I realized that it had been revealed concerning us and what we used to say about ourselves and what was said about us. I returned, mounted my camel, and joined the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Hisham was later martyred at Ajnadin during the caliphate of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him).

[Hasan]"
"17763","(17757) Abu 'Abd Allah narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us: Ahmad narrated to us: Yunus narrated from Ibn Ishaq: Hakim ibn Jubayr narrated to me from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: This verse was revealed concerning those who were subjected to tribulation among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Makkah: **""Then indeed, your Lord — for those who emigrated after they had been subjected to tribulation, then strove and were patient — indeed, your Lord, after that, is Forgiving and Merciful""** [al-Nahl 110].

[Da'if]"
"17764","(17758) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us: Warqa' narrated from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: 'Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah accepted Islam and emigrated to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then Abu Jahl ibn Hisham, his maternal brother, came to him along with another man and said: Your mother adjures you by her right and her bond that you return to her. So 'Ayyash went back with them, and they bound him until they brought him to Makkah, where they both subjected him to punishment.

[Hasan]"
"17765","(17759) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf informed us: Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi narrated to us: Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from 'Amr, from 'Ikrimah, who said: There were people in Makkah who had affirmed Islam. When the people set out for Badr, none of them was left behind except that they were taken out and brought along. Those who had affirmed Islam were killed. So the following verses were revealed concerning them: **""Indeed, those whom the angels take in death while they are wronging themselves""** until His words: **""and a terrible destination""** [al-Nahl 97–98]. The Muslims who were in Madinah wrote to those who were in Makkah. When the letter reached them, some of those who had affirmed Islam set out, but the polytheists pursued them and compelled them until they forced them into the tribulation. So Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, revealed concerning them: **""Except for one who is compelled while his heart is at rest in faith""** [al-Nahl 106].

[Sahih]"
"17766","(17760) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki both said: Abu Sahl Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Qattan narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Isa narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us: Shayban narrated from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said ""Allah hears the one who praises Him"" — before he would prostrate — he would say: ""O Allah, save 'Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah. O Allah, save Salamah ibn Hisham. O Allah, save al-Walid ibn al-Walid. O Allah, save the musta'afin (weak) among the believers. O Allah, make Your grip severe upon Mudar. O Allah, make it years like the years of Yusuf."" [Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Sahih from Abu Nu'aym, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shayban.]

[Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"17767","(17761) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us: Abu Mansur al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us: Hushaym narrated from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: A man from Khuza'ah was in Makkah and fell ill — he was Damrah ibn al-'As or al-'As ibn Damrah ibn Zinba'. He instructed his family, so they spread a bed for him, carried him, and set out with him heading towards Madinah. When he reached al-Tan'im, he died. So the verse was revealed: **""And whoever leaves his house as an emigrant to Allah and His Messenger, and then death overtakes him, his reward has already fallen upon Allah""** [al-Nahl 100]. And this is what al-Hasan and other exegetes have said.

[Sahih]"
"17768","(17762) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu Ja'far al-Baghdadi narrated to us: Abu 'Ula'athah narrated to me: My father narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: Al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him) had accepted Islam and continued in his role of providing water to the pilgrims, but he did not emigrate.

[Da'if]"
"17769","(17763) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, who said: Then Abu al-'As returned to Makkah after having accepted Islam, and did not witness any military expedition with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He later came to Madinah and died in Dhu al-Hijjah in the year twelve, during the caliphate of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and he bequeathed to al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam. Imam al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: He used to command his armies to say to those who accepted Islam: If you emigrate, you shall have what the emigrants have; and if you remain, you shall be like the Bedouin Muslims. And they were not given a choice except in what was lawful for them.

[Da'if]"
"17770","(17764) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Waki' narrated from Sufyan, from 'Alqamah ibn Murthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed a commander over a military detachment or an army, he would enjoin upon him in particular the fear of Allah, and goodness towards the Muslims who were with him. He would say: When you meet your enemy from among the polytheists, invite them to one of three qualities — whichever they respond to, accept it from them and desist from them. Invite them to Islam; if they respond, accept it from them and desist from them. Then invite them to move from their land to the land of the emigrants, and inform them that if they do so, they shall have what the emigrants have, and upon them shall be that which is upon the emigrants. But if they refuse and choose their own land, inform them that they shall be like the Bedouin Muslims — the judgment of Allah which applies to the believers shall apply to them — and they shall have no share in the fay' (spoils) orghanimah (war booty) unless they strive alongside the Muslims. [Narrated by Muslim in al-Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah from Waki'.]

The Sheikh said: Narrations of similar meaning have been transmitted. [Sahih. Muslim]"
"17771","(17765) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu 'Abd Allah Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Susi informed us, they both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Bayrutiyy informed us: my father informed me: al-Awza'iyy informed us: al-Zuhriyy informed me: 'Ata' ibn Yazid al-Laythiyy informed me: Abu Sa'id al-Khudriyy, may Allah be pleased with him, informed me: that a Bedouin came to the Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, and asked him about hijrah. He said: ""Indeed, hijrah is a matter of great difficulty. Do you have camels?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Do you lend them to others?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Do you milk them on the day they come to the watering place?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then work beyond the seas, for Allah will not cause any of your deeds to be lost."" Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from the hadith of al-Awza'iyy. [Sahih. Agreed upon.]"
"17772","(17766) Abu Muhammad al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn al-Mu'ammal informed us: Abu 'Uthman 'Amr ibn 'Abd Allah al-Basriyy informed us: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us: Surayj ibn al-Nu'man Abu al-Husayn informed us: Fulayh, meaning Ibn Sulayman, informed us, from Hilal ibn 'Ali, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: ""Whoever believes in Allah and His Messenger, establishes the Salah, and fasts in Ramadan, it is incumbent upon Allah to admit him into Paradise — whether he performs hijrah in the cause of Allah or remains seated in the land in which he was born."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall we not inform the people of this?"" He said: ""Indeed, in Paradise there are one hundred degrees, which Allah has prepared for the mujahidin in His cause; the distance between every two degrees is as vast as the distance between the heavens and the earth. When you ask of Allah, then ask Him for al-Firdaws, for it is the middle of Paradise and the highest part of Paradise, and above it is the Throne of Allah, and from it the rivers of Paradise gush forth."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Yahya ibn Salih, from Fulayh. [Sahih. Agreed upon.]"
"17773","(17767) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us: Abu Muslim informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah informed us: Jarir informed us — (h) — and Abu al-Hasan 'Ala' ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Sa'id al-Isfara'iniyy informed us with it: Abu Sahl Bishr ibn Ahmad informed us: Ibrahim ibn 'Ali al-Dhuhliyy informed us: Yahya ibn Yahya informed us: Jarir, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said on the day of the conquest, the conquest of Makkah: ""There is no hijrah, but there is jihad and intention; and when you are called forth, then go forth."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from 'Ali ibn al-Madiniyy and 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah, from Jarir; and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Agreed upon.]

His statement, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family: ""There is no hijrah"" — meaning, and Allah knows best — there is no hijrah as an obligation upon the people of Makkah after its conquest, for it has become a land of Islam and security, and no one therein fears being tempted away from his religion. The same applies to other places that come to share this meaning of security after the conquest."
"17774","(17768) In a similar vein, there is what 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdana informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us: al-Asfatiyy al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl informed us: Suwayd informed us — (h) — and Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Jarudiyy informed us: Suwayd ibn Sa'id informed us: 'Ali ibn Mushir, from 'Asim, from Abu 'Uthman, who informed him that Mujashi' ibn Mas'ud al-Sulamiyy said: I came to the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, with my father Ma'bad after the conquest, and I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, pledge his allegiance upon hijrah."" He said: ""Hijrah has passed to those for whom it was ordained."" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, then upon what shall You pledge his allegiance?"" He said: ""Upon Islam, jihad, and goodness."" So He pledged his allegiance. Abu 'Uthman said: ""I met Abu Ma'bad and informed him of Mujashi's statement, and he said: 'He has spoken the truth.'"" Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Suwayd ibn Sa'id, and al-Bukhari narrated it from another chain from 'Asim al-Ahwal. [Sahih. Agreed upon.]"
"17775","(17769) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqrī informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub informed us: Abu al-Rabi' Sulayman ibn Dawud informed us: Fulayh ibn Sulayman, from al-Zuhriyy, from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Umayyah, that his father informed him from Ya'la ibn Munayyah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, with my father on the day of the conquest, and I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, pledge my father's allegiance upon hijrah."" He said: ""Rather, I shall pledge his allegiance upon jihad, for hijrah has ceased on the day of the conquest."" Thus I found it, though he is actually 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-Rahman. [Da'if.]"
"17776","(17770) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed it to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam informed us: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me: 'Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Ibn Shihab, who informed him, who said: 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Umayyah ibn Ya'la informed me that his father informed him that Ya'la said: ""I spoke to the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, about my father Umayyah on the day of the conquest, and I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, pledge my father's allegiance upon hijrah.' The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: 'Rather, I shall pledge his allegiance upon jihad, for hijrah has ceased.'"" And 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated it from Ibn Shihab, saying: ""'Umar ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Umayyah, the brother of Ya'la."" [Da'if.]"
"17777","(17771) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah al-Khusrawjirdiyy informed us: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Isma'iliyy informed me: 'Abd Allah ibn Salih informed me: Ibn Kasib informed me: Sufyan, from 'Amr ibn Dinar and Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: It was said to Safwan ibn Umayyah while he was in the upper part of Makkah: ""There is no religion for the one who does not make hijrah."" He said: ""I shall not reach my house until I come to Madinah."" So he came to Madinah and alighted with al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib. Then he came to the Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, and said: ""What has brought you, O Abu Wahb?"" He said: ""It was said: 'There is no religion for the one who does not make hijrah.'"" The Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: ""Return, O Abu Wahb, to the open valleys of Makkah and abide in your dwellings, for hijrah has ceased; but there is jihad and intention, and if you are called forth, then go forth."" [Sahih.]"
"17778","(17772) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Uthman ibn Yahya al-Adami informed us: Muhammad ibn Mahan informed us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi informed us: Shu'bah, from al-Nu'man ibn Salim, from a man who heard Jubayr ibn Mut'im, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, some people say that we shall have no reward while we are in Makkah."" He said: ""Your reward shall most certainly reach you, even if you were in the den of a fox."" [Da'if.]"
"17779","(17773) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan informed us: Abu al-Azhar informed us: Fudayk ibn Sulayman informed us: al-Awza'iyy, from al-Zuhriyy, from Salih ibn Bashir ibn Fudayk, who said: Fudayk came to the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, they claim that whoever does not make hijrah is doomed."" The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: ""O Fudayk, establish the Salah, give the Zakah, abandon evil, and dwell wherever you wish in the land of your people."" He said: ""And I think he said: 'You will be a muhajir.'"" [Da'if.]"
"17780","(17774) Abu Tahir informed us: Abu Bakr al-Qattan informed us: Abu al-Azhar informed us: Ishaq ibn 'Isa informed us: Yahya ibn Hamzah, from Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydiyy, from al-Zuhriyy, from Salih ibn Bashir ibn Fudayk, from the Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, something similar; however, in the hadith of al-Zubaydiyy, the phrase ""You will be a muhajir"" is not present. [Da'if.]"
"17781","(17775) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Khālid ibn Makhlad narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmayr narrated to us: al-Maqburī narrated from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""Some Bedouin people came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, some of our relatives have come to us, and they claim that no deed is of benefit without emigration (hijrah) and jihād.' The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'Wherever you may be, worship Allah well, and give glad tidings of Paradise.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17782","(17776) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us: Rūḥ narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who informed me: ʿAṭāʾ informed me that he came to ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, along with ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, and she was residing in the neighbourhood. ʿUbayd said: 'O Umm al-Muʾminīn, I ask you about emigration (hijrah).' She said: 'There is no emigration after the Conquest (of Makkah). Emigration was only before the Conquest, when a man would emigrate with his religion to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him. But now that the Conquest has taken place, a man may worship Allah wherever he wishes, and he will not be prevented.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17783","(17777) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Maḥbūb ibn Mūsā informed us: Abū Isḥāq narrated from al-Awzāʿī from ʿAṭāʾ, who said: ""I visited ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, with ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, and he asked her about emigration (hijrah). She said: 'There is no emigration today. Emigration was only towards Allah and His Messenger; the believers would flee with their religion to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, lest they be put to trial. But Allah has now spread Islam, so a man may worship his Lord wherever he wishes. However, there remains jihād and intention (niyyah).'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of al-Awzāʿī and Ibn Jurayj, and we have narrated something similar from Ibn ʿUmar.

All of this refers to the cessation of the obligation of emigration for the people of Makkah and other lands after they became lands of security and Islam. As for a land of war (dār al-ḥarb) in which a person who has accepted Islam fears trial regarding his religion and has the means to reach the land of Islam, then upon him is the obligation to emigrate. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3090]"
"17784","(17778) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us in a similar narration: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā al-Rāzī informed us: ʿĪsā narrated from Ḥarīz from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAwf from Abū Hind from Muʿāwiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say: 'Emigration (hijrah) will not cease until repentance (tawbah) ceases, and repentance will not cease until the sun rises from its place of setting (the west).'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17785","(17779) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah, the judge of Damascus, narrated to us from ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī from Ibn Muḥayriẓ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Saʿdī, from Banū Mālik ibn Ḥisl, who said: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, among a group of his Companions. When they dismounted, they said: 'Guard our mounts for us until we attend to our needs, and then you may enter.' He was the youngest of the group. They attended to their needs, and then they said to him: 'Enter.' When he entered upon the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, he said: 'What is your need?' He said: 'My need is that you inform me: has emigration (hijrah) ceased?' He said: 'Your need is the best of their needs. Emigration does not cease as long as the enemy is fought.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other chains]"
"17786","(17780) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Gharrarah narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh and Abū Nuʿaym narrated from Sufyān al-Thawrī from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm from ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd from Saʿd ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, came to visit me, and he disliked that a person should die in the land from which he had emigrated. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, shall I bequeath all of my wealth?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'Then half?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'Then one-third?' He said: 'One-third, and one-third is much. Indeed, if you leave your heirs wealthy, it is better for them than leaving them destitute, begging people with their hands. And whatever you spend in charity, it is a ṣadaqah for you, even the morsel that you raise to your wife's mouth. And perhaps Allah will raise you, so that some people may benefit through you and others may be harmed.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
"17787","(17781) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad al-Sammāk narrated to us: Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrated it with its chain and its meaning, except that he said: ""He visited me while I was ill in Makkah, and he disliked that he should die in the land from which he had emigrated."" He said: ""May Allah have mercy on you, Ibn ʿAfrāʾ."" Then he mentioned the rest. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī and Muslim]"
"17788","(17782) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Yaḥyā Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyā ibn Asad narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī from ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Wqqāṣ, that his father informed him: ""He fell ill in the year of the Conquest (of Makkah) with an illness from which he nearly died. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, came to visit him while he was in Makkah."" He mentioned the hadith and said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, will I be left behind from my emigration?' He said: 'You will never be left behind after me, doing any deed by which you seek the Countenance of Allah, except that you will thereby increase in rank and degree. And perhaps you will be left behind, so that some people may benefit through you and others may be harmed. O Allah, complete the emigration of my Companions and do not turn them back on their heels. But the wretched one is Saʿd ibn Khawlah.' He said this lamenting him, for he had died in Makkah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
"17789","(17783) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhṭawayh narrated to us: Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us: al-Zuhrī narrated it with its chain and its meaning, except that he said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, lamented him for having died in Makkah."" Sufyān said: ""And Saʿd ibn Khawlah was a man from Banū ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥumaydī, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah and others from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī, narrated previously]"
"17790","(17784) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Hārūn ibn Yūsuf narrated to us: Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated from Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī from ʿAmr ibn Saʿīd from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥimyarī, from three of the sons of Saʿd, each of them narrating to him from his father: ""The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, entered upon Saʿd to visit him in Makkah, and he wept. He said: 'What makes you weep?' He said: 'I fear that I may die in the land from which I emigrated, just as Saʿd ibn Khawlah died.' The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'O Allah, heal Saʿd. O Allah, heal Saʿd.' Three times, and then he mentioned the rest of the hadith."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
"17791","(17785) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bashrān informed us; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Wahb narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn Khuthaym narrated from ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd al-Qārī, from his father, from his grandfather ʿAmr al-Qārī, that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) arrived and had left Saʿd behind as an ill man when he set out for Ḥunayn. When he returned from al-Jiʿrānah performing ʿUmrah, he visited him while he was in pain and overcome by illness. He said to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam): ""I possess wealth, and I leave no heir except kalālah (i.e., no parent or child). Shall I bequeath all of my wealth or give it in charity?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Shall I give two-thirds in charity?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Shall I bequeath half of it?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Shall I give one-third in charity?"" He said: ""Yes, and that is much."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall I be granted residence in the land from which I emigrated?"" He said: ""I hope that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, will raise your station, and that through you some people will be afflicted and others will benefit. O ʿAmr ibn al-Qārī, if Saʿd dies after me, then bury him here,"" and he pointed with his hand, ""in the direction of Madīnah."" This narration corresponds with the narration of Sufyān in that this occurred in the year of the Conquest (ʿĀm al-Fatḥ). However, the other narrators from al-Zuhrī said it occurred in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage (Ḥajjat al-Wadāʿ). There is also a difference of opinion in this narration regarding Ibn Khuthaym concerning the name of the grandson of ʿAmr ibn al-Qārī. [Weak]"
"17792","(17786) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū Yaḥyā Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Aʿraj, who said: The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) left a man in charge of Saʿd and said: ""If he dies, do not bury him here."" [Weak; Mursal]"
"17793","(17787) And Abū ʿAbdillāh and Abū Bakr both informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; Abū Yaḥyā narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Muḥammad ibn Qays, from Abū Burdah, who said: Saʿd said to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam): ""Is it disliked for a man to die in the land from which he emigrated?"" He said: ""Yes."" This is mursal, as is the one before it. [Authentic through other chains]"
"17794","(17788) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ in Naysābūr narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Khashram narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Muḥammad ibn Qays al-Asadī, from Abū Burdah ibn Abī Mūsā, from Saʿd ibn Abī Wāqqāṣ (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: I heard the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) say that it is disliked for a man to die in the land from which he emigrated. [Authentic]"
"17795","(17789) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; Yazīd ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Baysarī narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿīd ibn Abī Hind, from his father, from Ibn ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) entered Makkah, he would say: ""O Allah, do not cause our deaths to occur in it until You take us out of it."" Wābiʿ followed him (in narration) from ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿīd. [Authentic]"
"17796","(17790) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Aḥmad al-Qāsim ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ al-Hamadhānī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways narrated to us; my brother narrated from Sulaymān, from Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿAtīq, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim ibn ʿAbdullāh, from Ibn ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) say: ""People are like a hundred camels; you can scarcely find among them one suitable for riding."" [Authentic; Agreed upon]

Ibn Shihāb said: And ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) used to say: ""O company of the Muhājirūn, do not accumulate your wealth in Makkah, and do not keep it in the land of your emigration, for a man's heart is attached to his wealth."""
"17797","(17791) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed me; ʿAmr ibn Muḥammad al-Nāqid narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn ʿĪsā al-Ramlī narrated from al-Aʿmash, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Murrah, from Masrūq, who said: ʿAbdullāh (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said: ""The consumer of ribā (usury), the one who feeds it, its two witnesses when they are aware of it, the tattooer and the one who is tattooed, the one who withholds charity, and the one who reverts to Bedouin life after emigration — all of these are cursed upon the tongue of Muḥammad (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam)."" Yaḥyā ibn ʿĪsā is alone in narrating it thus. However, al-Thawrī and others narrated it from al-Aʿmash, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Murrah, from al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbdullāh, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd. And Ibn Numayr narrated it from al-Aʿmash, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Murrah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith, from Ibn Masʿūd (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu). [Weak]"
"17798","(17792) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd al-Thaqafī and Dāwūd ibn Mikhraq al-Fāriyābī both said: Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd, from Salamah ibn al-Akwʿ (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), that he entered upon al-Ḥajjāj, who said: ""O son of al-Akwʿ, have you turned back on your heels? Have you become a Bedouin after emigration?"" He said: ""No, but the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) gave me permission to live among the Bedouin."" Al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Authentic; Agreed upon]"
"17799","(17793) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; Ḥātim narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd, who said: When ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) was killed, Salamah went to al-Rabadhah, married a woman there, and had children. He remained there until a few nights before his death, when he came down to Madīnah. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Qutaybah. [Authentic; Agreed upon]"
"17800","(17794) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; Hishām narrated from Qatādah, from Muṭarrif ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr, from ʿIyāḍ ibn Ḥimār al-Mujāshiʿī, that the Prophet of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said one day in his sermon: ""Listen! My Lord — or indeed my Lord — has commanded me to teach you what you are ignorant of, from that which He has taught me this day."" Then he mentioned the hadith and said: ""O Muḥammad, I have sent you only to test you and to test others through you. I have sent down upon you a Book that water cannot wash away — you may recite it while asleep and while awake. Indeed, Allah has commanded me to burn Quraysh. I said: 'O my Lord, they will then crush my head and leave it like a morsel of bread.' He said: 'Expel them as they expelled you, and wage war against them — We will aid you. Spend, and We will spend on you. Send an army, and We will send five like it. And fight, with those who obey you, those who disobey you.'"" And he mentioned the hadith. Muslim recorded it from the hadith of Hishām al-Dastawāʾī and others from Qatādah. [Authentic; Muslim 2865]"
"17801","(17795) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to us: Abū al-Mughīrah narrated to us: Ṣafwān narrated to us: Abū Ziyād Yaḥyā ibn ʿUbayd al-Ghassānī narrated from Yazīd ibn Quṭayb, from Muʿādh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to say: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) sent me to Yemen and said: ""Perhaps you shall pass by my grave and my mosque. I have sent you to a people whose hearts are tender; they will fight you for the truth twice. Then fight, with those among them who obey you, those who disobey you. Then they shall hasten toward Islam, to the extent that a woman shall hasten to her husband, a child to his father, and a brother to his brother. So settle between the tribes of Sakūn and Sakāsik."" [Weak]"
"17802","(17796) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Abū al-Azhar narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Raqī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salmān narrated to us by dictation in Baghdād: Hilāl ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Raqī narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAmr al-Raqī narrated from Zayd ibn Abī Unaysah, from Jabalah ibn Suḥaym, who said: Abū al-Muthannā al-ʿAbdī narrated to us, saying: I heard Ibn al-Khiṣāṣiyyah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I came to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) to pledge allegiance to him upon Islam, and he stipulated upon me: ""You bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger, you perform the five prayers, you fast Ramadan, you pay the Zakāh, you perform the Ḥajj of the House, and you strive in the cause of Allah.""

He said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, as for two of them, I do not have the strength for them. As for the Zakāh, I possess only ten camels, which are the sustenance of my family and their mounts. And as for jihād, they claim that whoever turns back has incurred the wrath of Allah, and I fear that when battle is presented to me, I shall dislike death and my soul shall be gripped by cowardice."" He said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) withdrew his hand, then moved it, and then said: ""No charity and no jihād — by what means shall you enter Paradise?"" He said: Then I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I pledge allegiance to you,"" and he accepted my allegiance upon all of them. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbdullāh. [Weak]"
"17803","(17797) Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq informed us in Baghdād: Aḥmad ibn Salmān narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Haytham al-Baladī narrated to us: Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us: Shaybān narrated to us: Manṣūr narrated from al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah, from Maymūn ibn Abī Shabīb, from Muʿādh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, will you not tell me of a deed by which I may enter Paradise?"" He said: ""If you wish, I shall inform you of the head of the matter, its pillar, and the peak of its hump. As for the head of the matter, it is Islam; whoever submits is safe. As for its pillar, it is the Ṣalāh. And as for the peak of its hump, it is jihād."" And he mentioned the ḥadīth. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17804","(17798) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs al-ʿAnazī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Ḥammād narrated from Ḥumayd, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Strive against the polytheists with your wealth, your selves, and your tongues."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17805","(17799) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us: Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated from Abī Isḥāq, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAyyāsh, from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from Makḥūl, from Abī Umāmah, from ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Upon you is jihād in the cause of Allah, for it is a gate among the gates of Paradise, and Allah removes through it grief and sorrow."" And others added therein that he said: ""And strive in the cause of Allah against those near and those far, and establish the limits of Allah upon those near and those far, and let not the reproach of any reproacher deter you in the matter of Allah.""

The Shaykh said: And that was narrated from al-Ḥārith ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Kindī, from ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit (may Allah be pleased with him). [Weak]"
"17806","(17800) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥalīm al-Marwazī informed us: Abū al-Muwajjih informed us: ʿAbdān informed us: ʿAbdullāh informed us: Ṣafwān ibn ʿAmr informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr narrated from his father, who said: We sat with al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad (may Allah be pleased with him) in Damascus, and he was upon a coffin, having nothing extra upon it. A man said to him: ""If only you would refrain from military expedition this year."" He said: ""The Buḥūth — meaning Sūrat al-Tawbah — has prevented us."" Allah, the Blessed and Most High, said: {Go forth, light and heavy} [al-Tawbah: 41]. ""So I find myself only light (i.e., unburdened)."" [Ḥasan]"
"17807","(17801) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: ʿAffān narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Zayd and Thābit narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that Abū Ṭalḥah (may Allah be pleased with him) recited this verse: {Go forth, light and heavy} [al-Tawbah: 41]. He said: ""Our Lord calls us forth — old and young. Prepare me, O my sons, prepare me!"" His sons said: ""You have already witnessed battles with the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), so we shall go forth to fight."" He said: ""Prepare me!"" So he set out by sea and died. They did not find an island for him until after seven days, and they buried him there, and his body had not changed. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17808","(17802) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr informed us: Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from Muʿāwiyah ibn Isḥāq, from ʿĀʿishah bint Ṭalḥah, from ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: I sought permission from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) regarding jihād, and he said: ""Your jihād — or sufficient for you — is the Ḥajj."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Kathīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2875]"
"17809","(17803) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-ʿAlawī and Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Najjār al-Muqriʾ in Kūfah informed us, saying: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Qabīṣah narrated from Sufyān, from Muʿāwiyah ibn Isḥāq, from ʿĀʾishah bint Ṭalḥah, from ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). She said: I sought his permission regarding jihād, and he said: ""Sufficient for you is the Ḥajj — or your jihād is the Ḥajj."""
"17810","(17804) And Abū al-Qāsim ibn Abī Hāshim al-ʿAlawī and Abū al-Qāsim ibn al-Najjār al-Muqriʾ informed us, saying: Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Qabīṣah narrated from Sufyān, from Ḥabīb, from ʿĀʾishah bint Ṭalḥah, from ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her), something similar to this. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qabīṣah through both chains of narration together."
"17811","(17805) Abu ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Adīb informed us that Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us that Maḥmūd al-Wāsiṭī — his wording — and al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān both narrated to us that Wahb informed us that Khālid informed us from Ḥabīb ibn Abī ʿAmrah from ʿĀʾishah bint Ṭalḥah from ʿĀʾishah, Mother of the Believers (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā), who said: ""We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we consider jihād to be the most virtuous deed — should we not strive in jihād alongside you?' He replied: 'No. Rather, the most virtuous jihād is an accepted Ḥajj (ḥajj mabrūr).'"" And ʿĀʾishah was her paternal aunt. Al-Bukhārī narrated this in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Mubārak from Khālid ibn ʿAbdullāh. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17812","(17806) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us that Qabīṣah narrated to us that Sufyān narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid from Umm Salamah (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā) that she said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do the men go out on military campaigns while we do not, and do we not fight so that we may be martyred, when we only receive half the inheritance?"" Thereupon Allah, the Most High, revealed: {And do not wish for that by which Allah has made some of you excel others} [al-Nisāʾ 32]. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17813","(17807) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Quraysh informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr narrated to us that my father narrated to us that ʿUbaydullāh narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) presented me for battle on the day of Uḥud, and I was fourteen years of age, but he did not permit me. Then he presented me on the day of al-Khandaq, and I was fifteen years of age, and he permitted me."" Nāfiʿ said: ""I came to ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, who was the Caliph at that time, and narrated this ḥadīth to him. He said: 'Indeed, this is a clear distinction between the minor and the major.' He then wrote to his governors: 'Let those who have reached fifteen years of age be enrolled in combat, and those below that, send them back to their families.'"" Muslim narrated this in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr, and al-Bukhārī also recorded it through another chain from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh)]"
"17814","(17808) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us that ʿAmr ibn Marzūq informed us that Shuʿbah narrated from Abī Isḥāq from al-Barāʾ, who said: ""I and Ibn ʿUmar were considered too young on the day of Badr."" Al-Bukhārī narrated this in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm from Shuʿbah. Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāh al-Muzakkī informed us that Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us that Musaddad narrated to us that Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from ʿUbaydullāh from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā), who said: ""I and Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj were presented before the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) on the day of al-Khandaq, and we were both fifteen years of age, and he accepted us both."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17815","(17809) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me that Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us that al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Qabbānī narrated to us that Abū Bakr ibn Abī ʿAttāb al-Aʿyan narrated to us that Manṣūr ibn Salamah, Abū Salamah al-Khuzāʿī, narrated to us that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd ibn Jāriyah al-Anṣārī narrated to me that my uncle, ʿAmr ibn Zayd ibn Jāriyah, narrated to me that my father, Zayd ibn Jāriyah, said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) considered a number of people too young on the day of Uḥud, among them Zayd ibn Jāriyah — meaning himself — and al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, and Zayd ibn Arqam, and Saʿd Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, and he mentioned Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhum)."" Thus it is in my book from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbdullāh, and I saw it in another copy as Ibn ʿUbaydullāh. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17816","(17810) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us that ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Harwī narrated to us that Hushaym narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Jaʿfar al-Anṣārī narrated from his father from Samurah ibn Jundub (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""My mother brought me to Madīnah, and people proposed marriage to her. She said: 'I shall not marry except a man who will take charge of this orphan.' So a man from the Anṣār married her. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) would review the young boys of the Anṣār every year and would enlist those among them who had reached maturity. I was presented one year, and he enlisted a boy but turned me away. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, you have enlisted him and turned me away — if I were to wrestle him, I would defeat him.' He said: 'Then wrestle him.' So I wrestled him and defeated him, and he then enlisted me."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17817","(17811) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan both said that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that Ḥātim — meaning Ibn Ismāʿīl — narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father from Yazīd ibn Hurmuz, that Najdah wrote to Ibn ʿAbbās asking him about certain matters. Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) said: ""Some people claim that Ibn ʿAbbās corresponds with the Khawārij. Were it not that I fear you would conceal knowledge, I would never write to him."" So Najdah wrote to him: ""Now then — inform me: Did the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) take women on military expeditions? Did he allocate a share of the war spoils to them? Did he kill children? When does the orphanhood of an orphan cease? And to whom does the khums belong?"" Ibn ʿAbbās wrote back to him: ""You have asked me whether the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) took women on military expeditions. Indeed, he did take them along; they would tend to the sick and wounded and receive something from the war spoils. As for a fixed share, he did not allocate one for them. And the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) did not kill children, so do not kill them — unless you possess the kind of knowledge that al-Khiḍr had regarding the boy he killed, so that you can distinguish between the believer and the disbeliever, killing the disbeliever and leaving the believer. You have also asked when the orphanhood of an orphan ceases. By my life, a man's beard may grow while he is still weak in taking and giving — when he is able to take for himself what people customarily take, his orphanhood has ceased. And you have asked me about the khums. We used to say it was for our sake, but our people refused this, and we bore it with patience."" Muslim narrated this in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm from Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl. (T) And we have also narrated in the ḥadīth of Qays ibn Saʿd from Yazīd ibn Hurmuz from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā) regarding this ḥadīth: ""As for the women and slaves, they had no fixed share when they were present at the battle, but they would receive from the war spoils of the people."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17818","(17812) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī narrated to us that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us that Maḥbūb ibn Mūsā al-Anṭākī informed us that Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who informed me that ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Umayyah narrated from al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Rabīʿah, that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) was on one of his military expeditions and passed by a group of people from Muzaynah. A slave belonging to a woman among them followed him. When they met along the road, the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) greeted him and said: ""Are you So-and-so?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""I wish to perform jihād with you."" He said: ""Has your mistress given you permission?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Return to her, for the likes of you is like a slave who does not pray — if you die before you return to her, and convey my greeting of peace to her."" So he returned to her and informed her of the matter. She said: ""By Allah, did he command you to convey the greeting of peace to me?"" He said: ""Yes."" She said: ""Then go and perform jihād with him."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17819","(17813) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us that Abū al-Aḥwaṣ Muḥammad ibn al-Haytham ibn Ḥammād narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bukayr narrated to me that al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Khālid ibn Yazīd from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl from Yazīd ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Hādī from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu), from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said: ""The jihād of the elderly, the weak, and women is Ḥajj and ʿUmrah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17820","(17814) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh narrated to us that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us that Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar, Abū ʿUmar al-Ḍarīr, narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Abī Isḥāq from al-Barāʾ (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""When the verse was revealed {Not equal are those of the believers who sit [at home] and those who strive in the cause of Allah} [al-Nisāʾ 95], the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) commanded Zayd, and he wrote it down. Then Ibn Umm Maktūm came and complained of his blindness to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), whereupon Allah, the Almighty, revealed {except those who are disabled} [al-Nisāʾ 95]."" Al-Bukhārī narrated this in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar, and Muslim recorded it through another chain from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh): 17815]"
"17821","(17815) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yaḥyā al-Ḥulwānī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us —"
"17822","(17816) Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam al-Qiṭrī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated from Ṣāliḥ ibn Kaysān, from al-Zuhrī, from Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I entered the mosque and found Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam seated, so I sat with him. He said: Zayd ibn Thābit (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, saying: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) when the verse was revealed: ""Not equal are those of the believers who sit [at home] and those who strive in the cause of Allah"" [al-Nisāʾ 95]. He said: Then Ibn Umm Maktūm came to me while I was writing it, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, you see the affliction in my eyes, and had I the ability to fight, I would have fought. Zayd ibn Thābit said: The thigh of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) became so heavy upon my thigh that I feared it might break. Then the state passed from him, and he said to me: Write: ""Not equal are those of the believers who sit [at home] — other than those who are disabled — and those who strive"" [al-Nisāʾ 95]. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Qiṭrī.

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways and others, from Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon. 17817]"
"17823","(17817) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn al-Ḥakam ibn Abī Maryam narrated to me; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated to me; Abū al-Zinād narrated to me that Khārijah ibn Zayd ibn Thābit narrated to him from his father, that tranquillity (al-sakīnah) overcame the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). Zayd said: I was beside him, and the thigh of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) fell upon my thigh, and I found nothing heavier than the thigh of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). Then the state passed from him, and he said: Write: ""Not equal are those of the believers who sit [at home] and those who strive in the cause of Allah with their wealth and their lives"" [al-Nisāʾ 95] — the entire verse. Zayd said: So I wrote it upon a shoulder blade. Then Ibn Umm Maktūm stood up — he was a blind man — when he heard the merit of the mujāhidīn over those who sit at home, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, what of one who is unable to perform jihād alongside the believers? Zayd said: Ibn Umm Maktūm had scarcely finished his words — or it was no sooner than when his words concluded — when tranquillity overcame the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and his thigh fell upon my thigh, and I found its weight this time the same as I had found in the first instance. Then the state passed from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he said: Read. So I read: ""Not equal are those of the believers who sit [at home]"" [al-Nisāʾ 95]. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Other than those who are disabled"" [al-Nisāʾ 95]. Zayd said: I inserted it [the phrase] and its insertion was at a crack in the shoulder blade. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than the above]"
"17824","(17818) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥaḍramī narrated from Abī ʿUqayl, from Abī Naḍrah, who said: I asked Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) regarding the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: ""Not equal are those of the believers who sit [at home] — other than those who are disabled"" [al-Nisāʾ 95]. He said: The people of disability (ahl al-ḍarar) were a people who, during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), did not go on expeditions with him. Ailments and diseases prevented them, while others were healthy. Thus the sick were more excused than the healthy. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17825","(17819) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Abī Sufyān, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said during one of his journeys: Indeed, there are men in Madīnah — we have not undertaken any journey nor crossed any valley except that they were with us therein. Illness has held them back. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Aḥmad. It was narrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"17826","(17820) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated from Ḥumayd, from Mūsā ibn Anas ibn Mālik, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: You have left behind in Madīnah people — you have not undertaken any journey, nor spent any expenditure, nor crossed any valley except that they were with you therein.

They said: O Messenger of Allah, how can they be with us when they are in Madīnah? He said: An excuse has held them back.

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Zuhayr and Ḥammād ibn Zayd, from Ḥumayd, from Anas. Then he said: And Mūsā said, meaning Ḥammād ibn Salamah, from Ḥumayd, from Mūsā ibn Anas, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), something similar to it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī]"
"17827","(17821) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: My father Isḥāq ibn Yasār narrated to me from elders of Banī Salamah, who said: ʿAmr ibn al-Jamūḥ was severely lame. He had four young sons who would go on military expeditions with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) whenever he went on campaign. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) resolved to set out for Uḥud, his sons said to him: Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has granted you a concession, so remain behind, for we will suffice you. Allah has lifted jihād from you. So ʿAmr ibn al-Jamūḥ came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my sons prevent me from going out with you. By Allah, I hope to be martyred and to walk with this lameness of mine into Paradise. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to him: As for you, Allah has lifted jihād from you.

And he said to his sons: What prevents you from letting him go? Perhaps Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, will grant him martyrdom. So he went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and was killed on the day of Uḥud as a martyr. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17828","(17822) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: By the One in whose Hand is my soul, were it not that I would impose hardship upon the believers, I would not stay behind from any raiding party that goes forth in the cause of Allah. But I do not find the means to mount them, nor do they find the means to follow me, and their souls are not content to remain behind after me.

It was narrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
"17829","(17823) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf al-Azraq narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Abī Isḥāq, from Wahb ibn Jābir, from ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: It is sufficient sin for a person that he neglects those whom he is obligated to support. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17830","(17824) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Sarī ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us; Riyāḥ ibn ʿAmr narrated to us; Ayyūb narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: While we were seated with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), a young man appeared before us from the mountain pass of Thaniyyah. When we saw him with our eyes, we said: Would that this young man devoted his youth, his vigour, and his strength in the cause of Allah. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) heard our words and said: Is the cause of Allah only that one be killed? Whoever strives to serve his parents is in the cause of Allah; whoever strives to provide for his family is in the cause of Allah; whoever strives to preserve his own soul in order to keep it chaste is in the cause of Allah; and whoever strives for accumulation [of wealth] is in the cause of Satan. [Ḥasan]"
"17831","(17825) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn Salman ibn al-Hasan, the jurist in Baghdad — narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Mukarram narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun informed us: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, who said: A man came to the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, if I am killed in the cause of Allah, will Allah expiate my sins?"" The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said, ""If you are killed in the cause of Allah, being patient, seeking reward, advancing and not retreating, Allah will expiate your sins."" When he sat down, he called him and said, ""What did you say?"" He repeated it to him, and he said, ""Except for the debt — thus Jibril (peace be upon him) informed me."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and others, from Yazid ibn Harun. [Sahih — Muslim]"
"17832","(17826) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us by dictation: Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri' narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, from 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas, from al-Hubuli, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: ""Being killed in the cause of Allah expiates everything except debt."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Zuhayr ibn Harb, from al-Muqri'. (T) And the hadith of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) has preceded, from the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""The soul of the believer is suspended by his debt until it is discharged on his behalf."""
"17833","(17827) Abu 'Ali — al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Rudhbari — informed us: Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Mahmuyah al-'Askari in Basra — narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Qalanisi narrated to us: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us: Habib ibn Abi Thabit said: I heard Abu al-'Abbas al-Sha'ir, who was not accused in his hadith, say: I heard 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: A man came to the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and sought his permission for jihad. The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to him, ""Are your parents alive?"" He said, ""Yes."" He said, ""Then strive in their cause."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Adam, and Muslim also recorded it through another chain from Shu'bah. [Sahih — Agreed upon]"
"17834","(17828) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Balwayh — narrated to us: Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us: Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu al-'Abbas, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: A man came to the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said, ""I wish to engage in jihad."" He said, ""Are your parents alive?"" He said, ""Yes."" He said, ""Return to them, for in them there is striving to be done."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Hatim, from Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr. [Sahih]"
"17835","(17829) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to me: 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed me: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me: Yazid ibn Abi Habib informed him that Na'im, the freed slave of Umm Salamah, narrated to him that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: A man came to the Prophet of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said, ""I pledge allegiance to you upon emigration or jihad, seeking reward from Allah."" He said, ""Is one of your parents alive?"" He said, ""Yes, both of them."" He said, ""And you seek reward from Allah?"" He said, ""Yes."" He said, ""Then return to your parents and keep their company in goodness."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Sa'id ibn Mansur, from Ibn Wahb. [Sahih]"
"17836","(17830) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan — informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar al-Dabbi and Muhammad ibn Rashid al-Tammar both said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said, ""I have come to pledge allegiance to you upon emigration, and I left my parents weeping."" He said, ""Then go back and make them laugh as you made them weep."" [Hasan]"
"17837","(17831) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — both said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Darraj, from Abi al-Samh, from Abi al-Haytham, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), that a man emigrated to the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) from Yemen and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I have emigrated."" The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said, ""You have abandoned shirk, but there is jihad. Do you have anyone in Yemen?"" He said, ""My parents."" He said, ""Have they given you permission?"" He said, ""No."" He said, ""Then go back and seek their permission. If they permit you, then strive; otherwise, be dutiful to them."" [Da'if]"
"17838","(17832) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran in Baghdad informed us: Abu Ja'far — Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz — narrated to us: Ahmad ibn al-Walid al-Fahham narrated to us: Hajjaj — (H) and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr — both said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Hajjaj narrated to me: Ibn Jurayj informed me: Muhammad ibn Talhah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informed me, from his father Talhah, from Mu'awiyah ibn Jahimah al-Sulami, that Jahimah (may Allah be pleased with him) came to the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I wish to go on a military expedition and I have come to seek your counsel."" He said, ""Do you have a mother?"" He said, ""Yes."" He said, ""Then remain with her, for Paradise is at her feet."" Then he said it a second time and a third time, in various wordings to that effect. This is the wording of the hadith of al-Saghani. [Hasan]"
"17839","(17833) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran, the upright, in Baghdad, informed us: Abu 'Ali — Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar — narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd Allah al-Munadi narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Simak, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, from Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Four verses were revealed concerning me. He then mentioned the hadith, in which it is stated that the mother of Sa'd said, ""Has Allah not commanded kindness to the mother? By Allah, I shall not eat food nor drink water until she disbelieves or I die."" So when they wished to feed her or give her drink, they would pry open her mouth with a stick and then pour food and drink into it. Then the verse was revealed: ""And We have enjoined upon man goodness to his parents. But if they strive to make you associate with Me that of which you have no knowledge, then do not obey them"" [al-'Ankabut 8]. Muslim extracted it in his Sahih from the hadith of Shu'bah. [Sahih — Muslim]"
"17840","(17834) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: 'Amr ibn Zurarah narrated to us: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us: Sa'id ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, from 'Urwah ibn Sa'id al-Ansari, from his father, from Husayn ibn Wahwah, that Talhah ibn al-Bara' (may Allah be pleased with him), when he met the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family), said, ""O Prophet of Allah, command me with what you love, and I will not disobey you in any matter."" The Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was astonished by that, as he was still a young boy. He said to him at that moment, ""Then kill your father."" He went out intending to do so, but he called him back and said, ""I was not sent to sever family ties."" [Da'if]"
"17841","(17835) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us that Asad ibn Mūsā narrated to us that Ḍamrah ibn Rabīʿah narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shawdhab, who said: The father of Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ used to set up idols for Abū ʿUbaydah on the day of Badr, and Abū ʿUbaydah would keep away from him. When al-Jarrāḥ persisted excessively, Abū ʿUbaydah targeted him and killed him. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed concerning him this verse, when he had killed his father: ""You will not find a people who believe in Allah and the Last Day having affection for those who oppose Allah and His Messenger, even if they were their fathers or their sons"" (al-Mujādilah, 22) to the end of the verse. This narration is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected). [Weak]"
"17842","(17836) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us that Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Sumayʿ al-Ḥanafī from Mālik ibn ʿUmayr, who had lived during the Jāhiliyyah, who said: A man came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and said: ""I encountered the enemy, and I found my father among them. I heard from him a vile remark about you, so I could not restrain myself until I struck him with the spear—or until I killed him."" The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) remained silent about it. Then another man came and said: ""I encountered my father and left him, and I preferred that someone else deal with him."" And he (the Prophet) remained silent about it. This is a good mursal narration. [Weak]"
"17843","(17837) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā al-Rāzī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Ḥarb narrated—Abū Dāwūd said, and ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ḥarb narrated with the same meaning, and I am more precise in his narration—from Abū Salamah Sulaymān ibn Sulaym from Yaḥyā ibn Jābir al-Ṭāʾī from the brother of Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī from Abū Ayyūb (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) say: ""The cities will be conquered for you, and there will be organized military expeditions. Campaigns will be dispatched, and a man among you will dislike being sent on a campaign. He will escape from his people, then he will survey the tribes, presenting himself to them, saying: 'Who will suffice me for such-and-such a campaign? Who will suffice me for such-and such a campaign?' Beware! That one is a mercenary unto the last drop of his blood."" [Weak]"
"17844","(17838) Abū Bakr al-Ardistānī informed us that Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr al-ʿIrāqī narrated to us that Sufyān ibn Muḥammad al-Jawharī narrated to us that ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us that Sufyān narrated to us that al-Zubayr ibn ʿAdī narrated from Shaqīq ibn al-ʿAyzār al-Asadī, who said: I asked Ibn ʿUmar about the jaʿāʾil (rewards/compensation for military service), and he said: ""I would not take a bribe except what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has given me as a bribe."" And I asked ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr, and he said: ""Leaving it is better. But if you take it, then spend it in the cause of Allah."" [Weak]"
"17845","(17839) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us that ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us that my father narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Isḥāq from ʿUbayd ibn al-Aʿjam, who said: A man asked Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā) about the juʿl (compensation/reward). He said: ""If you allocate it for weapons or cavalry mounts, there is no harm in it. But if you allocate it for slaves, then do not."" And it has been narrated to us from Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī that he said: ""They preferred to be given rather than to take—meaning regarding the jaʿāʾil."" [Weak]"
"17846","(17840) Abū Dāwūd narrated in al-Marāsīl from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh from Maʿdān ibn Ḥudayr al-Ḥaḍramī from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""The likeness of those who go out to fight from my Ummah and take the juʿl, thereby strengthening themselves against their enemy, is like the likeness of the mother of Mūsā: she nursed her child and took her wages."" Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad informed us that Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Fasawī narrated to us that Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated it. [Weak]"
"17847","(17841) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī informed us that Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Kāmil al-Qāḍī in Baghdād narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Saʿd narrated to us that Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us that Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim narrated—and Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Abī al-Ḥajjāj, Abū Maʿmar, narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us that al-Ḥusayn narrated to us that Yaḥyā narrated to us that Abū Salamah narrated to me that Busr ibn Saʿīd narrated to me that Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Whoever equips a ghāzī (warrior) in the cause of Allah has indeed performed ghazw (military expedition), and whoever looks after his family in his absence with goodness has indeed performed ghazw."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Wārith, and the ḥadīth of Rūḥ is similar to it, except that he said: ""from"" (ʿan). Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Maʿmar, and Muslim narrated it from Abī al-Rabīʿ from Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ from Ḥusayn. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
"17848","(17842) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh narrated to us that Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us that ʿAffān narrated to us that Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Thābit from Anas (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) that a man from Banī Aslam came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and said: ""I wish to perform jihād, but I do not have the means with which to equip myself."" He said: ""So-and-so has equipped himself but then fell ill. Go to him and say: 'The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) conveys salām to you and commands you to give me the provisions with which I may equip myself.'"" So he went to him and said that. He then said to his wife: ""See that you give him what you prepared for me, and do not withhold anything from it, so that Allah may bless you in it."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from ʿAffān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
"17849","(17843) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us that Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us that al-Aʿmash narrated from Abū ʿAmr al-Shaybānī from Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: A man came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have been exhausted (lit. 'my mount has been cut off from me'), so carry me."" The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""I do not have anything."" A man said: ""Shall I not direct you, O Messenger of Allah, to someone who will carry him?"" The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Whoever directs to a good deed shall have a reward equal to the one who performs it."" Abū ʿAbd Allāh said in his narration: Abū Muʿāwiyah said: ""Ubidiya bī (I have been exhausted) means quṭiʿa bī (my way has been cut off)."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Kurayb from Abī Muʿāwiyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
"17850","(17844) Abū Muḥammad ibn al-Muʾammal informed us that Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us that Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us that al-Aʿmash narrated it, except that he said: He said: ""I do not find anything to carry you on. But go to so-and-so."" So he went to him, and he carried him. He then came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and informed him, and he said: ""Whoever directs to a good deed shall have a reward equal to the one who performs it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17851","(17845) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Ṣāliḥ and Muḥammad ibn Rumḥ narrated to us, saying: Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ al-Kindī al-Tujībī, from ibn Shufayy, from his father, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The warrior has his reward, and the one who equips him has his own reward, and the reward of the warrior is also his."" And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""A single expedition in the cause of Allah is like a military campaign."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17852","(17846) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dimashqī, Abū al-Naḍr, narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Shuʿayb narrated to me: Abū Zurʿah — Yaḥyā ibn Abī ʿAmr al-Saybānī — narrated from ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdullāh, who narrated to him from Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called out during the expedition of Tabūk. I went to my family and returned, and the first of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had already departed. I began to call out in the streets of Madinah: ""Is there anyone who will provide a mount for a man whose share of booty shall be his?"" An elder from the Anṣār said: ""We will have his share of booty on the condition that we provide him a mount and his provisions will be with us."" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then proceed by the blessing of Allah."" I went forth with an excellent companion until Allah granted us victory. I acquired some she-camels and watered them until I came to him. He came out and sat upon one of his saddlebags, then said: ""Water them as they are going away."" Then he said: ""Water them as they are coming forward."" He said: ""I see your she-camels to be noble ones."" He said: ""They are only the spoils of war that I stipulated for you."" He said: ""Take your she-camels, O brother; we did not intend anything beyond your share."" The elder (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""We did not intend by what we did to seek a wage; rather, our intention was to share in the reward and the recompense. And Allah knows best."""
"17853","(17847) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Abū Tawbah — al-Rabīʿ ibn Nāfiʿ — narrated to us: Bashīr ibn Ṭalḥah narrated from Khālid ibn Durayk, from Yaʿlā ibn Munayyah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to send me on his raiding parties. One day he sent me, and there was a man who used to ride my mule. I said to him: ""Saddle up."" He said: ""I am not going out with you."" I said: ""Why?"" He said: ""Until you assign me three dinars."" I said: ""Now, when I have bidden farewell to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I shall not return to him. Saddle up, and you shall have three dinars."" When I returned from my expedition, I mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Give them to him, for they are his share from his expedition."" (T) And it has already been mentioned in the Book of Division from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Daylamī, from Yaʿlā ibn Munayyah, with the same meaning. [Ḥasan]"
"17854","(17848) Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān — informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us: Abū Ṣāliḥ, that is, Maḥbūb ibn Mūsā, narrated to us: al-Fazārī narrated from Saʿīd al-Jurayrī, from Abī Naḍrah, from Abī Firās, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) addressed us and said in his sermon: ""O people! I have not sent my governors to you to strike your faces or to take your wealth. Rather, I have sent them to teach you your religion and your Sunnah. So whoever is subjected to anything other than that, let him bring it to me, so that I may exact justice from him. Beware! Do not strike the Muslims, lest you humiliate them. Do not deny them their rights, lest you drive them to disbelief. Do not burden them excessively, lest you put them to trial. Do not settle them in difficult terrain, lest you cause them to perish."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17855","(17849) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd informed us: Ibn Shihāb narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik al-Anṣārī, who said: A detachment of the Anṣār was in the land of Persia under their commander, and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to rotate the troops every year. ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) was occupied and could not do so. When the appointed term had passed, the people of that frontier returned. The commander dealt harshly with them and threatened them, and they were Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They said: ""O ʿUmar, you have neglected us and abandoned the command that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave regarding the rotation of some raiding parties with others."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Sijistānī]"
"17856","(17850) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshāʿ al-ʿAdl narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways narrated to me: Mālik narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) went out at night and heard a woman reciting:

""This night has grown long and its sides have darkened,
And it has deprived me of sleep that I have no beloved to sport with.
By Allah, were it not for Allah, I would watch over him,
The sides of this bed have shifted.""

ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said to Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both): ""How long can a woman endure separation from her husband?"" She said: ""Six or four months."" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I shall not detain any army beyond that."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17857","(17851) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from Yazīd ibn Hurmuz, that Najdah wrote to Ibn ʿAbbās asking: ""Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ever take women on military campaigns, and did he allocate a share of booty to them?"" He replied: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did take women on campaigns, and they would treat the wounded, but he did not allocate a share of booty to them; rather, they would receive gifts from the spoils."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ as it has already passed. Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""It is well-established that slaves and young boys participated in battles alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would give them gifts from the spoils."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"17858","(17852) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, from Yazīd ibn Hurmuz, who said: Najdah wrote to Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) asking him about the slave and the free woman who are present at the time of the spoils — do they have any share in the booty? He wrote back to them: ""They have nothing except that they may be given gifts."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim] And Abū Yūsuf mentioned in this ḥadīth from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah that he wrote to him asking about the boy: when does he emerge from the state of orphanhood, and when is a share of booty allocated to him? He replied: ""He emerges from orphanhood when he reaches puberty (iḥtulām), and a share of booty is allocated to him."""
"17859","(17853) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Yazīd ibn ʿIyāḍ narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah al-Qurashī, who mentioned this ḥadīth and said in it: ""And he asked about the orphan: when does he emerge from the state of orphanhood and when does his right in the fayʾ become established?"" He wrote back to him: ""When he reaches puberty, he has emerged from orphanhood, and his right in the fayʾ becomes established."" Yazīd ibn ʿIyāḍ is not used as a proof, and Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd has fallen from his isnād. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17860","(17854) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiế narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hilālī informed us: Abū Maʿmar narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: On the day of Uḥud, a number of people fled from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while Abū Ṭalḥah was before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), shielding him with his shield. He said: I saw ʿĀʾishah bint Abī Bakr and Umm Sulaym, and they were both rolling up their sleeves, their anklets visible, carrying water-skins on their backs, pouring water into the mouths of the people, then returning to fill them, then coming back and pouring water into the mouths of the people. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Maʿmar, and Muslim narrated it from ʿAbdullāh al-Dārimī from Abī Maʿmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"17861","(17855) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn and Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Harashi narrated to us, they both said — Yahya ibn Yahya informed us — Ja'far ibn Sulayman reported from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) used to go on military expeditions with Umm Sulaym and some women of the Ansar, who would provide water and treat the wounds when he went to battle. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and it was also narrated in this regard from al-Rubayyi' bint Mu'awwidh, Umm Atiyyah, and others. [Sahih. Muslim]"
"17862","(17856) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Balawayh informed me — Musa ibn al-Hasan narrated to us — al-Qa'nabi narrated to us — 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us — (h) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us through dictation — Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Farra' and Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, they both said — Yahya ibn Yahya informed us — 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Hazim reported from his father, that he heard Sahl ibn Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) being asked about the wound of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) on the day of Uhud. He said: The face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) was wounded, his front tooth (rabā'iyyah) was broken, and the crown of his head was crushed. Fatimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), was washing the blood, while 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) was pouring water upon it with a shield. When Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her) saw that the water only increased the flow of blood, she took a piece of matting, burned it until it became ashes, and applied it to the wound, whereupon the blood stopped. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from al-Qa'nabi, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya, both from 'Abd al-'Aziz. [Sahih. Agreed upon (muttafaq 'alayh)]"
"17863","(17857) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad informed us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us — Fudayl ibn Sulayman and Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Zayd narrated to us — 'Umayr, the freed slave of Abu Lahm (may Allah be pleased with him), said: I participated in the expedition of Khaybar with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) while I was a slave. He did not allocate a share of the war spoils for me, but he gave me a sword, which I hung around my neck, dragging it along the ground, and he commanded me regarding my worn-out belongings. [Sahih]"
"17864","(17858) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'd ibn Abi 'Amr said — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us — Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us — (h) And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Sa'd ibn Mansur narrated to us — Abu Mu'awiyah reported from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I used to carry water for my companions on the day of Badr. In the narration of Ahmad: ""I used to give them water to drink."" [Sahih. Sa'd ibn Mansur]"
"17865","(17859) Abu Sa'd ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman said — al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) went on a military expedition, and some people whose hypocrisy was known went out with him, but they lagged behind on the day of Uhud — three hundred of them. The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: This is elaborated upon in the works on military campaigns (al-maghāzī). [Sahih. For al-Shafi'i]"
"17866","(17860) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us — Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us — from Ibn Ishaq, who said: Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, 'Asim ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, and other scholars among us narrated to me about the day of Uhud, and they mentioned the story, in which it is said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) set out with one thousand of his companions, until he reached al-Shawt, between Madinah and Uhud, when 'Abd Allah ibn Ubayy, the hypocrite, lagged behind with a third of the people, taking back with him those of his people who followed him among the people of doubt and hypocrisy. [Da'if]"
"17867","(17861) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn 'Attab narrated to us — al-Qasim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mughirah informed us — Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us from his paternal uncle, Musa ibn 'Uqbah, in the story of Uhud, who said: 'Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul withdrew with three hundred, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) remained with seven hundred. [Da'if]"
"17868","(17862) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Ja'far al-Baghdadi narrated to us — Abu 'Ula'ah narrated to us — my father narrated to us — Ibn Luhay'ah narrated to us — from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) proceeded until he encamped at Uhud, and 'Abd Allah ibn Ubayy withdrew with three hundred, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) remained with seven hundred. [Da'if]"
"17869","(17863) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar ibn Shawdhb al-Wasiti narrated therein — Ahmad ibn Sinan narrated to us — Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated to us — (h) And Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us — Abu Muslim narrated to us — Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated to us — from 'Adiyy ibn Thabit, from 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid, who said: I heard Zayd ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) set out toward Uhud, a group of people turned back from the road. The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) were divided into two groups: one group saying, ""We shall fight them,"" and another saying, ""We shall not fight them."" So Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, revealed: *""And what is the matter with you that you are two factions regarding the hypocrites, while Allah has overturned them for what they earned?""* [al-Nisa' 88]. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Sulayman ibn Harb, and Muslim reported it through another chain from Shu'bah.

Al-Shafi'i said: Then they witnessed the Battle of the Trench (al-Khandaq) with him, and spoke of what Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, related of their words: *""Allah and His Messenger promised us nothing but delusion""* [al-Ahzab 12].

The Shaykh said: This is elaborated upon in the works on military campaigns from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yasar, and others. Musa ibn 'Uqbah said, with the chain of narration previously mentioned in the story of the Trench: When the trial became severe upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) and his companions, many people became hypocrites and spoke vile words. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) saw the state of the people in their affliction and distress, he began to give them glad tidings, saying: ""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, the severity and affliction you see shall surely be relieved. I hope that I will circumambulate the Ancient House in safety, that Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, will deliver to me the keys of the Ka'bah, that Allah will destroy Chosroes and Caesar, and that their treasures will be spent in the way of Allah."" A man among his companions said: ""Do you not marvel at Muhammad, promising us that we will circumambulate the Ancient House and divide the treasures of Persia and Rome, when here we are — none of us feels safe even to go to relieve himself? By Allah, he is giving us nothing but delusion!"" Others among them said: ""Give us permission, for our homes are exposed."" Still others said: ""O people of Yathrib, there is no place for you here, so go back!"" Ibn Ishaq named the first speaker as 'Utayb ibn Qushayr and the second as Aws ibn Qaydhi. [Sahih. Agreed upon (muttafaq 'alayh)]"
"17870","(17864) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Ja'far al-Baghdadi narrated to us — Abu 'Ula'ah narrated to us — my father narrated to us — Ibn Luhay'ah narrated to us — from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: When the trial became severe upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) and his companions, he mentioned this story in a manner similar to the account of Musa ibn 'Uqbah, except that at the end he said: Some men among them discouraged others from supporting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), saying: ""O people of Yathrib, there is no place for you here, so go back!""

Al-Shafi'i said: Then the Banu al-Mustaliq expedition took place, and a number of them participated in it with him, and they spoke of what Allah related of their words: *""If we return to Madinah, the most honorable of us will expel the most humiliated from it""* [al-Munafiqun 8], and other such words of hypocrisy that Allah related concerning them. [Da'if]"
"17871","(17865) Abū ʿAliyy al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmūyah al-ʿAskarī narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us: Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from al-Ḥakam, who said: I heard Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb al-Qaraẓī say: I heard Zayd ibn Arqam (may Allah be pleased with him) say:

When ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy said, ""Do not spend upon those who are in the company of the Messenger of Allah until they disperse,"" and also said, ""If we return to Madīnah, the most honourable among us will expel the most humbled therefrom,"" I informed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) of that. The Anṣār reproached me, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy swore that he had not said it. I returned to my home and fell asleep. Then the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) came to me, so I went to him, and he said: ""Indeed, Allah has confirmed your statement and has excused you.""

And Allah revealed: *""They are the ones who say, 'Do not spend upon those who are with the Messenger of Allah until they disperse.'""* [Al-Munāfiqūn: 7] — the verse.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh.]

---"
"17872","(17866) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: ʿAliyy al-Madīnī narrated to us: Sufyān said: ʿAmr said: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him) say:

We were on a military expedition — and Sufyān said on another occasion, ""We were with an army"" — when a man from the Muhājirūn struck a man from the Anṣār. Jābir said: ""Leave it, for it is a foul thing."" ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy heard that and said: ""They have indeed done it! By Allah, if we return to Madīnah, the most honourable among us will expel the most humbled therefrom."" This reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he said: ""Leave him, lest the people say that Muḥammad is killing his companions.""

He said: ""The Anṣār were more numerous than the Muhājirūn when they first arrived in Madīnah, but then the Muhājirūn increased in number thereafter.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAliyy ibn ʿAbdillāh, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and a group from Ibn ʿUyaynah.

And we have narrated from Ibn Isḥāq through the chain mentioned earlier that this occurred during the expedition of Banī al-Muṣṭaliq, and likewise from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: Then he went on the expedition of Tabūk, and a group of them accompanied him who set out on the night of al-ʿAqabah to kill him, but Allah protected them from their evil.

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: This is detailed in the works of Maghāzī.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh.]

---"
"17873","(17867) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq regarding the story of Tabūk. He said:

When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) reached the mountain pass (al-Thaniyyah), the announcer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) called out: ""Take the bottom of the valley, for it is wider for you, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) has taken the pass."" With him were Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yammān and ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (may Allah be pleased with them both). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) disliked that anyone should crowd him on the pass. A group of the hypocrites heard this announcement and lagged behind. Then a band of the hypocrites followed him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) heard the sound of the people behind him, so he said to one of his two companions: ""Strike their faces."" When they heard that and saw the man approaching them — and he was Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yammān — they all descended. The man began striking their mounts. They said: ""We are the companions of Aḥmad,"" while they were masked, nothing visible of them except their eyes. His companion came after the people had descended and said: ""Did you recognize the band?"" He said: ""No, by Allah, O Prophet of Allah, but I recognized their mounts."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) descended from the pass and said to his two companions: ""Do you know what the people intended? They intended to crowd me on the pass and throw me from it."" They said: ""Shall we not command us, O Messenger of Allah, so that we strike their necks when the people gather to you?"" He said: ""I dislike that the people should say that Muḥammad has set his hand upon his companions to kill them."" And he mentioned the story.

[Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Isḥāq's narration from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) is mursal.]

---"
"17874","(17868) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed him: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Baghdādī narrated to us: Abū ʿUlāthah Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Khālid narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Ibn Lahīʿah narrated from Abī al-Aswad from ʿUrwah, who said:

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was returning from Tabūk towards Madīnah, until when he was partway along the road, a group of his companions conspired against him and agreed to throw him down a cliff along the way. Then he mentioned the story with the same meaning as that of Ibn Isḥāq.

[Ḍaʿīf.]

---"
"17875","(17869) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me: My father narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr and Abū Nuʿaym both narrated to us: al-Walīd ibn Jumayʿ narrated to us: Abū al-Ṭufayl said:

There was between a man of the people of al-ʿAqabah and Ḥudhayfah some of the friction that occurs between people. The man said: ""I adjure you by Allah, how many were the companions of al-ʿAqabah?"" The people said to Ḥudhayfah: ""Inform him, since he has asked you."" He said: ""We were told that they were fourteen. If you were among them, then the company was fifteen. And I bear witness by Allah that twelve of them were at war against Allah and His Messenger in this worldly life and on the Day when the witnesses shall stand. And he excused three of them, saying: 'We did not hear the announcer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), nor did we know what the people intended. We were in the heat, and he walked and said: ""The water is scarce; let no one precede me to it."" But he found a people who had preceded him, and he cursed them that day.'""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Abī Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Zubayrī.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]

Al-Shāfiʿī said: Others among them lagged behind in his presence. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed upon him regarding the expedition of Tabūk or his return from it, reports about them, and He said: *""And had they wished to go forth, they would have made some preparation for it, but Allah disliked their going forth.""* [Al-Tawbah: 46] — reciting until His words: *""And they turn away while they are rejoicing.""* [Al-Tawbah: 50]

The Shaykh said: This is detailed in the Maghāzī of Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah and Ibn Isḥāq.

---"
"17876","(17870) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar al-Baghdādī narrated to us: Abū ʿUlāthah narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Ibn Lahīʿah narrated from Abī al-Aswad from ʿUrwah, who said:

Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prepared for an expedition, intending Shām, and he announced to the people that they should set out and commanded them to do so, during intense heat in the nights of autumn. Many people held back from it and feared the Romans. Those who hoped for reward set out, while the hypocrites lagged behind and said to themselves that he would never return. They discouraged those who obeyed them from joining him. Some Muslims also lagged behind for a matter for which they had an excuse. And he mentioned the story.

He said: Jadd ibn Qays came to him while he was sitting in the mosque with a group of his companions, and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, grant me permission to stay behind, for I have estates and a means by which I have an excuse."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Make preparations, for you are well-off; perhaps you will mount some of the daughters of al-Aṣfar (the Byzantines) behind you."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, grant me permission, but do not put me to trial with the daughters of al-Aṣfar."" So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed concerning him and his companions: *""And among them is he who says, 'Permit me and do not put me to trial.' Unquestionably, into trial they have fallen. And indeed, Hell will surround the disbelievers.""* [Al-Tawbah: 49] — ten verses, one following another.

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and the believers set out. Among those who lagged behind was Ibn ʿAnamah or ʿAnamah, from Banī ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf. It was said to him: ""What kept you back from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)?"" He said: ""Frivolity and amusement."" So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed concerning him and those hypocrites who lagged behind: *""And if you ask them, they will surely say, 'We were only conversing and playing.' Say, 'Is it Allah, His verses, and His Messenger that you were mocking?'""* [Al-Tawbah: 65] — three consecutive verses.

[Ḍaʿīf.]

---"
"17877","(17871) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl from Ibn Shihāb from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik, that ʿAbdullāh ibn Kaʿb — who was Kaʿb's guide when he became blind, from among his sons — said: I heard Kaʿb ibn Mālik narrate his account of when he lagged behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) during the expedition of Tabūk.

Kaʿb ibn Mālik said: I never lagged behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) in any expedition he undertook except the expedition of Tabūk — apart from the expedition of Badr, for Allah did not rebuke anyone for lagging behind from it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) only set out intending the caravan of Quraysh, until Allah brought them together with their enemy without any prior appointment. And I was present with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) at the Night of al-ʿAqabah, and I would not love in exchange for my presence there the witnessing of Badr, even though Badr is more widely remembered among the people.

As for my account when I lagged behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) in the expedition of Tabūk: I was never stronger nor more well-off than I was when I lagged behind from him in that expedition. By Allah, I had never before possessed two mounts at the same time until I acquired them for that expedition. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) would never intend an expedition without giving a misleading impression about another, until that expedition came. He undertook that expedition in severe heat, facing a long journey, a vast desert, and a numerous enemy. He made the matter clear to the Muslims so that they might prepare properly for their enemy, and he informed them of the direction he intended. The Muslims with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) were many — no register could encompass them — meaning the Dīwān.

Kaʿb said: No one wished to be absent but that he thought it would be hidden from him, so long as no revelation had come from Allah concerning it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) undertook that expedition when the fruits had ripened and the shade was pleasant. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and the Muslims with him made preparations. I began going out in the morning intending to make preparations with them, but I accomplished nothing. I would say to myself: ""I am capable of that whenever I wish it."" This continued to delay me until the people's earnestness became intense. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and the Muslims with him set out in the morning, and I had accomplished nothing of my preparations. I said: ""I shall make preparations after him, a day or two, and then catch up with them."" I went out after they had departed to make preparations, but I returned having accomplished nothing. Then I went out again and returned having accomplished nothing. This continued to delay me until they hastened and the campaign was well underway. I resolved to set out and overtake them — would that I had done so! — but it was not decreed for me. Whenever I went out among the people after the departure of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and moved among them, it grieved me that I saw only a man suspected of hypocrisy or a man whom Allah had excused among the weak. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) did not mention me until he reached Tabūk. He was sitting among the people at Tabūk and said: ""What has happened to Kaʿb?"" A man from Banī Salamah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, his two cloaks have detained him; he is looking at his sides."" Muʿādh ibn Jabal said: ""Evil is what you have said! By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we know nothing but good of him."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) remained silent.

Kaʿb said: When it reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was heading back from Tabīnah, my concern became urgent, and I began to think of lies and say: ""How shall I escape his anger tomorrow?"" And I sought the help of every person of sound judgment among my family in this regard. When it was said that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was approaching, having arrived, falsehood departed from me, and I knew that I would never escape from it by anything involving a lie. So I resolved upon truthfulness.

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) arrived in the morning. Whenever he arrived from a journey, he would begin with the mosque, praying two rakʿahs in it, and then sit for the people. When he did that, the ones who had lagged behind came to him and began making excuses to him and swearing oaths. They were eighty-three men. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) accepted their public statements, took their pledge of allegiance, sought forgiveness for them, and entrusted their secrets to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.

I came to him, and when I greeted him, he smiled the smile of one who is angry, and then said: ""Come."" I came walking until I sat before him. He said: ""What kept you back? Had you not purchased your mount?"" I said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah. By Allah, had I sat before anyone other than you among the people of the world, I would have thought that I could escape his anger by means of an excuse. I have been given eloquence. But by Allah, I knew that if I told you today a lie by which you might be pleased with me, Allah would soon make you angry with me. And if I told you the truth, you would be upset with me regarding it — yet I hope for Allah's forgiveness. By Allah, I have no excuse. By Allah, I was never stronger nor more well-off than when I lagged behind from you."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""As for this one, he has spoken the truth. Rise until Allah decides concerning you.""

I rose, and a group of men from Banī Salamah went to me and said: ""By Allah, we did not know that you had committed any sin before this. Why were you unable to present an excuse to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) as those who lagged behind presented excuses to him? The forgiveness of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) for you would have sufficed for your sin."" By Allah, they continued to reproach me until I resolved to go back and lie about myself. Then I said: ""Has anyone else met with this?"" They said: ""Yes, two men who said the same as you said, and the same was said to them as was said to you."" I said: ""Who are they?"" They said: ""Murārah ibn al-Rabīʿ al-ʿAmrī and Hilāl ibn Umayyah al-Wāqifī."" They mentioned to me two righteous men who had witnessed Badr — in them I found an example. I became confirmed in this when they mentioned them to me.

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade the people from speaking to the three of us among those who had lagged behind. The people avoided us and changed towards me, until the earth became unfamiliar to me in my own eyes — it was not the earth I knew. We remained in that state for fifty nights. As for my two companions, they became subdued and sat in their houses. As for me, I was the youngest and most vigorous of the people. I would go out and attend the prayer with the Muslims, and I would walk through the markets, and no one would speak to me. I would come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) while he was in his gathering after the prayer, and I would greet him. I would say to myself: ""Has he moved his lips in returning my greeting or not?"" Then I would pray, and I would steal glances at him. When I turned to my prayer, he would look at me. When I turned towards him, he would turn away from me. When the harshness of the Muslims became prolonged for me, I scaled the wall of the garden of Abū Qatādah — who was my cousin and the dearest of people to me — and greeted him. By Allah, he did not return my greeting. I said to him: ""O Abū Qatādah, I adjure you by Allah — do you not know that I love Allah and His Messenger?"" He was silent. I repeated it to him and adjured him, and he was silent. I adjured him a third time, and he said: ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" My eyes overflowed, and I turned away until I had scaled the wall.

Kaʿb said: While I was walking in the market of Madīnah, a Nabataean from the Nabataeans of Shām — one who had brought food to sell in Madīnah — was saying: ""Who will direct me to Kaʿb ibn Mālik?"" The people began pointing to me until he came to me and handed me a letter from the king of Ghassān. I was literate, so I read it. In it was: ""To proceed: It has reached me that your companion has treated you harshly. Allah has not placed you in a place of humiliation nor of loss. Join us, and we shall console you."" When I read it, I said: ""This too is a trial."" I made my way to a baking oven and burned it with the letter.

When forty nights of the fifty had passed, the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) came to me and said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) commands you to separate from your wife."" I said: ""Shall I divorce her, or what shall I do with her?"" He said: ""No, rather separate from her and do not approach her."" He sent the same message to my two companions. I said to my wife: ""Go to your family and remain with them until Allah decides this matter.""

Kaʿb said: Then the wife of Hilāl ibn Umayyah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, Hilāl ibn Umayyah is an old, helpless man who has no servant. Would you dislike that I serve him?"" He said: ""No, but let him not approach you."" She said: ""By Allah, he has no energy for anything, and he has not ceased weeping since his affair began until this day."" Some of my family said to me: ""Why do you not seek permission from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and your family) regarding your wife, as he permitted the wife of Hilāl ibn Umayyah to serve him?"" I said: ""By Allah, I will not seek permission from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) regarding her, for I do not know what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) would say to me if I sought his permission regarding her, and I am a young man.""

I remained after that for ten nights, until fifty nights were complete from the time the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade the people from speaking to us. When I had prayed the Fajr prayer on the morning of the fifty nights, while I was on the roof of one of our houses, and I was sitting in the state that Allah has described of us — my soul had become constrained for me, and the earth had become narrow for me despite its vastness — I heard the voice of a caller who had ascended the peak of the mountain of Salʿ: ""O Kaʿb ibn Mālik, receive glad tidings!"" I fell prostrate, and I knew that relief had come. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had given permission for Allah's repentance to be accepted for us when he prayed the Fajr prayer. The people went out giving me glad tidings. Messengers of glad tidings went out to my two companions. A man galloped a horse towards me, and a runner from Aslam ascended the mountain. The voice reached me before the horse. When the one whose voice I had heard came to me giving me glad tidings, I took off my two garments and clothed him with them as a token of glad tidings. By Allah, I possessed nothing else that day. I borrowed two garments, wore them, and set out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). The people met me in waves, congratulating me on the repentance, saying: ""May you receive the glad tidings of Allah's repentance upon you."" Until I entered the mosque. Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh came to me running until he shook my hand and congratulated me. No man from the Muhājirūn came to me other than him, and I have not forgotten that for Ṭalḥah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said — and his face was beaming with joy: ""Receive glad tidings of the best day that has come upon you since your mother bore you."" I said: ""Is it from you, O Messenger of Allah, or from Allah?"" He said: ""No, rather from Allah, the Blessed and Most High.""

Whenever the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) received glad tidings, his face would shine as if it were a piece of the moon, and that was recognized in him. When I sat before him, I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, part of my repentance is that I divest myself of my wealth as charity to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and to His Messenger."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Retain some of your wealth, for it is better for you."" I said: ""Then I shall retain my share that is in Khaybar."" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what saved me was truthfulness, and part of my repentance is that I shall not speak anything but truth for as long as I live. By Allah, I do not know any Muslim whom Allah has tested in truthfulness of speech — since I said that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) — who was tested more severely than I was tested. I have not deliberately lied since I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) until this day. And I hope that Allah will preserve me in what remains.""

So Allah revealed upon His Messenger: *""Certainly, Allah has accepted repentance from the Prophet, the Muhājirūn, and the Anṣār who followed him in the hour of difficulty, after the hearts of a party among them had almost inclined [to doubt], then He accepted their repentance. Indeed, He is Kind and Merciful to them. And [He also accepted repentance] upon the three who were left behind, until when the earth closed in on them despite its vastness, and their souls closed in on them, and they thought that there was no refuge from Allah except in Him, then He accepted their repentance so that they might repent. Indeed, Allah is the Accepting of Repentance, the Merciful. O you who have believed, fear Allah and be with the truthful.""* [Al-Tawbah: 117–119]

By Allah, Allah has not blessed me with any blessing after He guided me to Islam that is greater in my sight than my truthfulness to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) on that day, that I did not lie to him and thus perish as those who lied to him perished. For Allah said to those who lied to him when the revelation came, the worst thing that could be said to anyone. Allah, the Blessed and Most High, said: *""They will swear to you by Allah when you return to them, so that you may turn away from them. So turn away from them, for they is impure, and their refuge is Hell — a recompense for what they used to earn. They will swear to you that you may be pleased with them. But if you are pleased with them, indeed, Allah is not pleased with the defiantly disobedient.""* [Al-Tawbah: 95–96]

Kaʿb said: We three lagged behind from the matter of those from whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) accepted their excuses when they swore oaths to him, and he took their pledge of allegiance and sought forgiveness for them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) deferred our affair until Allah decided concerning it. Thus Allah, the Blessed and Most High, said: *""And upon the three who were left behind.""* [Al-Tawbah: 118] What Allah mentioned is not our lagging behind from the expedition; rather, it is His deferring our affair and His postponing our matter from those who swore oaths and made excuses, and from whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) accepted it.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh.]

---"
"17878","(17872) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb al-Ṭūsī narrated to us: Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī narrated to us: Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar informed me: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him) that:

A group of hypocrites in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) would, whenever the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) went out on an expedition, lag behind from him and rejoice in their sitting behind in opposition to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) returned, they would make excuses to him and swear oaths, and they loved to be praised for what they had not done. So the following was revealed concerning them: *""Let not those who rejoice in what they have done and love to be praised for what they not done — think that they will escape the punishment.""* [Āl ʿImrān: 188]

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam, and Muslim narrated it from al-Ḥulwānī and Ibn ʿAskar from Ibn Abī Maryam.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh.]

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed to His Messenger (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) their secrets and the reports of those who listened to them, and their desire to tempt those who were with him through lies, rumours, and discouragement. He informed that He disliked their going forth, since they were upon this intention. In this is evidence that Allah — exalted is His praise — commanded that those who are known for what they are known for should be prevented from joining the Muslims in expedition, for they are a harm to them. Then He further emphasized the clarification of this by His words: *""Those who were left behind rejoiced in their sitting in opposition to the Messenger of Allah.""* [Al-Tawbah: 81] — reciting until His words: *""So sit with those who remain behind.""* [Al-Tawbah: 83]

---"
"17879","(17873) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī narrated to us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Dallawayh al-Daqqāq narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Azhar ibn Manīʿ narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said:

""Indeed, Allah supports the religion even through a wicked man.""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Razzāq.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh.]

---"
"17880","(17874) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Muʿtamir narrated from ʿImrān ibn Ḥudayr from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUbayd, who said: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said:

""We seek the strength of the hypocrites, while their sin is upon them.""

This is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected). If it is authentic, it pertains only to hypocrites who were not known for discouragement and spreading rumours. And Allah knows best.

[Ḍaʿīf. ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUbayd is among the younger Tābiʿūn and is unknown in status.]"
"17881","(17875) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bashrān informed us that Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us that Ibn Numayr narrated from al-Aʿmash from Salamah ibn Kuhayl from Ḥabbah ibn Juwayn, who said: We were with Salmān (may Allah be pleased with him) in a military expedition, and we were drawn up in battle formation facing the enemy. He said: ""Who are these?"" They said: ""The polytheists."" He said: ""Who are these?"" They said: ""The believers."" Ḥabbah ibn Juwayn said: Salmān (may Allah be pleased with him) then said: ""These are the polytheists, and these are the believers, and the hypocrites. Allah strengthens the believers through the might of the hypocrites, and Allah grants victory to the hypocrites through the supplication of the believers."" [Weak. Ḥabbah ibn Juwayn is weak in ḥadīth.]"
"17882","(17876) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Ushnānī informed us that Abū al-Ḥasan al-Ṭarāʾifī narrated to us that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Bashshār al-ʿAbdī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar, meaning Ghundar, narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Murrah from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salamah from Ḥudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""You will indeed be aided in your military expeditions by the hypocrites."""
"17883","(17877) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakim informed us that Ibn Wahb informed us that Mālik ibn Anas informed me from al-Fuḍayl ibn Abī ʿAbd Allāh from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Niyār from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) set out towards Badr, and when he reached the Ḥarrah of al-Wabarah, a man overtook him who was known for his bravery and valour. The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) rejoiced when they saw him. When he caught up with him, he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have come to follow you and to obtain a share of the spoils with you."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to him: ""Do you believe in Allah and His Messenger?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Then go back, for I will not seek the aid of a polytheist."" He said: Then he proceeded on until, when he reached the tree, the man overtook him again and said to him what he had said the first time. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to him what he had said the first time. He said: ""No."" He said: ""Then go back, for I will not seek the aid of a polytheist."" She said: He went back. Then he overtook him at al-Baydāʾ and said to him what he had said the first time: ""Do you believe in Allah and His Messenger?"" He said: ""Yes."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Then go along."" Muslim narrated this in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Ṭāhir from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Perhaps he turned him away in the hope of his accepting Islam, and this is within the discretion of the Imam. He (the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him and his family) fought against the Jews of Banū Qaynuqāʿ after Badr, and Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah accompanied him at Ḥunayn after the Conquest, while Ṣafwān was a polytheist.

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: As for Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah's accompanying him at Ḥunayn while Ṣafwān was a polytheist, it is well-known among the scholars of maghāzī, and it has been transmitted with its chain of narration. As for his fighting with the Jews of Banū Qaynuqāʿ, I have not found it except in the ḥadīth of al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah, who is weak, from al-Ḥakim from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sought the aid of the Jews of Qaynuqāʿ and gave them a gratuity but did not assign them a share of the spoils."
"17884","(17878) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī narrated to us that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us that Yūsuf ibn ʿAmr al-Marwazī narrated to us that al-Faḍl ibn Mūsā al-Sīnānī narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr from Saʿīd ibn al-Mundhir from Abī Ḥumayd al-Sāʿidī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) set out until, when he had passed Thaniyyat al-Wadāʿ, he saw a military detachment. He said: ""Who are they?"" They said: ""Banū Qaynuqāʿ, and they are the clan of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām."" He said: ""Have they embraced Islam?"" They said: ""No, rather they are upon their religion."" He said: ""Tell them to go back, for we do not seek the aid of polytheists."" This chain of narration is more authentic. [Weak.]"
"17885","(17879) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Mukram ibn Aḥmad al-Qāḍī narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rūḥ al-Madāʾinī narrated to us that Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us that al-Mustalim ibn Saʿīd al-Thaqafī narrated from Khubayb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from his father from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) set out on one of his military expeditions. I came to him, along with another man, before we had embraced Islam. We said: ""We are ashamed that our people should witness a battle scene and we not witness it."" He said: ""Have you two embraced Islam?"" We said: ""No."" He said: ""We do not seek the aid of polytheists against polytheists."" So we embraced Islam and witnessed the battle with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). I killed a man, and a man struck me a blow. I married his daughter, and she used to say: ""May you never lose the man who adorned you with this necklace."" I would say: ""May you never lose the man who hastened your father to the Fire."" [Weak]

His grandfather is Khubayb ibn Yasāf, also said to be Isāf, and he has companionship (ṣuḥbah)."
"17886","(17880) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Zuhayr narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hāshim narrated from Wakīʿ from al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ from al-Shaybānī that Saʿd ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) went on a military expedition with a group of Jews and gave them a gratuity. [Weak. All its narrators are trustworthy except that al-Shaybānī's report from Saʿd ibn Mālik is mursal (disconnected).]"
"17887","(17881) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prepared for war and undertook what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, had commanded him of waging jihad against His enemy and fighting those whom He had commanded him to fight among the polytheists of the Arabs who were near to him. [Weak]

Al-Shāfiʿī said: If the condition of the enemy varies, some being more devastating than others or more feared than others, then the Imam should begin with the enemy that is more feared or more devastating, even if their territory is farther away, if Allah wills. This would be considered a matter of necessity.

He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) received news that al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Ḍirār was gathering forces against him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) launched a surprise attack upon him at night, even though there was an enemy closer to him than al-Ḥārith."
"17888","(17882) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyya ibn Ḥibbān, ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) received word that Banū al-Muṣṭaliq were gathering forces against him, and their leader was al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Ḍirār, the father of Juwayriyyah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) set out until he arrived at al-Muraysīʿ, a well belonging to Banū al-Muṣṭaliq. They prepared for battle against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). The people advanced towards one another and fought. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) defeated Banū al-Muṣṭaliq, killing those of them whom he killed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and their family) granted their children, their wealth, and their women as spoils, and he encamped at the area from the direction of Qudayd towards the coast.

Ibn Isḥāq said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) conducted this expedition in Shaʿbān of the sixth year. [Weak]"
"17889","(17883) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah and Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī both informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us that Sulaym ibn Akhḍar narrated from Ibn ʿAwn, who said: I wrote to Nāfiʾ asking him about supplication before fighting. He wrote back: ""That was only in the early days of Islam. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) launched a surprise attack upon Banū al-Muṣṭaliq while they were unaware and their livestock were being watered at the well. He killed their fighters and captured their women and children. On that day he obtained—I believe he said—Juwayriyyah, the daughter of al-Ḥārith."" ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated this ḥadīth to me, and he was in that army. Muslim narrated this in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: And it reached him that Khālid ibn Sufyān ibn Nubayḥ was gathering forces against him, so he sent Ibn Unays, who killed him, even though there was an enemy closer to him than Khālid. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
"17890","(17884) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, Abū Maʿmar, narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar from Ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Unays from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent me to Khālid ibn Sufyān al-Hudhalī, who was in the direction of ʿUranah and ʿArafāt. He said: ""Go and kill him."" He said: I saw him, and the time for the ʿAṣr prayer had arrived. I said: ""I fear that there may be something between me and him that would cause me to delay the prayer."" So I set out walking, praying with gestures towards him. When I drew near to him, he said to me: ""Who are you?"" I said: ""A man from the Arabs. It reached me that you are gathering forces against this man, so I came to you regarding that."" He said: ""Indeed, I am doing that."" I walked with him for a while, and when I found an opportunity, I struck him with my sword until he was killed. [Weak]"
"17891","(17885) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd Hishām ibn ʿAbd al-Malik narrated to us: Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: al-Layth ibn Saʿd informed me, from Ayyūb ibn Mūsá al-Qurashī, from Makḥūl, from Sharḥbīl, from Salmān al-Fārisī (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Whoever performs ribāṭ (guards the frontier) for one day and one night in the cause of Allah, it is for him the reward of fasting for an entire month and standing in prayer during its nights. And whoever dies while performing ribāṭ, the reward continues to flow for him, his provision is sustained upon him, and he is protected from the trial of the grave (al-Fattān).""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abī al-Walīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"17892","(17886) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: Muḥammad informed us: Ibn Wahb narrated to me: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sharīḥ narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Ḥārith, from Abī ʿUbaydah ibn ʿUqbah, from Sharḥbīl ibn al-Simṭ, from Salmān al-Khayr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), a hadith similar to it.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Ṭāhir from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"17893","(17887) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed us: My father narrated to me: Hāshim ibn al-Qāsim narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār narrated to us, from Abī Ḥāzim, from Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Ribāṭ (guarding the frontier) for one day in the cause of Allah is better than the world and all that is upon it. And the morning excursion (al-ghadwah) or the evening excursion (al-rūḥah) that a servant undertakes in the cause of Allah is better than the world and all that is upon it. And the place of the whip of any one of you in Paradise is better than the world and all that is upon it.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Munīr from Abī al-Naḍr Hāshim. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"17894","(17888) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us: ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us: Abū ʿAqīl Zuhrah ibn Maʿbad narrated to us, from Abī Ṣāliḥ, the freedman of ʿUthmān, who said: I heard ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) say from the pulpit: ""Indeed, I had concealed from you a hadith that I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), out of fear that you would disperse from me. Then it occurred to me to narrate it to you, so that each person among you may choose for himself what seems right to him."" I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""Ribāṭ (guarding the frontier) for one day in the cause of Allah is better than a thousand days in all other stations."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17895","(17889) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us by dictation, and Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī informed us by recitation, both saying: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥarashī informed us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated to us, from his father, from Sahl ibn Saʿd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) came to us while we were digging the trench and carrying the earth upon our shoulders. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""O Allah, there is no life except the life of the Hereafter. So forgive the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Qutaybah and others from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"17896","(17890) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Mihrān narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ṣuhayb narrated to us, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Muhājirūn and the Anṣār were digging the trench around Madīnah, carrying the earth upon their backs, and saying:

""We are those who pledged allegiance to Muḥammad,
Upon Islam, for as long as we remain.""

And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) would respond to them, saying:

""O Allah, there is no good except the good of the Hereafter.
So bless the Anṣār and the Muhājirūn.""

He (Anas) said: They would be brought two scoops of barley, and a thick, pungent stew would be prepared for them — it was harsh in the throat and had an unpleasant smell — and it would be placed before the people.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Maʿmar from ʿAbd al-Wārith. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"17897","(17891) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭūsī informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Munīb narrated to us: Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Jarīr narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abī Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: ""Allah has guaranteed the one who goes out in His cause — not driving him out except faith in Him and belief in His Messenger — that He will admit him into Paradise, or return him to his home from which he departed, having attained whatever reward or spoils of war he obtained. By the One in whose Hand is my soul, were it not that I would place hardship upon my Ummah, I would never remain behind from any raiding party that goes out in the cause of Allah.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Jarīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"17898","(17892) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj: Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""There will continue to be a group from my Ummah who fight upon the truth, prevailing, until the Day of Resurrection.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn ʿAbd Allāh and others from Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"17899","(17893) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from al-Ḥakam, from Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd, from Saʿd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) left ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) behind during the expedition of Tabūk. ʿAlī said: ""O Messenger of Allah, are you leaving me behind with the women and children?"" He said: ""Are you not pleased that you have the same station with me as Hārūn had with Mūsā, except that there is no prophet after me?""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"17900","(17894) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam informed us: al-Dārawardī narrated to me: Khuthaym ibn ʿIrāk ibn Mālik narrated to me, from his father, from Abī Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) set out for Khaybar and appointed Sibāʿ ibn ʿUrfuṭah as his deputy over Madīnah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17901","(17895) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus narrated from Ibn Isḥāq: al-Zuhrī narrated to me from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUtbah from Ibn ʿAbbāsh (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) set out on his journey to Makkah during the Year of the Conquest and appointed Abū Ruhm Kulthum ibn al-Ḥuṣayn ibn ʿUbayd ibn Khalaf al-Ghifārī as his deputy over Madīnah. [Weak]"
"17902","(17896) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʤAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from Yazīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, the freed slave of al-Mahriyy, from his father, from Abū Saʿīd (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) sent a delegation to Banī Liḥyān and said: ""Let one man out of every two go forth."" Then he said to those who remained behind: ""Whoever stays behind to look after the family and property of the one who goes forth, doing so in a good manner, shall have for him a reward equal to half the reward of the one who goes forth."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"17903","(17897) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Rūḥ narrated to us: Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Ḥarb ibn Shaddād narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr: Abū Saʿīd, the freed slave of al-Mahriyy, narrated to me from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) sent an expedition to Banī Liḥyān from Hudhayl and said: ""Let one of every two men set out, and the reward shall be shared between them."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ through various chains from Yaḥyā, and from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Wārith from Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"17904","(17898) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed us: Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād al-Makkī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd informed us — both saying: Ḥātim narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd, who said: I heard Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I went on seven military expeditions with the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), and I went out among the detachments he sent forth on seven occasions. Once our commander was Abū Bakr, and once our commander was Usāmah ibn Zayd. The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Qutaybah, and Muḥammad said in the second narration: ""nine expeditions."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Agreed upon]

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī and Muslim in their Ṣaḥīḥs from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād al-Makkī."
"17905","(17899) And Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Isfarāʾinī informed us therein: Abū ʿAmr Ismāʿīl ibn Nujayd al-Sulamī informed us: Abū Muslim al-Kajjī informed us: Abū ʿĀṣim narrated from Yazīd from Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I went on seven military expeditions with the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and nine expeditions with Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, who was appointed as our commander. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū ʿĀṣim. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Agreed upon]"
"17906","(17900) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us by dictation, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us by recitation, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from al-Mundhir ibn Thaʿlabah from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) sent ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ at the head of a raiding party in which were Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both). When they reached the place of battle, ʿAmr ordered them not to light any fire. ʿUmar became angry and was about to confront him, but Abū Bakr restrained him and informed him that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) had only appointed him as commander over you because of his knowledge of warfare. So ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) desisted. [Ḥasan due to supporting narrations]"
"17907","(17901) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr informed us: Muʿādh ibn Hishām narrated to us: My father narrated from Qatādah from Abū al-Malīḥ, that ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād visited Maʿqil ibn Yasār (may Allah be pleased with him) during his illness. Maʿqil (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: ""I am going to narrate to you a ḥadīth; were it not that I am in the throes of death, I would not narrate it to you. I heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) say: 'There is no leader who is in charge of the affairs of the Muslims and does not strive on their behalf and act sincerely towards them, but he shall not enter Paradise with them.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Ghassān and others from Muʿādh ibn Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Agreed upon]"
"17908","(17902) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us: Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Imām informed us: Shaybān ibn Farrūkh informed us: Abū al-Ashhab narrated from al-Ḥasan, who said: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād visited Maʿqil ibn Yasār al-Muzanī during the illness from which he died. Maʿqil said: ""I am going to narrate to you a ḥadīth which I heard from the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). Had I known that I would remain alive, I would not narrate it to you. I heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) say: 'There is no servant whom Allah entrusts with a flock, and who dies on the day he dies while having been deceitful towards his flock, but Allah has made Paradise forbidden for him.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Agreed upon]

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān ibn Farrūkh, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Abū Nuʿaym from Abū al-Ashhab. And it has been narrated to us in the authentic ḥadīth from Sulaymān ibn Buraydah from his father, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) appointed a commander over a raiding party or an army, he would counsel him to fear Allah in his private affairs and to treat the Muslims who were with him well."
"17909","(17903) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Isḥāq from his father, who said: We were with Jarīr ibn ʿAbdillāh on a military expedition when severe hunger afflicted us. So Jarīr wrote to Muʿāwiyah (may Allah be pleased with them both), saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) say: ""Whoever does not show mercy to people, Allah will not show mercy to him."" He said: Muʿāwiyah wrote ordering them to return, and he provided them with provisions. Abū Isḥāq said: I myself obtained a piece of cloth from what he provided them with. [Ṣaḥīḥ — without the story]"
"17910","(17904) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us by dictation: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī in Makkah narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, who said: Jarīr ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) say: ""Allah will not show mercy to the one who does not show mercy to people."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Agreed upon]"
"17911","(17905) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr ibn al-Ḥakam ibn Ḥabīb ibn Mihrān al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Abū Qābūs, the freedman of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The Merciful shows mercy to those who are merciful. Show mercy to those on earth, and the One in the heavens will show mercy to you."" [Ṣaḥīh in other chains]"
"17912","(17906) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiya narrated from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, from Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) appointed a man from Banī Asad over a task. When the man came to receive his instructions, ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought one of his children, so he kissed him. The man said: ""Do you kiss this one? I have never kissed a child at all!"" ʿUmar said: ""Then you are even less merciful toward people. Give us our instructions — you shall never work for me again."" [Ḥasan]"
"17913","(17907) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ narrated to us; Mahdī ibn Maymūn narrated to us; Saʿīd al-Jurayrī narrated from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Firās, who said: I witnessed ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) addressing the people in a sermon, and he said: ""O people, there has come upon me a time when I see that whoever recites the Qurʾān intends by it Allah and what is with Him, yet it occurs to me that there will come a people who recite it seeking the pleasure of people and desiring the world through it. So intend Allah by your recitation! Intend Allah by your deeds! Indeed, we used to know you when revelation was descending and when the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was among us, and when Allah informed us of your news. But revelation has ceased and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) has passed on. We now know you only by what I say to you: whoever among us we see doing good, we think well of him and love him for it; and whoever among us we see doing evil, we think ill of him and despise him for it. Your secrets are between you and your Lord. Indeed, I send my governors only to teach you your religion and to teach you your Sunnah — I do not send them to strike your backs or to seize your wealth. So if anything of that raises suspicion in anyone, let him bring it to me. By the One in whose hand is the soul of ʿUmar, I shall exact retribution from him."" Then ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ stood and said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, if you send a governor from among your governors and he disciplines a man from among his subjects by striking him, will you exact retribution from him?"" He said: ""Yes, by the One in whose hand is the soul of ʿUmar, I shall exact retribution from him. I have seen the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) exact retribution from himself. Do not strike the Muslims and thereby humiliate them; do not withhold their rights and thereby drive them to disbelief; do not confine them and thereby put them to trial; and do not settle them in the thickets and thereby cause them to perish."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17914","(17908) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; al-Thaqafī informed me from Ḥumayd, from Mūsā ibn Anas, from Anas ibn Mālik, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) asked him: ""When you besiege a city, what do you do?"" He said: ""We send a man toward the city and make for him a shield of leather."" He said: ""What if he is struck by a stone?"" He said: ""Then he is killed."" He said: ""Do not do that! By the One in whose hand is my soul, it would not please me to conquer a city containing four thousand fighters at the cost of losing a single Muslim."" [Ṣaḥīh]"
"17915","(17909) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram and Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us, saying: Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Muṭarrif narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, who said: A year of drought befell the people, and ghee became expensive. ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) would eat olive oil, and his stomach would gurgle. In the narration of Yaḥyā, it is said: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to eat it, but when it became scarce, he said: ""I shall not eat it until the people eat it."" He used to eat olive oil, and his stomach would gurgle. Ibn Mukram said in his narration: ʿUmar said: ""Gurgle as much as you wish! By Allah, you shall not eat ghee until the people eat it."" Then he said to me: ""Remove its heat with fire for me."" So I would cook it for him, and he would eat it. [Ṣaḥīh]"
"17916","(17910) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from Ṭāwūs and ʿIkrimah ibn Khālid, that Ḥafṣah, Ibn Muṭīʿ, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar spoke to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: ""If you were to eat wholesome food, it would give you greater strength for upholding the truth."" He said: ""Is this the opinion of all of you?"" They said: ""Yes."" He said: ""I know that there is none among you but a sincere advisor, but I have left my two Companions upon their path. If I were to abandon their path, I would not find them at the final abode."" He said: ""A year of drought befelt the people, and he ate neither ghee nor fat meat until the people recovered."" [Ṣaḥīh]"
"17917","(17911) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafī narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh, who is Ibn Yazīd al-Hudhalī, narrated to us. He said: I heard al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd say: When the year of al-Ramādah came, the people were afflicted with severe hunger. One day, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) rode his mount and saw a grain of barley in its dung. He said: ""By Allah, I shall not ride it until the condition of the people improves."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17918","(17912) It has been narrated to us from Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī that ʿUtbah ibn Farqad sent to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) from Azerbaijan a sweet dish (khabīṣ). ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Are the Muslims satisfied in their camps from this?"" The messenger said: ""No."" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I do not want it."" He wrote to ʿUtbah: ""To proceed: This is not from your toil, nor from the toil of your father, nor from the toil of your mother. So satisfy the Muslims in your vicinity from what satisfies you in your own camp."" Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Aḥmad al-Jurjānī informed us; Abū Yaʿlā al-Mawṣilī narrated to us; Abū Khaythamah narrated to us; Jarīr narrated from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, from Abū ʿUthmān, and he mentioned it. [Ḥasan]"
"17919","(17913) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Maslamah al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us; my father narrated to me, saying: I heard Ḥarmalah al-Miṣrī narrating from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Shumāsah, who said: I entered upon ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) and she said: ""From whom are you?"" I said: ""From the people of Egypt."" She said: ""How did you find Ibn Ḥudayj in your campaign?"" I said: ""The best of commanders. If any man among us lost a horse or a camel, he would replace it with a camel, and if any man among us lost a servant, he would replace him with a servant."" She said: ""His killing my brother does not prevent me from narrating to you what I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: 'O Allah, whoever is given charge of any affair of my Ummah and is gentle with them, be gentle with him; and whoever is harsh with them, be harsh with him.'""

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥassān al-ʿAṭṭār narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us; Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated, saying: I heard Ḥarmalah al-Ḥaḍramī narrating from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Shumāsah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), a similar narration.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī. [Ṣaḥīh — Muslim]"
"17920","(17914) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us by dictation; Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us in Makkah; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Abū al-ʿAbbās, from ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said — meaning when he besieged the people of al-Ṭāʾif and gained nothing from them: ""We shall depart tomorrow, if Allah wills."" The Muslims said: ""How can we leave without having conquered?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Then go forth to battle in the morning."" So they went forth against them in the morning and were afflicted with wounds. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""We shall depart tomorrow."" That pleased them, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) smiled.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīh — agreed upon]"
"17921","(17915) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us, saying: Sulaymān — meaning ibn al-Mughīrah — narrated from Thābit, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), who mentioned something from the story of Badr. He said: ""The polytheists drew near, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'Rise towards a Paradise whose width is as vast as the heavens and the earth.'""

ʿUmair ibn al-Ḥumām al-Anṣārī said: ""O Messenger of Allah, its width is as vast as the heavens and the earth?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Bakh! Bakh!""

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""What compels you to say 'Bakh! Bakh!'?"" He said: ""By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, nothing but the hope that I may be among its people."" He said: ""Then you are among its people."" He said: ""So he took out some dates from his quiver and began eating from them. Then he said: 'If I were to live until I finish eating these dates of mine, it would indeed be a long life.' He said: ""So he threw away whatever dates he had with him, then fought them until he was killed.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī al-Naḍr and others, from Abū al-Naḍr. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"17922","(17916) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, saying: Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us, saying: Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr, who heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""A man said to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) on the day of Uḥud: 'What do you think, O Messenger of Allah, if I am killed — where will I be?' He said: 'In Paradise.' So he threw down the dates that were in his hand, then fought until he was killed.""

They both (al-Bukhārī and Muslim) narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"17923","(17917) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, saying: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbdullāh ibn Bakr narrated to us, saying: Ḥumayd narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that al-Naḍr ibn Anas, the uncle of Anas ibn Mālik, was absent from the battle of Badr. When he arrived, he said: ""I was absent from the first battle that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) fought against the polytheists. If Allah grants me a battle, Allah will see what I shall do.""

When the day of Uḥud came, the Muslims were exposed, so he said: ""O Allah, I dissociate myself from what these have brought"" — meaning the polytheists — ""and I seek Your forgiveness for what these have done"" — meaning the Muslims. Then he walked forward with his sword. Saʿd ibn Muʿadh met him and he said: ""O Saʿd, by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I smell the fragrance of Paradise beyond Uḥud — ah, how sweet is the fragrance of Paradise!""

Saʿd said: ""I could not do, O Messenger of Allah, what he did."" We found him among the slain, with eighty-some wounds — from sword strikes, spear thrusts, and arrow shots — and they had mutilated him, until his sister recognized him by his fingertips.

Anas said: ""We used to say that this verse was revealed about him and his companions: { Among the believers are men who were true to their covenant with Allah } [al-Aḥzāb 23]."" Thus it is in my book, but the correct version is Anas ibn al-Naḍr. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ through various chains from Ḥumayd, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Thābit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him). [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"17924","(17918) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-ḤāfiẒ informed me, saying: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Khaṭṭāb ibn ʿUmar al-Anṣārī narrated to us in Baghdad, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Shabīb al-Maʿmarī narrated to us by dictation, saying: Hudbah ibn Khālid narrated to us, saying: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from ʿAlī ibn Zayd and Thābit, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was left alone on the day of Uḥud with seven men from the Anṣār and two men from Quraysh. When the enemy closed in on him, he said: ""Who will repel them from me, and for him is Paradise, or he will be my companion in Paradise?"" A man from the Anṣār stepped forward and fought until he was killed. Then they closed in on him again, and he said: ""Who will repel them from me, and for him is Paradise, or he will be my companion in Paradise?"" A man from the Anṣār stepped forward and fought until he was killed. This continued until all seven were killed. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to his two companions: ""We have not been just to our companions.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥaddāb ibn Khālid. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"17925","(17919) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, saying: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, saying: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, saying: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUthmān informed us, saying: ʿAbdullāh — meaning ibn al-Mubārak — informed us, saying: ʿUbaydullāh ibn al-Wāziʿ narrated: I heard Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī narrate from some of the sons of Anas ibn Mālik. ʿUbaydullāh said: I think he meant Thumāmah ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Anas, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I passed by Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās on the day of al-Yamāmah while he was applying his embalming perfume (taḥannuṭ). I said: 'O uncle, do not you see what the Muslims are facing — and you are here?' He smiled and then said: 'Now, O son of my brother.' He put on his armor, mounted his horse, and went to the ranks. He said: 'Uff to them and to what they are doing!' And to the enemy he said: 'Uff to them and to what they worship! Leave his way' — or he said: 'the way of his horse' — until he reached the heat of battle. He charged and fought until he was killed."" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains]"
"17926","(17920) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us, saying: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, saying: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, saying: Ibn ʿUthmān informed us, saying: ʿAbdullāh informed us, saying: Jaʿfar ibn Sulaymān narrated from Thābit al-Bunānī that ʿIkrimah ibn Abī Jahl dismounted from his mount on a certain day to fight on foot. Khālid ibn al-Walīd said to him: ""Do not do that, for your death would be grievous upon the Muslims."" He said: ""Leave me, O Khālid, for you had precedence with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), while my father and I were among the fiercest of people against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."" Then he walked forward until he was killed."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17927","(17921) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn al-Jahm narrated to us, saying: Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad al-Aʿwar informed me, saying: al-Sarī ibn Yaḥyā narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn that the Muslims came to a walled garden whose gate was closed, and inside it were men from among the polytheists. Barāʾ ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) sat upon a shield and said: ""Lift me up with your spears and throw me towards them."" So they lifted him with their spears and threw him over the wall. When they reached him, he had already killed ten of them."" [Ḥasan]"
"17928","(17922) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, saying: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Imām narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, saying: Jaʿfar ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿImrān al-Jawnī narrated from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Mūsā, from his father, that he was in the presence of the enemy. He said: ""I heard him say: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""Paradise is beneath the shadows of the swords.""'"" A man of wretched appearance stood up and said: ""O Abā Mūsā, did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say this?"" He said: ""Yes, by Allah."" He returned to his companions, bid them farewell, then broke the scabbard of his sword, charged at the enemy, and fought until he was killed."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"17929","(17923) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us, saying: Saʿid ibn ʿĀmir narrated from Shuʿbah, from Sulaymān, from Abī Wāʾil, who said: Ḥudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) said regarding the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: { And do not cast yourselves into destruction with your own hands } [al-Baqarah 195] — ""It was revealed concerning spending (al-nafaqah)."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Naḍr ibn Shuʿmayl, from Shuʿbah. Others narrated from al-Aʿmash regarding this, saying: ""It is the abandonment of spending in the way of Allah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"17930","(17924) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn al-Faḍl al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us, saying: Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us, saying: Shaybān narrated from Manṣūr ibn al-Muʿtamir, from Abī Ṣāliḥ, the freedman of Umm Hānīʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), regarding His words: { And spend in the way of Allah } [al-Baqarah 195] — the verse. He said: ""Let none of you say, 'I find nothing to give.' Even if he finds nothing but a spear-head, let him equip himself with it in the way of Allah: { And do not cast yourselves into destruction with your own hands } [al-Baqarah 195]."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17931","(17925) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri' narrated from Haywah ibn Shurayh: Yazid ibn Abi Habib informed us: Aslam Abu 'Imran narrated to me, he said: We were in Constantinople, and over the people of Egypt was 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir, and over the people of Sham was a man — Fadalah ibn 'Ubaid. A great formation of the Romans had emerged from al-Madinah, so we arrayed ourselves against them. A man from the Muslims charged at the Romans until he entered their ranks, then came back to us. The people cried out to him, saying: ""Subhan Allah! He has cast himself into destruction!"" Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, the companion of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him), stood and said: ""O people, you are interpreting this verse with this interpretation. Rather, this verse was revealed concerning us, the Ansar — when Allah honoured His religion and its supporters increased, we said among ourselves, secretly from the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him): 'Our wealth has been lost; if we were to remain in it and rectify what has been lost from it…' So Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, revealed, repelling from us what we intended: {And spend in the way of Allah and do not throw yourselves with your own hands into destruction} [al-Baqarah 195]. The destruction was in the staying we intended — that we should remain with our wealth to rectify it. So we were commanded with military expedition (ghazw). Abu Ayyub (may Allah be pleased with him) continued to campaign in the way of Allah until Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, took him. [Sahih]"
"17932","(17926) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us: Ibrahim narrated to us: Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated from Shu'bah: from Abi Ishaq, he said: A man said to al-Bara' (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Should I charge upon the squadron with the sword amidst a thousand — is that destruction?"" He said: ""No. Rather, destruction is that a man commits a sin, then casts himself into it, and then says: 'No one will forgive me.'"" [Sahih]"
"17933","(17927) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas — he is al-Asamm — narrated to us: Ahmad ibn al-Fudayl al-'Asqalani narrated to us: Adam narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from Simak ibn Harb: from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir (may Allah be pleased with him), regarding the words of Allah: {And do not throw yourselves with your own hands into destruction} [al-Baqarah 195], he said: ""He says: When one of you commits a sin, let him not cast himself into destruction, and let him not say: 'There is no repentance for me.' Rather, let him seek forgiveness from Allah and turn to Him in repentance, for Allah is Forgiving and Merciful."" [Da'if]"
"17934","(17928) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us: Abu 'Uthman 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Ibn 'Uthman informed us: 'Abd Allah informed us: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated from Qays, he being Ibn Abi Hazim: from Mudrik ibn 'Awf al-Ahmasi: that he was sitting with 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) when they mentioned a man who had sold himself (i.e., sacrificed his life) on the day of Nahawand. Mudrik said: ""By Allah, O Commander of the Faithful, that was my maternal uncle. The people claim that he cast himself into destruction."" 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Those people have lied. Rather, he is among those who purchased the Hereafter with this world."" Such is the narration of Ya'la. [Sahih]"
"17935","(17929) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fudayl informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Ibn 'Uthman informed us: 'Abd Allah informed us: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated from Qays ibn Abi Hazim: from Husayn ibn 'Awf, he said: When 'Umar was informed of the killing of al-Nu'man ibn Muqarrin, and it was said that so-and-so and so-and-so had been struck — others whom he did not know — he said: ""But Allah knows them."" And a man had sold himself. A man from the Ahmas, called Malik ibn 'Awf, said: ""That was my maternal uncle, O Commander of the Faithful. People claim that he cast himself into destruction."" 'Umar said: ""Those people have lied. Rather, he is among those who purchased the Hereafter with this world."" Qays said: The slain man was 'Awf ibn Abi Hayyah, and he was Abu Shibl. Ya'qub said: Malik is more correct. [Sahih]"
"17936","(17930) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me: Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-'Anazi narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi narrated to us — (h) — and Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us, they said: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us: 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib narrated from Murrah al-Hamdani: from 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Our Lord, the Almighty and Majestic, marvels at a man who campaigns in the way of Allah, then his companions are defeated, and he knows what is upon him (of danger), yet he returns until his blood is shed. Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, says to His angels: 'Look at My servant — he returned desiring what is with Me and fearing what is with Me, until his blood was shed.'"" [Munkar]"
"17937","(17931) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu Ahmad ibn Abi al-Hasan narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Musayyib ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Shahin narrated to us: Khalid ibn 'Abd Allah narrated from Khalid — meaning al-Hadha' — from 'Ikrimah: from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both): that the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) said while he was in his tent on the day of Badr: ""I adjure You by Your promise and Your covenant, O Allah — if You willed, You would not be worshipped after this day ever again."" Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) took his hand and said: ""It is enough for you, O Messenger of Allah. You have importuned your Lord."" And he was wearing the coat of mail. He came out reciting: {The host will be routed, and they will turn their backs in flight. Rather, the Hour is their appointed time, and the Hour is more grievous and more bitter} [al-Qamar 45-46]. Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Ishaq ibn Shahin. [Sahih — al-Bukhari]"
"17938","(17932) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us, dictating and reading: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abbad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me from his father, from his grandfather, from al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) when he went to ascend the rock. The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) had worn two coats of mail, and he was unable to ascend it. So Talhah ibn 'Ubaid Allah sat beneath him, and the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) ascended until he was settled upon it. The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Talhah has rendered it obligatory (i.e., Paradise)."" [Hasan]"
"17939","(17933) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us: Abu Hamid ibn Bilal narrated to us: Yahya ibn al-Rabi' al-Makki narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from Yazid ibn Khusayfah: from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid: that the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) wore two coats of mail on the day of Uhud. [Sahih]"
"17940","(17934) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdān informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ghalib narrated to me: Ibrahim ibn Bashar al-Ramadi — Abu Ishaq — narrated to us: Sufyan, he being Ibn 'Uyaynah, narrated from Yazid ibn Khusayfah: from al-Sa'ib, he said: Ibrahim said: ""I found in my book, from a man from Banu Taym, from Talhah ibn 'Ubaid Allah (may Allah be pleased with him): that the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) wore two coats of mail on the day of Uhud."" [Sahih]"
"17941","(17935) It was narrated by Bishr ibn al-Sarī, from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, from Yazīd ibn Khuṣayfah, from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd, from one who narrated to him from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh — Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Haytham informed us, ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād informed us, and Bishr ibn al-Sarī narrated it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17942","(17936) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ziyād informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ informed us, Ḥajjāj narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, ʿAbd al-Karīm informed me that he heard Miqsam, the mawlā of ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith, narrating from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The verse {Not equal are those of the believers who sit [at home]} [al-Nisāʾ 95] was revealed concerning those who stayed behind from Badr and those who went out to Badr. When the expedition of Badr occurred, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh al-Asadī and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shurayḥ — or Shurayḥ ibn Mālik ibn Rabīʿah ibn Ḍibāb, that is, Ibn Umm Maktūm — said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we are blind. Is there any concession for us?"" So the verse was revealed: {Not equal are those of the believers who sit [at home], other than those who have a disability. And Allah has preferred the mujāhidīn over those who sit [at home] by a degree} — these being those who sit [at home] without a disability. {And Allah has preferred the mujāhidīn over those who sit [at home] with a great reward — degrees from Him} [al-Nisāʾ 95–96] — over those who sit [at home] from among the believers other than those who have a disability. Al-Bukhārī narrated the beginning of this hadith without its full context through two other chains from Ibn Jurayj. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī]

Imām al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: It is clear that when Allah promised those who sit [at home] without a disability the good reward (al-Ḥusnā), it indicates that they do not sin by staying behind. And Allah, exalted be His praise, clarified in His statement regarding the call to arms when He commanded going forth: {Go forth, whether light or heavy} [al-Tawbah 41], and He said: {If you do not go forth, He will punish you with a painful punishment} [al-Tawbah 39], and He said: {And it is not for the believers to go forth all at once. So why not a group from every section of them go forth} [al-Tawbah 122]. Thus He made known to them that the obligation of jihād is upon sufficiency (kifāyah) among the mujāhidīn, and He clarified that if they all stayed behind together, they would all sin by staying behind, according to His statement, the Most High: {If you do not go forth, He will punish you with a painful punishment} [al-Tawbah 39]."
"17943","(17937) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Marwazī informed me, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated, from his father, from Yazīd al-Naḥwī, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The verse {If you do not go forth, He will punish you with a painful punishment} [al-Tawbah 39], and the verse {It was not for the people of Madinah and those around them of the Bedouin to stay behind from the Messenger of Allah} up to His words {they are working} [al-Tawbah 120–121] — these two verses were abrogated by the verse that follows them: {And it is not for the believers to go forth all at once} [al-Tawbah 122]. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17944","(17938) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAṭāʾ, from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: Allah, the Blessed and Most High, said: {Take your precautions and go forth in groups} [al-Nisāʾ 71] — meaning in bands — {or go forth all together} [al-Nisāʾ 71]. And He said: {Go forth, whether light or heavy} [al-Tawbah 41]. And He said: {If you do not go forth, He will punish you with a painful punishment} [al-Tawbah 39]. Then He abrogated these verses and said: {And it is not for the believers to go forth all at once} [al-Tawbah 122]. He said: So a group would go forth with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and a group would remain behind. He said: Those who remained with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) were those who acquired understanding in the religion and warned their people when they returned to them from the military expedition, so that they might be cautious regarding what Allah had revealed of His Book, His obligations, and His prescribed punishments. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17945","(17939) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, a man and ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Saʿīd, from Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""Whoever equips a ghāzī in the cause of Allah has indeed performed jihād, and whoever looks after his family in his absence with goodness has indeed performed jihād."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr and Abū al-Ṭāhir, from Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhārī narrated it as mentioned above. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī]"
"17946","(17940) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated, from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from Yazīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, the mawlā of al-Mahrī, from his father, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent [a detachment] to Banī Liḥyān and said: ""Let one man go forth from every two men."" Then he said to those who remained behind: ""Whoever among you looks after the family and wealth of the one who went forth with goodness shall have a reward equal to half the reward of the one who went forth."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"17947","(17941) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥalīm in Marw informed us, Abū al-Muwajjih informed us, ʿAbdān informed us, ʿAbd Allāh informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, Ḥibbān informed us, ʿAbd Allāh informed us, Wuhaib ibn al-Ward informed me, ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated, from Sumayy, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever dies without having performed jihād and without having resolved in his heart to perform jihād dies upon a branch of hypocrisy."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sahm, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"17948","(17942) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān narrated — and I read it to Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Rabbih al-Jurjusī — both said: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated, from Yaḥyā ibn al-Ḥārith, from al-Qāsim Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abī Umāmah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""Whoever does not perform jihād, or equip a ghāzī, or look after the family of a ghāzī with goodness, Allah will afflict him with a calamity."" Yazīd said in his narration: ""Before the Day of Resurrection."" [Ḥasan]"
"17949","(17943) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān al-ʿĀmirī informed us, Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb informed us, ʿAbd al-Muʾmin ibn Khālid al-Ḥanafī informed us, Najdah ibn Nufayʿ narrated, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) called a tribe of the Arabs to go forth for jihād, but they were sluggish, so the verse was revealed: {If you do not go forth, He will punish you with a painful punishment} [al-Tawbah 39]. He said: Their punishment was the withholding of rain from them. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17950","(17944) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated, from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Qatādah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) delivered a sermon and mentioned jihād, and he did not prefer anything over it except the obligatory [prayers].

(Q) This indicates that it is an obligation of sufficiency (kifāyah), since he gave precedence over it to a specific obligatory act. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ṭayālisī]"
"17951","(17945) 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us, 'Ubayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Farrā' narrated to us, Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī narrated from 'Abdullāh ibn 'Awn, who said: I wrote to Nāfi' asking him what had kept Ibn 'Umar from participating in military expeditions. He wrote back to me that Ibn 'Umar used to send his sons on campaign and would bear the financial responsibility, and nothing kept him from the expeditions except the will of 'Umar and the fact that his children were young. And Ibn 'Umar used to consider jihād in the cause of Allah to be the most virtuous of deeds after the obligatory prayer. [Hasan]"
"17952","(17946) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn 'Alī narrated to us, 'Abd al-Malik ibn Ibrāhīm al-Juddī narrated to us, Sa'īd ibn Khālid al-Khuzā'ī narrated to me, 'Abdullāh ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us, 'Ubaydullāh ibn Abī Rāfi' narrated from 'Alī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said — and Abū Dāwūd said that al-Ḥasan ibn 'Alī raised it (as a marfū' narration): It suffices for the congregation, when they pass by, that one of them offers the salām, and it suffices for those seated that one of them returns it. [Da'īf]"
"17953","(17947) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to me, 'Alī ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Īsā informed us, Abū al-Yamān informed us, Shu'ayb informed us from al-Zuhrī, Sa'īd ibn al-Musayyab informed me that Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""I have been commanded to fight the people until they say 'Lā ilāha illallāh.' Whoever says 'Lā ilāha illallāh' has thereby protected from me his life and his property, except by right, and his reckoning is with Allah."" Muḥammad ibn Ismā'īl al-Bukhārī narrated it from Abū al-Yamān, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīh. Agreed upon.]"
"17954","(17948) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād, Ismā'īl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, 'Abbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, 'Uthmān ibn 'Umar narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated from Mughīrah from al-Sha'bī from Muḥarrir ibn Abī Hurayrah from his father, who said: I was with 'Alī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent him with Barā'ah to the polytheists, and I was calling out until my voice became hoarse. I said: ""Father, what were you calling out?"" He said: ""We were commanded to call out that none shall enter Paradise except a believer, and that whoever has a covenant with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, his term shall be four months. When the four months have passed, then Allah and His Messenger are free of the polytheists, and no one shall circumambulate the Ka'bah naked, and no polytheist shall circumambulate the Ka'bah after this year"" — or he said: ""after this day."" [Ṣaḥīh]"
"17955","(17949) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū 'Abdullāh Muḥammad ibn 'Abdullāh al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Mihrān ibn Khālid al-Aṣbahānī informed us, 'Ubaydullāh ibn Mūsā informed us, Sufyān informed us — (H) And Abū 'Abdullāh informed us, and Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn 'Alī ibn 'Affān narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from 'Alqamah ibn Murthad from Sulaymān ibn Buraydah from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, appointed a commander over an army, he would counsel him regarding his own self with taqwā of Allah, and regarding those Muslims who were with him with goodness. Then he would say: ""Fight in the name of Allah and in the cause of Allah. Fight those who disbelieve in Allah. Fight, but do not commit treachery, do not act deceitfully, do not mutilate, and do not kill children. When you meet your enemy among the polytheists, invite them to one of three qualities — whichever of them they respond to, accept it from them and refrain from them. Invite them to Islam; if they respond to you, accept it from them and refrain from them. Then invite them to move from their land to the land of the Muhājirūn, and inform them that if they do so, they shall have what the Muhājirūn have and upon them shall be what is upon the Muhājirūn. If they refuse to move from their land to the land of the Muhājirūn, inform them that they shall be like the Bedouin Muslims, and the judgment of Allah that applies to the Arabs shall apply to them, and they shall have no share of the fay' or the spoils unless they fight alongside the Muslims. If they refuse, then ask them to pay the jizyah. If they do so, then refrain from them. If they refuse, then seek the help of Allah and fight them."" And he mentioned the remainder of the hadith. The complete hadith will come, Allah willing. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm from Yaḥyā ibn Ādam. [Ṣaḥīh. Muslim]"
"17956","(17950) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Qutaybah ibn Sa'īd informed us — (H) And Abū 'Amr Muḥammad ibn 'Abdullāh al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismā'īlī informed me, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Sa'īd narrated to us, al-Layth narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd from 'Umar ibn Kathīr from Abū Muḥammad, the mawlā of Abū Qatādah, from Abū Qatādah, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said on the day of Ḥunayn: ""Whoever produces evidence regarding a slain person, his spoils shall belong to him."" So I stood seeking evidence for my slain man, but I found no one to testify for me, so I sat down. Then it occurred to me, so I mentioned the matter to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. A man among his companions said: ""The weapon of this slain man whom he mentions is with me."" He said: ""Satisfy him with it."" Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""No! He shall not give a lowly one from Quraysh and leave a lion from the lions of Allah who fights for Allah and His Messenger."" He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came to know of it, so he delivered it to me, and I purchased from it some sheep, and it was the first wealth I ever accumulated. And Abū 'Amr said in his narration: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, stood and delivered it to me. Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Sa'īd according to the first wording. Then al-Bukhārī said: 'Abdullāh said from al-Layth: ""The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, stood and delivered it to me."" [Ṣaḥīh. Agreed upon.]"
"17957","(17951) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Amr said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us, al-Rabī' ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfi'ī said: It is known to more than one of the scholars of apostasy whom I have met that Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""The spoils belong only to those who were present at the battle."" [Ṣaḥīh. Shāfi'ī]"
"17958","(17952) With this chain of narration, al-Shāfi'ī said, narrating from Abū Yūsuf from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq from Yazīd ibn 'Abdullāh ibn Qusayṭ, that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, sent 'Ikrimah ibn Abī Jahl with five hundred Muslims as reinforcements to Ziyād ibn Labīd and al-Muhājir ibn Abī Umayyah. They met the army, who had already conquered al-Najīr in Yemen, and Ziyād ibn Labīd — who was among those who witnessed Badr — included them in the spoils. Al-Shāfi'ī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Ziyād wrote to Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, about this matter, and Abū Bakr wrote back: ""The spoils belong only to those who were present at the battle."" He did not consider anything for 'Ikrimah because he had not been present at the battle. So Ziyād spoke to his companions, and they willingly agreed to include 'Ikrimah and his companions voluntarily. And this is our position. [Da'īf]"
"17959","(17953) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us, Ādam narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, Qays ibn Muslim narrated to me, and Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb said: The people of Baṣrah fought the people of Nahāwand, and they were reinforced by the people of Kūfah under the command of 'Ammār ibn Yāsir. They arrived after the people of Baṣrah had already prevailed over the enemy. The people of Kūfah sought a share of the spoils, but the people of Baṣrah did not wish to distribute any of the spoils to the people of Kūfah. A man from Banī Tamīm said to 'Ammār ibn Yāsir: ""O you with the cut ear, do you want to share in our spoils?"" — and 'Ammār's ear had been cut off in the company of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. So they wrote to 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, and 'Umar wrote to them: ""The spoils belong to those who were present at the battle."" [Ṣaḥīh]"
"17960","(17954) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād, Ismā'īl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Sa'dān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Wakī' narrated from Shu'bah from Qays ibn Muslim from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb al-Aḥmasī, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote: ""The spoils belong to those who were present at the battle."" This is the authentic narration from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. [Ṣaḥīh]"
"17961","(17955) As for the one who informed us — Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr — they both said: Abu al-'Abbas informed us; al-Rabi' said: al-Shafi'i said, narrating from Abu Yusuf, from al-Mujalid, from 'Amir and Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, that 'Uradiyallaahu 'anhu wrote to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas: ""I have sent reinforcements to you among a people; so whoever among them reaches you before the fallen are gathered, include him in the war-booty.""

Al-Shafi'i, rahimahullah, said: ""This is not firmly established from 'Umar. Had it been established from him, we would have been quick to accept it from him."" Then he mentioned Abu Yusuf's opposition to this hadith of 'Umar.

Al-Shaykh said: ""It is munqati' (discontinuous), and its narrator is al-Mujalid, who is da'if (weak). The hadith of Tariq ibn Shahab has a sahih (authentic) chain of narration, in which there is no doubt. And Allah knows best.""

Al-Shafi'i, rahimahullah, said: ""Something has been narrated from the Prophet (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) that is established in the meaning of what has been narrated from Abu Bakr and 'Uradiyallaahu 'anhma, though I do not recall it precisely.""

Al-Shaykh said: ""He intended, and Allah knows best, the hadith of Abu Hurayrah concerning the incident of Aban ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As when he arrived with his companions to the Prophet (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) at Khaybar after he had conquered it, and he did not allot them a share. This has already passed with its chains of narration along with the rest of what has been narrated on this subject in the Kitab al-Qasm (Book of Division)."" [Da'if]"
"17962","(17956) Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us; Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz informed us; Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id informed us; Ahmad ibn al-Hasan informed us — reciting — my father informed us; Husayn ibn Mukhariq informed us; from Sufyan, from Bakhtari al-'Abdi, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mas'ud, from 'Ali (radiyallaahu 'anhu), who said: ""The war-booty belongs to him who was present at the encounter."" [Da'if]"
"17963","(17957) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us; Abu Ya'la informed us; Abu Kurayb informed us; Abu Usamah informed us; from Burayd ibn Abi Burdah, from Abu Musa (radiyallaahu 'anhu), who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) finished with Hunayn, he sent Abu 'Amir with an army to Awtas. He encountered Duryad ibn al-Simmah, and Duryad was killed, and Allah routed his companions."" And he mentioned the remainder of the hadith. Al-Bukhari and Muslim recorded it in the Sahih from Abu Kurayb. [Sahih. Muttafaq 'alayh]

Al-Shafi'i, rahimahullah, said: ""Abu 'Amir was in the army of the Prophet (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) and was with him at Hunayn. The Prophet (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) sent him in pursuit of them. This is one army, every contingent of which is a support for the other. When the army is thus, if the army obtains something without the detachment, or the detachment obtains something without the army, they are partners therein."""
"17964","(17958) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas — he is al-Aṣamm — informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar informed us; Yunus ibn Bukayr informed us; from Ibn Ishaq; 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb informed me; from his father; from his grandfather, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) delivered a sermon on the year of the Conquest, and he said therein: 'The Muslims are one hand against all others. The most humble among them in their protection shall have his due returned; the furthest among them shall have their detachments returned to those who remained behind.'""

Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated it from 'Amr, saying: ""The strong among them shall have their due returned to the weak among them, and the swift among them shall have their due returned to those who remained behind."" [Da'if]"
"17965","(17959) Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us; Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us; Sa'dan ibn Nasr informed us; Abu Mu'awiyah informed us; from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (radiyallaahu 'anhma), that the Prophet (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) allotted for a man and his horse three shares: one share for him and two shares for his horse.

Both al-Bukhari and Muslim recorded it in the Sahih from the hadith of 'Ubaydullah, as has already passed in the Kitab al-Qasm (Book of Division). All the other narrations on this subject have already passed therein. [Sahih. Muttafaq 'alayh]"
"17966","(17960) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Khamirawayh informed us; Ahmad ibn Najdah informed us; al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' informed us; Ibn al-Mubarak informed us; from Sharik, from al-Aswad ibn Qays al-'Abdi, from Kalthum ibn al-Aqmar, who said: ""The first of us to distinguish the purebred Arab horses was a man among us called Munaydhir al-Wa'di. He was a governor for 'Uradiyallaahu 'anhu over part of Syria. He sought the enemy. The horses caught up, but the non-Arab horses fell behind. He allotted shares for the horses and left the non-Arab horses. He wrote to 'Uradiyallaahu 'anhu, and 'Uradiyallaahu 'anhu wrote back: 'What an excellent opinion you have formed.' Thus it became an established practice.""

Al-Shafi'i narrated it from Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from al-Aswad ibn Qays. Then he said: ""What we hold regarding this is that equality is to be made between the horses, the purebred Arab horses, the non-Arab horses, the mixed-breed horses, and the horses of mixed parentage. Were we to establish a narration like this, we would not oppose it."" [Da'if]"
"17967","(17961) Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us; Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz informed us; Hanbal ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Himsi informed us; Ahmad ibn Abi Ahmad al-Jurjani informed us; Hammad ibn Khalid informed us; Mu'awiyah ibn Salih informed us; from al-'Ala' ibn al-Harith, from Makhul, from Ziyad ibn Jariyah, from Habib ibn Muslimah, that the Prophet (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) classified the purebred as purebred and the mixed-breed as mixed-breed.

This is how Ahmad ibn Abi Ahmad al-Himsi narrated it from Hammad ibn Khalid, with a connected chain. Al-Shafi'i, Ahmad ibn Hanbal, and a group narrated it from Hammad, as munqati' (discontinuous). Similarly, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi and Ziyad ibn al-Hubab narrated it from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, from Abu Bishr — he is al-'Ala' — from Makhul, that the Messenger of Allah (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) classified the mixed-breed as mixed-breed on the day of Khaybar and classified the purebred as purebred: for the purebred Arab horse, two shares, and for the mixed-breed, one share. This is munqati' (discontinuous), and no proof can be established by it. [Munkar]"
"17968","(17962) Another musnad hadith has been narrated on this subject with a weak chain of narration. Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Ya'qub al-Thaqafi informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah informed us; Abu Bilal al-Ash'ari informed us; al-Mufaddal ibn Sidqah informed us; from Wa'il ibn Dawud, from al-Bahi, from 'A'ishah (radiyallaahu 'anha), that the Prophet (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) did not give the kawdan anything, and he gave less than the share of the purebred Arab horse. The kawdan is the slow mule. Abu Bilal al-Ash'ari is not used as a basis for proof. [Da'if]"
"17969","(17963) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih informed us; 'Uthman ibn Sa'id informed us; Sulayman ibn Harb informed us; Shu'bah informed us; from 'Abdullah ibn Abi al-Safar and Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, from 'Urwah ibn Abi al-Ja'd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) said: ""Goodness is tied to the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection — reward and war-booty.""

Al-Bukhari said: ""And Sulayman ibn Harb mentioned it."" In this there is an indication that he attached the war-booty to the genus of horses, and the non-Arab horses (al-baradhīn) are included among the generality of horses.

We have narrated from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib that he was asked about the non-Arab horses (al-baradhīn): ""Is there any zakah upon them?"" He said: ""Is there any zakah upon horses?"" [Sahih. Muttafaq 'alayh]"
"17970","(17964) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Ibn 'Uyaynah informed us; from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from Yahya ibn 'Abbad ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, that al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam (radiyallaahu 'anhu) used to receive four shares from the war-booty: one share for himself, two shares for his horse, and one share for the near of kin — the share of his mother, Safiyyah — meaning on the day of Khaybar. He said: ""Ibn 'Uyaynah used to be reluctant to mention Yahya ibn 'Abbad, though the huffaz narrate it from Yahya ibn 'Abbad.""

Al-Shaykh said: ""Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated it from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from Yahya ibn 'Abbad, that the Messenger of Allah (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) did something similar. And despite the mention of Yahya ibn 'Abbad in it, it is mursal (discontinuous). Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman and Muhadir ibn al-Mawwa' connected it from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from Yahya ibn 'Abbad, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr."" [Da'if]

Al-Shafi'i said, with the chain that has passed, and Makhul narrated that al-Zubayr was present at Khaybar, and the Messenger of Allah (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) allotted him five shares: one share for himself and four shares for his two horses. Al-Awza'i went toward accepting this from Makhul as munqati' (discontinuous). And Hisham ibn 'Urwah was more eager — had al-Zubayr (radiyallaahu 'anhu) been given more for two horses — to adopt it, and it is more likely, since Makhul opposed him, that he was more established in his father's hadith than Makhul, given his eagerness for the increase, even though his hadith is maqtu' (severed) and no proof can be established by it. Thus it is like the hadith of Makhul. However, we turned to the people of military campaigns (ahl al-maghazi) and said: ""They did not narrate that the Prophet (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) allotted shares for two horses, and they do not disagree that the Prophet (sallallaahu 'alayhi wa aalihi wa sallam) was present at Khaybar with three horses of his own — al-Sakb, al-Dharib, and al-Murtajiz — and he took from them only for one horse.""

Al-Shaykh, rahimahullah, said: ""We have narrated the hadith of Hisham ibn 'Urwah in the Kitab al-Qasm (Book of Division) from the hadith of Muhadir, with a connected chain."""
"17971","(17965) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī informed us; Yūnus ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr, from his grandfather, that he used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) allocated, on the day of Khaybar, for al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām four shares: one share for himself, one share for his kinswoman — Ṣafiyyah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, the mother of al-Zubayr — and two shares for the horse. [Weak]"
"17972","(17966) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Umawī and Abū al-Faḍl al-Ḥasan ibn Yaʿqūb al-ʿAdl both said: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us — and the wording is his — Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Wahb ibn Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim informed us; my father informed me; he said: I heard Qays, who is Ibn Saʿd, narrating from Yazīd ibn Hurmuz that Najdah ibn ʿĀmir wrote to Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both): ""Write to me about the dhawī al-qurbā (kinsmen) whom Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, mentioned and for whom He prescribed a share in what Allah bestowed upon His Messenger; and when does the orphanhood of the orphan come to an end; and whether the children of the polytheists may be killed; and whether women and slaves, when they are present at the battle, have a fixed share."" Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""Were it not that I fear falling into something [unwarranted], I would not have written to him."" So he wrote to him while he was present: ""As for the dhawī al-qurbā, we used to consider them to be the relatives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), but our people rejected that. As for the children of the polytheists, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) did not kill any of them, so do not kill unless you possess the knowledge that al-Khiḍr possessed regarding the boy he killed. As for what you asked about the termination of the orphanhood of the orphan: when he reaches puberty and sound judgment is discerned in him, his orphanhood has ended, so hand over his property to him. As for women and slaves, they have no fixed share when they are present at the battle, but they are given something from the spoils of the people as a gratuity."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [Authentic. Muslim]"
"17973","(17967) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Ṭayyib Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Zāhid informed us; Sahl ibn ʿAmmār al-ʿAtakī informed us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī Abī Jaʿfar and al-Zuhrī, from Yazīd ibn Hurmuz, who said: ""Among what Najdah wrote to him in his letter, he asked about the orphan: when does he exit orphanhood and when does his right in the fayʾ become established?"" So he wrote to him: ""When he reaches puberty, he has exited orphanhood and his right in the fayʾ is established."""
"17974","(17968) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed us; Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal informed us from Muḥammad ibn Zayd; ʿUmayr, the mawlā of Ābī al-Laḥm, informed me. He said: I was present at Khaybar with my masters, and they spoke to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) about me. He gave a command concerning me, and I was handed a sword to carry. I was dragging it along when it was reported to him that I was a slave. So he ordered that I be given some worn-out goods from the surplus belongings. [Authentic]"
"17975","(17969) As for what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr informed us from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Dimashqī from Makhūl and Khālid ibn Maʿdān, who both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) allocated for the horseman, for his horse, two shares, and for its rider one share, thus he had three shares in total; and for the foot soldier, one share. And he allocated shares for women and children. This is a munaqaṭiʿ (disconnected) narration. The ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās is muttaṣil (connected) and ṣaḥīḥ (authentic), and it is more deserving. And with Allah lies success. [Weak]"
"17976","(17970) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī said: Abū Yūsuf informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah informed us from al-Ḥakam from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) sought the assistance of the Jews of Banī Qaynuqāʿ and gave them a gratuity but did not allocate a share of the spoils for them.

This is narrated solely by al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah, and he is matrūk (abandoned). No authentic ḥadīth has reached us on this matter. We have previously narrated regarding the dislike of seeking assistance from the polytheists. And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
"17977","(17971) As for the ḥadīth which Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us; Ḥafṣ informed us from Ibn Jurayj from al-Zuhrī that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) went on a military expedition with some Jews and allocated shares for them. This is munaqaṭiʿ (disconnected). Similarly, Yazīd ibn Yazīd ibn Jābir narrated it from al-Zuhrī as a disconnected narration. [Weak. Disconnected]

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""A disconnected ḥadīth is not considered a proof with us.""

The Sheikh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: And al-Wāqidī narrated from Ibn Abī Sabrah from Fuṭayr al-Ḥārithī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) set out with ten Jews from the Jews of Madīnah toward Khaybar and allocated for them shares like those of the Muslims. This is disconnected and its chain is weak."
"17978","(17972) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ayyūb informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Sulaym ibn Akhḍar informed us from Ibn ʿAwn, who said: I wrote to Nāfiʾ asking him about supplication before fighting. He wrote back: ""That was only in the early period of Islam. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) launched a surprise attack on Banī al-Muṣṭaliq while they were heedless and their livestock were being watered at the watering place. He killed their fighting men and took their women and children captive, and on that day he obtained — Yaḥyā said: I think he said — Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith."" ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated this ḥadīth to me, and he was present in that army. [Authentic. Agreed upon]

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Ibn ʿAwn."
"17979","(17973) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣūfī informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb informed us.

(Ḥ) And al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed me — and this is his ḥadīth — Qutaybah informed us. Both said: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar informed us from Rabiʿah from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān from Ibn Muḥayriẓ that he said: I and Abū Ṣirmah entered upon Abū Saʿīd (may Allah be pleased with him), and Abū Ṣirmah asked him: ""O Abū Saʿīd, did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) mention ʿazl (coitus interruptus)?"" He said: ""Yes. We went on the expedition of Banī al-Muṣṭaliq with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family). We captured noble Arab women, and our separation from our homes grew long, and we desired ransom. We wanted to enjoy them and practice ʿazl. We said: 'Shall we do so while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) is among us, and we do not ask him?' So we asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), and he said: 'There is no harm upon you if you do not do it. Whatever soul Allah has decreed to exist until the Day of Resurrection shall certainly come into being.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb and Qutaybah. In this there is evidence that he distributed their spoils among them before returning to Madīnah, as al-Awzāʿī and al-Shāfiʿī maintained. Abū Yūsuf said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) conquered the territory of Banī al-Muṣṭaliq and prevailed over them, and their land became Dār al-Islām, and he sent al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah to collect their ṣadaqah (charitable dues). [Authentic. Agreed upon]

Al-Shāfiʿī said in response to that: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) launched a surprise attack upon them while they were heedless among their livestock. He killed them, took them captive, and distributed their wealth and captives in their own territory in the year of five (5 AH). It was only after a considerable time that they embraced Islam. Al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah was sent to them as a collector of ṣadaqah in the year of ten (10 AH), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) had already departed from them, and their territory was still Dār al-Ḥarb (the abode of war).

The Sheikh said: As for his statement that this occurred in the year of five (5 AH), ʿUrwah and Ibn Shihāb also said the same."
"17980","(17974) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us; ʿUthmān ibn Ṣāliḥ informed us from Ibn Lahīʿah; Abū al-Aswad informed us from ʿUrwah.

(Ḥ) And Yaʿqūb informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir informed us; Muḥammad ibn Fulayḥ informed us from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah from Ibn Shihāb, in the mention of the military expeditions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), he said: ""Then he fought Banī al-Muṣṭaliq and Banī Liḥyān in Shaʿbān of the year of five (5 AH)."" This is more authentic than what was narrated from Ibn Isḥāq that it occurred in the year of six (6 AH). [Weak]"
"17981","(17975) As for his sending al-Walīd as a tax-collector, it was narrated to us by Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, who informed us that Aḥmad ibn Kāmil al-Qāḍī narrated to us, who said: Muḥammad ibn Saʿd al-ʿAwfi narrated to us, who said: my father, Saʿd ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAṭiyyah, narrated to me, who said: my paternal uncle, al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAṭiyyah, narrated to me, who said: my father narrated to me from my grandfather, ʿAṭiyyah ibn Saʿd, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ to Banī al-Muṣṭaliq to collect the ṣadaqah (charitable dues) from them. When the news of his arrival reached them, they rejoiced and went out to meet the envoy of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). When al-Walīd was told that they were coming out to meet him, he returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, Banī al-Muṣṭaliq have refused to pay the ṣadaqah."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) became exceedingly angry at that. While he was contemplating in his mind that he might raid them, the delegation arrived and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we were told that your envoy turned back from the midpoint of the journey, and we feared that he was turned back by a letter that came to him from you, on account of anger that you felt toward us. We seek refuge in Allah from the wrath of Allah and the wrath of His Messenger."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had concealed his intention from them and had resolved to attack them. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed their exoneration in the Book, saying: {O you who believe, if a sinful person comes to you with a report, verify it thoroughly, lest you harm a people out of ignorance and then become regretful for what you have done.} [al-Ḥujurāt: 6] [Very Weak]"
"17982","(17976) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, who said: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us, who said: Ādam narrated to us, who said: Warqāʾ narrated to us from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from Mujāhid, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ to Banī al-Muṣṭaliq to collect the ṣadaqah from them. They met him with gifts, so he returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said to him: ""Banī al-Muṣṭaliq have resolved to fight you."" Then Allah, the Blessed and Most High, revealed: {If a sinful person comes to you with a report, verify it thoroughly…} [al-Ḥujurāt: 6] — the verse.

The Sheikh said: What is cited as evidence that this occurred a considerable time after the expedition of Banī al-Muṣṭaliq, and it is likely to have been around the tenth year, is what al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) preserved — that al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah was a young boy at the time of the Conquest, which was in the eighth year, and he would not have been sent as a tax-collector until he had become a man. [Good]"
"17983","(17977) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, who said: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to us, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me, who said: my father narrated to us, who said: Fayyāḍ ibn Muḥammad al-Raqqī narrated to us, from Jaʿfar ibn Burqān, from Thābit ibn al-Ḥajjāj, from Abū Mūsā al-Hamdānī, from al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) conquered Makkah, the people of Makkah began bringing their young boys to him, and he would place his hands upon their heads and supplicate for them. I was brought to him, having been perfumed with khalūq (a fragrant unguent). When he saw me, he did not touch me, and nothing prevented him from doing so except the khalūq with which my mother had perfumed me. [Weak]"
"17984","(17978) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us concerning that, who said: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to us, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me, who said: my father narrated to us, who said: Fayyāḍ ibn Muḥammad al-Raqqī narrated to us, from Jaʿfar ibn Burqān, from Thābit ibn al-Ḥajjāj al-Kilābī, from ʿAbd Allāh al-Hamdānī, from al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) conquered Makkah — and he mentioned it with the same meaning. Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal said: It has also been narrated that he relieved himself (defecated) that day, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) found him repulsive and did not touch him, nor did he supplicate for him. He was deprived of the blessing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) due to Allah's prior knowledge of him. [Weak]"
"17985","(17979) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, who said: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, who said: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, who said: Musaddad narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī informed us, who said: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us, who said: Musaddad and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Ḥajabī both narrated, who said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ṣuhayb and Thābit al-Bunānī, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prayed the morning prayer in the darkness of dawn (ghalas), then mounted his riding animal and said: ""Allāhu Akbar! Khaybar is ruined! Indeed, when we descend upon the courtyard of a people, evil is the morning of those who have been warned."" They came out running through the streets, saying: ""Muḥammad and the army!"" Musaddad said: Ḥammād said: ""And al-khamīs means the army."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) overpowered them, killed the fighters, and took the children captive. Ṣafiyyah fell to the share of Dihyah al-Kalbī, and then Ṣafiyyah was transferred to the share of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). He then married her and made her emancipation her bridal gift (mahr). ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz said to Thābit: ""O Abū Muḥammad, did you ask Anas what he gave as her mahr?"" He said: ""He gave her freedom as her mahr."" He smiled. This was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad. [Sound — Agreed Upon]"
"17986","(17980) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, who said: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ and Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār both narrated, who said: al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, who said: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, who said: Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And he said: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, who said: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hāshim informed us, who said: Bahz narrated to us, who said: Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us, from Thābit, who said: Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us, who said: Ṣafiyyah fell to the share of Dihyah in the division, and they began praising her in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), saying: ""We have not seen anyone like her among the captives."" He sent for Dihyah and gave him in exchange for her whatever he wished, then he handed her over to my mother and said: ""Prepare her (attend to her needs).""

He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) departed from Khaybar until he placed her behind him (on his mount). He halted and pitched a tent for her. When morning came, he said: ""Whoever has surplus provisions, let us have some."" A man would come with surplus dates, surplus sawīq (barley flour), and surplus clarified butter, until they had accumulated a large quantity of ḥays (a mixture of dates, clarified butter, and flour). They began eating from that ḥays and drinking from water collected beside them from rainwater (water of the sky). Anas said: That was the walīmah (wedding feast) of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) for her.

He said: We set out until, when we saw the walls of al-Madīnah, we walked toward them. We quickened the pace of our mounts, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) quickened the pace of his mount. Ṣafiyyah was behind him, having been mounted behind him. The mount of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) stumbled, and he fell, and she fell. None of the people looked at him or at her until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) stood up, covering her. We came to him and he said: ""We are unharmed."" We entered al-Madīnah, and the slave-girls of his wives came out, looking at her and gloating over her fall. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Bahz ibn Asad.

It was narrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hāshim.

In this there is evidence that the spoils of Khaybar were divided at Khaybar itself. Abū Yūsuf said: When it was conquered, it became Dār al-Islām, and he employed them on the date-palms.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: As for Khaybar, I do not know that there was a single Muslim there. He made peace only with the Jews, and they remained on their religion. And all the surrounding area of Khaybar was Dār al-Ḥarb. [Sound — Agreed Upon]"
"17987","(17981) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdbārī informed us, who said: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, who said: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, who said: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us, who said: Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, who said: my father narrated to us, from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Nāfiʿ, the freedman of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, narrated to me, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O people! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) made an agreement with the Jews of Khaybar on the condition that we could expel them whenever we wished. Whoever among you has wealth, let him take it with him, for I am expelling the Jews."" So he expelled them. [Sound — Aḥmad]"
"17988","(17982) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, who said: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, who said: Ibrāhīm ibn Hāshim al-Baghawī, Abū Yaʿlā al-Mawṣilī, and al-Ḥasan al-Nasawī all said: Hudbah narrated to us, who said: Hammām narrated to us, who said: Qatādah narrated to us, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) performed four ʿumrahs, all of them in Dhū al-Qaʿdah, except the one that was part of his Ḥajj. There was the ʿumrah of al-Ḥudaybiyah, or at the time of al-Ḥudaybiyah, in Dhū al-Qaʿdah; and an ʿumrah the following year; and an ʿumrah from al-Jiʿrānah, where he divided the spoils of Ḥunayn, in Dhū al-Qaʿdah; and an ʿumrah performed along with his Ḥajj. This is the ḥadīth of Ibrāhīm. Al-Ḥasan said: ""An ʿumrah from al-Ḥudaybiyah."" And Abū Yaʿlā said: ""His ʿumrah from al-Ḥudaybiyah."" This was narrated by al-Bukhārī and Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Hudbah. In this there is evidence that he (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) divided the spoils of Ḥunayn at that place.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: As for what Abū Yūsuf cited as evidence — that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) did not divide the spoils of Badr until he reached al-Madīnah — and what is established from the ḥadīth, the evidence for that is that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) allotted shares to ʿUthmān and Ṭalḥah, even though they were not present at Badr. If it is as he claims, then it contradicts the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), because he claims that it is not permissible for the Imam to give a share to anyone who was not present at the battle and was not among the reinforcements. But it is not as he claimed; rather, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) divided the spoils of Badr at Sabr, a valley in the mountain passes of al-Ṣafrāʾ, near Badr. [Sound — Agreed Upon]"
"17989","(17983) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, who said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, who said: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us, who said: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) proceeded. When he emerged from a narrow pass called al-Ṣafrāʾ, he came out onto a mound called Sabr, at a distance of one night's journey from Badr, or more. There the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) divided the nafal (bonus shares) among the Muslims upon that mound. [Weak]"
"17990","(17984) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, who said: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī narrated to us, who said: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us, who said: Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān al-Juʿfī narrated to us, who said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, who said: Ḥuyayy narrated to me, from Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥubulī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) went out on the day of Badr with three hundred and fifteen fighters, just as Ṭālūt went out. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) supplicated for them when he set out, saying: ""O Allah, they are barefoot — carry them! O Allah, they are naked — clothe them! O Allah, they are hungry — feed them!"" Allah granted them victory on the day of Badr. They returned, and there was not a single man among them who did not come back with one or two camels, and they were clothed and satiated.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: The spoils of Badr, as narrated by ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, were acquired by the Muslims before the verse in Sūrat al-Anfāl was revealed. When they disputed over them, Allah took them from their hands by His words: {They ask you about the spoils of war. Say: The spoils of war belong to Allah and the Messenger.} [al-Anfāl: 1] — the verse. [Weak]"
"17991","(17985) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us — Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah — from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā al-Ashdaq — from Makḥūl — from Abī Salām — from Abī Umāmah al-Bāhilī — from ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him, who said:

The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, set out toward Badr and encountered the enemy there. When Allah defeated them, a group of Muslims pursued them, killing them, while another group surrounded the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, and yet another group took control of the spoils and the camp. When those who had pursued the enemy returned, they said: ""The spoils are ours — we pursued the enemy, and through us Allah drove them away and defeated them."" Those who had surrounded the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""You have no greater right to it than we do; rather, it is ours — we surrounded the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, lest the enemy should reach him unawares."" And those who had seized the camp and the spoils said: ""You have no greater right to it than we do; rather, it is ours — we seized it and secured it.""

Then Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, revealed to His Messenger, ʿalayhi al-salām: **""They ask you about the spoils. Say: The spoils belong to Allah and the Messenger.""** [Al-Anfāl, 8:1] The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, then divided it among them equally. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17992","(17986) Abū ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us — Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me — from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā al-Ashdaq — from Makḥūl — from Abī Umāmah al-Bāhilī, who said:

I asked ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him, about the spoils, and he mentioned the hadith with its meaning. He said at the end of it: ""When we disagreed and our dispositions soured, Allah seized it from our hands and turned it over to His Messenger, and he divided it among the people equally. In that there was fear of Allah, obedience to Him, obedience to His Messenger, and reconciliation between people."" Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, says: **""They ask you about the spoils. Say: The spoils belong to Allah and the Messenger. So fear Allah and reconcile between yourselves.""** [Al-Anfāl, 8:1]

And from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: I heard al-Zuhrī saying: ""Sūrat al-Anfāl was revealed in its entirety concerning the people of Badr."" [Ḍaʿīf]

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""It all belonged exclusively to the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, and he divided it among them. He also included with them eight men who had not witnessed the battle, from among the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār."" In another place he said: ""Seven or eight."""
"17993","(17987) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us — ʿAmr ibn Khālid and Ḥassān ibn ʿAbd Allāh both narrated to us — Ibn Lahīʿah narrated from Abī al-Aswad — from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, in the context of naming those who witnessed Badr and those who did not, after which the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, allotted him a share. Among those who did not witness it but were allotted a share was ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ ibn Umayyah ibn ʿAbd Shams, who had remained behind in Madinah on account of his wife Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, who was ill. The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, allotted him a share. He said: ""And my reward, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: **""And your reward.""**

And Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn ʿAmr ibn Kaʿb ibn Saʿd ibn Taym ibn Murrah — he was in Shām. He came and spoke to the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, and he allotted him a share. He said: ""And my reward, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: **""And your reward.""**

And Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl — he came from Shām after the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, had returned to Madinah. The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, allotted him a share. He said: ""And my reward, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: **""And your reward.""**

These three were from among the Muhājirūn. As for the Anṣār: Abū Lubābah — they say he went out with the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, toward Badr, but he placed him in charge of Madinah and allotted him a share along with the people of Badr. And al-Ḥārith ibn Ḥāṭib — the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, sent him back to Madinah, as they claim, and allotted him a share. And ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAdī — he set out, but the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, turned him back and allotted him a share along with the people of Badr. And Khawwāt ibn Jubayr ibn al-Nuʿmān — the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, allotted him a share among the people of Badr. And al-Ḥārith ibn al-Ṣimmah — he was injured at al-Rawḥāʾ, and the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, allotted him a share. Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yasār mentioned them, and so did Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, except that he did not mention al-Ḥārith ibn Ḥāṭib being sent back to Madinah. And Allah knows best.

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""He gave them from his own wealth, and the verse **'And know that whatever you obtain of spoils, then indeed one-fifth of it is for Allah and the Messenger'** [Al-Anfāl, 8:41] was revealed after the spoils of Badr."""
"17994","(17988) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah informed us — Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Naḍrawī narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us — Hushaym narrated from Abī Bishr — from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, who said:

I said to Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both: ""Sūrat al-Anfāl?"" He said: **""It was revealed concerning the people of Badr.""** [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān from Hushaym.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""As for the incident of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh and Ibn al-Ḥaḍramī that is cited as evidence, that was before Badr and before the revelation of the verse. Their incident occurred on the last day of a sacred month, so they held back regarding what they had done until the verse was revealed: **'They ask you about the sacred month — fighting in it. Say: Fighting in it is a grave matter.'** [Al-Baqarah, 2:217] And al-Awzāʿī had no grounds for differing on this matter.""

The Shaykh said: ""We have mentioned the story of Ibn Jaḥsh from the narration of Jundub ibn ʿAbd Allāh."""
"17995","(17989) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq — Yazīd ibn Rūmān narrated to me from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said:

The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, sent ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh to Nakhlah and said to him: ""Stay there until you bring us news of the Quraysh."" He did not command him to fight. This was in a sacred month. He had written him a letter before informing him of his destination, saying: ""Set out, you and your companions, and when you have traveled for two days, open your letter and see what is in it, and carry out what I have commanded you, and do not compel any of your companions to go with you.""

When he had traveled for two days, he opened the letter and found in it: ""Proceed until you descend to Nakhlah and bring us such information about the Quraysh as reaches you."" When he read the letter to his companions, he said: ""Hear and obey. Whoever among you desires martyrdom, let me go with him, for I am proceeding on the command of the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam. And whoever dislikes it, let him turn back, for the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, has forbidden me to compel any of you."" So the people went with him. When they reached Baḥrān, Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and ʿUtbah ibn Ghazwān lost a camel they had been taking turns riding, and they fell behind to search for it. The people continued until they descended upon Nakhlah. There passed by them ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥaḍramī and al-Ḥakam ibn Kayṣān, and ʿUthmān and al-Mughīrah, the two sons of ʿAbd Allāh, who were with them on a trading journey, having brought hides and raisins from al-Ṭāʾif. When the people saw them, Wāqid ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Tamīmī — who had shaved his head — looked out for them. When they saw him shaven-headed, they said: ""This is a pilgrim; there is no harm from them."" The people consulted one another — meaning the companions of the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam — on the last day of Rajab. They said: ""If you kill them, you will kill them in a sacred month; and if you leave them, they will enter the sanctuary tonight and be protected from you."" So the people resolved to kill them. Wāqid ibn ʿAbd Allāh shot ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥaḍramī with an arrow and killed him. They captured ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh and al-Ḥakam ibn Kayṣān. Al-Mughīrah escaped, and they could not overtake him. They took the caravan and brought it to the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam. He said to them: **""By Allah, I did not command you to fight in a sacred month.""** The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, withheld the captives and the caravan and took nothing from them. When the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said what he said, it fell from their hands, and they thought they had perished. Their brothers among the Muslims reproached them. And when the Quraysh heard of the matter of these men, they said: ""Muḥammad has shed blood in a sacred month, taken wealth, captured men, and violated the sacred month."" So Allah, the Most High, revealed concerning that: **""They ask you about the sacred month — fighting in it. Say: Fighting in it is a grave matter, and hindering from the way of Allah and disbelief in it and in the Sacred Mosque, and driving out its people from it, is graver in the sight of Allah. And fitnah is graver than killing.""** [Al-Baqarah, 2:17] — meaning: disbelief in Allah is graver than killing. When that was revealed, the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, took the caravan and ransomed the two captives. The Muslims said: ""Do you hope that this will be a raid for us?"" So Allah revealed concerning them: **""Indeed, those who believed and those who emigrated and fought in the way of Allah — those are the ones who hope for the mercy of Allah""** [Al-Baqarah, 2:218] to the end of the verse. They were eight in number, and their amīr, the ninth, was ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17996","(17990) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAttāb narrated to us — al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us — Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah narrated from his paternal uncle Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, who mentioned the story of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh with the same meaning. He said: ""And that was in Rajab, two months before Badr."" In this there is an indication that it occurred before the verse concerning spoils was revealed. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"17997","(17991) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us — Abū al-Aḥraz Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar ibn Jamīl al-Ṭūsī narrated to us — Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāh al-Marwazī al-Ḥarbī narrated to us — Abū al-Walīd narrated from Shuʿbah.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us — Abū Muslim Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us — Abū al-Walīd narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl — from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mufaḍḍal, may Allah be pleased with him, who said:

We were besieging Khaybar when a man threw a leather bag. I took it and turned around, and there was the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, so I felt shy before him.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Walīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"17998","(17992) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us — Shuʿbah and Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah al-Qaysī both narrated from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl al-ʿAdawī, who said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mufaḍḍal, may Allah be pleased with him, saying:

A leather bag of fat was lowered on the day of Khaybar. I took it and clutched it to myself and said: ""This is mine; I shall not give any of it to anyone."" I turned around and there was the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, so I felt shy before him.

Sulaymān said in his hadith — and it is not in Shuʿbah's hadith — that the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: **""It is yours.""**

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā from Abī Dāwūd from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"17999","(17993) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us — Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn Hāshim al-Baghawī narrated to us — Aḥmad, who is Ibrāhīm ibn Aḥmad al-Mawṣilī, narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb — from Nāfiʿ — from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said:

We used to obtain honey and fruit during the raids, and we would eat it and not bring it forward.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad from Ḥammād, except that he said: ""honey and grapes."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18000","(17994) Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Ḥammād ibn Zayd, and in the hadith he said: ""We used to go out on raids with the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, and obtain honey and ghee, and we would eat it."" Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us — ʿAbd al-Bāqī ibn Qāniʿ narrated to us — Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us — al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us — Ibn al-Mubārak narrated it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18001","Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us: al-Zubayri Ibrahim ibn Hamzah narrated to me: Anas ibn 'Iyad narrated to me from 'Ubayd Allah from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that an army obtained food and honey during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), and the one-fifth (khums) was not taken from it. [Hasan]"
"18002","'Uthman ibn al-Hakam al-Judhami narrated it from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar from Nafi', without mentioning Ibn 'Umar in it, that an army obtained booty. Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us of it: Abu al-'Abbas, who is al-Asamm, narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed me: 'Uthman ibn al-Hakam al-Judhami informed me from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar, and he mentioned it as a mursal narration. [Munkar]"
"18003","Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Hafiz narrated to us: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Hushaym informed us: al-Shaybani and Ash'ath ibn Sawwar narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Abi al-Mujalid, who said: The people of the mosque sent me to Ibn Abi Awfa (may Allah be pleased with him) to ask him what the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) did with the food of Khaybar. So I went to him and asked him about that. I said: Did he take the khums from it? He said: No, it was less than that. When any of us wanted something from it, he would take his need from it. [Sahih]"
"18004","Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Ya'qub al-Thaqafi narrated to us: Musa ibn Harun narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Mu'ammal ibn Hisham said: Isma'il narrated to us from Yunus from al-Hasan from Abu Barzah al-Aslami (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Arabs used to say: ""Whoever eats bread becomes fat."" When we conquered Khaybar, we drove them away from their bread. I sat upon it and ate from it until I was full. Then I kept looking at my sides to see whether I had become fat. This is what was said from Yunus, while others narrated it from Ayyub. [Da'if]"
"18005","Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us: Abu al-Fadl ibn Khamirawayh informed us: Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us from Ya'qub ibn al-Qa'qa' from al-Rabi' ibn Anas from Suwayd, the servant of Salman, that he obtained a basket during their military expedition and brought it to Salman (may Allah be pleased with him). He opened it and found in it white bread (huwwari) and cheese. So Salman ate from it. [Da'if]"
"18006","Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Qatadah informed us: Abu al-Fadl ibn Khamirawayh informed us: Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us from al-Awza'i: Asid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me from Khalid ibn al-Durayk, who said: I asked Ibn Muhayyiz about selling food and fodder in the land of Rome. He said: I heard Fadalah ibn 'Ubayd (may Allah be pleased with him) say: There are people who wish to turn me from my religion. By Allah, that shall not happen until I meet Muhammad (peace be upon him and his family) and his companions. Whoever sells food or fodder from what he has obtained in the land of Rome for gold or silver, in it is the khums of Allah and the fay' of the Muslims. [Sahih]"
"18007","Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us: Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us: Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh narrated to us from Ibn 'Awn: Khalid ibn Durayk narrated to me from Ibn Muhayyiz from Fadalah ibn 'Ubayd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: There are people who wish to turn me from my religion. By Allah, I hope to remain upon it until I die. Whatever is sold for gold or silver, in it is the khums of Allah and the shares of the Muslims. [Sahih]"
"18008","Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us: Abu al-Fadl ibn Khamirawayh informed us: Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us from Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash: Asid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us from Muqbil ibn 'Abdillah from Hani' ibn Kulthum that the commander of the army of Syria, when Syria was conquered, wrote to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him): ""We have conquered a land abundant in food and fodder, and I dislike taking any step in that matter without your command. So write to me your command regarding it."" So 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to him: ""Let the people eat and obtain fodder. Whatever is sold for gold or silver, in it is the khums of Allah and the shares of the Muslims."" [Sahih]"
"18009","Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Musaffa narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us from Yahya ibn Hamzah: Abu 'Abd al-'Aziz, a shaykh from the people of Jordan, narrated to me from 'Ubadah ibn Nusayy from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, who said: We kept watch over the city of Qinnasrin together with Shurahbil ibn al-Simt (may Allah be pleased with him). When he conquered it, he obtained sheep and cattle therein. He distributed a portion of it among us and placed the remainder in the war booty. I met Mu'adh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) and told him about it. Mu'adh said: We waged war alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) at Khaybar and obtained sheep therein. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) distributed a portion among us and placed the remainder in the war booty. [Hasan]"
"18010","Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz narrated to us: Ahmad ibn al-Khalil narrated to us: al-Waqidi narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Fudayl narrated to us from al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ashja'i from Abu Sufyan from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said on the day of Khaybar: ""Eat and obtain fodder, and do not carry it away."" [Da'if]"
"18011","(18005) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, and he mentioned it as a mursal narration. [Weak]"
"18012","(18006) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated; Musaddad narrated; Hushaym narrated; ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me that Ibn Ḥarshaf al-Azdī narrated to him from al-Qāsim, the freedman of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from some of the Companions of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said: ""We used to eat meat during military expeditions without distributing it, so much so that when we returned to our camp, our saddlebags would be filled with it."" [Weak]"
"18013","(18007) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; he said: al-Shāfiʿī said: ""It is narrated from the ḥadīth of some people the same as what I have said — that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) permitted them to eat in the land of the enemy without taking anything from the food. If this is established from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), then there is no argument for anyone alongside it. And if it is not established, because among its narrators is one who is unknown, then likewise among the narrators of the one from whom its permissibility is narrated is one who is unknown."" [Sound]

The Sheikh said: ""It is as though he intended by the first the ḥadīth of al-Wāqidī, and by the second what we have mentioned after it."""
"18014","(18008) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālūyah informed us; both said: Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Khazzāz narrated; Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated; Abū Ḥamzah al-ʿAṭṭār narrated; he said: ""I said to al-Ḥasan: 'O Abū Saʿīd, I am a man whose place of trade is al-Ubulleh. I fill my belly with food, then I go up to the land of the enemy and eat from its dates and unripe dates. What do you think?'""

Al-Ḥasan said: ""I participated in a military expedition with ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah and men from among the Companions of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). When they ascended to the fruits, they would eat from them without causing damage or carrying any away."" [Weak]"
"18015","(18009) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Khalīfah narrated; Musaddad narrated; Abū ʿAwānah narrated from Saʿīd ibn Masrūq from ʿAbāyah ibn Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj from his grandfather Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""We were with the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah when the people were afflicted by hunger. We obtained camels and sheep. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) was at the rear of the people. So they hastened, slaughtered, and set up the cooking pots. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) came to them and ordered that the cooking pots be overturned. Then he distributed, making ten sheep equal to one camel.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl from Abī ʿAwānah. [Sound — Agreed upon]"
"18016","(18010) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Hannād ibn al-Sarī narrated; Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Kulaib from his father from a man among the Anṣār, who said: ""We went out with the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) on a journey. The people were afflicted by severe need and hardship. They obtained sheep and plundered them. Our cooking pots were boiling when the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) came walking on his horse. He overturned our cooking pots with his bow, then he began mixing the meat with dust, and said: 'Plunder is not more lawful than carrion.' Or he said: 'Carrion is not more lawful than plunder.' The doubt is from Hannād."" [Sound]"
"18017","(18011) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muqriʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAṭṭār informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam al-Miṣrī informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb informed me from Rabīʿah ibn Sulaym al-Tujībī from Ḥanash ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Sabaʾī from Ruwayfiʿ ibn Thābit al-Anṣārī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), from the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) that he said on the day of Ḥunayn: ""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not water his crop with the water of another. And whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not take any animal from the war gains, ride it, and then, when he has worn it out, return it to the war gains. And whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not wear anything from the war gains, and when he has worn it out, let him return it to the war gains."" [Sound]"
"18018","(18012) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated; Musaddad narrated; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Badīl ibn Mayṣarah, Khālid, and al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrīt from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shaqīq from a man from Balqayn, who said: ""I came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) while he was in Wādī al-Qurā. I said: 'What do you say about the war gains?' He said: 'One-fifth belongs to Allah, and four-fifths belong to the army.' I said: 'Is anyone more entitled to it than anyone else?' He said: 'No. Even the arrow that you extract from your side — you are not more entitled to it than your Muslim brother.'"" [Sound]"
"18019","(18013) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Maʿmarī narrated; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated; ʿUthmān ibn ʿAlī narrated; al-Aʿmash narrated from Abī Isḥāq from Abī ʿUbaydah from ʿAbdullāh (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""I came upon Abū Jahl while he was prostrate (fallen), wearing a coat of mail, and with him was an excellent sword, while I had a poor sword. I began striking his head with my sword, recalling a blow he had struck my head with in Makkah, until his hand weakened. I took his sword. He raised his head and said: 'Upon whom was the defeat? Was it for us or against us? Were you not our shepherd in Makkah?' So I killed him. Then I came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and said: 'I have killed Abū Jahl.' The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: 'By Allah, besides whom there is no deity, did you kill him?' He made me swear three times. Then he stood with me and went to them and invoked curses upon them."" [Weak]"
"18020","(18014) Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ al-Qāḍī in al-Kūfah informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us; Minjāb ibn al-Ḥārith informed us; Sharīk narrated from Abī Isḥāq from Abī ʿUbaydah from ʿAbdullāh (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""I came upon Abū Jahl while he was among the slain, prostrate. I had with me a worn-out sword. I began striking him with my sword, but it did nothing. He looked at me and said: 'Were you not our shepherd in Makkah?' His sword fell, so I took it and struck him with it until I killed him. Then I came running to inform the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). He said: 'Did you kill him?' I said: 'Yes.' He made me swear three times, and I swore for him. Then he said: 'Go and show him to me.' So I went and showed him to him, and he said: 'This was the Pharaoh of this nation.'""

(T) And al-Aʿmash narrated it from Abī Isḥāq with the same meaning. [Sound]"
"18021","(18015) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us — Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Khumayrūyah informed us — Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us — al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Maʿmar — from Ayyūb — from Ibn Sīrīn — from Anas ibn Mālik — from Barāʾ ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: On the day of Muslimah, I met a man known as Ḥimār al-Yamāmah, a massive man with a white sword in his hand. I struck his legs, but it was as though I had missed him. He collapsed and fell on his back. I took his sword and sheathed my own, and I did not strike with it except a single blow until it broke, whereupon I threw it aside and took up my own sword again."
"18022","(18016) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us by dictation and recitation — Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us — Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us — Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us — Saʿīd narrated from Qatādah — from Anas — from Abū Ṭalḥah, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, prevailed over a people, he would prefer to encamp in their open ground for three days. [Authenticated by al-Bukhārī and Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Rūḥ from Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah. Al-Bukhārī stated, and Muʿādh followed him in it. (Ṣaḥīḥ, agreed upon)]"
"18023","(18017) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Muqriʾ ibn al-Ḥammāmī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us in Baghdad — Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh informed us — ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad narrated to us — Bishr ibn ʿUmar narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm — he said: I heard Abū Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Ḥunayf narrating from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, that when Banū Qurayẓah submitted to the judgment of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, may Allah be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent for him, and he came riding a donkey. When he was near the mosque, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Rise for your chief"" — or ""for your best one."" He said: ""These people have submitted to your judgment."" He said: ""Then I judge regarding them that their fighting men shall be killed and their children shall be taken as captives."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""You have judged with the judgment of the Sovereign"" — and perhaps he said, ""You have judged with the judgment of Allah."" [Authenticated by al-Bukhārī and Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ through various chains from Shuʿbah. (Ṣaḥīḥ, agreed upon)]"
"18024","(18018) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Jaʿfar Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Asadī al-Ḥāfiẓ in Hamadhān informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dayzīl narrated to us — Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad al-Farwī and Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways both narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ al-Tammār narrated to us — from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm — from ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd — from his father, that Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, may Allah be pleased with him, judged concerning Banū Qurayẓah that every male among whom the razor had passed should be killed, and that their wealth and children should be divided. This was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and he said: ""He has today judged concerning them with the judgment of Allah, which He decreed above the seven heavens."" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other than this chain]"
"18025","(18019) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us — Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us — Sufyān narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr — from ʿAṭiyyah al-Qurẓī, who said: I was among them. Those whose pubic hair had grown were killed, and those whose pubic hair had not grown were spared. I was among those whose pubic hair had not grown. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18026","(18020) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us — ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar al-Dabbī narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — Abū ʿAwānah narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr — from ʿAṭiyyah al-Qurẓī, who said: I was among those concerning whom Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, may Allah be pleased with him, rendered judgment. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, had commanded that their fighting men be killed and their children be taken as captives. He said: They came with me, and had they not seen that I would be killed, they would not have spared me. They uncovered my private area and found that my pubic hair had not grown, so they placed me among the captives. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18027","(18021) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī and Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh both informed us, saying: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us — Abū al-Azhar narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Sharḥbīl informed us — Ibn Jurayj narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah — from Nāfiʿ — from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Jews of Banū al-Naḍīr and Qurayẓah fought against the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, expelled Banū al-Naḍīr and left Qurayẓah, bestowing favor upon them, until Qurayẓah fought thereafter. He killed their men and divided their women, children, and wealth among the Muslims, except for some who joined the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, so he granted them safety and they accepted Islam. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, expelled the Jews of Madīnah — Banū Qaynuqāʿ, who were the people of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām, and the Jews of Banū Ḥārithah, and every Jew in Madīnah. [Authenticated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Ibn Jurayj. (Ṣaḥīḥ, agreed upon)]"
"18028","(18022) As for what he said regarding Khaybar: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me — Abū al-Walīd Ḥassān ibn Muḥammad al-Faqīh informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Muthanná narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated from Mālik ibn Anas — from Zayd ibn Aslam — from his father — from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Were it not for the last of mankind, no village would have been conquered without my dividing it as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, divided Khaybar. [Narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthanná. (Ṣaḥīḥ, agreed upon)]"
"18029","(18023) As for what he said regarding the children of Banū al-Muṣṭaliq: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us — Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us — Ibn ʿAwn narrated — (ḥ) and Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us — Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿAdī and Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh both narrated to us, saying: Ibn ʿAwn narrated — he said: I wrote to Nāfiʿ asking him about the call (al-daʿwah) before fighting. He replied: That call was only in the early period of Islam. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, launched a surprise attack upon Banū al-Muṣṭaliq while they were heedless and their livestock were being watered at the watering place. He killed their fighting men and took their captives, and on that day he acquired Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith. ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated this to me, and he was in that army. In the narration of Yazīd, it states: That was only after the call in the early period of Islam, and the rest is the same. [Narrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthanná from Ibn Abī ʿAdī.] It has already been mentioned in the ḥadīth of Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī: We raided al-Muṣṭaliq and captured the noble women of the Arabs, and we desired to enjoy them and practice coitus interruptus. We asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and he said: ""There is no harm upon you if you do not do so."" [Ṣaḥīḥ, agreed upon]"
"18030","(18024) As for what he said regarding Hawāzin: Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us — Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me — al-Ḥasan, who is Ibn Sufyān, narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyá narrated to us — Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to me — my brother's son narrated to me from his paternal uncle, and ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr claimed that Marwān and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah both informed him that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, stood up when the delegation of Hawāzin came to him as Muslims, asking him to return their wealth and their captives to them. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said to them: ""I have with me those whom I see, and the truest speech is most beloved to me. Choose one of the two categories — either the captives or the wealth. I have given you respite."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, had delayed their matter for about ten nights upon his return from Ṭāʾif. When it became clear to them that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, would return to them only one of the two categories, they said: ""Then we choose our captives."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, rose among the Muslims and praised Allah as He deserves, then said: ""To proceed: Your brothers have come repentant, and I have seen that I should return their captives to them. Whoever among you wishes to do so cheerfully, let him do so, and whoever among you wishes to keep his share until we give it to him from the first of what Allah restores to us — let him do so."" The people said: ""We have cheerfully agreed to that, O Messenger of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""We do not know who among you gave permission and who did not. Return until your chiefs (ʿurafāʾ) bring word to us."" The people departed and spoke with their chiefs, then returned to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and informed him that they had given permission and agreed willingly. This is what reached me regarding the captives of Hawāzin. [Narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq from Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm.] Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, took captives from the people of Badr. Among them were those he freed without taking anything from them, those from whom he took ransom, and those he killed. The two who were killed after being taken captive on the day of Badr were ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ and al-Naḍr ibn al-Ḥārith. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī, 4319]"
"18031","(18025) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: a number of people of knowledge among the Quraysh and others among the people of knowledge in the military campaigns (al-maghazi) informed us: that the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — captured al-Nadr ibn al-Harith al-'Abdiri on the day of Badr and executed him at al-Badiya or al-Athil, having been taken captive. And he captured 'Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt on the day of Badr and executed him, having been taken captive. The Shaykh said: We have transmitted this in the Book of the Division (al-Qasm) from Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yasar, the author of the military campaigns. [Sahih]"
"18032","(18026) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah al-Isbahani informed us: al-Hasan ibn al-Jaham informed us: al-Husayn ibn al-Faraj informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Umar informed us: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather: that the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — when he advanced with the captives until he reached 'Aq al-Dhubya, he ordered 'Asim ibn Thabit ibn Abi al-Aqlah to strike the neck of 'Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt. So 'Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt began to say: ""Woe unto me! For what reason am I being killed among these people?"" The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — said: ""For your enmity toward Allah and His Messenger."" He said: ""O Muhammad, you are the most excellent; treat me as you would treat one of your own people — if you kill them, kill me, and if you show them favor, show me favor, and if you take ransom from them, I shall be as one of them."" He said: ""O Muhammad, who will care for the little children?"" The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — said: ""The Fire."" Then he said: ""O 'Asim ibn Thabit, bring him forward and strike his neck."" So he brought him forward and struck his neck. [Very Weak]"
"18033","(18027) And Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hamdan al-Jallab in Hamadan informed us: Hilal ibn al-'Ala' al-Raqi informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us: 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Amr informed us: Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah informed us: 'Amr ibn Murrah informed us: Ibrahim said: Al-Dahhak ibn Qays wished to appoint Masruq as a governor. 'Umarah ibn 'Uqbah said to him: ""Are you appointing a man from among the remnant of the killers of 'Uthman — may Allah be pleased with him?"" Masruq said to him: ""Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud — may Allah be pleased with him — narrated to us, and he was held trustworthy in our hearts in what he narrated: that the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — when he intended to kill your father, he said: 'Who will care for the little children?' He said: 'The Fire.'"" He said: ""I am pleased for you with what the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — was pleased."" [Sahih]"
"18034","(18028) Al-Shafi'i — may Allah have mercy upon him — said: Among those who were released without ransom was Abu 'Azzah al-Jumahi. The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — left him for the sake of his daughters and took from him a pledge that he would not fight him. But he broke his pledge and fought him on the day of Uhud. The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — then invoked that he would not escape, and no man among the polytheists was captured other than him. He said: ""O Muhammad, show me favor and leave me for the sake of my daughters, and I give you a pledge that I shall never return to fighting you."" The Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — said: ""Do not wipe your cheeks at Makkah saying, 'I have deceived Muhammad twice.'"" Then he commanded regarding him, and his neck was struck. Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed him, Abu al-'Abbas informed him, al-Rabi' informed him, and al-Shafi'i mentioned it. And we have transmitted this from other than al-Shafi'i in the Book of the Division. [Sahih]"
"18035","(18029) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Isbahani informed us: al-Hasan ibn al-Jaham informed us: al-Husayn ibn al-Faraj informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Umar informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, who said: The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — granted safety among the captives of Badr to Abu 'Azzah 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn 'Umayr al-Jumahi, who was a poet. He had said to the Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — ""O Muhammad, I have five daughters who have nothing; so grant me as charity to them."" So he did. Abu 'Azzah said: ""I give you a solemn pledge that I shall not fight you or ever muster forces against you."" So the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — released him. When the Quraysh set out toward Uhud, Safwan ibn Umayyah came to him and said: ""Come out with us."" He said: ""I have given Muhammad a solemn pledge that I shall not fight him."" Safwan guaranteed that if he were killed, he would place his daughters with his own daughters, and if he survived, he would give him abundant wealth. He kept at him until he went out with the Quraysh on the day of Uhud. He was captured, and no one else from the Quraysh was captured. He said: ""O Muhammad, I was only brought out under duress, and I have daughters; so show me favor."" The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — said: ""Where is the pledge and covenant you gave me? No, by Allah, you shall not wipe your cheeks at Makkah saying, 'I have deceived Muhammad twice.'"" Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib said: The Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — said: ""A believer is not bitten from the same burrow twice. O 'Asim ibn Thabit, bring him forward and strike his neck."" So he brought him forward and struck his neck. Al-Shafi'i — may Allah have mercy upon him — said: Then the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — later captured Thumamah ibn Uthal al-Hanafi and then released him. Then Thumamah ibn Uthal later returned and embraced Islam, and his Islam was excellent. [Very Weak]"
"18036","(18030) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Faqih and Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim al-Muzakki both said: Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us: 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to me: Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to me: that he heard Abu Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — saying: The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — sent a cavalry force toward the land of Najd. They brought back a man called Thumamah ibn Uthal al-Hanafi, the chief of the people of Yamamah. They bound him to one of the pillars of the mosque. The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — came out to him and said: ""What do you have to say, O Thumamah?"" He said: ""I have good words, O Muhammad. If you kill me, you kill a man whose blood has a claim; if you show favor, you show favor to one who is grateful; and if you desire wealth, ask and you shall be given what you wish."" The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — left him until the following day. Then he said: ""What do you have to say, O Thumamah?"" He said: ""I have what I told you."" He repeated it to him. Then on the third day he came to him and repeated it. The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — said: ""Release Thumamah."" So Thumamah went to a palm grove near the mosque, bathed with the water, then entered the mosque and said: ""I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. O Muhammad, by Allah, there was no face on the face of the earth more hateful to me than your face, but now your face has become the most beloved of all faces to me. By Allah, there was no religion more hateful to me than your religion, but now your religion has become the most beloved of all religions to me. By Allah, there was no land more hateful to me than your land, but now your land has become the most beloved of all lands to me. And if your cavalry had taken me while I intended to perform 'Umrah, what would you have thought?"" The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — gave him glad tidings and commanded him to perform 'Umrah. When he arrived in Makkah, some men said to him: ""Have you become a renegade, O Thumamah?"" He said: ""No, by Allah, I have not become a renegade; rather, I have embraced Islam with the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family. By Allah, not a single grain of wheat shall come to you from Yamamah until the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — permits it."" Muslim transmitted it in the Sahih from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna. [Sahih; agreed upon]"
"18037","(18031) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq: Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Islam of Thumamah ibn Uthal al-Hanafi came about when the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — invoked Allah when what was presented to the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — was presented, that Allah might grant him power over him. And what was presented to him, while he still was a polytheist, was that he wished to kill him. Then Thumamah came performing 'Umrah while still upon his polytheism, until he entered al-Madinah and became bewildered therein until he was seized and brought to the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family. He commanded regarding him, and he was bound to one of the pillars of the mosque. The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — came out to him and said: ""What is the matter with you, O Thumamah? Has Allah granted you the opportunity?"" He said: ""That is so, O Muhammad. If you kill, you kill a man whose blood has a claim; if you pardon, you pardon one who is grateful; and if you ask for wealth, it shall be given."" The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — went away and left him until the following day. He passed by him and said: ""What is the matter with you, O Thumamah?"" He said: ""Good, O Muhammad. If you kill, you kill a man whose blood has a claim; if you pardon, you pardon one who is grateful; and if you ask for wealth, it shall be given."" Then the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — turned away from him. Abu Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — said: We, the poor, began to say among themselves: ""What is to be done with the blood of Thumamah? By Allah, a single fat camel from his ransom would be dearer to us than the blood of Thumamah."" When the following day came, the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — passed by him and said: ""What is the matter with you, O Thumamah?"" He said: ""Good, O Muhammad. If you kill, you kill a man whose blood has a claim; if you pardon, you pardon one who is grateful; and if you ask for wealth, it shall be given."" The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — said: ""Release him; I have pardoned you, O Thumamah."" So Thumamah went out until he came to a garden among the gardens of al-Madinah, bathed in it, purified himself, and cleansed his garments. Then he came to the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — while he was sitting in the mosque among his Companions, and said: ""O Muhammad, by Allah, there was no face more hateful to me than your face, no religion more hateful to me than your religion, and no land more hateful to me than your land. Now there has come to be no face more beloved to me than your face, no religion more beloved to me than your religion, and no land more beloved to me than your land. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His slave and His Messenger. O Messenger of Allah, I had set out to perform 'Umrah while still upon the religion of my people. May Allah bless you — facilitate my 'Umrah for me."" So he facilitated it for him and taught him, and he went out to perform 'Umrah. When he arrived in Makkah and the Quraysh heard him speaking about the matter of Muhammad and Islam, they said: ""Thumamah has become a renegade."" They angered him, and he said: ""By Allah, I have not become a renegade; rather, I have believed and trusted Muhammad and believed in him. By Allah, not a grain shall come to you from Yamamah — which was the fertile land of Makkah — until Muhammad — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — permits it."" Then he turned back to his land and prevented the transport of food to Makkah until the Quraysh were sorely distressed. They wrote to the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — asking him by their ties of kinship to write to Thumamah to let them transport food. So the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — did so. [Sahih; agreed upon]"
"18038","(18032) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Ja'far al-Baghdadi informed us: Abu 'Ulathah informed us: my father informed us: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah, who said: Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shamas came to the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — and said: ""Grant me al-Zabir al-Yahudi; I shall repay him for a favor he rendered me on the day of Bu'ath."" So he granted him to him. Thabit went to him and said: ""O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, do you recognize me?"" He said: ""Yes. Would a man not recognize his brother?"" Thabit said: ""I wish to repay you today for a favor you rendered me on the day of Bu'ath."" He said: ""Then do so, for the noble repays the noble."" He said: ""I have done so. I asked the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — and he granted you to me, so I released you from your bond."" Al-Zabir said: ""I have no guide, and you have taken my wife and my children."" Thabit went back to al-Zabir and said: ""The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — has returned to you your wife and your children."" Al-Zabir said: ""I have a wall with clusters of dates, and neither I nor my family have any livelihood except through it."" Thabit went back to the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — and he granted it to him. Thabit returned to al-Zabir and said: ""The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — has returned to you your family and your property. Embrace Islam and you shall be safe."" He said: ""What became of the two companions?"" — and he mentioned some men of his people. Thabit said: ""They have been killed and finished with. Perhaps Allah, Blessed and Exalted, has preserved you for some good."" Al-Zabir said: ""I ask you by Allah, O Thabit, and by the obligation I have toward you from the day of Bu'ath, that you join me with them, for there is no good in life after them."" Thabit mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — and he commanded regarding al-Zabir, so he was killed. Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yasar also mentioned it from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned that he was al-Zabir ibn Bata al-Qurathi. Musa ibn 'Uqbah also mentioned it, and mentioned that he was at that time old and blind. [Weak]"
"18039","(18033) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari in Baghdad informed us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us: Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi informed us: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his father, that the Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — said regarding the captives of Badr: ""If Mut'im ibn 'Adi were alive and spoke to me on behalf of these foul-smelling men, I would release them for his sake."" Al-Bukhari transmitted it in the Sahih from Ishaq ibn Mansur from 'Abd al-Razzaq. [Sahih; Bukhari 3139–4024]"
"18040","(18034) Abu Sahl Muhammad ibn Naysabur al-Marwazi informed us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khanb al-Bukhari informed us: Abu 'Ali al-Hasan ibn Salamah informed us: 'Affan ibn Muslim informed us: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Thabit, from Anas — may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Eighty men from the people of Makkah descended upon the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — and his Companions from Mount al-Tan'im at the time of the Fajr prayer. The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family — took them, but he pardoned them. And the Qur'an was revealed: {And it is He who restrained their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of Makkah after He had given you victory over them} [al-Fath 24]. Muslim transmitted it in the Sahih from the hadith of Yazid ibn Harun from Hammad. [Sahih; Muslim 1808]"
"18041","(18035) Abu Muhammad — Abd Allah ibn Yusuf — informed us: Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan — informed us: Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami — narrated to us: Abd al-Razzaq — informed us: Ma'mar — from al-Zuhri — from Abu Salamah — from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Prophet, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, encamped at a place, and the people dispersed among the thorny trees seeking shade beneath them. The people hung their weapons upon a tree. Then a Bedouin came to his sword, took it, and drew it. He then approached the Prophet, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, and said: ""Who will protect me from you?"" The Prophet, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, said: ""Allah."" The Bedouin sheathed the sword. The Prophet, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, called his Companions and informed them of the Bedouin's deed, while the Bedouin was sitting beside him — and he did not punish him. [Sahih. Agreed upon (muttafaq alayh).] Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Mahmud, and Muslim narrated it from Abd ibn Humayd — both from Abd al-Razzaq."
"18042","(18036) Abu Muhammad — Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari — informed us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar — narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi — narrated to us: Abd al-Razzaq — informed us: Ma'mar — from al-Zuhri — from Abu Salamah — from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the hadith with its meaning. Ma'mar said: Qatadah used to narrate something similar, and he used to mention that a group of Arabs intended to assassinate the Prophet, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, so they sent this Bedouin. And he recited: {And remember the favour of Allah upon you when a people intended…} [Al-Ma'idah 11], the verse. [Sahih. Previously referenced.]"
"18043","(18037) As for ransoming with a person's life, in this regard Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed me: Abu al-Walid — narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Ahmad ibn Nasr — narrated to us: Ali ibn Hujr — (h) and he said: Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim informed me, and the wording is his — narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Salamah — narrated to us: Amr ibn Zurarah ibn Waqid al-Kalabi — both said: narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim — narrated to us: Ayyub — from Abu Qilabah — from Abu al-Muhallab — from Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The tribe of Thaqif were allies of Banu Uqayl. The Thaqif captured two of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family. The Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, captured a man from Banu Uqayl and wounded him. The Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, passed by him while he was bound, and the man called out: ""O Muhammad! O Muhammad!"" He came to him and said: ""What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""Why have you taken me, and why have you taken the one who precedes the pilgrims?"" He said — magnifying the matter: ""You have been taken because of the crime of your allies, the Thaqif."" Then he turned away from him, but the man called him again: ""O Muhammad! O Muhammad!"" He, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, was merciful and tender-hearted, so he returned to him and said: ""What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""I am a Muslim."" He said: ""If you had said that while you had control over your affair, you would have achieved complete success."" Then he turned away from him, and the man called him: ""O Muhammad! O Muhammad!"" So he came to him and said: ""What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""I am hungry, so feed me, and I am thirsty, so give me drink."" He said: ""This is your need."" He (the narrator) said: So he was ransomed for the two men. Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Ali ibn Hujr and others. [Sahih. Muslim 164.]"
"18044","(18038) Abu al-Hasan — Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Abdan — informed us: Ahmad ibn Ubayd al-Saffar — narrated to us: Abu Abd Allah al-Saffar — narrated to us: Ibn Abi Umar — narrated to us: Sufyan — from Ayyub — from Abu Qilabah — from his uncle — from Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Prophet, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, ransomed two Muslims and gave a man from the polytheists. Sufyan said: That is, he took two Muslims and gave a man from the polytheists. [Sahih. Previously referenced.]"
"18045","(18039) As for ransoming with wealth, in this regard Abu al-Qasim — Abd al-Rahman ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abd al-Harfi — in Baghdad — narrated to us: Hamzah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Abbas — narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ghalib — narrated to us: Musa ibn Mas'ud — narrated to us: Ikrimah ibn Ammar — from Abu Zumayl — from Ibn Abbas — from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who said — and most of his hadith was from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: On the day of Badr, he (the Prophet) said: ""What do you see regarding these captives?"" Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""O Prophet of Allah, they are your cousins, your kinsmen, and your brothers. Rather, we should take ransom from them so that we may have strength against the polytheists, and perhaps Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, will guide them to Islam and they will be a support for us."" He said: ""What do you see, O son of al-Khattab?"" I said: ""O Prophet of Allah, I do not see what Abu Bakr sees. Rather, these are the leaders of disbelief and their champions. Bring them close and strike their necks."" But the Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, inclined toward what Abu Bakr said and did not incline toward what I said. So he took ransom from them. When morning came, I went to the Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, and lo, he and Abu Bakr were sitting, weeping. I said: ""O Prophet of Allah, tell me, what are you and your companion weeping for? If I find cause for weeping, I will weep; otherwise, I will feign weeping for your weeping."" He said: ""What my Companions presented to me — your punishment was presented to me closer than this tree"" — and there was a tree nearby at that time. So Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, revealed: {It is not for a Prophet to have captives until he inflicts a slaughter in the land. You desire the fleeting gains of this world, while Allah desires the Hereafter.} [Al-Anfal 67], the verse. Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from another chain from Ikrimah ibn Ammar, adding up to his words: {So enjoy what you have taken as lawful and good} [Al-Anfal 69]. So Allah made the war booty lawful for them, and this has already been discussed in the Book of Division. [Sahih. Muslim 1763.]"
"18046","(18040) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr — both said: narrated to us: Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Sulayman al-Burulusi — narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Ar'arah — (h) and Abu al-Husayn ibn Bashran in Baghdad informed me: he informed me: Abu al-Hasan — Ali ibn Muhammad al-Misri — narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Ishaq ibn Salih — narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Ar'arah — narrated to us: Azhar — from Ibn Awn — from Muhammad — from Ubaydah — from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, said regarding the captives on the day of Badr: ""If you wish, you may kill them; and if you wish, you may ransom them and enjoy the ransom, and a number of you equal to theirs will be martyred."" He said: The last of the seventy was Thabit ibn Qays, who was killed on the day of al-Yamamah. Al-Burulusi added in his narration: Ibn Ar'arah said: ""I referred this matter to Azhar, but he refused except to say: from Ubaydah — from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."" [Sahih.]"
"18047","(18041) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed me: Abd Allah ibn Sa'd — narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Abi Talib — narrated to us: Amr ibn Ali and Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam — both said: narrated to us: Abu Bahr al-Bakrawi — narrated to us: Shu'bah — narrated to us: Abu al-Anbas — from Abu al-Sha'tha' — from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, set the ransom for the captives of the people of Jahiliyyah at four hundred. [Sahih.]"
"18048","(18042) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz — narrated to us: Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Abd al-Jabbar — narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr — from Ibn Ishaq, in the story of Badr, who said: Among the captives was Abu Wadaghah al-Sahmi. His son al-Muttalib came to Madinah and ransomed his father for four thousand dirhams, then departed with him. Then Quraysh sent for the ransom of the captives. Mikraz ibn Hafs came with the ransom of Suhayl ibn Amr. He said: ""Place my leg in place of his leg and release him until he sends you his ransom."" So they released Suhayl and detained Mikraz. He said: Each people ransomed their captive with what they were pleased with. He said: The greatest ransom among the captives of Badr was that of al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, because he was a wealthy man. He ransomed himself with one hundred uqiyyahs of gold. [Da'if (Weak).]"
"18049","(18043) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us: Abu al-Hasan — Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Sakhtawayh — narrated to us: al-Qabani and Hasan ibn Ali ibn Ziyad and Salih ibn Muhammad al-Razi — they said: narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami — narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Fulayh — from Musa ibn Uqbah, who said: and Ibn Shahab said: narrated to us: Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him: that a group of the Ansar sought permission from the Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, and said: ""Give us leave, and we will leave the ransom of our nephew al-Abbas."" He said: ""By Allah, you will not leave a single dirham."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir, and the other hadiths in this chapter have already been discussed in the Book of Division. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 2537, 3049, 4018.]"
"18050","(18044) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed me: Abu Amr ibn Abi Ja'far — informed us: Hasan ibn Sufyan — narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah — narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ulayyah — from Khalid al-Hadha' — from Abu Qilabah — from Abu al-Ash'ath — from Shaddad ibn Aws, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I preserved two things from the Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him and his family. He said: ""Allah has prescribed excellence (ihsan) in everything. So when you kill, kill well; and when you slaughter, slaughter well. Let one of you sharpen his blade and let him give ease to his slaughtered animal."" Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. [Sahih. Muslim 1955.]"
"18051","(18045) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Shawdhab al-Muqriʾ in Wāsiṭ informed us; Aḥmad ibn Sinān narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAdīy ibn Thābit, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade mutilation (al-muthlah) and plundering (al-nuhbay).

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥajjāj ibn Minḥāl and others, from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 55161]"
"18052","(18046) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to me; Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated to me from Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj, from ʿAlqamah ibn Marthad, from Sulaymān ibn Buraydah al-Aslamī, from his father Buraydah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) appointed a commander over an army or a detachment, he would enjoin him to have taqwā of Allah in his own regard and to treat the believers who were with him well. Then he would say: ""Go forth in the name of Allah, and fight in the cause of Allah. Fight those who disbelieve in Allah. Go forth and do not engage in treachery, do not act treacherously, do not mutilate, and do not kill a child.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from another chain from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1731]"
"18053","(18047) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Hammām narrated to us; Qatādah narrated from al-Ḥasan, from Hayyāj ibn ʿImrān al-Burjumī, that a slave of his father ran away. He vowed that if he caught him, he would certainly cut off his hand. When he caught him, he sent me to ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn (may Allah be pleased with him) to ask him. He said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) encouraging charity in his sermon and forbidding mutilation (al-muthlah).

He said: He also sent me to Samurah, and he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) encouraging charity and forbidding mutilation.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: If someone says, ""He cut off the hands and feet of those who stole his camels and gouged out their eyes,"" then Anas ibn Mālik and another man both narrated this from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and they narrated — or one of them narrated — that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) did not deliver any sermon thereafter without enjoining charity and forbidding mutilation.

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar and Anas ibn Mālik narrated it, and this addition is in the hadith of Anas. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18054","(18048) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAbd al-Majīd narrated to us; Ḥumayd narrated from Anas.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Qāsim Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Sūsī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Bashshār narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Hushaym narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ṣuhayb and from Ḥumayd, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), that some people from ʿUraynah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) in Madīnah, but they found its climate uncongenial. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to them: ""If you wish, go out to the camels of the ṣadaqah and drink of their milk and their urine."" They did so and recovered. Then they turned upon the shepherds, killed them, apostatized from Islam, and drove away the herd of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). This reached the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), so he sent in pursuit of them. They were brought, and he cut off their hands and feet, gouged out their eyes, and left them in the Ḥarrah until they died.

This is the wording of the hadith of Hushaym. In the narration of ʿAbd al-Wahhāb from Ḥumayd, he said: ""I do not recall: 'drink their urine.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
"18055","(18049) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us; Abū Saʿīd informed us; al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Abān narrated from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), with the same meaning as the hadith of Ḥumayd, except that he said: ""A group from ʿUkl."" He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) thereafter forbade mutilation (al-muthlah). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded above]"
"18056","(18050) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us; Ibn Abī ʿAdīy narrated from Hishām, from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), with this hadith, and he added: ""Then he forbade mutilation (al-muthlah) thereafter."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded above]"
"18057","(18051) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ narrated to us; Saʿīd narrated from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), that a group from ʿUkl and ʿUraynah — and he mentioned this hadith.

Qatādah said: It reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) would thereafter encourage charity in his sermon and forbid mutilation (al-muthlah).

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn used to reject the hadith of Anas regarding the people of the camels (aṣḥāb al-liqāḥ). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded above]"
"18058","(18052) Abū Zakariyyā and Abū Bakr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Yaḥyā narrated to me; Jaʿfar narrated from his father, from ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn (peace be upon them both), who said: ""No, by Allah! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) did not gouge out any eye, and he did not go beyond cutting off the hands and feet of the people of the camels.""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: The hadith of Anas is a firmly established, sound hadith, and with it is the narration of Ibn ʿUmar, and in both of them together it is stated that he gouged out their eyes. So there is no point in the rejection of the one who rejects it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18059","(18053) The best interpretation is to apply the principle of abrogation (naskh), as Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAffān ibn Muslim narrated to us; Hammām narrated to us; Qatādah narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), that a group from ʿUraynah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) — and he mentioned the hadith.

Qatādah said: And Ibn Sīrīn narrated to me that this was before the ḥudūd were revealed.

In the narration of Hishām from Qatādah there is evidence for this, or it may be interpreted to mean that he did to them what they had done to the shepherds. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
"18060","(18054) That which indicates it is what Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Isḥāq — meaning Ibn Ibrāhīm al-Marwazī — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ghaylān narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Mihrān and Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Sarrāj both said: al-Faḍl ibn Sahl al-Aʿraj narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ghaylān narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ narrated from Sulaymān al-Taymī, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) only gouged out the eyes of those people because they had gouged out the eyes of the shepherds.

This is the wording of the hadith of al-Aʿraj.

In the narration of al-Marwazī: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) only gouged out their eyes because they had gouged out the eyes of the shepherds.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Faḍl ibn Sahl. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1671]"
"18061","(18055) 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us — Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Ja'far al-Rusafi in Baghdad informed us — al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Hasan ibn Sa'id al-Qurashi informed us from his grandfather al-Hasan ibn Sa'id — from Hasin ibn Mukhariq — from Dawud ibn Abi Hind — from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, only likened them to such because they had likened themselves to shepherds."
"18062","(18056) Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Shawdhb al-Wasiti informed us — Ahmad ibn Sinan narrated to us — Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated to us — from 'Adiyy ibn Thabit — from Sa'id ibn Jubayr — from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, said: ""Do not take any living being as a target."" Muslim recorded it in his Sahih from the hadith of Shu'bah, and al-Bukhari also mentioned it."
"18063","(18057) Al-Minhal ibn 'Amr also narrated it from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, as Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Adan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us — Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi informed us — Sulayman ibn Harb informed us — Shu'bah informed us — al-Minhal ibn 'Amr from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, set out on one of the roads of Madinah and saw some boys who had tied up a hen and were shooting at it. When they saw him, they fled. He became angry and said: ""Who did this? Indeed, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, cursed whoever mutilates animals."" Al-Bukhari mentioned it in his Shawahid, and likewise Abu Bishr narrated it from Sa'id ibn Jubayr."
"18064","(18058) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Amr al-Hayri informed me — Abu Ya'la informed us — Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us — Hushaym ibn Bashir informed us — Abu Bishr informed us — from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, passed by some young men of Quraysh who had tied up a bird and were shooting at it, having made every errant arrow belong to the owner of the bird. When they saw Ibn 'Umar, they dispersed. Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: ""Who did this? May Allah curse whoever did this. Indeed, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, cursed whoever takes any living being as a target."" Muslim recorded it in his Sahih from Zuhayr ibn Harb, and both al-Bukhari and Muslim recorded it from the hadith of Abu 'Awanah, from Abu Bishr."
"18065","(18059) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan informed us — 'Ali ibn al-Hasan al-Darabadjirdi informed us — Abu 'Asim informed us — from 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far — from Yazid ibn Abi Habib — from Bukayr ibn 'Abd Allah — from his father — from 'Ubayd ibn Tighlan — from Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, forbade the binding of animals. Abu Ayyub said: ""Even if it were a hen, I would not bind it."""
"18066","(18060) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us — they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Abu Zur'ah al-Dimashqi narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us — from Bukayr ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj — from his father — from 'Ubayd ibn Tighlan — from Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We went out on a military expedition with 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn al-Walid, who was the commander of the people at that time on the frontiers. We encamped in a place in the land of the Byzantines and stayed there. Abu Ayyub had built a mosque, and we would go and sit with him, and he would lead us in prayer, and we would delight in his conversation. He said: By Allah, one evening while we were with him, a man came and said: ""The commander has now been brought four Byzantine captives, and he has ordered that they be bound and shot with arrows until they are killed."" Abu Ayyub stood up in alarm until he came to 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid and said: ""Did you bind them? I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, forbid the binding of animals. I would not like for me to have such and such, even though I bound a hen."" 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid then called for four of his young slaves and freed them in their place. Abu Zur'ah said: 'Ubayd ibn Tighlan was from the people of Palestine; his residence was in 'Asqalan. 'Amr ibn al-Harith also narrated it from Bukayr."
"18067","(18061) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Isa and Ziyad ibn Ayyub both said — Hushaym informed us — Mughirah informed us — from Shibak — from Ibrahim — from Haniy ibn Nuwayrah — from 'Alqamah — from 'Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, said: ""The people most protected from killing are the people of faith."""
"18068","(18062) Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Bistami informed us — Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us — Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Abbad informed us — Sufyan said: I saw 'Amr ibn Dinar, Ayyub, and 'Ammar al-Duhni gathered together, and they mentioned those whom 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, had burned. Ayyub narrated from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he had heard him say: ""If I were there, I would not have burned them, because of the saying of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family: 'Do not punish with the punishment of Allah.' And I would have killed them because of the saying of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family: 'Whoever changes his religion, kill him.'"" 'Ammar said: ""He did not burn them; rather, he dug trenches for them and pierced some of them into others, then smoked upon them until they died."" 'Amr said: The poet has said: Let fate cast me wherever it wills, if it does not cast me into the two pits, when they have gathered firewood and flame — there, death is immediate, not deferred. Al-Bukhari recorded it in his Sahih from 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah from Sufyan, without the statement of 'Ammar and 'Amr."
"18069","(18063) Abu al-Qasim 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Iyadi in Baghdad informed us — Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Nasibi informed us — al-Harith ibn Abi Usamah informed us — Abu al-Nadr informed us — al-Layth narrated to me — Bukayr. And Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us — Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me — al-Hasan ibn Sufyan informed us — Qutaybah ibn Sa'id informed us — al-Layth from Bukayr — from Sulayman ibn Yasar — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, sent us on an expedition and said: ""If you find so-and-so and so-and-so"" — referring to two men of Quraysh — ""then burn them with fire."" Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, said when we were about to set out: ""I had commanded you to burn so-and-so and so-and-so with fire, but none punishes with fire except the Lord of fire. So if you find them, kill them."" The wording of both is the same. Al-Bukhari recorded it in his Sahih from Qutaybah ibn Sa'id."
"18070","(18064) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran in Baghdad informed us — Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz informed us — Yahya ibn Ja'far informed us — al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad informed us — Ibn Jurayj informed us — Ziyad ibn Sa'd informed him — Abu al-Zinad informed him — Hanzalah ibn 'Ali informed him — from Hamzah ibn 'Amr al-Aslami, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, sent a man and said: ""If you find so-and-so or so-and-so, burn him with fire."" When the man turned to leave, he called him back and said: ""None punishes with fire except its Lord."""
"18071","(18065) And it was narrated by Mughirah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman from Abu al-Zinad, as Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abu Dawud informed us: Sa'id ibn Mansur informed us: Mughirah ibn 'Abd al-Rahram al-Hizami informed us, from Abu al-Zinad, who said: And Muhammad ibn Hamzah al-Aslami narrated to me, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) appointed him as commander of a military detachment. He said: ""I set out with it, and he said: 'If you find so-and-so, burn him with fire.' I turned to leave, and he called me back, so I returned to him, and he said: 'If you find so-and-so, kill him, but do not burn him, for none shall punish with fire except the Lord of the Fire.'""

As for the hadith of Usamah ibn Zayd, in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) commanded him to burn upon the Ubnay, and what has been narrated regarding the setting up of the catapult against Ta'if, it does not contradict what we have stated, for it pertains only to fighting the polytheists when they were fortified and resisting. And what has been narrated of the prohibition regarding burning polytheists applies when they are taken captive.

Al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy upon him) likened this to shooting game while it is still able to escape, and then the prohibition against shooting a hen that is not able to escape. And by Allah is the success. [Authenticated; see its predecessor.]"
"18072","(18066) Abu 'Abd al-Hafiz al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Abd al-Majid al-Thaqafi informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from 'Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) captured a man from Banu 'Uqayl and bound him, then threw him into the Harrah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) passed by him while we were with him — or he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) came upon him — riding a donkey with a velvet cloth beneath him. The man called out: 'O Muhammad! O Muhammad!' So the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) came to him and said: 'What is the matter with you?' He said: 'Why have I been taken, and why has the pilgrim been taken?' He said: 'You have been taken in retaliation for the crime of your allies, Thaqif.' Thaqif had captured two of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). He left him and moved on, but the man called out: 'O Muhammad! O Muhammad!' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had mercy on him, so he returned to him and said: 'What is the matter with you?' He said: 'I am a Muslim.' He said: 'If you had said that while you were in control of your own affair, you would have achieved all success.' He said: He left him and moved on, but the man called out: 'O Muhammad! O Muhammad!' He returned to him, and the man said: 'I am hungry, so feed me.' He said — and I think he said: 'And I am thirsty, so give me to drink.' He said: 'This is your need.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) ransomed him for the two men whom Thaqif had captured, and took his she-camel.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, from 'Abd al-Wahhab. [Authenticated. Muslim 1641.]"
"18073","(18067) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) captured Banu al-Mustaliq and Hawazin and other Arab tribes, and imposed slavery upon them, then later showed them favor by freeing them. The scholars of military expeditions differed, and some of them claimed that when the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) released the captives of Hawazin, he said: 'If there had been a complete right of capture over any Arab, it would have been over these people, but it is only captivity and ransom.'"" Al-Shafi'i said: ""Whoever affirms this hadith claims that slavery does not apply to an Arab under any circumstance. This is the view of al-Zuhri, Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, and al-Sha'bi, and it is narrated from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) and 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz."" [Authenticated.]"
"18074","(18068) Al-Shafi'i said: Sufyan informed us, from Yahya ibn Yahya al-Ghassani, from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz.

(Additionally) He said: And Sufyan informed us, from a man, from al-Sha'bi, that 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""An Arab shall not be enslaved.""

He said: And Sufyan informed us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said regarding a mawla who marries a slave woman: ""His child shall be enslaved."" And regarding an Arab who marries a slave woman: ""His child shall not be enslaved; rather, their value shall be owed upon him.""

Al-Shafi'i said: ""And whoever does not affirm the hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) holds that Arabs and non-Arabs are equal, and that slavery applies to them just as it applies to non-Arabs. And Allah knows best.""

Al-Rabi' said: ""And this is what al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy upon him) holds.""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""As for the narration from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) regarding this, al-Shafi'i only mentioned it in the older version, from Muhammad — that is, Ibn 'Umar al-Waqidi — from Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith, from his father, from al-Saluli, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said on the day of Hunayn: 'If there had been a confirmed right of capture over any Arab after this day, it would have been over these people, but it is only captivity and ransom.'""

This is a weak chain of narration, and one cannot use such as evidence.

As for the narration from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding this: [Weak.]"
"18075","(18069) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed us: Abu al-Hasan al-Karazi informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz informed us: Abu 'Ubayd informed us: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash informed us, from Abu Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, who said: ""When 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) stood up to deliver a sermon, he said: 'There is no ownership over an Arab, and we are not one to dispute with a man over something upon which he has shown gratitude (i.e., Islam), but we shall appraise them at five camels.'""

Abu 'Ubayd said: ""He means: the captive in his hand — we shall not take it from him without compensation, because he accepted Islam while in his possession, and neither shall we leave him enslaved when he is an Arab. Rather, he appraised his value at five camels for the one who captured him, and he returns to his lineage as an Arab, as he was.""

The Shaykh said: ""And this narration is disconnected from 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him)."" [Weak.]"
"18076","(18070) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us: Abu Bakr ibn 'Atab informed us: al-Qasim — that is, al-Jawhari — informed us: Ibn Abi Uways informed us: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn 'Uqbah informed us, from his uncle Musa ibn 'Uqbah, who said: Ibn Shihab said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab informed me that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) imposed, for every captive who was ransomed from among the Arabs, six obligatory camels, and he used to judge by that regarding those who married slave women from among the Arabs.""

This is also a mursal narration, though it is sound. [Weak.]"
"18077","(18071) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Isbahani informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us: Ibn Mani' informed us: Dawud ibn Rushayd informed us: Muhammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Qusayt, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, who said: ""A slave woman belonging to some Arabs ran away and came to Wadi al-Qura, arriving at the clan from whom she had fled. A man from Banu 'Udhrah married her, and she bore him a child. Then her master discovered her and took her and her child. 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) judged in favor of the 'Udhri — that is, he awarded him his child — and imposed upon him a ghurrah (a slave or a slave girl) for every male slave and a ghurrah for every female slave. He set the price of the ghurrah, if one was not found, at sixty dinars or seven hundred dirhams upon the people of the towns, and six camels upon the people of the desert.""

The Shaykh said: ""And this pertains to intercourse based on uncertainty (wat' al-shubhah), so the child shall be free, and upon him shall be the value of the child for the owner of the slave woman. It is as though 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) determined the value according to what is narrated in this report, if it is authentic. And Allah knows best. And the imposition of slavery upon the captives of Banu al-Mustaliq and Hawazin is authentic and established, and the favor shown to them by releasing the captives was an act of grace."" [Weak.]"
"18078","(18072) This is clarified in what Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abu Dawud informed us: al-Qa'nabi informed us, from Malik, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Ibn Muhayriz, who said: ""I entered the mosque and saw Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), so I sat with him and asked him about coitus interruptus ('azl). Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) on the expedition of Banu al-Mustaliq and captured captives from among the Arab women. We desired women, and celibacy became difficult for us, and we favored ransom. So we intended to practice coitus interruptus, but then we said: We shall not practice coitus interruptus while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) is among us before we ask him about it. So we asked him about it, and he said: ""It is no harm if you do not do it. There is no soul that is destined to exist until the Day of Judgment except that it will exist.""'""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf, from Malik. [Authenticated. Agreed upon.]"
"18079","(18073) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar informed us: Yunus ibn Bukayr informed us, from Ibn Ishaq, who said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) distributed the captives of Banu al-Mustaliq, Juwayriyyah bint al-Harith fell in the share of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas or a cousin of his. She contracted a mukatabah (manumission contract) with him for herself. She was a woman of great beauty and charm — none who saw her could help but be captivated by her. She came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) seeking his assistance regarding her manumission contract."" 'A'ishah said: ""By Allah, no sooner did I see her than I disliked her, and I said: 'You will experience from her what I have seen.' When she entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), she said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I am Juwayriyyah bint al-Harith, the chief of my people, and there has befallen me of affliction what is not hidden from you. I have contracted a manumission contract for myself, so help me with my contract.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'Or would you like something better than that? I shall pay your contract on your behalf and marry you.' She said: 'Yes.' So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) did that. When the people learned that he had married her, they said: 'These are the in-laws of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family),' and they released what was in their hands of the captives of Banu al-Mustaliq. By Allah, a hundred households of Banu al-Mustaliq were set free because of her. And I do not know any woman who brought greater blessing upon her people than she did."" [Hasan.]"
"18080","(18074) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan informed us: Abu al-'Abbas informed us: Ahmad informed us: Yunus informed us, from Ibn Ishaq, who said: 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: ""We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) at Hunayn, and when he took from Hawazin what he took of their wealth and their captives, the delegation of Hawazin caught up with him at al-Ji'ranah, having accepted Islam. They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we have families and kin, and there has befallen us of affliction what is not hidden from you. Show us favor, as Allah has shown you favor.' Their spokesman, Zuhayr ibn Surad, stood and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, in the enclosures of captives are your aunts, your maternal aunts, and your foster mothers who used to care for you in your childhood.' He mentioned some words and some verses. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'Are your women and children more beloved to you, or your wealth?' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, you have given us a choice between our lineage and our wealth — our children and our women are more beloved to us.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'As for what belongs to me and to the Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib, it is yours. And when I lead the people in prayer, stand and say: ""We seek the intercession of the Messenger of Allah to the Muslims, and of the Muslims to the Messenger of Allah, regarding our children and our women."" I shall give you at that time and intercede for you.' When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) led the people in the Zuhr prayer, they stood and said what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had commanded them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'As for what belongs to me and to the Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib, it is yours.' The Emigrants said: 'And what belongs to us belongs to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family).' And the Ansar said: 'And what belongs to us belongs to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family).' Then al-Aqra' ibn Habis said: 'As for me and Banu Tamim, no.' Al-'Abbas ibn Mirdas al-Sulami said: 'As for me and Banu Sulaym, no.' Banu Sulaym said: 'Rather, what belongs to us belongs to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family).' And 'Uyaynah ibn Badr said: 'As for me and Banu Fazarah, no.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'Whoever among you withholds his right, for every person he withholds, he shall have six camels from the first war booty that comes in. Return to the people their women and their children.'""

The hadith of al-Miswar ibn Makhramah regarding the captives of Hawazin has already been mentioned. [Hasan, without the statement of Surad.]"
"18081","(18075) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym informed us: Ḥāmid ibn ʿUmar al-Bakrāwī informed us: Maslamah ibn ʿAlqamah al-Māzinī narrated from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind from ʿĀmir from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: ""Three things I heard regarding Banī Tamīm from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family); after them I shall never bear hatred for Banī Tamīm. ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) had a vow to free a slave from the progeny of Ismāʿīl. Captives were taken from Balʿanbar, and when those captives were brought, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to her: 'If it pleases you to fulfil your vow, then free one of these as a freedman.' Thus he counted them among the progeny of Ismāʿīl. Then some camels from the ṣadaqah camels were brought, and when their herdsman saw their beauty, he said: 'These are the camels of my people.' Thus he made them his people. And he said: 'They are the fiercest of people in the great battles.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥāmid ibn ʿUmar, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abū Zurʿah from Abū Hurayrah.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2525]"
"18082","(18076) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī in Marw informed us: Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Misʿar narrated from ʿUbayd ibn al-Ḥasan from Ibn Mughaffal that captives from Khawlān arrived, and ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) had a slave from the progeny of Ismāʿīl (whom she had vowed to free). Then captives arrived from Yemen, and she wished to free one of them, but the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade her. Then captives arrived from Muḍar — I think he said, from Banī al-ʿAnbar — and he commanded her to free one.

(T) Shuʿbah corroborated it from ʿUbayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18083","(18077) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī al-Ḥāfiẓ in Baghdād informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad — that is, Ibn Ḥamdān al-Naysābūrī — informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād informed us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Uwaysī informed us: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from Thawr ibn Zayd from Abī al-Ghayth from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Avoid the seven destructive sins."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what are they?"" He mentioned them, and among them he mentioned ""turning one's back on the day of the advance (i.e., fleeing from battle).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Uwaysī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"18084","(18078) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us: Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr informed us: Abū Isḥāq narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah from Sālim, Abū al-Naḍr, the freedman of ʿUmar ibn ʿUbayd Allāh — who was his scribe — said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Awfá (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Do not wish for encounter with the enemy; rather, ask Allah for safety. But when you meet them, be patient, and know that Paradise is beneath the shadows of swords.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad from Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"18085","(18079) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Aḥmad ibn Shaybān al-Ramlī informed us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated — (H) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them) who said: ""When this verse was revealed: {If there are among you twenty steadfast, they will overcome two hundred} [al-Anfāl 65], it was prescribed upon them that twenty should not flee from two hundred. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {Now Allah has lightened your burden, for He knows that there is weakness in you. So if there are among you one hundred steadfast, they will overcome two hundred} [al-Anfāl 66]. Thus He lightened it for them and prescribed upon them that one hundred should not flee from two hundred.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4562]"
"18086","(18080) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Naḍr al-Marwazī informed us: al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah informed us: ʿAffān ibn Muslim informed us: Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated — (H) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: al-Ḥasan — that is, Ibn Sufyān — informed us: Ḥibbān informed us: ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated: al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrīt narrated from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them) who said: ""{If there are among you twenty steadfast, they will overcome two hundred} [al-Anfāl 65] was revealed."" He said: ""It was prescribed upon them that a man should not flee from ten, nor a group from ten times their number. This proved difficult for the people, so {Now Allah has lightened your burden, for He knows that there is weakness in you. So if there are among you one hundred steadfast, they will overcome two hundred} [al-Anfāl 66] was revealed."" He said: ""They were commanded that a man should not flee from two, nor a group from double their number."" Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""The reduction in victory was proportionate to the reduction in the number (required to stand firm).""

This is the wording of ʿAffān's ḥadīth. In the narration of ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak: ""This proved difficult for the Muslims when it was prescribed that one should not flee from ten. Then the alleviation came, and He said: {Now Allah has lightened your burden} [al-Anfāl 66], the verse. When Allah lightened the number for them, the patience (required) was reduced in proportion to what He lightened for them.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Sulamī from Ibn al-Mubārak. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4653]"
"18087","(18081) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Aḥmad ibn Shaybān informed us: Sufyān narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from ʬAṭāʾ from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them) who said: ""If a man flees from two, he has indeed fled. But if he flees from three, he has not fled."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18088","(18082) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylá from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent us on a military expedition. We encountered the enemy, and the people scattered in a rout. We came to Madīnah and opened its gate, and we said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we are those who fled!' He said: 'Rather, you are the ones who return (to the fight), and I am your group (fiʾah).'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18089","(18083) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bushrān informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim informed us: Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylá from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent us on a military expedition. We encountered the enemy, and the Muslims scattered in a rout. I was among those who scattered. I said to myself: 'We shall not enter Madīnah, for we have returned bearing the wrath of Allah.' Then we said: 'Let us enter it and purchase provisions from it.' So we entered and met the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) as he was coming out for the prayer. We said: 'We are those who fled!' He said: 'Rather, you are the ones who return.' We said: 'O Prophet of Allah, we had intended not to enter Madīnah and to take to the sea.' He said: 'Do not do so, for I am the group (fiʾah) of every Muslim.'"" [Ḍaʿīf. Previously mentioned]"
"18090","(18084) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I am the group (fiʾah) of every Muslim."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18091","(18085) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Simāk, who heard Suwayd, who heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""When Abū ʿUbayd was defeated: had they come to me, I would have been their refuge (fiat).""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) mentioned, in the narration of Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Baghdādī from him, aḥādīth regarding the pledge of allegiance upon hearing and obedience according to one's ability, and we have already mentioned them in the chapter on fighting the people of rebellion. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18092","(18086) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us by dictation, Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us in Makkah, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik, from his paternal uncle, that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent him to Ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq, he forbade him from killing women and children. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18093","(18087) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānī informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥarashī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūnus narrated to us, Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Nāfiʿ, that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) informed him: ""A woman was found killed in one of the military expeditions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) disapproved of the killing of women and children.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others from Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18094","(18088) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Walīd informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bashr and Abū Usāmah both said: ʿUbaydullāh, meaning Ibn ʿUmar, narrated to us, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""A woman was found killed in one of those expeditions, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade the killing of women and children.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm from Abū Usāmah. And there has already preceded in the ḥadīth of Buraydah from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""Do not kill a child."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"18095","(18089) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us in Baghdād, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd ibn Nāṣiḥ narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb, meaning Ibn ʿAṭāʾ al-Khaffāf, narrated to us, Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd narrated from al-Ḥasan, from al-Aswad ibn Sarīʿ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and went on an expedition with him. We achieved victory, and the people killed that day to the extent that they killed the children. This reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), so he said: 'What is the matter with a people that killing has exceeded them to the point that they have killed the children?' A man said: 'O Messenger of Allah, they are merely the children of the polytheists.' He said: 'Beware, the best among you are the children of the polytheists.' Then he said: 'Do not kill the children.' He said it three times. And he said: 'Every soul is born upon the fitrah until its tongue expresses it for it, and its parents make it a Jew or a Christian.'""

Abū Jaʿfar said: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd meant by his statement ""Every soul is born upon the fitrah"" — the fitrah upon which He created them when He brought them out from the loins of Ādam, when they affirmed His oneness. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18096","(18090) And likewise Hushaym narrated it from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, and he mentioned in it the hearing of al-Ḥasan from al-Aswad ibn Sarīʿ. Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Muʾammal narrated to us, al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad al-Shaʿrānī narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd informed us from al-Ḥasan, who said: al-Aswad ibn Sarīʿ (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us, saying: ""We were in an expedition of ours…"" and he mentioned the ḥadīth. And Qatādah also narrated it from al-Ḥasan. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18097","(18091) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: al-Ṣaʿb ibn Jathāmah (may Allah be pleased with him) informed me: ""I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) being asked about the people of a settlement of polytheists who are attacked at night, and their women and children are struck. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'They are from them.'"" And ʿAmr ibn Dīnār added from al-Zuhrī: ""They are from their fathers.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbdillāh. And in their narration, and sometimes Sufyān said in the ḥadīth: ""They are from their fathers.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdullāh, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others, all of them from Sufyān. [Ḍaʿīf. Preceded before it]"
"18098","(18092) Abū ʿAbdillāh, Abū Zakariyyā, and Abū Bakr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us from Sufyān, from al-Zuhrī, from Ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik, from his paternal uncle, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""When he sent him to Ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq, he forbade the killing of women and children."" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbdillāh.

Abū ʿAbdillāh added in his narration: al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Sufyān used to hold that the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), 'They are from them,' was a permissibility for their killing, and that the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq abrogated it."" He said: ""And al-Zuhrī, when he narrated the ḥadīth of al-Ṣaʿb ibn Jathāmah, would follow it with the ḥadīth of Ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""The ḥadīth of al-Ṣaʿb ibn Jathāmah was during the ʿUmrah of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). If it was during his first ʿUmrah, then Ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq was killed before it. And it was said: during its year. And if it was during his last ʿUmrah, then it is after the matter of Ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq without doubt. And Allah knows best."" He said: ""And we do not know that he permitted the killing of women and children and then forbade it. The meaning of his prohibition, according to us — and Allah knows best — regarding the killing of women and children is that they be deliberately targeted with killing while they are known and distinguishable from those among them who are commanded to be killed."" He said: ""And the meaning of his statement, 'They are from them,' is that they combine two characteristics: they do not have the ruling of faith that prevents bloodshed, and they do not have the ruling of the abode of faith that prevents raiding the abode.""

Al-Shaykh (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""As for his statement regarding the ḥadīth of al-Ṣaʿb ibn Jathāmah, that it was during his ʿUmrah…"" [Ḍaʿīf. Preceded under no. 18086]"
"18099","(18093) He said that only as a deduction from what Abū ʿAmr al-Bisṭāmī informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, Jaʿfar al-Fāriyābī narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us, al-Zuhrī narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from al-Ṣaʿb ibn Jathāmah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) passed by me while I was at al-Abwāʾ or Waddān, and I presented to him the meat of a wild donkey, but he returned it to me. When he saw the displeasure on my face, he said: 'It is not that we are refusing it to you, but we are in a state of iḥrām.'""

He said: ""And he was asked about the children of the polytheists, that they be attacked at night and their women and children be struck, so he said: 'They are from them.'""

He said: ""And I heard him say: 'There is no protected sanctuary except for Allah and His Messenger.'"" ʿAlī said: ""Sufyān repeated it in this gathering two times, then said: 'I have preserved it other than once that I heard it.' And when he narrated this ḥadīth, he would say: 'And Ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik informed me from his paternal uncle that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), when he sent him to Ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq, forbade the killing of women and children.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"18100","(18094) As for the chronology of the killing of Ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq and the chronology of his ʿUmrah, Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, that is Ibn Yasār, who said: ""When the affair of the Khandaq and the affair of Banū Qurayẓah had concluded, and Abū Rāfiʿ Salām ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq was among those who had rallied the confederates against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), the Khazraj asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) for permission to kill Salām ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq, and he was in Khaybar, so he gave them permission regarding him. Then he raided Banī al-Muṣṭaliq in Shaʿbān in the sixth year. Then he went out in Dhū al-Qaʿdah performing ʿUmrah in the year of al-Ḥudaybiyyah.""

Al-Shaykh (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""Then there was his ʿUmrah that is called ʿUmrat al-Qaḍāʾ, then the ʿUmrah of al-Jiʿrānah, then his ʿUmrah during the year of his Ḥajj — all of these were after that. And the killing of Ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq was before all of them. So how can his prohibition in the story of Ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq regarding the killing of women and children be an abrogation of the ḥadīth of al-Ṣaʿb ibn Jathāmah, which came after it? And they claimed that he emigrated to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and died during the caliphate of Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). If his hearing the ḥadīth from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was after his emigration, then that would also be after the story of Ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq. For in the ḥadīth of al-Hudnah there is evidence that the first time he met the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was… so the meaning of the two aḥādīth would be as al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) indicated, namely the difference between the two situations. And Allah knows best."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18101","(18095) And al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, also cited in support of the permissibility of night raids what Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāā al-Muzakkī — informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: ʿUmar ibn Ḥabīb informed us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAwn, that Nāfiʿ wrote to him informing him that Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, launched a surprise attack on Banī al-Muṣṭaliq while they were heedless among their livestock at al-Muraysīʿ. He killed the fighters and took the offspring captive. Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it in their Ṣaḥīḥ collections from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAwn, as previously mentioned. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"18102","(18096) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī informed us: ʿAbd al-Ṣamad and Abū ʿĀmir informed us, from ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār, who said: Iyās ibn Salamah informed us from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, appointed over us Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and we set out on an expedition against a group of the polytheists. We launched a night raid against them to kill him, and our battle cry that night was ""Amīt, amīt"" (Kill, kill). Salamah said: That night I killed with my own hands seven households of the polytheists. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18103","(18097) As for the ḥadīth which Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us: al-Qaʿnabī informed him, reciting to Mālik, from Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl, from Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, set out for Khaybar and arrived there at night. Whenever he came upon a people by night, he would not launch a raid upon them until morning. When morning came, the Jews came out with their spades and tools. When they saw him, they said: ""Muḥammad, by Allah! Muḥammad and the army!"" The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""Allāhu Akbar! Khaybar is ruined. When we descend upon the courtyard of a people, the morning is wretched for those who have been warned."" Al-Bukhārī recorded it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"18104","(18098) As for the ḥadīth which Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us — both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī informed us, from Ḥumayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, set out for Khaybar and reached it at night. Whenever the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, came upon a people, he would not launch a raid upon them until morning. If he heard the call to prayer (adhān), he would hold back. And if they were not praying, he would launch a raid upon them when morning came. When morning came, he mounted, and the Muslims mounted, and the people of the village came out with their tools and spades. When they saw the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, they said: ""Muḥammad and the army!"" The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""Allāhu Akbar! Khaybar is ruined. When we descend upon the courtyard of a people, the morning is wretched for those who have been warned."" Anas said: ""I was riding behind Abū Ṭalḥah, and my foot was touching the foot of the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"18105","(18099) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī said, in the narration from Anas, that the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, would not launch a raid until morning — this is not a prohibition against launching a raid by night or by day, nor against a surprise attack while the enemy is heedless, and Allah knows best. Rather, it was so that those with him could see how they should launch the raid, as a precaution against being taken by ambush or from a direction they did not expect. For battle can become confused when they launch a raid at night, and some Muslims may kill some others. This befell them in the killing of Ibn ʿAtīk, for they cut off the foot of one of them. Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, had ordered a raid upon one of the Jews, and they killed him — that being the killing of Abū Rāfiʿ, ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq, also known as Salām ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18106","(18100) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Jawharī informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Aḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Shaṭawī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Sābiq informed us: Isrāʾīl informed us, from Abī Isḥāq, from al-Barāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, sent a detachment of the Anṣār against Abū Rāfiʿ the Jew, who lived in the land of the Ḥijāz. He appointed ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAtīk as their commander. Abū Rāfiʿ used to harm the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, and aid others against him. He had a fortress in the land of the Ḥijāz. When they drew near him, the sun had set and the people had taken their flocks to pasture. ʿAbdullāh said to them: ""Stay in your places. I am going to approach the gates and try to enter and kill him."" When he drew near the gate, he wrapped his garment around himself as though he were answering a call of nature, and the people had entered. The gatekeeper called out to him: ""O ʿAbdullāh, if you wish to enter, come in, for I want to close the door."" He said: ""So I entered."" When the people had all entered, he closed the door and then hung the keys on a peg. He said: ""I went to the keys, took them, and opened the door."" Abū Rāfiʿ used to have evening gatherings with his companions in an upper chamber. When those who had been talking with him departed, I climbed up to him. Every time I opened a door, I closed it behind me from the inside. I said: ""If the people become aware of me, they will not let me reach him until I kill him."" I made my way to him, and there he was, in a dark house, in the midst of his family. I did not know where he was in the house. I said: ""Abā Rāfiʿ!"" He said: ""Who is that?"" I lunged toward the voice and struck him a blow that was not decisive, and I was bewildered. It did not avail me anything. He cried out, and I left the house. I did not go far, then I came back and said: ""What was that sound, O Abā Rāfiʿ?"" He said: ""Woe to your mother! A man in the house struck me with a sword."" I struck him a second blow but did not kill him. Then I placed the point of my sword in his belly and leaned upon it until I heard it come out from his back, and I knew that I had killed him. I began opening the doors one by one until I reached a stairway. I placed my foot, thinking I had reached the ground, and I fell on a moonlit night. My leg broke, so I bound it with my turban. Then I set out until I sat by the door. I said: ""By Allah, I will not leave tonight until I know whether I have killed him or not."" When the rooster crowed, the crier stood upon the wall and announced: ""I announce the death of Abā Rāfiʿ, the merchant of the people of the Ḥijāz."" I went quickly to my companions and said: ""Rejoice! Allah has killed Abū Rāfiʿ."" We went to the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, and I informed him. He said: ""Extend your leg."" I extended it, and he wiped it, and it was as though I had never complained of it at all. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 3022, 3023, 4038, 4040]"
"18107","(18101) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā — (ḥ) and al-Manīʿī informed me: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā informed us: Isrāʾīl informed us, from Abī Isḥāq, from al-Barāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, sent men of the Anṣār against Abū Rāfiʿ the Jew, and appointed over them ʿAbdullāh ibn Fulān. He mentioned the ḥadīth in similar terms, except that he said: ""I am going and will approach the gatekeeper."" He said: ""So I entered and concealed myself. When the people entered, he closed the door and then hung the keys on a peg."" Al-Bukhārī recorded it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yūsuf ibn Mūsā, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā. And it is mentioned from another direction that this took place at Khaybar, and that ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays was the one who killed him. And in another ḥadīth, it is stated that ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays struck him and Ibn ʿAtīk finished him off. And in all the narrations, it is stated that Ibn ʿAtīk fell and his leg was broken."
"18108","(18102) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī informed us: Sufyān said: ʿAmr ibn Dīnār informed us, and I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh — (ḥ) and Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Aḥmad ibn Sahl and Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed us, both saying: Ibn Abī ʿUmar informed us: Sufyān informed us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, who heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""Who will deal with Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf? For he has indeed harmed Allah and His Messenger."" Muḥammad ibn Maslamah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, would you like me to kill him?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then permit me to say something."" He said: ""Say it."" So Muḥammad ibn Maslamah went to him and said: ""This man has taken charity from us and has troubled us, and we have grown weary of him."" The vile one, when he heard these words, said: ""By Allah, you will indeed grow weary of him, and you will indeed grow weary of him!"" And he said: ""I knew that your affair would come to this."" He said: ""We cannot surrender him until we see what he will do. And we dislike to leave him after having followed him, until we see what his affair will come to. I have come to you to lend me some dates."" He said: ""Yes, on the condition that you pledge your women as security."" Muḥammad said: ""We will pledge our women to you, when you are the most handsome of the Arabs."" He said: ""Then pledge your children."" He said: ""The people would disgrace our children, saying that we pledged them for a wasq or two wasqs. And it might be said of one of our sons: 'He was pledged for a wasq or two wasqs.'"" He said: ""Then what will you pledge?"" He said: ""We will pledge our weapons (al-ʾumah) to you."" He said: ""Yes."" So he made an appointment to come to him. Muḥammad returned to his companions, and Abū Nāʾilah, the foster-brother of Kaʿb, came with him, along with two other men. He said: ""I will get a firm hold on his head. When I place my hand in his head, then seize the man."" He came at night, and he commanded his companions, and they stood in the shade of the palm trees. Muḥammad came and called out: ""O Abā al-Ashraf!"" His wife said: ""Where are you going at this hour?"" He said: ""It is only Muḥammad ibn Maslamah and my brother Abū Nāʾilah."" He came down wrapped in a single garment, from which the scent of perfume was emanating. Muḥammad said to him: ""How fine your body is and how pleasant your scent!"" He said: ""I have the daughter of so-and-so, and she is the most fragrant of the Arabs."" He said: ""Will you permit me to smell her?"" He said: ""Yes."" Muḥammad placed his hand in his head, then said: ""Will you permit me to let my companions smell her?"" He said: ""Yes."" So he placed his hand in his head, and his companions smelled her. Then he placed his hand in his head a second time, until he felt safe. Then he entangled his hand in his head and pulled him down, and said to his companions: ""Seize the enemy of Allah!"" They went out against him and killed him. Then he came to the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, and informed him. Al-Bukhārī recorded it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdullāh, and Muslim recorded it from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad, both from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"18109","(18103) Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, regarding this story, said: Silkān ibn Salamah embraced him and said: ""Kill me and the enemy of Allah!"" They kept striking him with their swords until one of them thrust him in the belly with a sword, and his intestines came out. They reached him and struck him with their swords. While they were striking him and Silkān was embracing him, they struck ʿAbbād ibn Bishr in the face or the leg without noticing. Then they went out, hastening swiftly, until they reached the cliff of Buʿāth and missed their companion. They went back and found him behind the cliff. They carried him and brought him to his family that night. Ibn Isḥāq mentioned this story from Muḥammad ibn Maslamah, and said: al-Ḥārith ibn Aws ibn Muʿādh was wounded — he was struck in the head and the leg by some of our swords. And Ibn Luhayʿah mentioned something similar from Abī al-Aswad from ʿUrwah."
"18110","(18104) As further evidence, Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī informed us: ʿUmar ibn al-Murraqiʿ ibn Ṣayfī informed me: my father informed me, from his grandfather Rabāḥ ibn Rabīʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, on a military expedition, and he saw people gathered around something. He sent a man and said: ""See what these people have gathered around."" The man came and said: ""Around a slain woman."" He said: ""This one would not have fought."" He said: ""And at the vanguard was Khālid ibn al-Walīd."" He sent a man and said: ""Tell Khālid: Do not kill a woman or a hired servant (ʿasīf)."" [Ḥasan]"
"18111","(18105) And in what Abu Dawud narrated in *al-Marāsīl*, from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl, from Wuhaib, from Ayyūb, from ʿIkrimah: that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) saw a slain woman in al-Ṭāʾif and said: ""Did I not forbid the killing of women? Who is the one responsible for this slain woman?"" A man from the people said: ""It was me, O Messenger of Allah. I had her ride behind me, and she intended to throw me off and kill me."" So the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) ordered that she be buried. [Weak]"
"18112","(18106) And from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl, from Wuhaib, and from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr, from Ḥammād ibn Zayd — both of them from Ayyūb, from ʿIkrimah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) besieged the people of al-Ṭāʾif, a woman appeared and exposed her private parts, saying: ""Here I am — shoot!"" So a man from the Muslims shot at her and did not miss her. In the ḥadīth of Wuhaib: ""They did not fail to kill her, so the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) ordered that she be buried."" Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad — informed us both; Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Fasawī al-Dāwūdī informed us; Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us, and he narrated the two aḥādīth. [Weak. Preceded before it.]"
"18113","(18107) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Isḥāq; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhā), that she said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) did not kill any woman from Banī Qurayẓah except one woman. By Allah, she was with me laughing heartily — on her back and on her belly — while the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) was killing their men in the marketplace, when a caller called out her name: 'Where is so-and-so?' She said: 'Here I am, by Allah.' I said: 'Woe to you! What is the matter with you?' She said: 'I am to be killed, by Allah.' I said: 'Why?' She said: 'For a deed I committed.' So she was taken away and her neck was struck. I have never ceased to marvel at her cheerfulness and her abundant laughter, even though she knew that she would be killed.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) mentioned, in the narration of Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Baghdādī from him, from his companions, that she had directed a millstone upon Maḥmūd ibn Maslamah, crushing his head, and so she was killed in retaliation for that. He said: ""It may also be that she had accepted Islam and then apostatized and joined her people, and so she was killed for that. And other possibilities also exist.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) said: ""The report is not established as sound for any specific reason for which she was killed. And it has also been said that Maḥmūd ibn Maslamah was killed at Khaybar and was not killed on the day of Banī Qurayẓah."" [Ḥasan]"
"18114","(18108) He cited as evidence the text of the ḥadīth which Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of; Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus narrated to us, from Ibn Isḥāq; ʿAbdullāh ibn Sahl — one of Banī Ḥārithah — narrated to me, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""Marḥab the Jew came out from the fortress of Khaybar, having gathered his weapons, and he was chanting in rajaz verse, saying: 'Who will come forth to duel?' The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: 'Who will take on this man?' Muḥammad ibn Maslamah said: 'I will, O Messenger of Allah. By Allah, I am the one whose kinsman has been killed (al-mawtūr), seeking vengeance — they killed my brother yesterday.'"" And he mentioned the ḥadīth.

The Shaykh (raḥimahullāh) said: ""And what is transmitted to us regarding the story of this woman is…"" [Ḥasan]"
"18115","(18109) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAlī — Makhlad ibn Jaʿfar al-Daqqāq — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Jarīr narrated to us, from what Muḥammad ibn Ḥumayd narrated to them, from Salamah, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq; and al-Ḥārith ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Saʿd, from al-Wāqidī, that they said: ""Indeed, a woman from Banī Qurayẓah directed a millstone upon Khallād ibn Suwayd ibn Thaʿlabah al-Khazrajī, crushing his head. And it was mentioned that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: 'For him is the reward of two martyrs.' So the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) killed her, as was mentioned. And Khallād ibn Suwayd had witnessed Badr, Uḥud, al-Khandaq, and Banī Qurayẓah.""

This is from the statement of Ibn Isḥāq and al-Wāqidī, and it is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected). [Weak]"
"18116","(18110) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — and Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqrīʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāriṣ al-Aṭṭār said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; al-Layth ibn Saʿd informed me.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us; Tamīm ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Rumḥ narrated to us. Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā said: ""informed us,"" and the others said: ""narrated to us"" — al-Layth, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) set fire to the palm trees of Banī al-Naḍīr and cut them down, and they were at al-Buwayrah. So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: **""Whatever palm trees you cut down or left standing on their trunks, it was by the permission of Allah, so that He might disgrace the defiantly disobedient.""** [Al-Ḥashr 5]

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, Qutaybah, and Ibn Rumḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
"18117","(18111) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Abū al-Qāsim — Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭabarānī — informed us; Muʿādh ibn al-Muthannā and Yūsuf al-Qāḍī said: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us, from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) cut down the palm trees of Banī al-Naḍīr and set them on fire.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Kathīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
"18118","(18112) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Hannād ibn al-Sarī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) cut down the palm trees of Banī al-Naḍīr and set them on fire. Regarding this, Ḥassān ibn Thābit said:

*""And easy upon the nobles of Banī Luʾayy was a blazing fire at al-Buwayrah, spreading far and wide.""*

Regarding this, this verse was revealed: **""Whatever palm trees you cut down or left standing on their trunks…""** [Al-Ḥashr 5]

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Hannād ibn al-Sarī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
"18119","(18113) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād; Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī — informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Maryam informed us; ʿAmr ibn Abī Salamah informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ al-Ṣāʾigh informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) set fire to some of the palm trees of Banī al-Naḍīr and cut down some. And regarding this, a poem was recited:

*""And easy upon the nobles of Banī Luʾayy was a blazing fire at al-Buwayrah, spreading far and wide.*

*You left your cooking pots with nothing in them, while the people's pots were boiling hot.""*

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
"18120","(18114) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Ḥusayn — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — informed me; Abū al-Mundhir — Rajāʾ ibn al-Jārūd — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; Juwayriyah ibn Asmāʾ informed us, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) set fire to the palm trees of Banī al-Naḍīr. He said: Regarding this, Ḥassān said:

*""And easy upon the nobles of Banī Luʾayy was a blazing fire at al-Buwayrah, spreading far and wide.""*

Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith responded to him:

*""May Allah perpetuate that deed, and may fire blaze in its outskirts.*

*You will know which of us is free from blame, and you will know which of our lands suffers loss.""*

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Naṣr, from Ḥabbān, from Juwayriyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
"18121","(18115) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak — informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far al-Isbahani — informed us; Yunus ibn Habib — narrated to us; Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi — narrated to us; Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar — narrated from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Usamah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) commanded me to launch a surprise raid upon Ubnay at dawn and to set it ablaze. [Da'if]"
"18122","(18116) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari — informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasah — narrated to us; Abu Dawud al-Sijistani — narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr al-Ghazzi — said: I heard Abu Mushir being addressed: ""Ubnay."" He said: ""We know it — it is Yubna in Palestine."" [Sahih]"
"18123","(18117) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz — informed us; Abu Ja'far — Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Baghdadi — narrated to us; Abu 'Ulaathah — Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Khalid — narrated to us; my father — narrated to us; Ibn Lahi'ah — narrated from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) encamped at the elevated ground near the fortress of Ta'if and besieged them for more than ten nights. The tribe of Thaqif fought him with arrows and stones from within the fortress of Ta'if. Many casualties occurred among the Muslims and among the Thaqif. The Muslims cut down some of the grapevines of the Thaqif to enrage them thereby. 'Urwah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) had commanded the Muslims, when they besieged the Thaqif, that every man among them should cut down five date palms or five grapevines. Then 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) came to him and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, they are untrodden lands whose fruits have not been eaten."" So he commanded them to cut down only those whose fruits had been eaten, one after another. [Da'if]"
"18124","(18118) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Faddal al-Qattan — informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Attab — narrated to us; al-Qasim ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Mughirah — narrated to us; Ibn Abi Uways — narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn 'Uqbah — narrated to me; Musa ibn 'Uqbah, regarding the expedition of Ta'if, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) encamped at the elevated ground near the fortress of Ta'if for more than ten nights, fighting them. He mentioned that they cut down a portion of their grapevines to enrage them with it. So the Thaqif said: ""Do not destroy the properties, for they belong to us or to you."" He said: The Muslims sought his permission to assault the fortress. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""I do not think we will conquer it, and we have not been granted permission regarding it at this time."" [Da'if]"
"18125","(18119) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr — narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas — informed us; al-Rabi' — said: al-Shafi'i said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) set up a manjaniq (catapult) or an 'arradah (battering ram) against the people of Ta'if. [Da'if]"
"18126","(18120) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakkī — informed us; Ahmad ibn Sulman — said: It was recited to 'Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad while I was listening; 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr al-Basri, who was a hafiz, narrated to us; Hisham ibn Sa'd — narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Abu 'Ubaydah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) besieged the people of Ta'if and set up the manjaniq against them for seventeen days. Abu Qilabah said: This hadith was rejected regarding him.

The Sheikh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: It is as though the rejection was directed at its chain being connected. And it is also possible that what was rejected was their being pelted with stones by the catapults on that day. For Abu Dawud narrated in al-Marasil from Abu Salih, from Abu Ishaq al-Fazari, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya — that is, Ibn Abi Kathir — who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) besieged them for a month. I said: ""Has it reached you that he pelted them with the manjaniq?"" He rejected that and said: ""We do not know of this.""

The Sheikh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Thus said Yahya — that it had not reached him — while others claimed that it had reached him. Abu Dawud narrated in al-Marasil from Muhammad ibn Bashshar, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sufyan, from Thawr, from Makhul, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) set up the manjaniq against the people of Ta'if. And al-Shafi'i mentioned it in al-Qadim. [Da'if]"
"18127","(18121) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Muhammad — informed us with this and with the hadith of Yahya; Abu al-Husayn al-Fasawi — narrated to us; Abu 'Ali al-Lu'lu'i — narrated to us; Abu Dawud — and he mentioned both of them. And al-Waqidi mentioned it from his sheikhs, as Makhul mentioned it, and he claimed that the one who suggested it was Salman al-Farisi. And al-Shafi'i mentioned in al-Qadim the hadith of Ibn al-Mubarak from Musa ibn 'Ali, from his father, that 'Amr ibn al-'As set up the manjaniq against the people of Alexandria. [Da'if]"
"18128","(18122) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah — informed us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Khamirawayh — informed us; Ahmad ibn Najdah — narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' — narrated to us; Ibn al-Mubarak — informed us; Ibn Lahi'ah — narrated to me; al-Harith ibn Yazid and Yazid ibn Abi Habib, regarding the conquest of Caesarea, said: They used to pelt it every day with sixty manjaniqs. This was during the time of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), until Allah granted conquest at the hands of Mu'awiyah and 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr. [Da'if]"
"18129","(18123) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr — both said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Hilal ibn al-'Ala' — narrated to us; Abu Rabi'ah al-'Amiri — narrated to us; Abu 'Awanah — narrated from Harun ibn Sa'id, from Abu Salih al-Hanafi, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) commanded me to contaminate the waters of the wells of Badr.

(T) And similarly it was narrated by Yusuf ibn Khalid ibn 'Umayr from Harun. (J) And Yusuf and Abu Rabi'ah — Fihd ibn 'Awf — are both weak.

(T) And Abu Dawud narrated in al-Marasil from Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd, from Hammad, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) consulted on the day of Badr, and al-Hubab ibn al-Mundhir said: ""It seems to me that you should contaminate all the waters except one, so that we may meet the people at it."" [Mawdu']"
"18130","(18124) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz — narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar — narrated to us; Yunus — narrated from Ibn Ishaq; Talhah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr al-Siddiq — narrated to me, saying: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) used to command his commanders when he sent them during the Apostasy Wars: ""When you approach a settlement, launch a surprise raid from all sides, kill, set fire, and wreak havoc in killing and wounding. Let no weakness be seen on your account on account of the death of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family)."" [Da'if]"
"18131","(18125) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Khamīrūyah al-Karābīsī al-Harawī informed us therein: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Yūnus ibn Yazīd from Ibn Shihāb from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab that Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) when he dispatched the troops towards Syria — Yazīd ibn Abī Sufyān, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and Sharḥabīl ibn Ḥasanah — he said: When they mounted, Abū Bakr walked with the commanders of his troops to bid them farewell, until he reached Thaniyyat al-Wadāʿ. They said: ""O Khalīfah of the Messenger of Allah, do you walk while we are mounted?"" He said: ""Indeed, I seek reward for these steps of mine in the cause of Allah."" Then he began to admonish them, saying: ""I enjoin you with taqwā of Allah. Fight in the cause of Allah and combat those who disbelieve in Allah, for Allah is the One who aids His religion. Do not commit treachery, do not deceive, do not show cowardice, do not cause corruption in the earth, and do not disobey what you are commanded. If you meet the enemy from among the polytheists, if Allah wills, then invite them to three things. If they respond to you, then accept from them and refrain from them. Invite them to Islam; if they respond to you, then accept from them and refrain from them. Then invite them to move from their land to the land of the Muhājirūn; if they do so, then inform them that they shall have the same rights as the Muhājirūn and upon them shall be the same obligations as upon the Muhājirūn. If they enter Islam and choose their own land over the land of the Muhājirūn, then inform them that they are like the Bedouin Muslims: the decree of Allah that is binding upon the believers shall be binding upon them, and they shall have no share in the fayʾ (war spoils) and the ghanāʾim (booty) until they strive in jihād alongside the Muslims. If they refuse to enter Islam, then invite them to the jizyah; if they comply, then accept from them and refrain from them. If they refuse, then seek Allah's help against them and fight them, if Allah wills. Do not drown bees, do not burn them, do not hamstring livestock, nor cut down a fruit-bearing tree, do not demolish a church, do not kill children, the elderly, or women. You will find people who have confined themselves in monasteries — leave them and what they have confined themselves for. And you will find others in whom Satan has made nests in the crowns of their heads — when you find such people, strike their necks, if Allah wills."" [Weak]"
"18132","(18126) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. He said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal say: I heard my father say: ""This is a munkar (objectionable) hadith. I do not think there is anything of this sort. This is the speech of the people of Syria."" My father rejected it from Yūnus, from the hadith of al-Zuhrī, as though he (Yūnus) had it from other than al-Zuhrī."
"18133","(18127) Abū Saʿīd informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""Perhaps the matter of Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) in prohibiting the cutting of fruit-bearing trees was because he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) inform that the lands of Syria would be conquered for the Muslims. Since it was permissible for him to cut or to leave, he chose to leave them out of consideration for the Muslims — not because he deemed it forbidden, for he was present with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) during the burning of the trees of Banū al-Naḍīr, Khaybar, and al-Ṭāʾif."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18134","(18128) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Hishām ibn Zayd from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever kills a sparrow or anything smaller than that without its right, Allah will question him about its killing."" It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what is its right?"" He said: ""That you slaughter it and eat it, and that you do not cut off its head and throw it."" Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade the muṣbūrah (a bird tied up and used as a target)."" [Weak]"
"18135","(18129) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Hishām ibn Zayd who said: I entered with Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) upon al-Ḥakam ibn Ayyūb, and he saw boys or young men who had set up a hen as a target to throw at it. Anas said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade that animals be tied up and used as targets (ṣabr al-bahāʾim)."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Walīd, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"18136","(18130) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hāshim narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Abī al-Zubayr from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade that any animal be killed by being tied up and used as a target (ṣabr)."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1959]"
"18137","(18131) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrānjānī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) dispatched armies to Syria, and he mentioned the hadith regarding his admonition, in which he said: ""Do not hamstring a sheep or a camel except for food."" [Weak]"
"18138","(18132) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīrūyah informed us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Maʿmar from Abī ʿImrān al-Jawnī that Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) dispatched Yazīd ibn Abī Sufyān to Syria and walked with him. He mentioned the hadith, in which he said: ""Do not slaughter a camel or a cow except for food."" [Weak]"
"18139","(18133) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us, and he said: al-Shāfiʿī said: Abū Yūsuf narrated to us: Some of our shaykhs narrated from ʿUbādah ibn Nusayy from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ghanm that it was said to Muʿādh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him): ""The Romans take what is captured from our horses and breed them and fight upon them. Should we hamstring what is captured from our horses?"" He said: ""No. They are not the people to take vengeance upon you. Tomorrow they will be your slaves or your ahl al-dhimmah."" Abū Saʿīd added in his narration in another place: Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""And it has reached us from Abī Umāmah al-Bāhilī (may Allah be pleased with him) that he instructed his son not to hamstring a horse that has become exhausted. And from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz (may Allah have mercy on him) that he forbade the hamstringing of a beast when it stands still. And from Qabīṣah: his horse became exhausted in the land of the Romans, and he left it and forbade its hamstringing."" He informed us: Whoever heard Hishām ibn al-Ghāz narrating from Makḥūl that he asked him about it and he forbade it, and said: ""Indeed, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade mutilation (al-muthlah)."""
"18140","(18134) Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ narrated to us: Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Asadī al-Hamadhānī narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Kisāʾī narrated to us: Ādam narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us: al-Minhāl narrated: I was walking with Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, and he said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: ""May Allah curse the one who mutilates an animal."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 5515]"
"18141","(18135) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — informed us, they both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us: Abu 'Utba — informed us: Baqiyya — informed us: Khalid ibn Humayd — informed us: 'Umar ibn Sa'id al-Lakhmi — from Yazid ibn Abi Habib — from Abi Rahm al-Samā'i, the companion of the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) — that the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Whoever hamstrings an animal, a quarter of his reward is lost; whoever burns a hive of bees, a quarter of his reward is lost; whoever deceives his partner, a quarter of his reward is lost; and whoever disobeys his imam, all of his reward is lost."" In this chain of narration there is weakness, and in the first part there is sufficiency. [Weak]"
"18142","(18136) As for the hadith which Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar — informed us: Yunus ibn Bukayr — from Ibn Ishaq — informed me: Yahya ibn 'Abbad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr — from his father — said: informed me my father who nursed me, and he was one of the children of Murra ibn 'Awf, who said: By Allah, it is as though I am looking at Ja'far ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) on the day of Mu'ta, when he leapt from his bay mare and hamstrung her, then advanced and fought until he was killed. [Hasan]"
"18143","(18137) For Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr — informed us: Abu al-'Abbas — informed us: al-Rabi' — said: al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""If someone objects that it has been narrated that Ja'far ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) hamstrung his horse at the time of battle, I do not preserve this from any transmission that stands up when isolated, nor do I know it to be well-known among the common scholars of military campaigns."" [Sahih]"
"18144","(18138) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari — informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasa — said: Abu Dawud al-Sijistani said: ""This hadith is not that strong, and there have been many narrations from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) concerning it."" The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: The hafizs are cautious about what Ibn Ishaq narrates on his own authority, and even if it is authentic, perhaps the prohibition did not reach Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with him). And Allah knows best. [Sahih]"
"18145","(18139) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr — informed us: Abu al-'Abbas al-Asamm — informed us: al-Rabi' — said: al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""Hanzala ibn al-Rahib hamstrung the horse of Abu Sufyan ibn Harb on the day of Uhud, and his horse was so affected by it that he fell off it. He then sat upon his chest to slaughter him, but Ibn Sha'ub saw him and rushed upon him like a lion, killed him, and rescued Abu Sufyan from beneath him."" He said: Abu Sufyan thereafter recited:

Had I willed, a lean, dark-bay mare would have saved me,
And my colt would have continued to drive away their dog.

I would fight them all and cry: ""Come to Ghalib!""

And I would not bear any gratitude to Ibn Sha'ub,
From them, from the morning until the approach of sunset,
And I would repel them with a firm pillar.

[Weak]"
"18146","(18140) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar — informed us: Yunus ibn Bukayr — from Ibn Ishaq — from al-Zuhri and others, regarding the story of Uhud. He mentioned the story of Hanzala with Abu Sufyan and the assistance of Ibn Sha'ub to Abu Sufyan and his killing of Hanzala, except that he did not mention the hamstringing. Then he mentioned the verses of Abu Sufyan in a manner similar to what al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy upon him) had mentioned, and added to them. Ibn Ishaq said: And the name of Ibn Sha'ub was Shaddad ibn al-Aswad — thus he said. And al-Waqidi has mentioned in this story that he hamstrung his horse. [Weak]"
"18147","(18141) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us: Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Isfahani — informed us: al-Hasan ibn al-Jahm — informed us: al-Husayn ibn al-Faraj — informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Umar al-Waqidi — from his shaykhs, who mentioned the story of Hanzala. They said: Hanzala ibn Abi 'Amir (may Allah be pleased with him) took his weapon and joined the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) at Uhud while he was straightening the ranks. When the polytheists were exposed, Hanzala intercepted Abu Sufyan ibn Harb and struck the hamstring of his horse. The horse was affected and Abu Sufyan fell to the ground. He began to shout: ""O company of Quraysh, I am Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, and Hanzala wishes to slaughter me with the sword!"" His voice reached men who did not turn to him in the rout, until al-Aswad ibn Sha'ub saw him. He charged at Hanzala with his spear and pierced him, and Abu Sufyan fled. [Very Weak]"
"18148","(18142) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us: Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim — informed us: Ahmad ibn Salama — informed us: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim — informed us: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi — informed us: 'Ikrima ibn 'Ammar al-Yamami — from Iyas ibn Salama — from his father, who mentioned the hadith regarding al-Hudaybiyyah and their return to Madinah. He said: The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent some mounts with Rabah, a slave of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family). He said: I went out with him on the horse of Talha, driving the mounts with the caravan. When we awoke in the morning, lo, 'Abd Rahman ibn 'Uyayna had raided the mounts of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and drove them all off and killed his herdsman. I said: ""O Rabah, take this horse and deliver it to Talha ibn 'Ubayd Allah, and inform the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) that the polytheids have raided his flocks."" He said: Then I stood upon a hillock and faced Madinah, calling out three times: ""O morning!"" Then I went out in the tracks of the people, shooting them with arrows and reciting:

I am the son of al-Akwa',
And today is the day of al-Rudda'.

He said: I shot a man and the arrow would strike his shoulder, and I would say:

Take it! I am the son of al-Akwa',
And today is the day of al-Rudda'.

He said: By Allah, I continued to shoot them and hamstring their mounts. Whenever a horseman returned to me, I would go to a tree and sit in its shade, shoot him, and hamstring his horse. When the mountain narrowed and they entered a narrow pass, I climbed the mountain and began pelting them with stones. He said: I continued in that manner pursuing them until there did not remain a single camel from the mounts of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) except that I had placed it behind my back, and they left between me and it. He mentioned the hadith until he said: I did not leave my place until I saw the horsemen of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) emerging through the trees, and the first of them was al-Akram al-Asadi, followed by Abu Qatada al-Ansari, followed by al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad al-Kindi. I took the reins of al-Akram's horse and said: ""O al-Akram, the enemy is few; beware lest they cut you off before the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and his Companions reach us."" He said: ""O Salama, if you believe in Allah and the Last Day, and know that Paradise is true and Hellfire is true, then do not come between me and martyrdom."" So I let him go. He met 'Abd Rahman ibn 'Uyayna, and al-Akram hamstrung 'Abd Rahman's horse, and 'Abd Rahman stabbed and killed him. 'Abd Rahman then mounted his own horse. Abu Qatada caught up with 'Abd Rahman, stabbed and killed him, and 'Abd Rahman hamstrung his horse. Abu Qatada then mounted al-Akram's horse, and they went out fleeing. And he mentioned the hadith. Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim. [Sahih — agreed upon]"
"18149","(18143) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari — informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasa — informed us: Abu Dawud — informed us: Qutayba ibn Sa'id — informed us: al-Layth ibn Sa'd — from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id — that he heard Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: The Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent a cavalry force toward Najd, and they brought a man from Banu Hanifa called Thumama ibn Uthal, the chief of the people of al-Yamama. They bound him to one of the pillars of the mosque, and he mentioned the hadith. Both al-Bukhari and Muslim have narrated it in full as mentioned. [Sahih — agreed upon]"
"18150","(18144) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Hafiz — informed us: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan al-Hilali — informed us: Abu Ma'mar 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr — informed us: 'Abd al-Warith — informed us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq — from Ya'qub ibn 'Utba — from Muslim ibn 'Abd Allah — from Jundub ibn Makith, who said: The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent 'Abd Allah ibn Ghalib al-Laythi on a military expedition, and I was among them. He ordered them to launch a surprise raid on Banu al-Mulawwah at al-Kadid. We set out until, when we were at al-Kadid, we met al-Harith ibn al-Barsa' al-Laythi. We captured him and he said: ""I have only come desiring Islam, and I have only gone out to the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."" We said: ""If you are a Muslim, our binding you for a day and a night will not harm you; but if you are otherwise, we will secure you firmly."" So we bound him tightly. [Weak]"
"18151","(18145) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq: al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Maʿbad narrated to me from some of his family, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: When the evening of the day of Badr came and the captives were bound in fetters, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) remained awake during the first part of the night. His Companions said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, why do you not sleep?"" A man from the Anṣār had taken al-ʿAbbās captive. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""I heard the groaning of my uncle al-ʿAbbās in his bonds."" So they released him, and he fell silent, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) slept.

[Weak]"
"18152","(18146) And with his chain of narration, from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr narrated to me from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Asʿad ibn Zurārah, who said: The captives were brought when they were brought to Madīnah, and Sawdah bint Zamʿah (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), was at the house of the family of ʿAfrāʾ, in the resting place of ʿAwf and Muʿawwidh, the two sons of ʿAfrāʾ — and this was before the injunction of ḥijāb was revealed. Sawdah said: ""By Allah, I was with them when we were brought and it was said: 'These are the captives; they have been brought.' I returned to my house, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was in it. And there was Abū Yazīd — Suhayl ibn ʿAmr — in a corner of the room, his two hands bound together to his neck with a rope. By Allah, when I saw Abū Yazīd in that state, I could not restrain myself from saying: 'O Abū Yazīd, have you surrendered your hands? Would you not die with honour?'"" She said: ""I did not come to my senses until the voice of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) called from the house: 'O Sawdah, by Allah and by His Messenger!' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, by the One who sent you with the truth, when I saw Abū Yazīd with his two hands bound to his neck with the rope, I could not restrain myself from saying what I said.'""

[Ḥasan]"
"18153","(18147) Al-Shaykh al-Imām Abū al-Ṭayyib Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated to us by dictation: Abū ʿAmr Ismāʿīl ibn Nujayd al-Sulamī narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Abū ʿĀṣim al-Nabīl narrated to us: Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAṭāʾ, from Dhakwān, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) brought a captive to her while women were with her, and they distracted her from him, so the captive escaped. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) came and said: ""O ʿĀʾishah, where is the captive?"" She said: ""Women were with me and they distracted me from him, so he left."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""May Allah cut off your hand."" He went out and sent someone in pursuit of him, and the captive was brought back. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) entered, and ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) had extended her hands. He said: ""What is the matter with you?"" She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you invoked the cutting off of my hand against me, and I am hanging my hand, waiting for someone to cut it off."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Have you gone mad?"" Then he raised his hands and said: ""O Allah, for whomever I invoked a curse upon, make it an expiation and a purification for him.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18154","(18148) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) dispatched armies to Syria. He went out walking with Yazīd ibn Abī Sufyān, who was the commander of one of the four quarters of those forces. They claimed that Yazīd said to Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Either you ride, or I shall dismount."" Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: ""You shall not dismount, nor shall I ride. I seek reward for these steps of mine in the cause of Allah."" Then he said: ""You will find a people who claim that they have devoted themselves to Allah; leave them and what they claim they have devoted themselves to Him. And you will find a people who have shaved the tops of their heads of hair; strike what they have shaved with the sword. And I enjoin you upon ten things: do not kill a woman, nor a child, nor a decrepit old man; do not cut down a fruit-bearing tree; do not destroy any inhabited place; do not slaughter a sheep or a camel except for food; do not burn bees nor drown them; and do not commit treachery, and do not show cowardice.""

And we have narrated it in the ḥadīth of al-Zuhrī from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him), as has preceded in the issue of burning.

[Weak]"
"18155","(18149) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn al-Qāsim narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Mālik al-Shāmī, who said: Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) equipped Yazīd ibn Abī Sufyān and sent him to Syria as a commander, and he walked with him, and he mentioned the ḥadīth with its meaning.

[Weak]"
"18156","(18150) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq: Ṣāliḥ ibn Kaysān narrated to me, saying: When Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) sent Yazīd ibn Abī Sufyān to Syria over one of the four quarters, Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) went out with him, advising him, while Yazīd was riding and Abū Bakr was walking. Yazīd said: ""O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah, either you ride, or I shall dismount."" He said: ""You shall not dismount, nor shall I ride. I seek reward for these steps of mine in the cause of Allah. O Yazīd, you will come upon a land in which you will be given varieties of food; so mention the name of Allah over the first of it and praise Him for the last of it. And you will find a people who have confined themselves in these monasteries; leave them and what they have confined themselves for. And you will find a people upon whose heads the Satan has taken his seats — meaning the shāmisah (tonsured monks) — so strike those necks. Do not kill a decrepit old man, nor a woman, nor a child. Do not destroy any inhabited place. Do not cut down a tree except for benefit. Do not slaughter any beast except for benefit. Do not burn bees nor drown them. Do not act treacherously, nor mutilate, nor show cowardice, nor commit embezzlement. And Allah will surely aid whoever aids Him and His Messengers, even in absence. Indeed, Allah is All-Powerful, All-Mighty. I entrust you to Allah and convey my salām to you."" Then he departed.

[Weak]"
"18157","(18151) And with his chain of narration, from Ibn Isḥāq: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, and he said to me: ""Do you know why Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) made a distinction, commanding the killing of the shāmisah (tonsured monks) while prohibiting the killing of monks (ruhbān)?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""It is because of their confining themselves."" He said: ""Yes, but the shāmisah engage in fighting and fight, whereas the monks hold the opinion that they should not fight. And Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has said: 'And fight in the cause of Allah those who fight you.' (Al-Baqarah, 190)""

[Weak]"
"18158","(18152) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah narrated to us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīruwayh narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Maʿmar, from Abū ʿImrān al-Jawnī, that Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) sent Yazīd ibn Abī Sufyān to Syria, and he walked with him, bidding him farewell. Yazīd said: ""I dislike that you should be on foot while I am mounted."" He said: ""You have gone out as a warrior in the cause of Allah, and I seek reward for this walking of mine with you."" Then he advised him and said: ""Do not kill a child, nor a woman, nor an elderly old man, nor a sick person, nor a monk. Do not cut down a fruit-bearing tree. Do not destroy any inhabited place. Do not slaughter a camel nor a cow except for food. Do not drown bees nor burn them."" And it has been narrated in that regard from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family).

[Weak]"
"18159","(18153) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam and ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated from Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ, from Khālid ibn al-Fizr: Anas ibn Mālik narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Go forth in the name of Allah, by the help of Allah, and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah. Do not kill a decrepit old man, nor a child, nor a young one, nor a woman. Do not commit embezzlement. Gather your spoils of war. Set things right and do good, for Allah loves those who do good.""

[Weak]"
"18160","(18154) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Naẓīr al-Farrāʾ al-Miṣrī narrated to us in Makkah (may Allah have mercy on him): Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Mawt narrated to us by dictation: Aḥmad ibn Ḥammād Zughbah narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Ḥabībah narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥuṣayn narrated from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), when he dispatched an army — and in the narration of Ibn Abī Uways it is: ""from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) that when he dispatched his armies"" — he would say: ""Go forth in the name of Allah, fighting in the cause of Allah against those who disbelieve in Allah. Do not act treacherously, nor mutilate, nor commit embezzlement, nor kill the children, nor the keepers of monasteries.""

In the narration of the Egyptian (al-Miṣrī), his words ""nor commit embezzlement"" are not present. And the remainder is the same.

[Weak]"
"18161","(18155) 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf informed us; Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani informed us; 'Asim ibn 'Ali informed us; Qays ibn al-Rabi' informed us, from 'Umar, the freedman of 'Anbasah al-Qurashi, from Zayd ibn 'Ali, from his father, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) dispatched an army of Muslims against the polytheists, he would say: ""Set out in the name of Allah."" He then mentioned the hadith, which includes: ""Do not kill a young child, a woman, or an elderly old man. Do not gouge out an eye. Do not cut down a tree except a tree that impedes your fighting or stands between you and the polytheists. Do not mutilate any human being or any animal. Do not betray and do not commit treachery.""

In this chain of narration there is a break (irsal) and weakness; however, with its corroborating evidences alongside the narrations contained therein, it is strengthened. And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
"18162","(18156) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Isbahani informed us; al-Hasan ibn al-Jahm informed us; al-Husayn ibn al-Faraj informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Umar informed us; Ibn Safwan and 'Attaf ibn Khalid informed us, from Khalid ibn Zayd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) went out bidding farewell to the people of Mu'tah until he reached Thaniyat al-Wada'. He stopped, and they stopped around him, and he said: ""Wage war in the name of Allah, and fight the enemy of Allah and your enemy in Syria. You will find among them men in hermitages, secluded from the people; so do not interfere with them. And you will find others upon whose heads the Satan has made nests — cleave them with swords. Do not kill a woman, a small child, or a frail old man. Do not cut down a tree, nor cut down a palm tree, and do not demolish a house.""

This narration is also disconnected and very weak. [Very Weak]"
"18163","(18157) Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Sa'd al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi informed us; Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr, Abu Zakariyya, informed us; al-Mughirah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi informed me; Abu al-Zinad informed me; al-Murraqi' ibn Safiyy informed me, from his grandfather Riyah ibn al-Rabi', the brother of Hanzalah al-Katib, who informed him that he informed him: He went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) on a military expedition he undertook, and Khalid ibn al-Walid was at the vanguard. Riyah and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) passed by a slain woman whom the vanguard had struck down. They stopped, looking at her and marveling at her form, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) caught up with them on his she-camel. He said: They made way for the woman, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) stood over her and said: ""Ha! This one was not one who fought."" Then he looked at the faces of the people and said to one of them: ""Go to Khalid ibn al-Walid and let him not kill any offspring or any servant.""

Al-Bukhari said: Rabbah ibn al-Rabi' is more correct, and whoever said ""Riyah"" made an error. Abu 'Isa said likewise. [Hasan. Previously cited under number 18104]"
"18164","(18158) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan informed us; Yahya ibn Adam informed us; Hammad ibn Zayd and Wuhaib ibn Khalid informed us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from a man, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade the killing of hired servants and slaves. [Weak]"
"18165","(18159) And Abu Sa'id informed us; Abu al-'Abbas informed us; al-Hasan informed us; Yahya informed us; Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Fear Allah regarding the tillers of the soil; do not kill them unless they wage war against you."" [Weak]"
"18166","(18160) And Abu Sa'id informed us; Abu al-'Abbas informed us; al-Hasan informed us; Yahya informed us; 'Abd al-Rahim al-Razi informed us, from Ash'ath, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, who said: They would not kill the merchants of the polytheists. [Weak]"
"18167","(18161) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Harithi informed us; Abu Usamah informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abu 'Amr, that is Ibn Hamdan, informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Barrad informed us; Abu Usamah informed us, from Burayd, from Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa, from his father, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) finished with Hunayn, he sent Abu 'Amir to the army of Awtas. He encountered Duryad ibn al-Simmah, and Duryad was killed, and Allah put his companions to rout. He mentioned the hadith until he said, on the authority of Abu Musa: When I returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), I entered upon him in a house on a woven bed, and there was a bedding upon which the traces of the bed's frame had marked the back and sides of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). I informed him of my news and the news of Abu 'Amir, and he mentioned the hadith.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abdullah ibn Barrad, and they both narrated it in full from Abu Kurayb, from Abu Usamah. [Sound. Agreed upon]"
"18168","(18162) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar informed us; Yunus ibn Bukayr informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yasar, in the story of Awtas, who said: Rabi'ah ibn Rufay' caught up with Duryad ibn al-Simmah and took hold of the reins of his camel, thinking he was a woman, because he was among his trees. When he made the camel kneel, behold, it was an old man, and it was Duryad, and the young man did not recognize him. Duryad said: ""What do you want?"" He said: ""To kill you."" He said: ""And who are you?"" He said: ""I am Rabi'ah ibn Rufay' al-Sulami."" He said: Then he struck him with his sword, but it availed nothing. Duryad said: ""How poorly has your mother armed you! Take my sword — this one — from behind the tree, then strike with it, and raise away from the bones and lower away from the brain, for thus I used to kill men."" And so he killed him. [Weak]"
"18169","(18163) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us; Abu al-'Abbas al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us. Al-Shafi'i said: Duryad ibn al-Simmah was killed on the day of Hunayn at the age of one hundred and fifty years, in a grove of trees, unable to sit up. When his killing was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), he did not disapprove of his killing.

Al-Shafi'i said: And a blind man from Banu Qurayzah was killed after capture. This indicates that a grown man who does not fight may be killed if he refuses Islam and the jizyah. The Sheikh said: He is al-Zubayr ibn Bata al-Quradhi; we have recounted his story in what has passed. [Sound]"
"18170","(18164) And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; Sa'id ibn Mansur informed us; Hushaym informed us, from Hajjaj, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Kill the elders of the polytheists and spare their youth.""

Al-Shafi'i said: If it were permissible to find fault with the killing of anyone other than monks on the grounds that they do not fight, then neither a captive nor a wounded person who is incapacitated would be killed. Yet finishing off the wounded was carried out in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) among them — Abu Jahl ibn Hisham was finished off by Ibn Mas'ud and others. [Weak]"
"18171","(18165) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʾAṭāʾ informed us: Sulaymān al-Taymī reported on the authority of Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Who will see what has become of Abū Jahl?"" He (the narrator) said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him) set out and found that the two sons of ʿAfrāʾ had struck him down. He descended and seized him by his beard and said: ""Are you Abū Jahl?"" He said: ""Is there any man greater than the one you have killed, or whose people have killed him?""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from various chains on the authority of Sulaymān al-Taymī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"18172","(18166) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Abū Wakīʿ reported on the authority of Abū Isḥāq, on the authority of ʿAmr ibn Maymūn, on the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: On the day of Badr, I came upon Abū Jahl while he was prostrate (struck down). I struck him with my sword, but it did nothing. His sword fell, so I struck him again. Then I brought him to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) on a hot day, as though I were carrying something small from the earth. I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, this is the enemy of Allah, Abū Jahl; he has been killed."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""By Allah, he has been killed?"" I said: ""By Allah, he has been killed.""

He said: ""Then take us to him so that we may see him."" He came and looked at him and said: ""This was the Pharaoh of this Ummah.""

Thus he narrated it on the authority of ʿAmr ibn Maymūn. The preserved narration from Abū Isḥāq is on the authority of Abī ʿUbaydah, on the authority of his father, and that has already been mentioned. [Ḍaʿīf — previously mentioned under nos. 18013–18014]"
"18173","(18167) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād: Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us: Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Mahdī narrated to us: Ibn al-Mubārak informed us: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah reported on the authority of his father, on the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr, that he was with his father on the day of al-Yarmūk. When the polytheans were defeated and taken captive, he (al-Zubayr) would ransom their wounded.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: I do not know of any narration established from Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) that contradicts this. Had it been established, it would be more appropriate that he commanded them to exert themselves in fighting those who fought them and not to be occupied with remaining in the positions of these (captives).

The Shaykh said: He said this because all the narrations we have mentioned from Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) are mursal, except that they have been narrated from various chains. Ibn al-Musayyab narrated it, and he is ḥasan in mursal narrations.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) mentioned, in the narration of Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Baghdādī on his authority, the ḥadīth of al-Muraffaʿ, then he weakened it by saying that al-Muraffaʿ is not well-known. He also mentioned the ḥadīth of Ayyūb, on the authority of a man, on the authority of his father, and then said: ""This is like what we mentioned before — it is from the unknown."" As for the ḥadīth of Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Ḥabībah, al-Shāfiʿī did not mention it, and it is weaker than what he rejected on account of the unknown narrators. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18174","(18168) The Imam Abū al-Ṭayyib Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān (may Allah have mercy upon him) narrated to us by dictation: Abū ʿAmr Ismāʿīl ibn Najayd al-Sulamī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb ibn Yaḥyā al-Rāzī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr informed us: Sufyān reported on the authority of al-Aʿmash, on the authority of Ibrāhīm al-Taymī, on the authority of his father, on the authority of ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The covenant of the Muslims is one and the same; the lowest among them may act upon it. Whoever violates the pledge of a Muslim, upon him be the curse of Allah, the angels, and all of mankind. No obligatory or supererogatory deed will be accepted from him. And whoever grants patronage to a believer without the permission of his masters, upon him be the curse of Allah, the angels, and all of mankind. No obligatory or supererogatory deed will be accepted from him.""

Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ on the authority of Muḥammad ibn Kathīr, and Muslim recorded it from another chain on the authority of al-Thawrī.

And there has already passed the ḥadīth of Qays ibn ʿUbād on the authority of ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), on the authority of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""The believers are equal in their blood; they are one hand against those other than them, and the lowest among them may act upon their covenant.""

And that has also already passed in the ḥadīth of ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, on the authority of his father, on the authority of his grandfather, on the authority of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"18175","(18169) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl informed me: My grandfather narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah al-Zubayrī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim reported on the authority of Kathīr ibn Zayd, on the authority of al-Walīd ibn Rabāḥ, on the authority of Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The lowest among my Ummah may grant protection."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain]"
"18176","(18170) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Umawī narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us: Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj reported on the authority of ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, on the authority of Fuḍayl ibn Zayd, who said: We were arrayed in battle formation against the enemy. He said: A slave wrote a guarantee of safety for the polytheans and shot it at them. They came and said: ""You have granted us safety."" They (the Muslims) said: ""We have not granted you safety; rather, a slave has granted you safety."" So they wrote concerning this matter to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him). ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote: ""The slave is from among the Muslims, and his covenant is their covenant, and he has granted them safety."" [Ḥasan]"
"18177","(18171) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā reported on the authority of Ibn al-Mubārak, on the authority of Maʿmar, on the authority of Ziyād ibn Muslim, that a man from India came with the guarantee of safety of a slave, and then a man from among the Muslims killed him. He said: ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz sent his blood-money to his heirs. [Ḥasan]"
"18178","(18172) And it has been narrated in the ḥadīth of Ahl al-Bayt: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd ibn Sulaymān al-Ṣūfī said: It was read to my father Abū ʿAlī Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ashʿath al-Kūfī in Egypt while I heard: Abū al-Ḥasan Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Mūsā ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: My father Ismāʿīl narrated to me, on the authority of his father, on the authority of his grandfather Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, on the authority of his father, on the authority of his grandfather ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn, on the authority of his father al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, on the authority of his father ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The slave has no share in the war-booty except for the lowly goods of the camp, and his granting of protection is valid, and the granting of protection by a woman is valid when she grants the people safety."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18179","(18173) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah reported on the authority of Mālik.

(Ḥ) And Abū Jaʿfar Kāmil ibn Aḥmad al-Mustamlī informed us: Abū Sahl Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāyinī narrated to us: Abū Sulaymān Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Bayhaqī in Khusrawjird narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā said: I read to Mālik, on the authority of Abū al-Naḍr, that Abū Murrah, the freedman of Umm Hānīʾ bint Abī Ṭālib, informed him that he heard Umm Hānīʾ bint Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with her) say: I went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) in the year of the Conquest and found him performing ghusl, while his daughter Fāṭimah (peace be upon her) was screening him with a garment. She said: I gave salām and he said: ""Who is this?"" I said: ""Umm Hānīʾ, daughter of Abī Ṭālib."" He said: ""Welcome to Umm Hānīʾ."" When he finished his ghusl, he stood and prayed eight rakʿahs wrapped in a single garment. When he turned away, I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my brother ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib claims that he intends to kill a man whom I have granted protection — Fulān ibn Hubayrah."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""We grant protection to the one you have granted protection, O Umm Hānīʾ."" Umm Hānīʾ said: And that was in the forenoon (ḍuḥā).

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. In the ḥadīth of al-Qaʿnabī, it reads: ""Then he turned away, and I said…"" and the remainder is the same.

Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ on the authority of al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim recorded it on the authority of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"18180","(18174) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Aḥmad al-Muqrīʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAṭṭār said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Ibn Abī Dhiʾb reported on the authority of Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī, on the authority of Abū Murrah, the freedman of ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him), on the authority of Umm Hānīʾ (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: I granted protection to two of my polythean in-laws. ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) came and hastened upon them to kill them and said: ""Why do you grant protection to the polytheans?"" She said: ""By Allah, you shall not kill them until you begin with me before them."" She went out and said: ""Close the door against him."" Then she went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and informed him. He said: ""That was not for him. We grant safety to the one you have granted safety, and we grant protection to the one you have granted protection."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18181","(18175) Abu 'Abd Allah, Abu Bakr, Abu Muhammad, and Abu Sadiq informed us — they said: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — 'Iyad ibn 'Abd Allah informed me, from Makhrama ibn Sulayman, from Kurayb, the mawla of Ibn 'Abbas, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with both of them — that Umm Hani bint Abi Talib related to him that she said to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him: ""My brother 'Ali claims that he will kill the one I have granted protection."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""We have granted protection to the one you have granted protection."" [Sahih]"
"18182","(18176) Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr — informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan narrated to us — Abu Usama narrated to us — from Sufyan, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""If a woman granted protection from the Muslims, they would consider it permissible for her."" [Sahih]"
"18183","(18177) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan — informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us — Ibn Wahb informed us — Ibn Lahi'a informed me — from Musa ibn Jubayr al-Ansari, from 'Irak ibn Malik al-Ghifari, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Salama, the wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him — that Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent her husband, Abu al-'As ibn al-Rabi', to her requesting her to obtain protection for him from her father. She went out and raised her head from the door of her chamber while the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, was performing the Fajr prayer with the people. She said: ""O people, I am Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and I have granted protection to Abu al-'As."" When the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, completed the prayer, he said: ""O people, I was not informed of this until you heard it. Know that the lowest of the Muslims in rank may grant protection and it is binding upon them."" [Sahih, without the story]"
"18184","(18178) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us — Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us — from Ibn Ishaq — Yazid ibn Roman narrated to me, saying: ""When Abu al-'As ibn al-Rabi' came to Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and sought her protection, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, went out for the Fajr prayer. When he pronounced the takbir in the prayer, Zaynab cried out: 'O people, I have granted protection to Abu al-'As ibn al-Rabi'.' When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, completed his prayer with the salam, he said: 'O people, did you hear what I heard?' They said: 'Yes.' He said: 'By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, I knew nothing of this until I heard from her what you have heard. Indeed, the lowest of the Muslims in rank may grant protection and it is binding upon them.' Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, entered upon Zaynab and said: 'O my daughter, honor his dwelling, but let him not approach you, for you are not lawful to him nor is he lawful to you.'""

Thus he reported it to us in the Kitab al-Maghazi in a disconnected form, and he narrated it to us in the Kitab al-Mustadrak from Yazid ibn Roman from 'Urwa from 'A'isha, who said: Zaynab cried out, and he mentioned it. [Da'if]"
"18185","(18179) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' — informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq — narrated to us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Kathir — narrated to us — Sufyan ibn Sa'id — from W'il ibn Dawud, from 'Abd Allah al-Bahi, from Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""I said to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him: 'Abu al-'As ibn al-Rabi', if he is near, he is a cousin, and if he is distant, he is a father of children. I have granted him protection.' So the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, granted him protection.""

And it is also reported from 'Abd Allah that Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with her, said to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him — and this is a mursal narration."
"18186","(18180) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us — Abu Ahmad — Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab — informed us — Ja'far ibn 'Awn — informed us — al-A'mash — from Abu W'il, who said: ""A letter from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came to us: 'When you besiege a fortress and they wish to come down upon the judgment of Allah, do not bring them down, for you do not know what Allah's judgment concerning them is. Rather, bring them down upon your own judgment, and then decide among them as you wish. And if a man says to another man: ""Do not fear,"" he has granted him safety. And if he says: ""Mutarris"" (do not be afraid), he has granted him safety. And if he says to him: ""I think you should not enter,"" he has granted him safety, for Allah knows what is in the tongues (i.e., the true intent behind words).'""

And al-Thawri narrated it from al-A'mash, and at the end of his version he added: ""And if he says: 'Do not enter (la tadkhul),' he has granted him safety, for Allah knows what is in the tongues."" [Sahih]"
"18187","(18181) Abu Nasr ibn Qatada informed us — Abu 'Amr ibn Matar — narrated to us — Abu Khalifa — narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Kathir — informed us — Sufyan — from al-A'mash, from Abu W'il, who said: ""A letter from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came to us while we were besieging a fortress,"" and he mentioned it with its meaning. [Sahih; previously cited]"
"18188","(18182) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, word for word, and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr, by recitation to him — informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us — Hilal ibn al-A'la al-Raqqi — narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far — narrated to us — al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman — narrated to us — Sa'id ibn 'Ubayd Allah — narrated to us — Bakr ibn 'Abd Allah al-Muzani and Ziyad ibn Jubayr — from Jubayr ibn Hayya, who said: ""'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sent people from the outskirts of the garrison cities to fight the polytheists. While 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was doing that, a man from the polytheists of the people of al-Ahwaz was brought as a captive. When he was brought, some people said to Hormuzan: 'Would it not please you if you were not killed?' He said: 'Yes, and what is that?' They said: 'When you are brought near the Commander of the Faithful and he speaks to you, say: ""I fear to speak to you (alone)."" Then if he wants to kill you, say: ""I am under your protection — you said: Do not fear (la tafraq).""' The man memorized these words. When he was brought before 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, 'Umar said to him, during some of his questioning: 'I fear (afraqu),' meaning 'I am afraid.' He said: 'Do not fear (la tafraq).' When he finished his conversation, he asked him — Allah willed what He willed — then said to him: 'I am going to kill you.' He said: 'But you have granted me safety!' He said: 'Woe to you! When did I grant you safety?' He said: 'You said: Do not fear (la tafraq).' He said: 'He has spoken the truth. As for me, then embrace Islam.' He said: 'Yes,' and he embraced Islam. Then he mentioned the hadith at length."""
"18189","(18183) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq — narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us — al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman — informed us — al-Shafi'i — informed us — al-Thaqafi — from Humayd, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""We besieged Tustar, and Hormuzan came down upon the judgment of 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. I brought him to 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. When we reached him, 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: 'Speak.' He said: 'The speech of a living one or the speech of a dead one?' 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'Speak — there is no harm.' He said: 'We and you, O company of Arabs — Allah has left nothing between us and you — we used to worship you, kill you, and seize your property. But when Allah was with you, we had no power.' 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'What do you say?' I said: 'O Commander of the Faithful, I have left behind many enemies and severe resistance. If you kill him, the people will despair of life and their resistance will grow stronger.' 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'Should I feel shy about killing Burayd ibn Malik and Majza'a ibn Thawr?' When I feared that he would kill him, I said: 'There is no way for you to kill him — you said to him: Speak, there is no harm.' 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'You have taken a bribe and you have been deceived by him.' He said: 'By Allah, I have neither taken a bribe nor been deceived by him.' He said: 'You will bring me a witness other than yourself for what you have witnessed, or I will begin with your punishment.' He said: 'I went out and met al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, and he testified with me.' So 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, held back, and Hormuzan embraced Islam and was assigned a stipend."""
"18190","(18184) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih — narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ayyub — informed me — Abu al-Walid — narrated to us — Shu'ba — informed me — Sa'd ibn Ibrahim — said: I heard Abu Umama ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf narrating from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the people of Qurayza came down upon the judgment of Sa'd. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent for him, and he came. He said: ""Go to your chief or the best among you."" He sat with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and said: ""These people have come down upon your judgment."" He said: ""I judge that their fighting men should be killed.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Abu al-Walid, and Muslim reported it from the hadith of Shu'ba. [Sahih; agreed upon]"
"18191","(18185) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah and ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad both said: Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr both said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said:

Saʿd was wounded on the day of al-Khandaq. A man from Quraysh called Ḥibbān al-ʿArqah shot him in the median vein (al-akḥal). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had a tent erected for him in the mosque so that he could visit him from nearby. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) returned from al-Khandaq, laid down his arms, and performed his bath, Jibrīl (peace be upon him) came to him while he was shaking the dust from his head. He said: ""You have laid down the arms! By Allah, we have not laid them down. Go out to them."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Where?"" He said: ""Here,"" and he pointed to Banū Qurayẓah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) went out to them, and they submitted to the judgment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). He referred their judgment to Saʿd. Saʿd said: ""I judge regarding them that the fighting men be killed, the offspring be taken captive, and their wealth be divided.""

My father said: I was informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""You have judged regarding them by the judgment of Allah.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyā, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others, all of them from Ibn Numayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"18192","(18186) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Mihrān narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Sufyān narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAlqamah ibn Murthad, from Sulaymān ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said:

When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) appointed a commander over an army, he would enjoin upon him the fear of Allah regarding his own self and regarding the Muslims who were with him. He mentioned the hadith and said: ""When you besiege the people of a fortress and they ask you to place them under the judgment of Allah, do not place them under it, for you do not know whether you will hit upon the judgment of Allah or not.""

Wakīʿ added in his narration from Sufyān: ""But rather place them under your own judgment, then decide among them afterwards as you wish."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1731]"
"18193","(18187) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Anbārī narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated from Sufyān, and he mentioned it.

Muslim extracted it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm, from Yaḥyā ibn Ādam. And he extracted it from the hadith of Wakīʿ. And we narrated concerning that from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) in the chapter before it."
"18194","(18188) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Salamah narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Ḥujayn ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Salamah narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Faḍl, from Sulaymān ibn Yasār, from Jaʿfar ibn ʿAmr al-Ḍamrī, who said:

I went out with ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAdī ibn al-Khiyār to Shām. When we arrived in Ḥimṣ, ʿUbaydullāh said to me: ""Would you like us to ask Waḥshī about the killing of Ḥamzah?"" And Abū Dāwūd said in his narration from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Faḍl al-Hāshimī narrated to us, from Sulaymān ibn Yasār, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAdī ibn al-Khiyār — thus it is in my book — he said: ""We were coming from the land of the Romans, and when we drew near to Ḥimṣ, we said: 'Let us pass by Waḥshī and ask him about the killing of Ḥamzah.' We met a man and mentioned that to him. He said: 'He is a man overcome by wine. If you find him sober, ask him about anything and he will inform you. But if you find him drinking, do not ask him.' So we set out until we reached him. Something had been spread for him at his door and he was sitting, sober. He said: 'Ibn al-Khiyār?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'I have not seen you since I carried you to your mother at Dhī Ṭuwā when she gave birth to you. I saw your feet and recognized them.' He said: I said: 'We have come to you to ask you about the killing of Ḥamzah.' He said: 'I shall tell you just as I told the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) when he asked me. I was a slave of the family of Muṭʿim. My cousin Muṭʿim said to me: ""If you kill Ḥamzah, my uncle, you are free."" So I went out on the day of Uḥud with my spear. I was an Ethiopian man who played with it as they play. I went out that day not wanting to kill anyone or fight anyone except Ḥamzah. I went out and suddenly there was Ḥamzah — he was like a grey camel. No one raised against him but he struck him down with the sword. I was afraid of him, and a man from the children of the descendants of Sibāʿ hastened toward him. I heard Ḥamzah saying: ""Come here, O son of one who cuts the pudenda!"" He rushed upon him and killed him. I kept evading him, and I took refuge behind a tree. I had my spear with me. When I had a firm position, I shook the spear until I was satisfied with it, then I released it. It landed between his two collarbones. He heaved to rise but could not. So I killed him. Then I took my spear. I had not killed anyone nor fought anyone. When I came back, I was set free. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) arrived, I wanted to flee from him, intending to go to Shām. A man came to me and said: 'Woe to you, O Waḥshī! By Allah, no one comes to Muḥammad bearing his testimony but that he lets him go.' So I set out, and I was not aware until I found myself standing before him, bearing witness to the truth. He said: 'Is it Waḥshī?' I said: 'Waḥshī.' He said: 'Woe to you, tell me about the killing of Ḥamzah.' I began to narrate to him as I have narrated to you both. He said: 'Woe to you, O Waḥshī! Hide your face from me, and let me not see you.' So I used to avoid being seen by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). Then Allah took His Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). When the affair of Musaylimah occurred and an expedition was dispatched against him, I was sent with it. I took my spear. We met, and I and a man from the Anṣār rushed upon him. Your Lord knows best which of us killed him. If I killed him, then I have killed the best of people and the worst of people.'""

Sulaymān ibn Yasār said: I heard Ibn ʿUmar say: ""I was in the army that day, and I heard a caller saying regarding Musaylimah: 'The black slave killed him.'""

The wording is that of the hadith of Abū Dāwūd, and the hadith of Ḥujayn has the same meaning, with additions and omissions. It does not mention the hadith of drinking, nor his statement: ""If I killed him.""

al-Bukhārī extracted it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh, from Ḥujayn ibn al-Muthannā. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī 4072]"
"18195","(18189) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib, and Zakariyyā ibn Dāwūd al-Khaffāf said: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: Ḥajjāj narrated from Ibn Jurayj: Yaʿlā ibn Muslim informed me from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, that he heard him narrate from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), that:

A number of the people of shirk killed many and committed adultery many times. Then they came to Muḥammad (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: ""What you say and call to is good. If only you could tell us that there is an expiation for what we have done."" So the verse was revealed: {And those who do not invoke with Allah another deity, nor kill the soul which Allah has forbidden except by right, nor commit adultery} [al-Furqān 68]. And the verse was revealed: {O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, do not despair of the mercy of Allah} [al-Zumar 53], the verse.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim and others, from Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad. And al-Bukhārī extracted it from another route from Ibn Jurayj. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"18196","(18190) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr and others said: Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us: Ḥaywah ibn Shurayḥ narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrated to me from Ibn Shumāsah al-Mahrī, who said:

We were present with ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) as he was in the throes of death. He mentioned the hadith and said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) to pledge allegiance to him upon Islam. I said: ""Extend your hand so that I may pledge allegiance to you, O Messenger of Allah."" He extended his hand, but I withdrew my hand. He said: ""What is the matter with you, O ʿAmr?"" I said: ""I wished to stipulate a condition."" He said: ""What condition do you wish to stipulate?"" I said: ""That I be forgiven."" He said: ""Do you not know, O ʿAmr, that Islam demolishes what came before it?""

And he mentioned the hadith. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 121]"
"18197","(18191) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās informed me: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Daghūlī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Karīm narrated to us: al-Haytham ibn ʿAdī narrated to us: Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, who said:

ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) was struck by an arrow on the day of al-Ṭāʾif. It festered after the death of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) by forty nights, and he died. He mentioned a story and said: A delegation of Thaqīf came to him, and that arrow was still in his possession. He brought it out to them and said: ""Does anyone among you recognize this arrow?"" Saʿīd ibn ʿUbayd, the brother of Banī al-ʿAjlān, said: ""This is an arrow that I fashioned, feathered, and fitted with a shaft, and I am the one who shot it."" Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Then this is the arrow that killed ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr. All praise is due to Allah, who honored him by your hand and did not disgrace you by his hand, for He is vast over you both."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18198","(18192) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr, from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, who said:

When ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) was afflicted with a calamity, he would say: ""I was afflicted because of Zayd ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), and I was patient."" He saw the killer of his brother Zayd and said to him: ""Woe to you! You have killed a brother for me. Not a breath of the morning breeze blows but that I remember him."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18199","(18193) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated in Baghdād: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Zuhrī narrated to us: Ḥumayd narrated to us: Anas narrated:

That al-Hurmuzān came under the judgment of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him). ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Anas, I seek refuge in Allah from the killer of al-Barāʾ ibn Mālik and Majzaʾah ibn Thawr."" So he accepted Islam and a stipend was assigned for him. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18200","(18194) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Abū al-Qāsim ʿAlī ibn Saqar ibn Naṣr al-Sukkarī narrated to us: ʿAffān ibn Muslim narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah narrated from Thābit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), in the story of the Qurraʾ and the killing of Ḥarām ibn Milḥān. At the end of it he said:

After that, Abū Ṭalḥah would say to me: ""What is your view regarding the killer of Ḥarām?"" I said: ""What is the matter with him? May Allah deal with him as He wills."" He said: ""Do not do anything, for he has accepted Islam."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18201","(18195) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ reported from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from Aslam Abī ʿImrān, who said: We raided the city, heading for Constantinople, and the commander of the army was ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Khālid ibn al-Walīd. The Romans had their backs pressed against the wall of the city. A man charged at the enemy, and the people said: ""Stop, stop! There is no god but Allah — he is casting himself into destruction!"" Abū Ayyūb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""This verse was revealed concerning us, the company of the Anṣār. When Allah aided His Prophet and made Islam prevail, we said: 'Come, let us stay with our wealth and set it right.' So Allah, the Most High, revealed: {And spend in the way of Allah and do not cast yourselves into destruction} [Al-Baqarah 195]. Casting ourselves into destruction means that we remain with our wealth, set it right, and abandon jihād.""

Abū ʿImrān said: Abū Ayyūb continued to strive in the cause of Allah until he was buried in Constantinople.

There are other hadiths to this effect. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced as no. 17925]"
"18202","(18196) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Shaybān al-Ramlī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah reported from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, who heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him) say: A man said to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) on the day of Uḥud: ""O Messenger of Allah, if I am killed, where shall I be?"" He said: ""In Paradise."" So he threw away the dates that were in his hand, then fought until he was killed.

This is the wording of Aḥmad ibn Shaybān. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad, and Muslim narrated it from Saʿīd ibn ʿAmr, both from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
"18203","(18197) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Abū al-Naḍr Hāshim ibn al-Qāsim narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah reported from Thābit, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent Buṣaysah as a spy to see what the caravan of Abū Sufyān had done. He returned when there was no one in the house besides myself and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). He said: ""I do not know whether he made an exception for some of his wives."" He (Anas) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) went out and spoke, saying: ""We have a pursuit; whoever has his mount present, let us ride with us.""

Some men sought permission, saying their mounts were in the upper part of Madinah. He said: ""No, only those whose mounts are present."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and his Companions set out until they reached Badr ahead of the polytheists. The polytheists arrived, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""None of you should advance toward anything until I give permission."" The polytheists drew near, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Rise to a Paradise whose width is as the heavens and the earth."" ʿUmayr ibn al-Ḥumām al-Anṣārī said: ""O Messenger of Allah, a Paradise whose width is as the heavens and the earth?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Bakh, bakh!"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""What has prompted you to say 'bakh, bakh'?"" He said: ""By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, nothing but the hope that I may be among its people."" He said: ""Then you are among its people."" He took out some dates from his quiver and began eating them, then said: ""If I live long enough to eat these dates of mine, it would indeed be a long life."" He threw away the dates he had with him and fought until he was killed.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī al-Naḍr, Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ, and others, from Abū al-Naḍr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1901]"
"18204","(18198) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr reported from Ibn Isḥāq: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmara ibn Qatādah informed me, saying: When the people met on the day of Badr, ʿAwf ibn ʿAfrāʾ ibn al-Ḥārith (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what makes the Lord, blessed and exalted, laugh at His servant?"" He said: ""When He sees him plunge his hand into battle, fighting bare-chested."" So ʿAwf removed his coat of mail, then advanced and fought until he was killed. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18205","(18199) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad informed us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah reported from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from Mujāhid, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) sent ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd and Khabbāb as a military expedition, and he sent Ḥiḥyah as a military expedition by himself. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18206","(18200) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī said: A man from the Anṣār lagged behind the Companions of Biʾr Maʿūnah. He saw birds hovering over the site where his Companions had been killed. He said to ʿAmr ibn Umayyah: ""Shall I advance upon these enemies so that they kill me, rather than stay behind from a scene in which our Companions were killed?"" He did so and was killed. ʿAmr ibn Umayyah returned and mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who spoke well of him. It is also said that he said to ʿAmr: ""Why did you not advance and fight until you were killed?"" Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent ʿAmr ibn Umayyah al-Ḍamrī and a man from the Anṣār as a military expedition, the two of them alone, and he sent ʿAbd Allāh ibn Unays as a military expedition by himself. We have cited their chains of narration in this book. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18207","(18201) Abū Saʿd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us: Jubārah narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Tammīm narrated to me: Maymūn ibn Mihrān reported from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) that a slave from the slaves of the one-fifth share stole from the one-fifth. The matter was raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), but he did not cut off his hand. He said: ""It is the property of Allah; some of it stole from some."" This chain contains weakness. It has also been narrated through another route from Maymūn ibn Mihrān from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) as a mursal narration.

It has also been narrated to us from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) that a man stole a mail coat from the war booty, and he did not cut off his hand. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18208","(18202) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAṭṭār informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam al-Miṣrī informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Mālik ibn Anas reported from Thawr ibn Zayd al-Dīlī, from Sālim Abī al-Ghayth, the freedman of Ibn Muṭīʿ, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) to Khaybar. We did not acquire gold or silver; we only acquired goods and livestock. Then we turned toward Wādī al-Qurā. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had a slave whom Rifāʿah ibn Badr, a man from Banī Ḍubayb, had given to him. While the slave was unloading the saddle of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), a stray arrow struck him and he died. The people said to him: ""Congratulations, Paradise!"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""No! By the One in whose Hand is my soul, the cloak he stole on the day of Khaybar from the war booty — before the shares were distributed — is burning upon him as fire."" A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) with a sandal strap or two sandal straps. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""A sandal strap of fire, or two sandal straps of fire.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Ṭāhir from Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another route from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
"18209","(18203) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us orally: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us in Makkah: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah reported from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: There was a man in charge of the Prophet's (peace and blessings be upon him) baggage whose name was Kirkirah. He died, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""He is in the Fire."" They went to look at him and found upon him a cloak that he had stolen (from the booty).

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī from Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3074]"
"18210","(18204) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Muqriʾ, Ibn al-Ḥammāmī (may Allah have mercy on him), informed us in Baghdād: Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Najjād informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ, Abū ʿAmr al-Ghudānī, narrated to us: ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār reported from Simāk Abī Zuml: Ibn ʿAbbās narrated to me: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, saying: On the day of Khaybar, some of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) were killed — that is, a number of people. They said: ""So-and-so is a martyr, and so-and-so is a martyr,"" until they passed by a man and said: ""So-and-so is a martyr."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""No! I saw him in the Fire because of a cloak he stole, or a mantle he stole."" Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""O son of al-Khaṭṭāb, go and announce among the people that none shall enter Paradise except the believers."" So I went out and announced among the people that none shall enter Paradise except the believers.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 114]"
"18211","(18205) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Munādī narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī, Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqrīʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq al-ʿAṭṭār informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Mālik ibn Anas and al-Layth ibn Saʿīd informed me, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān, from Abū ʿAmrah, from Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: A man died on the day of Khaybar, and they mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). He said to them: ""Pray over your companion."" The faces of the people changed because of that. He (the narrator) asserted that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Indeed, your companion has committed ghulūl (misappropriation) in the cause of Allah."" We then searched his belongings and found beads from the beads of the Jews, worth no more than two dirhams.

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ibn Wahb. [Weak]"
"18212","(18206) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, and the wording is his: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated from Abū Ḥayyān al-Taymī: Abū Zurʿah ibn ʿAmr ibn Jarīr narrated to me: Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) stood among us one day and mentioned ghulūl (misappropriation of war booty), declaring it grave and emphasizing its gravity. He said: ""I must not find any one of you coming on the Day of Resurrection with a camel on his neck, bellowing, saying: 'O Messenger of Allah, help me!' And I will say: 'I can avail you nothing; I have already conveyed to you.' I must not find any one of you coming on the Day of Resurrection with a sheep on his neck, bleating, saying: 'O Messenger of Allah, help me!' And I will say: 'I can avail you nothing; I have already conveyed to you.' I must not find any one of you coming on the Day of Resurrection with a horse on his neck, neighing, saying: 'O Messenger of Allah, help me!' And I will say: 'I can avail you nothing; I have already conveyed to you.' I must not find any one of you coming on the Day of Resurrection with a soul on his neck, crying out, saying: 'O Messenger of Allah, help me!' And I will say: 'I can avail you nothing; I have already conveyed to you.' I must not find any one of you coming on the Day of Resurrection with gold and silver on his neck, saying: 'O Messenger of Allah, help me!' And I will say: 'I can avail you nothing; I have already conveyed to you.' I must not find any one of you coming on the Day of Resurrection with garments flapping on his neck, saying: 'O Messenger of Allah, help me!' And I will say: 'I can avail you nothing; I have already conveyed to you.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad. [Sound — agreed upon]"
"18213","(18207) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us by dictation: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd ibn Ḥayyān, from Abū Zurʿah ibn ʿAmr ibn Jarīr, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) mentioned ghulūl (misappropriation of war booty) and declared it grave. Then he said: ""Let one of you beware lest he come on the Day of Resurrection with a camel on his neck, saying: 'O Muḥammad, help me!' And I will say: 'I can avail you nothing; I have already conveyed.' And a man will come with a horse on his neck, its hooves striking, saying: 'O Muḥammad, help me!' And I will say: 'I can avail you nothing; I have already conveyed.' And a man will come with garments on his neck, saying: 'O Muḥammad, help me!' And I will say: 'I can avail you nothing; I have already conveyed.'""

Ḥammād said: ""I heard it from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, and he brought it in approximately this form."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī, from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb. [Sound — agreed upon]"
"18214","(18208) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: al-Asfaṭī narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Qatādah, from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, from Maʿdān, from Thawbān (may Allah be pleased with him), saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Whoever dies free from three things — arrogance, ghulūl (misappropriation of war booty), and debt — will enter Paradise.""

Abū ʿĪsā said: Saʿīd narrated it from Qatādah and said: ""al-kanz (hoarding)"" instead of ""al-kibr (arrogance)."" [Sound]"
"18215","(18209) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baghdādī narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Bashshār narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn Dīnār narrated, having heard ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father, from his grandfather; and Ibn ʿAjlān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him): When the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was returning from the expedition of Ḥunayn, the people crowded around him asking for a share. His she-camel bolted and a tree snatched his cloak. He said: ""Return my cloak to me. Do you fear miserliness from me? By Allah, had Allah bestowed upon you camels equal to the number of samur trees of Tihāmah, I would have distributed them among you, and you would not find me miserly, cowardly, or a liar."" Then he took a handful of hair from the hump of a camel, raised it, and said: ""I have no right to anything from what Allah has bestowed upon you, except the fifth, and the fifth is returned to you."" When it came to the division of the fifth, a man came seeking to keep a needle or thread. He said: ""Return the needle and the thread, for ghulūl (misappropriation) is a disgrace, a fire, and a shame on the Day of Resurrection."" [Hasan — previously referenced under no. 7/13177]"
"18216","(18210) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī narrated to me: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Maḥbūb ibn Mūsā informed us: Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shawdhab narrated to me: ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to me, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with both of them), saying: Whenever the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) obtained war booty, he would order Bilāl, who would announce among the people. They would bring their spoils, and he would extract the fifth and divide the rest. After that, a man came with a bridle of hair and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, this is part of what we obtained from the war booty."" He said: ""Did you hear Bilāl announce three times?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""What prevented you from bringing it?"" He offered an excuse. He said: ""You will bring it on the Day of Resurrection, and I will not accept it from you.""

The ḥadīth of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr regarding the kirkarah (saddlebow) has preceded in this chapter, and none of these narrations mention that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) ordered the burning of the belongings of the one who committed ghulūl. [Hasan — previously referenced under no. 6/12719]"
"18217","(18211) In that is evidence of the weakness of what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Aṣbahānī al-Zāhid informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Baḥr al-Barrī narrated to me: My father narrated to me: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us: Zuḥayr ibn Muḥammad narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them) burned the belongings of the one who committed ghulūl, withheld his share, and beat him.

This is how more than one narrator narrated it from al-Walīd ibn Muslim. It has also been narrated from him as a mursal (disconnected) narration. [Weak]"
"18218","(18212) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah and ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Najdah narrated, saying: al-Walīd narrated from Zuḥayr ibn Muḥammad, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb — his own statement. ʿAbd al-Wahhāb did not mention the withholding of his share. It is said that this Zuḥayr is unknown and is not the Makkan one."
"18219","(18213) As for the ḥadīth which Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah and Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī narrated, saying: Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated to me.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah al-Qurashī narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated to me: Ṣāliḥ ibn Muḥammad ibn Zāʾidah narrated, saying: I entered the land of the Romans with Muslimah ibn ʿAbd al-Malik. A man who had committed ghulūl was brought, and he asked Sālim about it. Sālim said: ""I heard my father narrate from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: 'When you find a man who has committed ghulūl, burn his belongings and beat him.'"" He said: ""We found a muṣḥaf (copy of the Qurʾān) in his belongings, and Sālim was asked about it. He said: 'Sell it and give its price in charity.'"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Saʿīd. This is weak. [Weak]"
"18220","(18214) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Anṭākī narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq narrated from Ṣāliḥ ibn Muḥammad, saying: We went on a military expedition with al-Walīd ibn Hishām, and among us were Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar and ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. A man committed ghulūl with some belongings, so al-Walīd ordered his belongings to be burned, and he was paraded around. He was not given his share.

Abū Dāwūd said: ""This is the more sound of the two narrations."" More than one narrator narrated that al-Walīd ibn Hishām burned the belongings of Ziyād ibn Saʿd, who had committed ghulūl, and beat him. [Weak]"
"18221","(18215) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Farisi — informed us; Abu Ishaq — Ibrahim ibn Abdullah — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Faris — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Bukhari — said: Salih ibn Muhammad ibn Za'idah, Abu Waqid al-Laythi al-Madani, was abandoned by Sulayman ibn Harb. He is munkar al-hadith (a narrator whose hadith is rejected). He narrates from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, who raised it (as a marfu' hadith): ""Whoever commits ghulul (embezzlement of war spoils), burn his belongings.""

Ibn Abbas also narrated from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) regarding ghulul, but he (the Prophet) did not burn (the belongings).

Al-Bukhari said: ""Our colleagues use this as evidence regarding ghulul, but this is baseless and is nothing."" [Sahih]"
"18222","(18216) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz — narrated to us; Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; al-Abbas ibn Muhammad — said: I heard Yahya ibn Ma'in saying: ""The hadith of Salih ibn Muhammad ibn Za'idah is not of that (standard)."""
"18223","(18217) Abu Ja'far — Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Qirmisini — narrated to us there; Abu al-Husayn — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Kuhayli — informed us; al-Hadrami — narrated to us; Abdullah ibn al-Hakam — narrated to us; Ruh — narrated to us; Usamah ibn Zayd — from Ibn Shihab — narrated to me Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar al-Zuhri (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) on the day of Hunayn, moving through the people, asking about the dwelling place of Khalid ibn al-Walid. A drunken man was brought, so he ordered those who were present to strike him with whatever was in his hands. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) threw dust upon him.

And he mentioned the hadith. [Da'if. Previously cited under number 8/17537]"
"18224","(18218) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz — informed us; Abu Abdullah al-Isbahani — narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn al-Jahm — narrated to us; al-Husayn ibn al-Faraj — I think from al-Waqidi — narrated to me Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from his father, from his grandfather, regarding the story of Khaybar and what was brought out from the fortress of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh. He said: There were skins of wine, which were poured out. On that day, a man from among the Muslims deliberately drank from that wine. This was reported to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he disapproved of it when it was brought to his attention. He struck him with his sandal and ordered those who were present to strike him with their sandals. He was known as Abdullah al-Himar, and he was a man who could not abstain from drinking. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) struck him several times. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Allah, curse him, for how often he is beaten!"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Do not do so, O Umar, for he loves Allah and His Messenger."" [Da'if]"
"18225","(18219) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan — in Baghdad — informed us; Abdullah ibn Ja'far — narrated to us; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Wahb — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Salamah — from Abu Abd al-Rahim — narrated to me Mansur — from Abu Yazid Ghaylan, the mawla of Kinanah — from Abu Sallam al-Habashi — from al-Maqdad ibn Ma'dikarib — from al-Harith ibn Mu'awiyah — narrated to us: Ubadah ibn al-Samit, and with him was Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with them both), said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prayed toward a camel from the al-Maqsam (the camels of war spoils). When he finished his prayer, he took from it a tick between his fingers, from its wool, and said: ""Verily, this is from your war spoils, and I have no share from it except the fifth, and the fifth is returned to you. So return the thread and the needle and what is smaller than that and what is greater, for ghulul (embezzlement) is a disgrace upon its people in this world and the Hereafter. And wage jihad for the sake of Allah against the near among them and the distant, and let not the reproach of any reproacher deter you in the cause of Allah. And establish the limits (hudud) of Allah in travel and in residence, and adhere to jihad, for it is a great door among the doors of Paradise, through which Allah delivers from worry and grief."" [Da'if]"
"18226","(18220) Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated it from Abu Sallam, from al-Maqdad ibn Ma'dikarib, who said: He sat with Ubadah, Abu al-Darda', and al-Harith ibn Mu'awiyah al-Kindi, and they discussed the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) regarding the fifths (al-ams). Ubadah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prayed toward a camel during an expedition, and he mentioned it similarly, and said in it: ""And establish the limits (hudud) of Allah in travel and in residence.""

Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah — informed us; Abu Amr ibn Matar — informed us; Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Mustafad — narrated to us; Abu Abdullah — Muhammad ibn A'idh — narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Ayyash — narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam — mentioned it. [Da'if]"
"18227","(18221) Abu Dawud narrated in al-Marasil, from Hisham ibn Khalid al-Dimashqi, from al-Hasan ibn Yahya al-Khushani, from Zayd ibn Waqid, from Makhul, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Establish the limits (hudud) in residence and in travel, upon the near and the distant, and do not care, in the matter of Allah, for the reproach of any reproacher.""

Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad — informed it to us; Abu al-Husayn al-Fasawi — narrated to us; Abu Ali al-Lu'lu'i — narrated to us; Abu Dawud — mentioned it. And that was also narrated from Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit. [Da'if]"
"18228","(18222) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan — informed us; Abdullah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustuyah — narrated to us; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan — narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' —

(And) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah — informed us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Khaymawayh — informed us; Ahmad ibn Najdah — narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' — narrated to us; Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak — from Kahmas — from Harun ibn al-Asamm, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) sent Khalid ibn al-Walid with an army. Khalid sent Dirar ibn al-Azwar on a raiding expedition with cavalry. They launched a surprise attack on a clan of Banu Asad and captured a beautiful bride. She impressed Dirar, so he asked his companions, and they gave her to him, and he had intercourse with her. When he returned, he felt remorse and fell into despair. When he was brought to Khalid and informed him of what he had done, Khalid said: ""I have made her lawful for you and purified her for you."" He said: ""No, until you write to Umar about this."" So Umar wrote: ""Pelt him with stones."" The letter of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) arrived after he (Dirar) had passed away. He (the narrator) said: ""Allah would not humiliate Dirar ibn al-Azwar."" [Da'if]"
"18229","(18223) Abu Ali al-Rudhbari — informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasah — narrated to us; Abu Dawud — narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salih — narrated to us; Ibn Wahb — informed me; Haywah — from Ayash ibn al-Abbas al-Qitbani — from Shuyaym ibn Baytan and Yazid ibn Subh al-Asbahi — from Junadah ibn Abi Umayyah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We were with Busr ibn Abi Artah at sea when a thief called Misdaq was brought, who had stolen a Bactrian (two-humped) camel. He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: 'Hands are not to be cut off during travel.' Were it not for that, I would have cut off his hand.""

This is a Syrian (Shami) chain of narration.

Yahya ibn Ma'in used to say: ""The people of Madinah deny that Busr ibn Abi Artah heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."" And Yahya said: ""Busr ibn Abi Artah is a wicked man."" [Sahih]"
"18230","(18224) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz — informed us of that; Abu al-Abbas — narrated to us; al-Abbas al-Duri — from Yahya ibn Ma'in.

The Sheikh said: ""Yahya only said that because of the evil of his actions that became apparent in his fighting against the people of al-Harrah and otherwise. And Allah knows best."" [Sahih]"
"18231","(18225) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī said: Abū Yūsuf narrated to us: some of our shaykhs narrated to us from Makḥūl, from Zayd ibn Thābit (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: ""Ḥudūd (prescribed punishments) are not to be carried out in Dār al-Ḥarb (the Abode of War), for fear that its people might join the enemy."" [Weak]"
"18232","(18226) He said: and some of our companions narrated to us from Thawr ibn Yazīd, from Ḥakīm ibn ʿUmayr, that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to ʿUmayr ibn Saʿd al-Anṣārī and to his governors: ""Do not carry out any ḥadd (prescribed punishment) upon any Muslim in the Abode of War until they come out to the territory of reconciliation (al-muṣālaḥah).""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""What has been narrated from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) is reprehensible (munkar). It is objectionable to cite as evidence a hadith that is not firmly established, and to say, 'A shaykh narrated to us' — and who is this shaykh? — and to say, 'Makḥūl narrated from Zayd ibn Thābit,' when Makḥūl never saw Zayd ibn Thābit."" Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""And his statement, 'He might join the polytheists' — if he does join them, that is worse for him. And whoever abandons the ḥadd out of fear that the one liable to the punishment might flee to the lands of the polytheists, should carry it out in the coastal regions of the Muslims and their frontier territories that border the Abode of War."" [Weak]"
"18233","(18227) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Rāzī, the son-in-law of Salamah ibn al-Faḍl al-Anṣārī, narrated to us: Salamah narrated to me: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to me from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, and from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, who said:

""ʿAbd ibn al-Azwaḍ, Ḍirār ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, and Abū Jandal ibn Suhayl ibn ʿAmr drank wine in al-Shām. They were brought before Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ (may Allah be pleased with him). Abū Jandal said: 'By Allah, I only drank it based on an interpretation (taʾwīl). I heard Allah say: {Upon those who believe and do righteous deeds there is no blame for what they have eaten, provided they are conscious of Allah, believe, and do righteous deeds} [al-Māʾidah 93].' So Abū ʿUbaydah wrote to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) about their matter.

Then ʿAbd ibn al-Azwaḍ said: 'Our enemy is present before us. If you see fit to postpone our matter until we meet our enemy tomorrow, then if Allah honors us with martyrdom, that will suffice you, and you will not carry out the punishment upon us in a state of disgrace. And if we return, you may then carry out what your companion (the Prophet) has commanded you to do.' Abū ʿUbaydah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'Very well.'

When the people met in battle, ʿAbd ibn al-Azwaḍ was killed as a martyr. Then ʿUmar's letter (may Allah be pleased with him) arrived, stating: 'The one who led Abū Jandal into sin has indeed prepared the argument for himself. When this letter of mine reaches you, carry out their prescribed punishings upon them. And peace.'

So Abū ʿUbaydah (may Allah be pleased with him) summoned the two and carried out the ḥadd upon them. Abū Jandal had a noble standing, as did his father. He began talking to himself until it was said that he had become afflicted with waswās (insinuating whispers). So Abū ʿUbaydah wrote to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him): 'To proceed: I have carried out the ḥadd upon Abū Jandal, but he has been talking to himself to the point that we feared he might perish.'

So ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to Abū Jandal: 'To proceed: That which led you into sin has, in fact, sealed repentance upon you. In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. Ḥā Mīm. The revelation of the Book is from Allah, the Almighty, the All-Knowing, the Forgiver of sin, the Acceptor of repentance, the Severe in punishment, the Possessor of bounty — there is no deity except Him; to Him is the final return} [Ghāfir 1–3].'

When he read ʿUmar's letter (may Allah be pleased with him), that which had afflicted him departed, as though he had been released from a tether."" [Weak]"
"18234","(18228) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, saying: al-Layth was of the view that the ḥadd should be carried out in the land of the Romans (ar-Rūm), because Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: {And whoever Allah wills to put to trial, you will have no power for him against Allah} [al-Māʾidah 41]. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18235","(18229) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū ʿAmr al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿAmmār and Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah both said: Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to me from his father, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him), regarding the story of the Prophet's (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) Ḥajj, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said in his sermon: ""Beware! Indeed, all matters of the Jāhiliyyah (pre-Islamic era of ignorance) are placed beneath my feet, and the usury of the Jāhiliyyah is abolished. The first usury I abolish is the usury of al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, for it is entirely abolished.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ as it has passed. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"18236","(18230) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us: Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us: Ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated to me: Abū Ḥāzim narrated to me that he heard Sahl ibn Saʿd (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say on the day of Khaybar: ""Tomorrow I shall give the banner to a man by whose hands Allah will grant victory.""

The people spent the night discussing among themselves who would be given it. When morning came, the people went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), each of them hoping to be given it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Where is ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib?""

They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, he is complaining of his eye."" So he sent for him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) spat into his eye and supplicated for him, and he was healed on the spot, as though nothing had been wrong with him. He gave him the banner.

ʿAlī said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall I fight them until they become like us?"" He said: ""Proceed at your pace until you alight in their courtyard, then invite them to Islam and inform them of what is obligatory upon them of the rights (of Allah). By Allah, if Allah guides a single person through you, that is better for you than red camels.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"18237","(18231) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā, meaning al-Dhuhalī, narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us. Both said: Naṣr ibn ʿAlī al-Jahḍamī informed me: my father narrated to me: Khālid ibn Qays narrated to us from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) wrote to Kisrā, Qayṣar, and to every tyrant ruler, inviting them to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Naṣr ibn ʿAlī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1774]"
"18238","(18232) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Sufyān al-Thawrī informed me.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Abū al-Qāsim Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭabarānī informed us: Muʿādh ibn al-Mathannā and Yūsuf al-Qāḍī both said: Ibn Kathīr narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) never fought any people without first inviting them (to Islam)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18239","(18233) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawyah al-ʿAskarī informed us: Abū ʿAmr Mūsā ibn ʿĪsā ibn al-Mundhir al-Ḥimṣī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Muṣaffá informed us: Baqiyyah narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn Musāfir narrated to me: Muqātil ibn Ḥayyān narrated to me from Abū al-ʿĀliyah, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

""Captives from (the worshippers of) al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā were brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."" He said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'Did you invite them to Islam?' They said: 'No.' He said to them: 'Did they invite you to Islam?' They said: 'No.' He said: 'Release them until they reach their place of safety.'""

Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) recited these two verses: {Indeed, We have sent you as a witness, a bearer of glad tidings, and a warner} [al-Aḥzāb 45–46], and {And this Qur'an has been revealed to me that I may warn you thereby and whomever it reaches. Do you indeed bear witness?} [al-Anʿām 19] to the end of the verse.

Rūḥ ibn Musāfir is weak. [Weak]"
"18240","(18234) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Qāsim ibn al-Qāsim al-Sayyārī in Marw informed us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ḥātim informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Shaqīq informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us from Ibn ʿAwn, who said:

""I wrote to Nāfiʿ asking him about the invitation (to Islam) before fighting. He wrote back: 'That was only in the early period of Islam. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) launched a surprise raid upon Banī al-Muṣṭaliq while they were unaware and their livestock were being watered at the watering place. He killed their fighters and took their women and children captive, and on that day he obtained Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith.' ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated this to me, and he was in that army.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan, and Muslim narrated it as it has passed. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"18241","(18235) 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, Hishām ibn 'Alī narrated to us, Ibn Rajā' informed us, 'Ikrimah informed us, from Iyās ibn Salamah ibn al-Akw', my father narrated to me, he said: We set out with Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had appointed him as our commander during an expedition. When we drew near, Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) commanded us, so we made camp. When we had prayed the morning prayer (al-ṣubḥ), Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) commanded us, and we launched a raid. We came upon the water source and killed whomever we killed, and he mentioned the hadith.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ through another chain from 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammār, and the hadiths that have preceded regarding the permissibility of night raids are evidence in this matter. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18242","(18236) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us, 'Abdullāh ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Thābit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to launch raids at daybreak and then listen. If he heard the adhān, he would hold back; otherwise, he would raid.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Ḥammād ibn Salamah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
"18243","(18237) Abū al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, 'Ubayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Abū Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, Abū Isḥāq narrated from Ḥumayd, he said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) raided a people, he would not launch a raid until morning. If he heard the adhān, he would hold back. If he did not hear the adhān, he would raid after daybreak.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Mu'āwiyah ibn 'Amr from Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — as previously mentioned]"
"18244","(18238) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād, Ismā'īl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Sa'dān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Sufyān ibn 'Uyaynah narrated from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Nawfal, from a man from Muzaynah known as Ibn 'Iṣām, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to say when he dispatched a raiding party: ""When you hear a mu'adhdhin or see a mosque, do not kill anyone."" [Ḍa'īf]"
"18245","(18239) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us, 'Uthmān ibn Sa'īd al-Dārimī narrated to us, al-Qa'nabī narrated — reciting it to Mālik — from Nāfi', from 'Abdullāh ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade travelling with the Qur'ān to the land of the enemy.

Mālik said: I believe it was out of fear that the enemy might seize it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18246","(18240) Abū 'Abdullāh informed us, 'Alī ibn 'Īsā ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn, Muḥammad ibn 'Amr al-Ḥarashī, and Ibrāhīm ibn 'Alī said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, he said: I read it to Mālik, and he mentioned it similarly, without mentioning Mālik's statement.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qa'nabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā."
"18247","(18241) Abū Muḥammad 'Abdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Sa'īd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī informed us in Makkah, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Za'farānī narrated to us, Ismā'īl ibn 'Ulayyah narrated from Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī, from Nāfi', from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade travelling with the Qur'ān to the land of the enemy, out of fear that the enemy might seize it.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Ismā'īl ibn 'Ulayyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — as previously mentioned]"
"18248","(18242) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, 'Īsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Abū Isḥāq, from Dhī al-Jawshan — a man from the Ḍibāb — he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) after he had finished with the people of Badr, bringing a colt of mine called al-Qarḥā'. I said: ""O Muhammad, I have brought you the offspring of al-Qarḥā' so that you may take it."" He said: ""I have no need of it. If you wish, I may exchange for you the choicest of the coats of mail of Badr."" I said: ""I would not exchange it today for a slave."" He said: ""Then I have no need of it.""

The Shaykh said: His statement ""I may exchange for you"" (uqīḍuka) refers to al-muqāyaḍah, which is barter. [Ḍa'īf]"
"18249","(18243) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, al-Rabī' ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfi'ī informed us, 'Abd al-Wahhāb ibn 'Abd al-Majīd informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, 'Abd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī informed us, Ayyūb narrated from Abū Qilābah, from Abū al-Muhallab, from 'Imrān ibn Ḥuṣayn (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) captured a man from Banī 'Uqayl, and he mentioned the hadith. He said: The she-camel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was taken, and a woman from the Anṣār was captured. The she-camel had been taken before her. She was among them, and they used to bring the livestock to them. He said: She broke free one night from her bonds and came to the camels. Whenever she approached a camel, it would bellow, until she came to that she-camel and hobbled it, but it did not bellow. It was a she-camel that was prone to much bellowing. She sat upon its hindquarters, then urged it on, and it set off. She was searched for that night but could not be found. She made a vow to Allah concerning it: if Allah delivered her by means of it, she would surely sacrifice it. When they arrived, they recognized the she-camel and said: ""It is the she-camel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)."" She said: ""I have vowed to Allah concerning it that if Allah delivers me by means of it, I will surely sacrifice it."" They said: ""No, by Allah, do not sacrifice it until we seek permission from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)."" They came to him and informed him that So-and-so woman had come upon his she-camel and that she had vowed to Allah concerning it that if Allah delivered her by means of it, she would surely sacrifice it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Glory be to Allah! What a poor recompense she has given it — that if Allah delivered her by means of it, she would surely sacrifice it! There is no fulfilment of a vow in disobedience to Allah, and no fulfilment of a vow regarding that over which the servant has no authority"" — or he said: ""over which the son of Ādam has no authority.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1641]"
"18250","(18244) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū 'Amr al-Ḥīrī informed us, Abū Ya'lā narrated to us, Abū al-Rabī' narrated to us, Ḥammād narrated from Ayyūb, from Abū Qilābah, from Abū al-Muhallab, from 'Imrān ibn Ḥuṣayn (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The she-camel called al-'Aḍbā' belonged to a man from Banī 'Uqayl and was among the foremost camels of the pilgrims. The man was captured and al-'Aḍbā' was taken. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) passed by him while he was in bonds, and he mentioned the hadith up to his saying: Then the man was ransomed for two men, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) kept al-'Aḍbā' for his own mount. Then the polytheists raided the grazing herds of al-Madīnah and took them, and al-'Aḍbā' was among those herds, and they captured a woman from the Muslims. Then he mentioned the hadith regarding the story of her escape, in a manner similar to the hadith of al-Thaqafī.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Rabī' al-Zahrānī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — as previously mentioned]"
"18251","(18245) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān and ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us, from Ayyūb, from Abū Qilābah, from Abū al-Muhallab, from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn (may Allah be pleased with him), that a group of people raided and captured a woman of the Anṣār and a she-camel belonging to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). The woman and the she-camel remained in their possession. Then the woman escaped, mounted the she-camel, and came to Madīnah. The she-camel of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was recognized. She said, ""I vowed that if Allah saves me by means of it, I shall surely sacrifice it."" They prevented her from sacrificing it until they could mention the matter to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). He said, ""What a poor reward you have given it! If Allah has saved you by means of it, would you sacrifice it? There is no vow in disobedience to Allah, nor in that over which the son of Ādam has no power."" Both of them said together, or one of them said, in the hadith: and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) took his she-camel.

Abū Saʿīd added in his narration: al-Shāfiʿī said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) took his she-camel after the polytheists had seized it and the Anṣārī woman had recovered it from the polytheists."
"18252","(18246) Abū al-Qāsim Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ṣaqr informed us in Baghdād: ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Abī Rūbā narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Hārūn narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān Luwayn narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated to us, from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), that a slave of theirs fled to the enemy, then the Muslims prevailed over him. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) returned him and had not divided him.

Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the Sunan from Ṣāliḥ ibn Suhayl from Yaḥyā. [Ḥasan]"
"18253","(18247) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār informed us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), that a slave of his joined the enemy on a horse of his. Khālid ibn al-Walīd (may Allah be pleased with him) prevailed over them both and returned them to him. This is what Abū Muʿāwiyah said. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr clarified from ʿUbayd Allāh what occurred during the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and what occurred after him. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18254","(18248) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Anbārī and al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Maʿnī both said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from ʿUbayd Allāh.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bisṭāmī informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: al-Ḥasan, i.e., Ibn Sufyān, narrated to us: Ibn Numayr, i.e., Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr, narrated to us: my father narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: A horse of his was taken by the enemy. The Muslims prevailed over them, and it was returned to him during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). He said: A slave of his fled and joined the Byzantines. The Muslims prevailed over him, and Khālid ibn al-Walīd returned him to him after the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him).

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ, saying: Ibn Numayr said: ʿUbayd Allāh narrated to us, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3068–9069]"
"18255","(18249) Abū ʿAmr al-Bisṭāmī informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Zuḥayr narrated to us: Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), that he was on a horse of his on the day the Muslims encountered Ṭayyiʾ and Asad, and the commander of the Muslims was Khālid ibn al-Walīd, whom Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) had dispatched. The horse bolted with ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar into a ravine and threw him, and ʿAbd Allāh fell. The horse ran away and the enemy seized it. When Allah defeated the enemy, Khālid returned the horse to ʿAbd Allāh. [Ṣaḥīḥ]

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus. It is therefore possible that the slave was returned during the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and the horse after him, so as to accord with the narration of Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah and then this narration of Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah. And Allah knows best. None of the narrations contain any mention of division. Perhaps the reference to division in the narration of Yaḥyā is from some of the narrators rather than from Ibn ʿUmar."
"18256","(18250) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: al-Thiqah narrated to us, from Makhramah ibn Bukayr, from his father — I do not recall from whom he narrated — that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) said regarding what the enemy seized of the Muslims' property, whether they overpowered it or it fled to them, and then the Muslims recovered it: its owner has the greatest right to it, both before and after the division. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18257","(18251) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Khamīruwayh informed us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, from Zāʾidah, from al-Rukayn ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Fazārī, from his father, who said: The polytheists seized a horse of theirs during the time of Khālid ibn al-Walīd; they had taken possession of it. Then the Muslims recovered it during the time of Saʿd. We spoke to him, and he returned it to us after it had been divided and had fallen into the fifth of the governorship. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18258","(18252) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us: al-Qāsim ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmarah narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Malik al-Zarrād, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said, ""I have found my camel in the war booty; the polytheists had taken it."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him, ""Go. If you find your camel before it has been divided, take it. But if you find that it has been divided, then you have the greatest right to it at its price, if you wish.""

This hadith is known through al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmarah from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Mayṣarah. Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmarah is abandoned (matrūk); no proof is established by him.

It was also narrated by Maslamah ibn ʿAlī al-Khushanī from ʿAbd al-Malik, and he is also weak. It was narrated through another chain, also unknown, from ʿAbd al-Malik, and none of that is authentic. It was also narrated from Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Farwah and Yāsīn ibn Muʿādh al-Zayyāt from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh from his father, marfūʿ, with a difference between them in its wording. (J) Isḥāq and Yāsīn are both abandoned; no proof is established by them. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18259","(18253) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah and Abū Bakr al-Fārisī both narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us, from Simāk, from Tamīm ibn Ṭarfah, who said: A man recognized a she-camel of his in the possession of another man. He brought him to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). He was asked about the matter of the she-camel and it was found that its origin was that it had been purchased from the hands of the enemy. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to the one who recognized it: ""If you wish, you may take it at the price for which he purchased it, for you have the greatest right to it. Otherwise, leave him and his she-camel."" He said: Two witnesses were asked. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18260","(18254) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīruwayh informed us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from Tamīm ibn Ṭarfah, that the enemy seized a she-camel of a man of the Muslims. A man of the Muslims purchased it. Its owner recognized it and brought a dispute to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). He said: ""Return to him the price for which he purchased it, or leave him and his she-camel.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said in the narration of Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Baghdādī from him: Tamīm ibn Ṭarfah did not meet the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) nor hear from him. A mursal narration does not establish a proof, because it is unknown from whom he took it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18261","(18255) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīrūyah informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah, from Qatādah, from Rajāʾ ibn Ḥaywah, from Qabīṣah ibn Dhūʾayb that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh, may Allah be pleased with him, said regarding what the polytheists had seized and then the Muslims recovered, and the owner recognized it: ""If he finds it before it has been divided, then it is his. But if the shares have been apportioned, then he has nothing."" Qatādah said, and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""It belongs to the Muslims, whether it has been divided or not."" This is munqaṭiʿ. Qabīṣah did not meet ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, and the narration of Qatādah from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, is also munqaṭiʿ. [Weak]"
"18262","(18256) And Abū Naṣr informed us, Abū al-Faḍl informed us, Aḥmad informed us, al-Ḥasan narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh narrated from Ibn Lahīʿah, Sulaymān ibn Mūsā narrated to me, from Rajāʾ ibn Ḥaywah, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Abū ʿUbaydah regarding what the enemy had seized of the Muslims' property and then the Muslims recovered thereafter: ""It should be returned to its owners before it is divided."" [Weak]"
"18263","(18257) And with his chain of narration, ʿAbd Allāh narrated from Saʿīd, from a man, from al-Shaʿbī, who said: ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to al-Sāʾib ibn al-Aqraʿ: ""Any Muslim who finds his slaves and his possessions in their original form has the greater right to them. But if he finds them in the hands of traders after they have been divided, then he has no claim. And any free person whom the traders have purchased — their capital should be returned to them, for a free person may not be sold or purchased.""

Another narrator narrated it from Saʿīd ibn Abī ʌArūbah, from Abū Ḥarīz, from al-Shaʿbī.

Al-Shāfiʿī said in the narration of Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from him: This narration from ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, is mursal. It was narrated only from al-Shaʿbī on the authority of ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, and from Rajāʾ ibn Ḥaywah on the authority of ʿUmar, and neither of them met ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, nor came close to that. Al-Shāfiʿī said: And the ḥadīth of Saʿd is more established than the ḥadīth from ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, because it is from Rukayn ibn al-Rabīʿ from his father that Saʿd did it, whereas the ḥadīth from ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, is mursal. [Weak]"
"18264","(18258) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīrūyah informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Ibn Lahīʿah, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Jaʿfar, who narrated to him from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulaymān ibn Yasār and from Zayd ibn Thābit, may Allah be pleased with him, who both said: ""Whatever property the enemy seizes from the Muslims and is then recovered, and its owners recognize it before it is divided, it shall be returned to them. But if they do not recognize it until after it has been divided, it shall not be returned to them.""

Thus I found it in my book, and it is thus munqaṭiʿ. And Ibn Lahīʿah is not used as a basis for argument, and Allah knows best.

And it has been narrated from Sulaymān, from Zayd ibn Thābit. [Weak]"
"18265","(18259) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad al-Dimashqī narrated to us, Hishām narrated — and Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Khuraym narrated to us, Hishām ibn Khālid narrated to us, Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, Yāsīn ibn Muʿādh al-Zayyāt narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Whoever embraces Islam over something, it is his.""

Yāsīn ibn Muʿādh al-Zayyāt is a Kūfan, weak (ḍaʿīf). Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn, al-Bukhārī, and other ḥuffāẓ criticized him.

This ḥadīth is narrated only from Ibn Abī Mulaykah from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, as mursal, and from ʿUrwah from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, as mursal.

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: The meaning of that is: whoever embraces Islam over something whose ownership is permissible for him, then it is his. [Weak]"
"18266","(18260) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated. He said: Maʿmar said: al-Zuhrī said: ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam, in the story of al-Ḥudaybiyyah and what ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd al-Thaqafī said to al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah when al-Mughīrah said to him: ""Keep your hand away from the beard of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him."" He said: ""O traitor! Am I not striving in your cause of treachery?"" Al-Mughīrah had accompanied a people in the Jāhiliyyah and killed them and took their wealth, then he came and embraced Islam. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""As for Islam, I accept it. As for the wealth, I have nothing to do with it.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Razzāq.

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, refrained from taking the khums, as Yūnus narrated from al-Zuhrī that it was the wealth of treachery (māl ghadr), and as ʿUqayl narrated from al-Zuhrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""We do not take khums from wealth that was taken by usurpation."" So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, left the wealth in the hands of al-Mughīrah, and in that is an indication that he owned it by taking it, and Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2745]"
"18267","(18261) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us, Abū Shaykh al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us, Mūsā ibn Aʿyan narrated from Layth ibn Abī Sulaym, from ʿAlqamah, from Sulaymān ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, that he used to say regarding the Ahl al-Dhimmah: ""They have what they embraced Islam upon of their wealth, their slaves, their homes, their land, and their livestock. Nothing is obligatory upon them except the ṣadaqah."" [Weak]"
"18268","(18262) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me, my father narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Jews of Banū al-Naḍīr and Qurayẓah waged war against the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, exiled Banū al-Naḍīr and left Qurayẓah and showed them favor, until Qurayẓah waged war after that. He killed their men and divided their women, their wealth, and their children among the Muslims, except for some of them who joined the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, so he granted them safety and they embraced Islam. And he mentioned the ḥadīth.

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
"18269","(18263) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah narrated to me, from a shaykh of Banū Qurayẓah, who said: ""Do you know what was the Islam of Thaʿlabah and Asīd, the two sons of Saʿyah, and Asad ibn ʿUbayd — a group of men from Hudhayl who were not from Banū Qurayẓah or Naḍīr but were above that?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""A man from the Jews of Shām came to us, called Ibn al-Haybān, and stayed with us. By Allah, we never saw a man who prayed the five prayers better than him. He came to us some years before the mission of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. When we suffered drought and rain was scarce, we would say to him: 'O Ibn al-Haybān, go out and pray for rain for us.' He would say: 'No, by Allah, not until you offer ṣadaqah before your going out.' We would say: 'How much should we offer?' He would say: 'A ṣāʿ of dates or two mudd of barley.' Then he would go out to the open area of our ḥārah, and we would be with him, and he would pray for rain. By Allah, he would not rise from his place before the ravines would flow with water. He did this more than once, twice, or three times. Then his death approached, and we gathered around him, and he said: 'O assembly of Jews, what do you think has brought me out from the land of wine and leavened bread to the land of misery and hunger?' We said: 'You know best.' He said: 'Indeed, it was only that I was anticipating the emergence of a prophet whose time has drawn near. These lands are his place of migration. Follow him, and do not be outpaced to him when he emerges, O assembly of Jews, for he will shed blood and take captives — the children and women of those who oppose him. Let nothing prevent you from him.' Then he died. On the night when Qurayẓah was conquered, those three young men — and they were young lads — said: 'O assembly of Jews, what Ibn al-Haybān mentioned to you —' They said: 'What is it?' They said: 'Yes, by Allah, it is indeed he, O assembly of Jews, by Allah, it is indeed he, by his description.' Then they descended and embraced Islam and left their wealth, their children, and their families. Their wealth had been in the fortress with the polytheists, and when it was conquered, that was returned to them."" [Weak]"
"18270","(18264) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb Abū Ḥafṣ narrated to us, al-Firyābī narrated to us, Abān narrated — and ʿUmar, who was ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ḥāzim, said: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather Ṣakhr, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, raided Thaqīf. When Ṣakhr learned of this, he rode with a cavalry to reinforce the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, but he found that the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, had already returned without conquering. So Ṣakhr made a covenant of Allah and His protection that he would not leave this fortress until they submitted to the judgment of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. He did not leave them until they submitted to the judgment of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. Then Ṣakhr wrote to him: ""To begin with: Thaqīf have submitted to your judgment, O Messenger of Allah, and I am coming to them with cavalry."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, commanded the congregational prayer, and he made ten supplications for Aḥmas: 'O Allah, bless Aḥmas in its cavalry and its men.' The people came, and al-Mughīrah spoke and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, Ṣakhr has taken my paternal aunt, and she has entered into what the Muslims have entered into."" So he called him and said: ""O Ṣakhr, when a people embrace Islam, they secure their blood and their wealth. Return al-Mughīrah's paternal aunt to him."" So he returned her. And he asked the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, for a water source of Banī Sulaym who had fled from Islam and left that water. He said: ""O Prophet of Allah, grant it to me and my people."" He said: ""Yes."" So he granted it to him. He embraced Islam — meaning the Sulamīs — and they came to Ṣakhr and asked him to return the water to them, but he refused. So they came to the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and said: ""O Prophet of Allah, we have embraced Islam and come to Ṣakhr to return our water to us, but he has refused us."" So he called him and said: ""O Ṣakhr, when a people embrace Islam, they secure their wealth and their blood. Return the people's water to them."" He said: ""Yes, O Prophet of Allah."" And I saw the face of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, change at that moment with redness, out of modesty for having taken the slave-girl and the water.

The Shaykh said: The inference is drawn from his statement, peace and blessings be upon him: ""When a people embrace Islam, they secure their wealth and their blood."" As for the recovery of the water from Ṣakhr after the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, had granted it to him, it is as though it was by his own voluntary consent, and that is why the trace of modesty appeared on his face, and Allah knows best. As for al-Mughīrah's paternal aunt, if she embraced Islam after being captured, then it is as though he considered her Islam before the division as securing her wealth. And it is also possible that her Islam was before the capture, and Allah knows best. And this Ṣakhr is Ibn al-ʿAylah. Al-Bukhārī said it from Abū Nuʿaym, from Abān, from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Ḥāzim, from Ṣakhr ibn al-ʿAylah, without mentioning ""from his father."" And in the story of the flock of al-Suḥaymī there is what indicates the apparent meaning of the story of al-Mughīrah's paternal aunt, for he embraced Islam and then said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my family and my wealth."" He said: ""As for your wealth, it has been divided among the Muslims. As for your family, see whom you are able to reach among them."" So his son was returned to him. And it is possible that the people of the spoils voluntarily returned it, as he did in the captives of Ḥawāzin, and he compensated the people of the khums from their share, and Allah knows best. And the chain of narration of both ḥadīths is not strong. [Weak]"
"18271","(18265) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to me; Fayyāḍ narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar reported from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent Khālid ibn al-Walid — I think he said — to Banū Jadhīmah. He invited them to Islam, but they could not bring themselves to say ""We have accepted Islam."" Instead they said, ""We have turned, we have turned"" (ṣabaynā, ṣabaynā). So Khālid began killing them and taking captives. He then gave each one of us a captive. When morning came one day, he commanded us, saying: ""Let each one of us kill his captive."" Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""By Allah, I shall not kill my captive, and none of my companions shall kill his captive either."" He said: ""So we came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and mentioned to him what Khālid had done. He raised his hands and then said: 'O Allah, I declare myself innocent before You of what Khālid has done.'"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Maḥmūd, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq."
"18272","(18266) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Sufyān reported from ʿAmr, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: A group of Muslims came upon a man among his sheep. He said, ""Al-salāmu ʿalaykum (Peace be upon you)."" So they seized him and killed him, and they took those sheep. Then the verse was revealed: {And do not say to anyone who offers you peace, ""You are not a believer""} [al-Nisāʾ 94]. Ibn ʿAbbās recited it as al-salām. al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from Sufyān, and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm."
"18273","(18267) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Mihrān narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us; Isrāʾīl reported from Simāk, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: A man from Banū Sulaym passed by a group of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he had sheep with him. He greeted them with salām, but they said, ""He only greeted you to seek protection from you."" So they set upon him and killed him and took his sheep. They brought the sheep to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), so Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {O you who believe, when you go forth in the way of Allah, be discerning, and do not say to anyone who offers you peace, ""You are not a believer,"" seeking the fleeting goods of the life of this world. With Allah are abundant gains. Even thus were you before, but Allah conferred His favour upon you; so be discerning} [al-Nisāʾ 94]."
"18274","(18268) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq; Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qusayṭ narrated to me; from Abū al-Qaʿqāʿ ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ḥadrad; from his father Abū Ḥadrad (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent us to Iḍam. I went out among a group of Muslims among whom were Abū Qatādah al-Ḥārith ibn Ribʿī and Muḥallim ibn Jathāmah. We set out until we were in the lower reaches of Iḍam. ʿĀmir ibn al-Aḍbaṭ passed by us on his camel. When he passed by us, he greeted us with the greeting of Islam. We held back from him, but Muḥallim ibn Jathāmah attacked him and killed him and took his camel and what was with him. We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and informed him of the matter. Then the Qurʾān was revealed concerning us: {O you who believe, when you go forth in the way of Allah, be discerning, and do not say to anyone who offers you peace, ""You are not a believer""} [al-Nisāʾ 94] to the end of the verse. Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated it thus from Ibn Isḥāq. And Muḥammad ibn Salamah narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Yazīd, from Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ḥadrad, from his father. And Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar narrated it from Ibn Isḥāq, from Yazīd, from al-Qaʿqāʿ ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ḥadrad, from his father. And Yaḥyī ibn Saʿīd al-Umawī said it thus from Ibn Isḥāq. And Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated it in the narration of Ḥajjāj from him, from Ibn Isḥāq, from Yazīd, from Abū Ḥadrad al-Aslamī, from his father, and it was said otherwise. And ʿAbd Allāh ibn Idrīs narrated it from Ibn Isḥāq, from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ḥadrad al-Aslamī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I was in a raiding party that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent to Iḍam, a valley among the valleys of Ashjaʿ. And Sulaymī al-Taymī narrated it from Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qusayṭ, from al-Qaʿqāʿ ibn ʿUbayd Allāh — or he said ibn ʿAbd Allāh — from Abī ʿAbd Allāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent us."
"18275","(18269) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī narrated to us; Abū Usāmah reported from al-Walīd ibn Kathīr, from Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qusayṭ, that a man from Aslam narrated to him that he heard Ibn Abī Ḥadrad al-Aslamī (may Allah be pleased with him) relate: that he was in a raiding party. A man saw them while he was on a mountain. He came down to them and greeted them with salām. They seized him and killed him. Concerning him was revealed: {And do not say to anyone who offers you peace, ""You are not a believer,"" seeking the fleeting goods of the life of this world} [al-Nisāʾ 94]. And the man whom they killed was ʿĀmir ibn al-Aḍbaṭ al-Ashjaʿī."
"18276","(18270) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me. He said: I heard Ziyāḍ ibn Ḍumayrah ibn Saʿd al-Sulamī relating to ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr that his father and his grandfather witnessed Ḥunayn with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) led us in the Ẓuhr prayer, then he went to the shade of a tree. al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis and ʿUyyaynah ibn Badr came to him, disputing over the blood of ʿĀmir ibn al-Aḍbaṭ al-Ashjaʿ, who had been killed by Muḥallim ibn Jathāmah ibn Qays. ʿUyyaynah was demanding the blood of the Ashjaʿī ʿĀmir ibn al-Aḍbaṭ because he was from Qays, and al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis was defending Muḥallim ibn Jathāmah because he was from Khindif, and he was the chief of Khindif at that time. We heard ʿUyyaynah saying: ""By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I shall not let him be until I make his womenfolk taste of grief what he made my womenfolk taste of."" And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was saying: ""You shall receive the blood-money — fifty on this journey of ours, and fifty when we return."" But he refused. Then a man from Banū Layth stood up — he was called Miktal, a short, stout man — and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I can find no likeness for this slain man in the early days of Islam except that of a herd of camels that was driven and the first of them was shot and the last of them bolted. Establish a Sunnah today and alter it tomorrow."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) raised his hand and then said: ""You shall receive the blood-money — fifty on this journey of ours, and fifty when we return."" So the people accepted it. Then he said: ""Bring your companion so that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) may seek forgiveness for him."" So they brought him. A tall, dark-complexioned man stood up wearing a striped garment, having prepared himself for killing. He sat before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). He said to him: ""What is your name?"" He said: ""Muḥallim ibn Jathāmah."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""O Allah, do not forgive Muḥallim ibn Jathāmah. O Allah, do not forgive Muḥallim ibn Jathāmah. O Allah, do not forgive Muḥallim ibn Jathāmah."" Then he said to him: ""Stand up."" So he stood, catching the overflow of his tears with the excess of his cloak. As for us, among ourselves we used to say: ""We indeed hope that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) has sought forgiveness for him."" But he displayed this openly so that the people would desist from one another. What appeared from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was this. And Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated something similar from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq."
"18277","(18271) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Wahb ibn Bayān and Aḥmad ibn Saʿīd al-Hamdānī both said: Ibn Wahb narrated to me; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated to me; from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith; from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar; that he heard Ziyād ibn Saʿd ibn Ḍumayrah al-Sulamī relating to ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father: that Muḥallim ibn Jathāmah al-Laythī killed a man from Ashjaʿ in Islam. This was the first blood-money case upon which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) ruled. He mentioned the meaning, except that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""O ʿUyyaynah, will you not accept the blood-mīrah?"" — meaning the blood-money. And he said at its end: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Did you kill him with your weapon in the early days of Islam? O Allah, do not forgive Muḥallim."" He said it in a loud voice, and he did not mention what came after it."
"18278","(18272) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār and Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh both said: Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah narrated from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl, who said: Naṣr ibn ʿĀṣim al-Laythī came to us. Naṣr said: ʿUqbah ibn Mālik, who was from his people, narrated to us: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent a raiding party. They made a raid upon a group of people. A man from the people broke away, and a man from the raiding party pursued him with his sword drawn. The one who had broken away from the people said, ""I am a Muslim,"" but he paid no heed to him and struck him and killed him. The report reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he spoke a severe word. The killer said: ""By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, he only said what he said to seek protection from being killed."" He turned away from him three times. He repeated it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) turned to him, his displeasure visible in his face, and then said: ""Indeed Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has forbidden [the killing of] anyone who kills a believer."" He said it three times. Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd corroborated it from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl."
"18279","(18273) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah narrated. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — and the wording is his — Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm and ʿImrān ibn Mūsā both said: Shaybān ibn Farūkh narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us; Thābit al-Bunānī narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rabāḥ, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Delegations came to Muʿāwiyah, and that was in Ramaḍān. Some of us would prepare food for others. Abū Hurayrah used to frequently invite us to his abode. I said: Shall I not prepare food and invite them to my abode? So I ordered food to be prepared. Then I met Abū Hurayrah in the evening and said: ""The invitation is at my place tonight."" He said: ""You have preceded me."" I said: ""Yes."" So I invited them. Abū Hurayrah said: ""Shall I not teach you a hadith from your hadith, O company of the Anṣār?"" Then he mentioned the Conquest of Makkah and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) advanced until he came to Makkah. He sent al-Zubayr on one of the two flanks and sent Khālid ibn al-Walid on the other flank, and he sent Abū ʿUbaydah on the infantry. They took the lower part of the valley, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was in his battalion. He looked and saw me and said: ""Abū Hurayrah!"" I said: ""Here I am, O Messenger of Allah."" He called the Anṣār and said: ""Let no one come to me except an Anṣārī."" So they surrounded him. Abū Dāwūd added: He said: ""Call the Anṣār, and do not let anyone come to me except an Anṣārī."" He said: I did that. Shaybān said in his narration: Quraysh gathered their riffraff and followers and said: ""Let us go ahead of them. If they gain something, we will be with them, and if they are afflicted, we will give what we have been asked."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Do you see the riffraff of Quraysh and their followers?"" Then he placed one hand upon the other and said: ""Until you meet me at al-Ṣafā."" Abū Dāwūd added in his narration: ""Cut them down, cut them down."" Shaybān said in his narration: He said: ""We set out, and there was no one among us who wished to kill anyone but he killed him, and nothing was directed at us."" He said: Abū Sufyān came and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, the green herbage of Quraysh has been destroyed. There will be no Quraysh after today."" He said: ""Whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān is safe."" Abū Dāwūd added in his narration: ""Whoever lays down his weapon is safe."" Shaybān said in his narration: The Anṣār said to one another: ""As for the man, he has been overtaken by longing for his town and compassion for his clan."" Abū Hurayrah said: Revelation came, and when it came it was not hidden from us. When it came, no one would raise his gaze to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) until the revelation had finished. When the revelation was completed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""O company of the Anṣār!"" They said: ""Here we are, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""You said: 'As for the man, he has been overtaken by longing for his town.'"" They said: ""That was so."" He said: ""No! I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger. I have migrated to Allah and to you. My life is your life and my death is your death."" They came to him weeping and saying: ""By Allah, we only said what we said out of concern for Allah and His Messenger."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Indeed Allah and His Messenger confirm you and accept your excuse."" The people went to the house of Abū Sufyān, and the people closed their doors. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) advanced until he came to the Ḥajar [al-Aswad] and kissed it. He performed ṭawāf of the House. Then he came to an idol beside the House that they used to worship. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had a bow in his hand, holding it by the curved end. When he came to the idol, he began stabbing its eye and saying: ""Truth has come and falsehood has vanished. Indeed, falsehood is ever bound to vanish."" When he finished his ṭawāf, he came to al-Ṣafā and ascended upon it until he could see the House. He raised his hands and began praising Allah and supplicating with whatever he wished to supplicate. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān ibn Farūkh, and he also narrated it from the hadith of Bahz ibn Asad from Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah, and he mentioned the additional wording that Abū Dāwūd added."
"18280","(18274) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Thābit al-Bunānī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rabāḥ, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him). He mentioned the hadith and said in it: The Anṣār came and surrounded the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) at al-Ṣafā. Abū Sufyān came and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, the green herbage of Quraysh has been destroyed. There will be no Quraysh after today."" He said: ""Whoever enters his house is safe, and whoever lays down his weapon is safe, and whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān is safe, and whoever closes his door is safe."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Yaḥyā ibn Ḥassān from Ḥammād, except that he did not mention his words: ""Whoever enters his house is safe."""
"18281","(18275) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm informed us — Salām ibn Miskīn informed us — Thābit al-Bunānī informed us — from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rabāḥ al-Anṣārī — from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered Makkah, he dispatched al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ, and Khālid ibn al-Walīd with the cavalry and said: ""O Abū Hurayrah, call out to the Anṣār: 'Take this route, and let no one appear before you except that you put him to death.'"" Then a caller proclaimed: ""No Quraysh shall remain after this day."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: ""Whoever enters his house is safe, and whoever lays down his weapons is safe."" The notables of Quraysh entered the Kaʿbah, filling it. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, performed ṭawāf and prayed behind the Maqām. Then he took hold of the two sides of the door, and they came out and pledged allegiance to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, upon Islam.

Al-Qāsim ibn Salām added on the authority of his father, Salām ibn Miskīn, with this isnād: ""Then he came to the Kaʿbah, took hold of the two doorposts, and said: 'What do you say, and what do you think?' They said: 'We say: the son of a brother, the son of an uncle — forbearing, compassionate.' They said this three times. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: 'I say what Yūsuf said: *No reproach upon you this day. Allah will forgive you, and He is the Most Merciful of the merciful.*' [Yūsuf 92] They went forth as though they had risen from the graves, and they entered into Islam. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"18282","(18276) Abū Bakr ibn al-Muʾammal informed him — Abū Saʿīd al-Rāzī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us — al-Qāsim ibn Salām informed us, and he mentioned it.

And in what al-Shāfiʿī related from Abū Yūsuf regarding this story, he said: When they gathered in the Masjid, he said to them: ""What do you see me doing with you?"" They said: ""Good — a noble brother and the son of a noble brother."" He said: ""Go, you are free (al-ṭulaqāʾ).""

The Shaykh said: He granted them freedom through the first amnesty he had stipulated on the condition of their acceptance. When they accepted, he said: ""You are free"" — meaning by the first amnesty. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited.]"
"18283","(18277) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Ādam informed us — Ibn Idrīs informed us — from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — from al-Zuhrī — from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtban — from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: In the year of the Conquest, al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib brought Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He had accepted Islam at Marr al-Ẓahrān. Al-ʿAbbās said: ""O Messenger of Allah, Abū Sufyān is a man who loves this honor — could you grant him something?"" He said: ""Yes. Whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān is safe, and whoever closes his door is safe."" [Ḥasan]"
"18284","(18278) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Rāzī informed us — Salamah ibn al-Faḍl informed us — from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — from al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maʿbad — from one of his family — from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, encamped at Marr al-Ẓahrān, al-ʿAbbās said: ""By Allah, if the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, enters Makkah by force before they come to him and seek his protection, it will be the destruction of the Quraysh."" So I sat upon the mule of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: ""Perhaps I may find someone with a need who will come to the people of Makkah and inform them of the location of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so that they may come out to him and seek his protection."" I set out and overheard the conversation of Abū Sufyān and Budayl ibn Warqāʾ. I said: ""O Abū Ḥanẓalah!"" He recognized my voice and said: ""Abū al-Faḍl?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""What is the matter? May my father and mother be your ransom."" I said: ""This is the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the people."" He said: ""What is the recourse?"" He rode behind me, and his companion went back. In the morning, I brought him to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he accepted Islam. I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, Abū Sufyān is a man who loves this honor — grant him something."" He said: ""Yes. Whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān is safe, whoever closes his door is safe, and whoever enters the Masjid is safe."" The people dispersed to their homes and to the Masjid. [Ḥasan]"
"18285","(18279) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī informed us — al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād informed us — Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ informed us — Abū Usāmah informed us.

(Ḥ) And Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Nasawī informed me, and the wording is his: Ḥammād ibn Shākir informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl informed us — ʿUbayd ibn Ismāʿīl informed us — Abū Usāmah informed us — from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah — from his father, who said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, set out in the year of the Conquest, word reached the Quraysh. Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb, Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām, and Budayl ibn Warqāʾ went out seeking news of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. They traveled until they reached Marr al-Ẓahrān, and lo, there were fires like the fires of ʿArafah. Abū Sufyān said: ""What are these? They are like the fires of ʿArafah."" Budayl ibn Warqāʾ said: ""The fires of Banū ʿAmr."" Abū Sufyān said: ""ʿAmr is too few for that."" Then some of the guards of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, spotted them, seized them, and brought them to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Abū Sufyān accepted Islam. When the Prophet set out, he said to al-ʿAbbās: ""Detain Abū Sufyān at the mouth of the mountain pass so that he may look upon the Muslims."" Al-ʿAbbās detained him. The tribes passed by the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, company by company, before Abū Sufyān. A company would pass, and he would say: ""O ʿAbbās, who are these?"" He would say: ""This is Ghifār."" He would say: ""What have I to do with Ghifār?"" Then Juhaynah passed, and he said the same. Then Saʿd ibn Hudhaym passed, and he said the same. Then Sulaym passed, and he said the same. Then a great company passed, the like of which he had never seen. He said: ""Who are these?"" He said: ""These are the Anṣār, with Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah among them carrying the banner."" Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah said: ""O Abū Sufyān, today is the day of battle (yawm al-malḥamah); today the Kaʿbah is rendered permissible [to fight for]."" Abū Sufyān said: ""O ʿAbbās, what a day of defense this is!"" Then another company passed, the smallest of the companies, among whom were the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and his Companions, with the banner of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, carried by al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām. When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by Abū Sufyān, he said: ""Do you not know what Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah said?"" He said: ""What did he say?"" He said: ""Such and such."" He said: ""Saʿd has lied. But this is a day in which Allah magnifies the Kaʿbah, and a day in which the Kaʿbah is clothed."" He said: ""And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered that his banner be planted at al-Ḥajūn."" ʿUrwah said: ""And Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim informed me, saying: I heard al-ʿAbbās saying to al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām: 'O Abū ʿAbd Allāh, here the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, has commanded you to plant the banner.'"" He said: ""And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on that day ordered Khālid ibn al-Walīd to enter Makkah from Kadāʾ, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered from Kudayy. Two men were killed from Khālid ibn al-Walīd's cavalry that day: Ḥubaysh ibn al-Ashʿarī and Kurz ibn Jābir al-Fihrī.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ thus. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4280]"
"18286","(18280) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us — Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Zakariyyā al-Adīb informed us — al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Qabbānī informed us — Abū Kurayb informed us — Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb informed me — ʿUmar ibn ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿīd al-Makhzūmī informed me — from his grandfather — from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said on the day of the Conquest of Makkah: ""The people are safe except these four — they are not safe in ḥill nor ḥaram: Ibn Khaṭal, Miqyāṣ ibn Ṣubābah, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Sarḥ, and Ibn Nuqayd."" As for Ibn Khaṭal, al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām killed him. As for ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ, ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, interceded for him, and he was granted safety. He was his brother through nursing, so he was not killed. Miqyāṣ ibn Ṣubābah was killed by a cousin of his whose name I know. And ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, killed Ibn Nuqayd and two singing girls who belonged to Miqyāṣ. One of them was killed, and the other escaped and accepted Islam.

Abū al-Qāsim Saʿīd ibn Yarbūʿ al-Makhzūmī said it. Al-Qabbānī said it.

And in the ḥadīth of Anas ibn Mālik regarding those he ordered to be killed is Umm Sārah, a freed slave woman of Quraysh.

And in the narration of Ibn Isḥāq in al-Maghāzī, Sārah was a freed slave woman of some of Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, and she was among those who used to harm him in Makkah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18287","(18281) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baghdādī informed us — Abū ʿUlāthah Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Khālid informed us — his father ʿAmr ibn Khālid informed us — Ibn Lahīʿah informed us — Abū al-Aswad informed us — from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us — Abū Bakr ibn ʿAttāb informed us — al-Qāsim al-Jawharī informed us — Ibn Abī Uways informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah informed us — from his paternal uncle Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, and this is the wording of the ḥadīth of Mūsā. And the ḥadīth of ʿUrwah has the same meaning. He said: Then Banū Nufāthah, from Banū al-Dīl, raided Banū Kaʿb during the truce that was between the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Quraysh. Banū Kaʿb were in a truce with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Banū Nufāthah were in a truce with Quraysh. Banū Bakr aided Banū Nufāthah, and Quraysh aided them with weapons and slaves. He mentioned the story. He said: A riding party from Banū Kaʿb went out until they came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and mentioned to him what had befallen them and what Quraysh had done to them in that regard. Then he mentioned the story of the departure of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, toward Makkah, and the story of al-ʿAbbās and Abū Sufyān when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, brought him to Marr al-Ẓahrān, along with Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām and Budayl ibn Warqāʾ. He said: Abū Sufyān and Ḥakīm said: ""O Messenger of Allah, call the people to safety. What do you think — if Quraysh withdraw and restrain their hands, are they safe?"" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: ""Yes. Whoever restrains his hand and closes his door is safe."" They said: ""Then send us to announce this among them."" He said: ""Go. Whoever enters your house, O Abū Sufyān, and your house, O Ḥakīm, and restrains his hand, is safe.""

The house of Abū Sufyān was in upper Makkah, and the house of Ḥakīm was in lower Makkah. When they set out, al-ʿAbbās said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I fear that Abū Sufyān may turn back from his Islam. Detain him so that we may keep him and he may see the soldiers of Allah with you."" Al-ʿAbbās overtook him and detained him. Abū Sufyān said: ""Is this treachery, O Banū Hāshim?"" Al-ʿAbbās said: ""You will know that we do not betray. But I have a need of you. Stay until you see the soldiers of Allah."" Then he mentioned the story of detaining Abū Sufyān until the troops passed by him.

He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, may Allah be pleased with him, with the Muhājirūn and their cavalry, and ordered him to enter from Kadāʾ in upper Makkah. He gave him his banner and ordered him to plant it at al-Ḥajūn and not to leave it until he came to him. He sent Khālid ibn al-Walīd with those who had accepted Islam from Quḍāʿah, Banū Sulaym, and people who had accepted Islam before that, and ordered him to enter from lower Makkah. He ordered him to plant his banner at the nearest houses in lower Makkah. In lower Makkah were Banū Bakr, Banū al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd Manāh, Hudhayl, and those with them of the Aḥābīsh, whom Quraysh had sought for support. He ordered them to be in lower Makkah.

The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah with the company of the Anṣār in the vanguard of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered them to restrain their hands and not to fight anyone except those who fought them. He ordered them to kill four men: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ, al-Ḥārith ibn Nuqayd, Ibn Khaṭal, and Miqyāṣ ibn Ṣubābah. And he ordered the killing of two singing girls of Ibn Khaṭal who used to sing satirical verses against the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him.

The companies passed before Abū Sufyān, Ḥakīm, and Budayl — no company passed without them asking about it — until the company of the Anṣār passed, among whom was Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah. Saʿd called out to Abū Sufyān: ""Today is the day of battle; today the ḥurmah is rendered permissible."" When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by Abū Sufyān among the Muhājirūn, Abū Sufyān said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you have ordered that your people be killed. Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah and those with him passed by me, and Saʿd called out and said: 'Today is the day of battle; today the ḥurmah is rendered permissible.' And I adjure you by Allah regarding your people.""

The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent for Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah and removed him, appointing al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām in his place over the Anṣār with the Muhājirūn. Al-Zubayr marched with the people until he stood at al-Ḥajūn and planted the banner of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, there. Khālid ibn al-Walīd advanced until he entered from lower Makkah. Banū Bakr met him and fought them. They were defeated. Nearly twenty men from Banū Bakr were killed, and three or four from Hudhayl. They were defeated and killed at al-Ḥazwarah until their killing reached the door of the Masjid. The scattered remnants of them fled and entered the houses, and a group of them ascended the mountains. The Muslims pursued them with swords.

The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered among the first Muhājirūn, among the rear of the people. Abū Sufyān called out when he entered Makkah: ""Whoever closes his door and restrains his hand is safe."" His wife Hind bint ʿUtbah said: ""May Allah curse you as a vanguard of a people, and may your clan with you be cursed!"" She seized Abū Sufyān's beard and called out: ""O Āl Ghālib, kill this foolish old man! Why did you not fight and defend yourselves and your lands?"" Abū Sufyān said to her: ""Woe to you! Be silent and go into your house. He has come with creation.""

When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ascended the heights of Kadāʾ, he saw the flashing of weapons on the mountain with the scattered polytheists. He said: ""What is this? I have forbidden fighting."" The Muhājirūn said: ""We think Khālid has been fought, and fighting was initiated against him, and he had no choice but to fight those who fought him. He would not disobey you or go against your command."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, descended from the heights and halted at al-Ḥajūn. Al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām advanced until he stood at the door of the Kaʿbah. He mentioned the story. In it, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Khālid ibn al-Walīd: ""Why did you fight when I had forbidden you from fighting?"" He said: ""They initiated fighting against us, used weapons against us, and shot us with arrows. I restrained my hand as much as I could."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: ""The decree of Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, is best."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"18288","(18282) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Karīm informed me — Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAqīl ibn Maʿqil informed me — from his father — from Wahb, who said: I asked Jābir: ""Did they gain any spoils on the day of the Conquest?"" He said: ""No."" [Ḥasan]"
"18289","(18283) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us — Yūnus informed us — from Ibn Isḥāq — Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād informed me — from his father — from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, may Allah be pleased with her, regarding the story of Abū Quḥāfah and his daughter, the youngest of his children, whom she was leading on the day of the Conquest. When she brought him down to al-Abṭaḥ, the cavalry met her, and around her neck was a silver necklace. A man severed it from her neck. When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered the Masjid, Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, went out until he came with his father. He mentioned the story of his Islam. Then Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, stood up, took his sister by the hand, and said: ""I adjure them by Allah and Islam for the necklace of my sister."" By Allah, no one responded. He said it a second time, and no one responded. He said: ""O my sister, consider your necklace lost. By Allah, trustworthiness among the people today is scarce.""

This indicates that they gained no spoils, and that it was a conquest by treaty, for had it been by force, she and those with her would have had spoils of war, and Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, would not have sought her necklace. [Ḥasan]"
"18290","(18284) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ dictated to me by way of dictation and recitation. He said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Baḥr ibn Naṣr al-Khawlānī informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed me — from Ibn Shihāb — ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn informed me — that ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān informed him — from Usāmah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: ""O Messenger of Allah, will you settle in your house in Makkah?"" He said: ""Has ʿAqīl left for us any houses or dwellings?"" ʿAqīl had inherited Abū Ṭālib, he and Ṭālib, and ʿAlī and Jaʿfar inherited nothing, because the latter two were Muslims, while ʿAqīl and Ṭālib were disbelievers.

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥs from the ḥadīth of Ibn Wahb, as above. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
"18291","(18285) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us, he said: I asked al-Shafi'i about the people of the abode among the people of war who divide the abode and some of them become owners over one another according to that division, and then they embrace Islam, and thereafter some of them wish to annul that division and divide it according to the division of properties. He said: That is not permissible for them. I said: What is the proof for that? He said: The deduction by way of the meaning of consensus (ijma') and the Sunnah — that they shall not be held accountable for their killing of one another, their enslavement of one another, and their usurpation of one another. Then he said: And Malik informed us from Thawr ibn Zayd al-Dili, he said: It reached me that the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Any house or land that was divided during the pre-Islamic period (Jahiliyyah) shall remain according to its Jahiliyyah division, and any house or land that Islam reached without being divided shall be divided according to the division of Islam.""

Al-Shafi'i said: And we narrate regarding it a hadith more established than this, with a similar meaning.

The Shaykh said: And perhaps he intended what [has been previously mentioned]. [Sahih]"
"18292","(18286) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Nahwi informed us — Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Humayd ibn Nu'aym al-Marwazi informed us — Musa ibn Dawud informed us — (and) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us — Tamtam informed us — Musa ibn Dawud informed us — Muhammad ibn Muslim informed us — from 'Amr ibn Dinar — from Abu al-Sha'tha': Jabir ibn Zayd — from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) — from the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family), he said: ""Every division that was made during the Jahiliyyah shall remain according to what it was made upon, and every division that was made in Islam shall remain according to what it was made upon in Islam.""

This is the wording of the hadith of Tamtam. [Hasan]"
"18293","(18287) And the hadith of Malik has been narrated with a connected chain. Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Muhammad ibn al-Muzaffar al-Hafiz informed me — Abu Bakr ibn Abi Dawud informed us — Ahmad ibn Hafs informed me — my father informed me — Ibrahim ibn Tahman informed us — from Malik — from Thawr ibn Zayd — from 'Ikrimah — from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said — he mentioned it similar to the narration of al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him). [Hasan. Previously referenced]"
"18294","(18288) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu al-'Amri al-Muqri' informed us — al-Hasan ibn Sufyan informed us — Hisham ibn 'Ammar and Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said — Hatim ibn Isma'il informed us — Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed us — from his father — from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), in the story of the Hajj of the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family), that the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said in his sermon: ""Beware! Indeed, everything pertaining to the affairs of the Jahiliyyah is placed beneath my feet, and the bloods of the Jahiliyyah are disregarded. And the first blood of ours that I shall disregard is the blood of Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith"" — meaning the son of 'Abd al-Muttalib, who had been nursed among Banu Sa'd, and who was killed by Hudhayl.

Extracted by Muslim in al-Sahih. [Sahih. Muslim 1218]"
"18295","(18289) And 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us — 'Ubayd ibn Sharik informed us — Yahya, that is Ibn Bukayr, informed us — al-Layth informed us — from Yunus — from Ibn Shihab — Muslim ibn Yazid, one of the Banu Sa'd ibn Bakr ibn Qays, informed me — that Abu Shuraykh al-Khuza'i (may Allah be pleased with him), who was among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family), informed him: that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family), on the Day of the Conquest, met a man from Hudhayl whom they were seeking in requital for a blood-wound from the Jahiliyyah, within the Sanctuary. He was heading toward the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) to pledge his allegiance upon Islam, but they killed him. When this reached the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family), he became angry. So Banu Bakr went to Abu Bakr and 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), seeking their intercession with the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family). When evening came, the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) rose among the people and extolled Allah as He deserves, then said: ""To proceed: indeed, Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, sanctified Makkah, and He did not make it lawful for the people"" — or he said: ""nor did the people sanctify it"" — ""rather He made it lawful to me for only one hour of the day, and then it became sacred again, just as Allah had sanctified it the first time. Indeed, the most grievous offenses against Allah are threefold: a man who kills within it, a man who kills one who is not his killer, and a man who seeks requital for a Jahiliyyah blood-wound. By Allah, I shall certainly pay the blood-wit of the man whom you have killed.""

Abu Shuraykh said: So the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) paid his blood-wit. [Sahih. Without the full story]"
"18296","(18290) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas al-Asamm informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar informed us — Yunus ibn Bukayr informed us — from Ibn Ishaq — Yazid ibn Abi Habib informed me — from Rashid, the freed slave of Habib — from Habib ibn Abi Aws, he said: 'Amr ibn al-'As (may Allah be pleased with him) informed me, and he mentioned the hadith in the story of his acceptance of Islam. He said: Then I stepped forward and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I shall pledge my allegiance to you on the condition that I be forgiven for what has preceded of my sins"" — and I did not mention what was to come. He said to me: ""O 'Amr, pledge your allegiance, for Islam effaces what came before it, and Hijrah effaces what came before it."" So I pledged my allegiance to him. [Sahih]"
"18297","(18291) Abu 'Ali — al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu 'Umar Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahid al-Nahwi, the pupil of Tha'lab, informed us — Bishr ibn Musa al-Asadi informed us — Khallad ibn Yahya informed us — Sufyan informed us — from Mansur and al-A'mash — from Abu Wa'il — from 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: A man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall we be held accountable for what we used to do during the Jahiliyyah?"" He said: ""Whoever does good in Islam shall not be held accountable for what he did during the Jahiliyyah, and whoever does evil in Islam shall be held accountable for both the earlier and the later deeds.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Khallad ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Agreed upon]"
"18298","(18292) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr informed us — from al-A'mash — from Shaqiq — from 'Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall we be held accountable for what we used to do during the Jahiliyyah?"" He said: ""Whoever does good in Islam shall not be held accountable for what he did during the Jahiliyyah, and whoever does evil shall be held accountable for both the earlier and the later deeds.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr, from his father. And he intended thereby the Hereafter, as though he made expiation (kaffarah) for his past disbelief through faith, and made righteous deeds thereafter expiation for his past sins other than disbelief. [Sahih. Previously referenced]"
"18299","(18293) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bashran in Baghdad informed us — Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us — Ahmad ibn Mansur informed us — 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us — Ma'mar informed us — from al-Zuhri — from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr — from Hakim ibn Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what is your view of matters I used to perform as acts of devotion during the Jahiliyyah — such as freeing slaves and maintaining ties of kinship — shall I have any reward for them?"" The Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to him: ""You have accepted Islam upon whatever good preceded for you.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Ishaq ibn Rahawayh and 'Abd from 'Abd al-Razzaq, and al-Bukhari extracted it from another direction from Ma'mar. [Sahih. Agreed upon]"
"18300","(18294) Imam Abu al-Fath informed us — Abu Muhammad ibn Abi Shuraykh informed us — Abu al-Qasim al-Baghawi informed us — Dawud ibn Rushayd informed us — Muhammad ibn Rabi'ah informed us — Yazid ibn Ziyad al-Dimashqi informed us — from al-Zuhri — from 'Urwah — from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Avert the prescribed punishments (hudud) as much as you are able. If you find a way out for the Muslims, then leave them to their way, for it is better for the leader (imam) to err in pardoning than to err in punishment.""

And we have narrated regarding that from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with them both) and others. And the most authentic of the narrations regarding it from the Companions is the narration of 'Asim from Abu Wa'il from 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, as his own statement, and it has already been mentioned in the Book of Prescribed Punishments. [Da'if]"
"18301","(18295) Abu Bakr al-Ardasṭānī, the ḥāfiẓ, informed us: Abu Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī informed us: Sufyān ibn Muḥammad al-Jawharī informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Walīd informed us: Sufyān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid narrated from Abū al-Sarīyah that Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) was asked about a slave-girl jointly owned by two men, one of whom had intercourse with her. He said: ""He is a traitor. No ḥadd punishment is to be imposed upon him; rather, he shall be made to pay her value."" [Weak]"
"18302","(18296) It is possible that by this he meant the valuation of the private part upon him, which would refer to the dowry. However, Wakīʿ narrated it from Ismāʿīr from ʿUmayr ibn Numayr — and he is the one named Abū al-Sarīyah — and he said: ""Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) was asked about a slave-girl who was jointly owned by two men, and one of them had intercourse with her. He said: 'There is no ḥadd upon him; her value shall be assessed upon him and he shall take her.'""

Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me through ijāzah: Abū al-Walīd informed us: Ibn Zuhayr informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Hāshim narrated from Wakīʿ, and he mentioned it. And it is possible that this applies if she becomes pregnant from him, and Allah knows best. [Weak; previously mentioned]"
"18303","(18297) Abū Zakariyyā — Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā — informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Haytham informed us: Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd informed us: Sharīk narrated from Qays ibn Wahb and al-Mujālid from Abū al-Wadāk from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""We obtained captives on the day of Awṭās, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'A pregnant woman shall not be approached until she delivers her burden, and a non-pregnant woman shall not be approached until she has one menstrual cycle.'"" [Hasan due to corroborating narrations]"
"18304","(18298) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq: Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrated to me from Abū Marzūq, the mawlā of Tujayb, from Ḥanash al-Ṣanʿānī, who said: ""We went on campaign with Abū Ruwayfiʿ al-Anṣārī (may Allah be pleased with him) in the Maghrib expedition. He conquered a village and then stood up as an orator and said: 'I say to you only what I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say among us on the day of Khaybar.' He (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) stood among us and said: 'It is not permissible for any person who believes in Allah and the Last Day to water the crop of another' — meaning intercourse with pregnant women from the war-booty. 'And it is not permissible for any person who believes in Allah and the Last Day to have intercourse with a captive woman who is a thayyib until he has her undergo istibrāʾ (waiting period to confirm the state of the womb). And it is not permissible for any person who believes in Allah and the Last Day to sell any portion of the war-booty until it has been divided. And it is not permissible for any person who believes in Allah and the Last Day to ride an animal from the Muslims' war-booty until, when he has emaciated it, he returns it to the common pool. And it is not permissible for any person who believes in Allah and the Last Day to wear a garment from the Muslims' war-booty until, when it is worn out, he returns it.'""

Yūnus ibn Bukayr said ""on the day of Khaybar,"" but it is in fact ""on the day of Ḥunayn,"" as others narrated it from Ibn Isḥāq, and others besides Ibn Isḥāq also narrated it thus. Some said ""Ruwayfiʿ ibn Thābit,"" and this is the correct version.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""This indicates that the very act of capture severs the bond of matrimony between the spouses. This is why he did not command the intercourse with a married woman after one menstrual cycle except that this constitutes the severance of the marital bond. Ibn Masʿud mentioned that the words of Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, {And married women are forbidden to you except those whom your right hands possess} [al-Nisāʾ 24] refers to those married women whom you have taken as captives.""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""We have narrated something similar to the view of Ibn Masʿud (may Allah be pleased with him) from Ibn ʿAbbās in the Book of Marriage."" [Hasan]"
"18305","(18299) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bashshār informed us: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā informed us: Saʿīd narrated from Qatādah from Abū al-Khalīl that Abū ʿAlqamah al-Hāshimī narrated to him that Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) dispatched a military expedition on the day of Ḥunayn. They encountered an army of the Arabs on the day of Awṭās, fought them, and routed them. They captured women who had husbands, and it was as though some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) felt it sinful to have intercourse with them on account of their husbands. So Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, revealed: {And married women are forbidden to you except those whom your right hands possess} [al-Nisāʾ 24] — meaning they are lawful for you.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Muslim 1456]"
"18306","(18300) He (Muslim) also narrated it from ʿUbaydullāh al-Qawārīrī from Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ from Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah, with the same meaning, adding therein: ""They are lawful for them when their waiting period (ʿiddah) has ended.""

Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Mayṣarah informed us: Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ informed us: Saʿīd narrated it. [Ṣaḥīḥ; previously mentioned]"
"18307","(18301) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Mīkāl — informed us: ʿAbdān al-Ahwāzī informed us: Zayd ibn al-Ḥarīsh and al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥārith both said: Abū Hammām — meaning Muḥammad ibn al-Zibriqān — informed us: Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān from Ibn Muḥayrīz from Abū Saʿīd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""We obtained captives among the captives of Banī al-Muṣṭaliq. We wished to practice coitus interruptus (ʿazl) and that they not become pregnant. We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about that, and he said: 'There is no harm upon you if you do not do so, for Allah has already decreed what He will create until the Day of Resurrection.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj, the mawlā of Banī Hāshim, from Muḥammad ibn al-Zibriqān.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) consummated his marriage with Ṣafiyyah at al-Ṣahbāʾ, and that was not yet within the Abode of Islam at that time."" [Ṣaḥīḥ; agreed upon]"
"18308","(18302) Abū al-Qāsim — Zayd ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAlawī — informed us in Kūfah from the original of his hearing: Abū Jaʿfar — Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym — informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Ḥunayn informed us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Abī ʿAmr from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to Abū Ṭalḥah when he intended to set out for Khaybar: ""Find me a young servant from among your boys to serve me."" So Abū Ṭalḥah set out with me, placing me behind him on his mount, and I was a boy who had nearly reached puberty. Whenever he dismounted, I served him, and I heard him frequently saying: 'O Allah, I seek refuge in You from anxiety and grief, from incapacity and laziness, from miserliness and cowardice, from the burden of debt, and from the domination of men.' When the fortress was conquered, the beauty of Ṣafiyyah was mentioned to him. She was a bride, and her husband had been killed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) chose her for himself. When we reached the pass of al-Ṣahbāʾ, she became available (i.e., her waiting period ended), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) consummated the marriage with her. He prepared a meal of ḥīs (a dish of dates, clarified butter, and curd) on a small leather cloth, and it was her wedding feast. I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) spread a cloak behind him for her to ride, and he would sit by his she-camel, placing his knee forward. Ṣafiyyah would come and place her foot upon his knee, then mount. When Mount Uḥud came into view, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'This is a mountain that loves us and we love it.' When he overlooked Madīnah, he said: 'O Allah, Ibrahīm sanctified Makkah, and I sanctify the area between its two lava fields. O Allah, bless them in their ṣāʿ and their mudd.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr, and both (al-Bukhārī and Muslim) narrated it from Qutaybah from Yaʿqūb.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) went on the expedition of al-Muraysīʿ with one or two of his wives. Taking women on campaign is preferable, even if there were an element of dislike in it, that one should exercise caution.""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""The narrations concerning this have been mentioned in the Book of Division, and the narrations concerning the Prophet's (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) campaigns with women have been mentioned in this book."" [Ṣaḥīḥ; agreed upon]"
"18309","(18303) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī — in Marw informed us: Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd informed us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā informed us: Shaybān narrated from al-Aʿmash from Mujāhid from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prohibited on the day of Khaybar the consumption of the meat of domestic donkeys, and he prohibited intercourse with pregnant captive women until they deliver what is in their wombs, and he prohibited the consumption of the flesh of every predatory animal with fangs, and he prohibited the sale of the khums (one-fifth share) until it is divided."" And he said in another place: ""And he prohibited the sale of war-booty until it is divided."" [Hasan]"
"18310","(18304) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Muʿādh ibn al-Muthannā informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah informed us: al-Mughīrah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from his father from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prohibited intercourse with pregnant women until they deliver their burdens, and he said: ""It is like watering another's crop."" He also prohibited the sale of war-booty before it is divided, the consumption of the meat of domestic donkeys, and the consumption of the flesh of every predatory animal with fangs. The evidence for this is that once it has been divided, its sale is permissible.

The evidences for the permissibility of dividing it in the Abode of War have already been mentioned. [Hasan]"
"18311","(18305) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hamdan al-Jallab informed us — Abu Hatim al-Razi informed us — 'Abd al-Mu'min ibn Khalid al-Razi informed us — 'Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us — from Yazid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, Abu Khalid al-Dalani — from al-Hakam — from Maymun ibn Abi Shaybah — from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him: that he sold a slave-girl and her child, separating the two, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade him from that. [Da'if (Weak)]"
"18312","(18306) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah informed us — Ishaq ibn Mansur informed us — 'Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us — he mentioned it with a similar chain of narration: that he separated between a slave-girl and her child, and the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade him and annulled the sale. Abu Dawud said: Maymun did not meet 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. [Da'if (Weak); previously referenced]"
"18313","(18307) Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us — Abu Ja'far — Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Duha'im informed us — Ahmad ibn Hazim informed us — 'Awn ibn Salam informed us — from Abu Maryam — from al-Hakam ibn 'Utaybah — from Maymun ibn Abi Shaybah — from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I acquired a slave-girl from the captives along with her son, and I intended to sell her while keeping her son. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said to me: ""Sell them both together, or keep them both together."" [Da'if (Weak)]"
"18314","(18308) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both said — informed us — Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam ibn A'yan al-Misri informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Ibn Abi Dhub and Anas ibn Iyyad — from Ja'far ibn Muhammad — from his father. Ibn Abi Dhub said: from Ja'far ibn Muhammad — from his father — from his grandfather: that Abu Usayd al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, arrived with captives from Bahrain, and they were lined up in rows. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came and looked at them, and lo, there was a woman weeping. He said: ""What makes you weep?"" She said: ""My son has been sold to the tribe of 'Abs."" The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said to Abu Usayd: ""You shall surely ride and bring him back at the same price for which you sold him."" So Abu Usayd rode and brought him back. Although this contains an irsal (broken link), it is a hasan (good) mursal (disconnected) hadith serving as corroboration for what preceded it. [Da'if (Weak)]"
"18315","(18309) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both said — informed us — Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Hayy ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ma'afiri — from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman — from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, saying: ""Whoever separates between a mother and her child, Allah shall separate between him and his loved ones on the Day of Resurrection."" And this was also reported from another route from Abu Ayyub. [Da'if (Weak)]"
"18316","(18310) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Bakr al-Qadi, and Abu Sadiq ibn Abi al-Fawaris all said — informed us — Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub through ijazah (permission) — informed us — Abu 'Utbah informed us — Baqiyyah informed us — Khalid ibn Humayd — from al-'Ala' ibn Kathir — from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, saying: ""Whoever separates between a child and his mother, Allah shall separate between him and his loved ones on the Day of Resurrection."" [Da'if (Weak)]"
"18317","(18311) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr al-Qadi both said — informed us — Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Ibn Abi Dhub — from Husayn ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Dumayrah — from his father — from his grandfather Dumayrah: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, passed by Umm Dumayrah while she was weeping. He said: ""What makes you weep — are you hungry, or are you unclothed?"" She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have been separated from my son."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""A mother and her child shall not be separated."" Then he sent for the one who had Dumayrah in his possession, summoned him, and purchased her from him with a young she-camel. [Da'if Jiddan (Very Weak)]"
"18318","(18312) Abu Nasr — 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah informed us — Abu al-Fadl — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Khamirawayh informed us — Ahmad ibn Najdah informed us — al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak — from Ash'ath — from al-Sha'bi: that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, appointed Sharahbil ibn al-Simt over al-Madinah while his father was in Syria. He wrote to 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: ""You have commanded that captives not be separated from their children, yet you have separated me from my father."" So 'Umar wrote to him: ""Join your father."" [Da'if (Weak)]"
"18319","(18313) And with his chain of narration — 'Abd Allah narrated to us — from Ma'mar — from Ayyub: that 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, ordered that slaves be purchased for him and said: ""Do not separate between a parent and his child."" And this was also reported in a connected (mawsul) form. [Sahih (Authentic)]"
"18320","(18314) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — 'Ali ibn Hamshadh informed me — Yazid ibn al-Haytham informed me — that Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Layth informed them — al-Ashja'i — from Sufyan — from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani — from Humayd ibn Hilal — from Hakim ibn 'Iqal, who said: 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade me from separating between a parent and his child in a sale. [Sahih (Authentic)]"
"18321","(18315) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us — Abu al-Fadl ibn Khumayruwayh informed us — Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated from Ibn Abi Dhubyan, from one who heard Salim ibn 'Abd Allah narrating from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""The slave woman and her child shall not be separated during the division (of estate)."" Salim ibn 'Abd Allah said to him: ""Even if the division is not equal?"" 'Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him) replied: ""Even if the division is not equal."" [Da'if]"
"18322","(18316) Abu 'Abd Allah the Hafiz and Abu Bakr the Qadi both said — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn al-Jahm narrated to us — 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' al-Khaffaf informed us — Shu'bah narrated from al-Hakam ibn 'Utaybah — from 'Abd Rahman ibn Abi Layla — that 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) commanded me to sell two slave brothers, so I sold them and separated them. I mentioned that to the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family), and he said: 'Find them, take them back, and do not sell them except together, and do not separate between them.'"" This was also narrated by Yahya ibn Abi Talib and others from 'Abd al-Wahhab. [Hasan]"
"18323","(18317) Al-Za'farani narrated it from 'Abd al-Wahhab, from Sa'id, from al-Hakam — Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf informed us — Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us — 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' al-Khaffaf narrated to us — Sa'id narrated from al-Hakam ibn 'Utaybah, and he mentioned something similar to the above, except that he said: from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""He commanded me…"" Thus I found it in his original manuscript from Sa'id. Ahmad ibn Hanbal also narrated it from 'Abd al-Wahhab, from Sa'id, from a man, from al-Hakam."
"18324","(18318) Abu 'Abd Allah the Hafiz informed us — Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn al-Khurasani informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us — my father narrated to us — 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated from Sa'id, from a man, from al-Hakam, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family), something similar to the above. Ibn al-Khurasani said: ""And this is the correct version."" The Sheikh said: ""This is more likely, for the rest of the companions of Shu'bah did not mention it from Shu'bah, while the rest of the companions of Sa'id did mention it from Sa'id in this manner."""
"18325","(18319) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us — Ibn Sawwa' narrated from Ibn A'rubah, from a man, from al-Hakam ibn 'Utaybah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned something similar to the above. Al-Hajjaj ibn Artah also narrated it from al-Hakam, from Maymun ibn Abi Shabib, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"18326","(18320) Abu Bakr ibn Furak narrated to us — informed us 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far — Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from al-Hajjaj. (H) And Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us — Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us — al-Za'farani narrated to us — 'Affan narrated to us — Hammad informed us — al-Hajjaj narrated from al-Hakam, from Maymun ibn Abi Shabib, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) gave me two slave brothers, so I sold one of them. The Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) said: 'What happened to the two slaves?' I said: 'I sold one of them.' He said: 'Return him.'"" [Hasan] Thus al-Hajjaj narrated it. And al-Hajjaj is not used as a sole authority (la yuhtajju bihi). The hadith of Abu Khalid al-Dalani from al-Hakam is more deserving of being preserved due to the abundance of its corroborating narrations, and Allah knows best."
"18327","(18321) Abu 'Abd Allah the Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Ali the Hafiz informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Najay'ah narrated to us — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yunus al-Sirraj narrated to us — Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Tulaq ibn Muhammad, from 'Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said: ""Cursed is the one who separates."" Thus said Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash, and it was also reported from him regarding this from Talaq ibn Muhammad. [Da'if]"
"18328","(18322) Abu Bakr the Qadi informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Ali narrated to us — 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us. (H) And Abu 'Abd Allah the Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr said — Abu al-'Abbas, that is, al-Asamm, narrated to us — al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us — 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa informed us — Ibrahim ibn Isma'il narrated from Talaq ibn 'Imran, from Abi Burdah, from Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) cursed the one who separates between a parent and his child, and between a brother and his sister."" The Sheikh said: ""Ibrahim ibn Isma'il ibn Mujamma' — this one is not used as a sole authority."" And it was also reported from him from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Talaq ibn 'Imran ibn Husayn, from Abi Burdah, from Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family), regarding the parent and his child. [Da'if]"
"18329","(18323) Abu Bakr ibn Furak narrated to us — informed us 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far — Yunus ibn Habib informed us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Shayban narrated from Jabir, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, from 'Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him), that when captives were brought to the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family), he would give them to the people of the household all together, and he disliked that they be separated. [Da'if]"
"18330","(18324) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us — Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Abu 'Awanah, Shayban, and Qays, all of them, narrated from Jabir, from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from 'Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Captives were brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), and he began giving them to the people of the household as they were, all together, and he disliked that they be separated."" This Jabir is Ibn Yazid al-Ju'fi, who is the sole narrator of these two chains of transmission. [Da'if]"
"18331","(18325) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīrūyah informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Sufyān, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Farrūkh, from his father, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — wrote that two enslaved brothers should not be separated in a sale. [Weak]"
"18332","(18326) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Isḥāq ibn al-Khurāsānī al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us, Aḥmad ibn al-Haytham al-ʿAskarī informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥassān informed us, Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Tanūkhī said: I heard Makhūl say: Nāfiʿ ibn Maḥmūd ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us from his father, that he heard ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit — may Allah be pleased with him — say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prohibited separating a mother from her child. It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, until when?"" He said: ""Until the boy reaches puberty and the girl menstruates."" [Fabricated]"
"18333","(18327) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith both said: Abū al-Ḥasan al-Dāraquṭnī — may Allah have mercy upon him — said: This ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr is al-Wāqiʿī, and he is weak in hadith. ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī accused him of lying, and no one other than him narrated this from Saʿīd."
"18334","(18328) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī — may Allah have mercy upon him — said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) captured the women of Banū Qurayẓah and their offspring and sold them to the polytheists. Abū al-Shaḥm al-Yahūdī purchased an elderly woman and her child from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) dispatched the remainder of the captives in three thirds: one third to Tihāmah, one third to Najd, and one third toward the road to al-Shām, and they were sold in exchange for horses, weapons, camels, and goods. [Authentic]"
"18335","(18329) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, in the account of Qurayẓah, who said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent Saʿd ibn Zayd, the brother of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, with the captives of Banū Qurayẓah to Najd, and he purchased horses and weapons in exchange for them.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: Likewise, regarding adult women, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) gave an adult slave-girl to one of his Companions, and he ransomed two men with her. [Weak]"
"18336","(18330) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, al-Asfāṭī — that is, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl — informed us, Abū al-Walīd informed us, ʿIkrimah informed us, Iyās ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwʿ narrated from his father, who said: We set out with Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had appointed over us, and we raided Fazārah. When we drew near the water, Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — commanded us, so we made camp. When we had prayed the morning prayer, Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — commanded us, and we launched a raid and descended upon the water. Salamah said: I looked at a group of people among whom were children and women, and I feared they would reach the mountain before me, so I followed their tracks and shot an arrow between them and the mountain, and they stopped. I drove them to Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — and among them was a woman from Banū Fazārah wearing a garment of tanned leather, and with her was a daughter of hers, among the most beautiful of the Arabs. Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — gave me her daughter as a bonus share. I did not remove any of her garments until I arrived in Madinah, and I did not remove any of her garments. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) met me in the market and said: ""O Salamah, give me the woman."" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, she has pleased me, and I have not removed any of her garments."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was silent and left me. Then, the following day, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) met me in the market and said to me: ""O Salamah, give me the woman — may Allah keep your father!"" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, she has pleased me, and by Allah, I have not removed any of her garments. She is yours, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""So he sent her to the people of Makkah and ransomed Muslim men who were in their hands with her."" Muslim narrated this in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUmar ibn Yūnus from ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār.

Al-Shāfiʿī — may Allah have mercy upon him — said: ""What is your opinion regarding maintaining ties with the people of war through wealth and feeding them food — is it not stronger for them in many situations than selling one or two slaves from among them? For the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) gave permission to Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr — may Allah be pleased with both of them — when she said: 'My mother came to me while she was desirous of the covenant of Quraysh. Shall I maintain ties with her?' He said: 'Yes.'"" [Authentic. Muslim 1255]"
"18337","(18331) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed him, Abū al-ʿAbbās — that is, al-Aṣamm — informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Sufyān narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from his mother Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr — may Allah be pleased with both of them — who said: My mother came to me, desirous of the covenant of Quraysh, so I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""Shall I maintain ties with her?"" He said: ""Yes.""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ as previously mentioned.

Al-Shāfiʿī — may Allah have mercy upon him — said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) gave permission to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — to clothe a polytheist relative of his in Makkah. [Authentic. Agreed upon]"
"18338","(18332) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik, from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — saw a striped silk garment at the door of the mosque and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, if you were to purchase it and wear it on Fridays and when delegations come to you."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Only he who has no share in the Hereafter wears this."" Then some garments came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he gave ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — one of them. ʿUmar said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you have clothed me with it, yet you said what you said about the garment of ʿUṭārid."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""I did not give it to you to wear it."" So ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — clothed a polytheist brother of his in Makkah with it.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, both from Mālik.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: Allah, the Most High, said: **""And they give food, despite their love for it, to the poor, the orphan, and the captive.""** [Al-Insān 8] [Authentic. Agreed upon]"
"18339","(18333) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us, Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād narrated from Shuʿbah, from ʿUthmān al-Battī, from al-Ḥasan, regarding His words — **""And they give food, despite their love for it, to the poor, the orphan, and the captive""** [Al-Insān 8] — he said: They were from among the polytheists. [Authentic]"
"18340","(18334) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn al-Munādī informed us, Yūnus ibn Muḥammad al-Muʾaddib informed us, Abān ibn Yazīd narrated from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from al-Aswad ibn Sarīʿ, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) dispatched a military expedition on the day of Ḥunayn, and they fought the polytheists, and the killing extended to the children. When they came, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""What compelled you to kill the children?"" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, they were merely the children of the polytheists."" He said: ""Are your best not also the children of the polytheists? By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, no soul is born except upon the fiṭrah, until its tongue expresses it.""

Al-Shāfiʿī — may Allah have mercy upon him — said, in the narration of Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from him: ""It is the fiṭrah upon which Allah created the people. He made them, so long as they do not express themselves in speech, without any ruling over their own selves; rather, the ruling over them is through their fathers."" [Weak. Previously cited under number 18089]"
"18341","(18335) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Yahya ibn Abi Talib informed us — Yazid ibn Harun informed us — Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us — from 'Ali ibn Zayd — from Yusuf ibn Mihran — from Ibn 'Abbas: that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, would not grant inheritance to the captive (al-hamil). [Da'if]"
"18342","He said, and Yazid informed us — al-Ash'ath ibn Sawwar narrated to us — from al-Sha'bi: that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Shurayh: ""Do not grant inheritance to the captive except with clear proof, even if it comes written on a scrap of cloth."" [Hasan, without the final addition]"
"18343","And Abu 'Abd Allah informed us — Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan narrated to us — Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us — from Mujalid — from al-Sha'bi — from Shurayh, who said: 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to me: ""Do not grant inheritance to the captive except with clear proof."" [Hasan]"
"18344","He said, and Sufyan narrated to us — from Ibn Abjar — from al-Sha'bi — from Shurayh, a similar narration."
"18345","And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us — Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us — from Ibn Ishaq, who said: Yazid ibn Roman narrated to me — from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr — and also al-Zuhri, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, 'Asim ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah, and 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, and other scholars among us — all mentioned the story of Badr, in which it is recorded: Then 'Utbah ibn Rabi'ah, Shaybah ibn Rabi'ah, and al-Walid ibn 'Utbah came forth and called for single combat. Three young men from the Ansar came out to them. They said: ""Who are you?"" They replied: ""A group of the Ansar."" They said: ""We have no need of you."" Then their herald called out: ""O Muhammad, bring forth our equals from among our own people."" The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: ""Rise, O Hamzah; rise, O 'Ali; rise, O 'Ubaydah ibn al-Harith."" When they stood and drew near them, they said: ""Who are you?"" Hamzah said: ""I am Hamzah ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib."" 'Ali said: ""I am 'Ali ibn Abi Talib."" 'Ubaydah said: ""I am 'Ubaydah ibn al-Harith."" They said: ""Yes, noble equals."" 'Ubaydah fought 'Utbah, and they exchanged two blows, each one felling his opponent. Hamzah fought Shaybah and killed him on the spot. 'Ali fought al-Walid and killed him on the spot. Then they both turned upon 'Utbah and finished him, and carried their companion back to the camp. [Da'if]"
"18346","He said, and al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrated to us — from Habib ibn Abi Thabit: that 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""The captive is not granted inheritance except with clear proof."" And all of these chains of narration from 'Umar and 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both, are weak. [Da'if]"
"18347","Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad narrated to us — 'Amr ibn Zurarah narrated to us — Hushaym narrated to us — from Abu Hashim — from Abu Mijlaz — from Qays ibn 'Ubad, who said: I heard Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, swearing an oath that this verse: ""These are two disputants who disputed concerning their Lord"" [al-Hajj 19] was revealed concerning those who came forth to fight on the Day of Badr: Hamzah, 'Ali, and 'Ubaydah ibn al-Harith, may Allah be pleased with them, and 'Utban and Shaybah, the two sons of Rabi'ah, and al-Walid ibn 'Utbah. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Amr ibn Zurarah, and al-Bukhari narrated it from Ya'qub al-Dawraqi from Hushaym. [Sahih]"
"18348","And al-Thawri narrated it from Abu Hashim, adding in it: ""They disputed concerning the Hajj on the Day of Badr."" Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us — Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us — Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Karim narrated to us — Bundar narrated to us — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us — from Abu Hashim, and he mentioned it. [Sahih; previously cited]"
"18349","Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Asbahani informed us — Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us — Shababah narrated to us — Isra'il narrated to us — from Abu Ishaq — from Harithah — from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding the story of Badr, who said: Then 'Utban, his brother, and his son al-Walid came forth in fury and said: ""Who will fight us?"" A young man from the Ansar came out. 'Utban said: ""We do not want these; rather let us fight our paternal cousins from Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib."" The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: ""Rise, O 'Ali; rise, O Hamzah; rise, O 'Ubaydah ibn al-Harith."" Allah, mighty and sublime, killed 'Utban and Shaybah, the two sons of Rabi'ah, and al-Walid ibn 'Utban, and 'Ubaydah ibn al-Harith was wounded. We killed seventy of them and captured seventy. And he mentioned the hadith. [Da'if]"
"18350","Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us — Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us — from Ibn Ishaq, who said: Yazid ibn Roman narrated to me — from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr — and also al-Zuhri, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, 'Asim ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah, and 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, and other scholars among us — all mentioned the story of Badr, in which it is recorded: Then 'Utbah ibn Rabi'ah, Shaybah ibn Rabi'ah, and al-Walid ibn 'Utbah came forth and called for single combat. Three young men from the Ansar came out to them. They said: ""Who are you?"" They replied: ""A group of the Ansar."" They said: ""We have no need of you."" Then their herald called out: ""O Muhammad, bring forth our equals from among our own people."" The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: ""Rise, O Hamzah; rise, O 'Ali; rise, O 'Ubaydah."" When they stood and drew near them, they said: ""Who are you?"" Hamzah said: ""I am Hamzah ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib."" 'Ali said: ""I am 'Ali ibn Abi Talib."" 'Ubaydah said: ""I am 'Ubaydah ibn al-Harith."" They said: ""Yes, noble equals."" 'Ubaydah fought 'Utbah, and they exchanged two blows, each one felling his opponent. Hamzah fought Shaybah and killed him on the spot. 'Ali fought al-Walid and killed him on the spot. Then they both turned upon 'Utbah and finished him, and carried their companion back to the camp. Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: And Muhammad ibn Maslamah fought Marhab on the Day of Khaybar by the command of the Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, and on that day al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, fought Yasir. [Da'if]"
"18351","(18345) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us; Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us from Ibn Ishaq; 'Abdullah ibn Sahl, one of the Banu Harithah, narrated to me from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

Marhab the Jew came out from the fortress of Khaybar, having donned his armour, chanting verses of rajaz poetry, saying: ""Who will come forth to duel?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Who will go forth against this man?"" Muhammad ibn Maslamah said: ""I will, O Messenger of Allah. By Allah, I am the one whose brother has been slain and who seeks retribution — they killed my brother only yesterday."" He (the Prophet) said: ""Go forth against him. O Allah, aid him against him."" He (Jabir) then mentioned the hadith describing the manner of their combat, and said: Muhammad ibn Maslamah struck him until he killed him.

Ibn Ishaq said: Then Yasir came forth, and al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) went out to meet him. When al-Zubayr went out against him, Safiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""O Messenger of Allah, he will kill my son!"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Rather, your son will kill him, if Allah wills."" So al-Zubayr went forth, chanting verses of rajaz, and the two met in combat; al-Zubayr killed him.

He (the narrator) said: And it was mentioned in this narration that it was 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who killed Yasir, and likewise in this narration that it was Muhammad ibn Maslamah who killed Marhab. [Hasan. Previously referenced under number 18108]"
"18352","(18346) And Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us; 'Abd al-Samad ibn 'Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us; 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar narrated to us; Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated to me; my father narrated to me, who said:

We came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)… and he mentioned the hadith in its entirety. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent for 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), while he was suffering from an eye ailment, and said: ""Today I shall give the standard to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, and whom Allah and His Messenger love.""

He (Iyas) said: ""I was brought to him, being led by the hand."" He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) spat into his eyes, and he was immediately cured. He then gave him the standard.

He said: Marhab then came forth, reciting:

""Khaybar knows well that I am Marhab,
Armed with weapons, a battle-tested warrior,
When wars blaze forth and rage.""

He said: 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came forth to meet him, reciting:

""I am the one whom my mother named Haydarah,
Like a lion of the thickets, fearsome in appearance,
I shall repay them in full measure, a devastating blow.""

He said: He struck Marhab and split his head open, killing him, and the conquest was achieved. Muslim recorded this in his Sahih through another chain from 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar. [Sahih. Agreed upon.]"
"18353","(18347) And Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran and Abu 'Abdillah al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan al-Ghadairi narrated to us in Baghdad; they both said: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us; Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us from al-Musayyib ibn Muslim al-Azdi; 'Abdullah ibn Buraydah narrated to us from his father, who mentioned the account of Khaybar and the emergence of Marhab with his rajaz verses, and the words of 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) to the same effect, except that he said: ""I shall devour them in full measure, a crushing blow.""

He said: They exchanged two blows, and 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) was the first to strike, and he struck him, cleaving through the stone (helmet), the mail-coat, and his head, and the sword reached his molars. [Hasan]"
"18354","(18348) And Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu Bakr Yahya ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Talib narrated to us; Zayd ibn al-Hubab al-'Ukli narrated to us; al-Husayn ibn Waqid narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn Buraydah narrated to us from his father, who said:

On the day of Khaybar… and he mentioned part of the account. He said: Then the standard was called for, and 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) was summoned while he was complaining of his eyes. He (the Prophet) wiped his eyes and then handed him the standard, and victory was granted at his hands.

I heard 'Abdullah ibn Buraydah say: My father narrated to me that he had been a companion of Marhab. [Hasan]"
"18355","(18349) Abu Sa'id al-Malini narrated to us; Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz narrated to us; al-Sajiyy and Badr ibn al-Haytham al-Qadi both narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn Husayn al-Ashqar narrated to us; my father narrated to us from Abu Qabus, from his father, from his grandfather, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

""I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) with the head of Marhab.""

And it was narrated by Salih ibn Ahmad from his father from Husayn ibn Hasan al-Ashqar to the same meaning.

Al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: And on the day of the Trench (al-Khandaq), 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) dueled 'Abd 'Amr ibn 'Abd Wudd. [Very weak]"
"18356","(18350) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us; Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us from Ibn Ishaq, who said:

On the day of the Trench, 'Amr ibn 'Abd Wudd came forth and called out: ""Who will come forth to duel?"" 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) stood up, clad in armour, and said: ""I will, O Prophet of Allah."" He said: ""He is 'Amr — sit down."" 'Amr then called out: ""Is there no man?"" He was reproaching them, saying: ""Where is your Paradise that you claim, in which whoever among you is killed shall enter? Will no man come forth to meet me?""

'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) stood up and said: ""I will, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Sit down."" Then he called out a third time and recited poetry. 'Ali stood up and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I will."" He said: ""He is 'Amr."" He said: ""Even if he is 'Amr."" So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) gave him permission. He walked toward him until he reached him, and he recited poetry. 'Amr said to him: ""Who are you?"" He said: ""I am 'Ali."" He said: ""The son of 'Abd Manaf?"" He said: ""I am 'Ali ibn Abi Talib."" He said: ""Someone other than you, O nephew — one of your uncles who is older than you — for I dislike shedding your blood.""

'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""But by Allah, I do not dislike shedding your blood."" He became angry, dismounted, and unsheathed his sword as though it were a blazing flame. Then he advanced toward 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) in fury. 'Ali met him with his shield. 'Amr struck the shield with his sword, splitting it, and the sword became embedded in it, and he inflicted a wound upon his head. Then 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) struck him upon the tendon of the shoulder, and he fell. Dust rose, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) heard the takbir and knew that 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) had killed him. [Weak]"
"18357","(18351) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah narrated to us; Abu al-Fadl Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn Khamirawayh narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us from Sa'id ibn Yazid Abu Shuja' from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from 'Ali ibn Rabbah from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir al-Juhani, who said:

'Amr ibn al-'As and Shurahbil ibn Hasanah sent 'Uqbah as a messenger to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) with the head of Yannaq, along the road to Syria. When he presented it to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), he disapproved of it. 'Uqbah said to him: ""O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), for they do this to us."" He said: ""Have you then adopted the practice of Persia and Rome? No head should be brought to me. A letter and a report are sufficient."" [Sahih]"
"18358","(18352) And Abu Nasr narrated to us; Abu al-Fadl narrated to us; Ahmad narrated to us; al-Hasan narrated to us; 'Abdullah narrated to us from Ibn Lahi'ah; al-Harith ibn Yazid narrated to me; 'Ali ibn Rabbah narrated to me. He said: I heard Mu'awiyah ibn Hudayj say:

We emigrated during the time of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him). While we were with him, he ascended the pulpit. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: ""The head of Yannaq the patrician has been brought to us, though we have no need of it. This is the practice of the non-Arabs."" [Weak]"
"18359","(18353) He said: And 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us from Ma'mar, from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, who narrated to him that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought a head, and he said: ""You have rebelled.""

He said: And 'Abdullah narrated to us from Ma'mar; a companion of ours narrated to me from al-Zuhri, who said: No head was ever brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) in Madinah, not even on the day of Badr. A head was brought to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and he disliked it. He said: The first to whom heads were brought was 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr. [Weak]"
"18360","(18354) The Shaykh said: And what Abu Dawud narrated in his Marasil, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Jarrah, from Hammad ibn Usamah, from Bashir ibn 'Uqbah, from Abu Nadrah, who said:

The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) met the enemy and said: ""Whoever brings a head shall have from Allah whatever he desires."" Two men came with a head and disputed over it, and he (the Prophet) adjudicated it in favour of one of them.

Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad narrated it to us; Abu al-Husayn al-Fasawi narrated to us; Abu 'Ali al-Lu'lu'i narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated it. This is a disconnected (munqati') hadith. And if it is established, it contains encouragement to kill the enemy, but it does not contain the transporting of heads from the lands of shirk to the lands of Islam."
"18361","(18355) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Kathīr al-ʿAbdī informed us, Sufyān narrated from Ibn Abī Laylā, from al-Ḥakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both): that the Muslims captured one of the chieftains of the polytheists and killed him. The polytheists then asked them to sell the body to them, but the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade them from selling the corpse of a polytheist. [Weak]"
"18362","(18356) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād, Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī narrated to us, ʿAffān narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah informed us, Ḥajjāj narrated from al-Ḥakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both): that a man from among the polytheists was killed on the day of al-Aḥzāb. The polytheists then sent a message to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""Send us his body and we will give you twelve thousand."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""There is no good in his body, nor in its price."" [Weak]"
"18363","(18357) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, and he said: al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""I do not know what to say about the land of al-Sawād except as a conjecture coupled with knowledge. This is because I found the most authentic hadith narrated by the Kufans regarding al-Sawād to contain no explicit clarification, and I found among their hadiths some that contradict it. Among them are those who say al-Sawād was acquired through peace (ṣulḥ), and those who say al-Sawād was acquired through force (ʿanwah), and those who say part of al-Sawād was through peace and part of it was through force."" [Sound]"
"18364","(18358) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Abū Zubayd narrated from Ashʿath, from Ibn Sīrīn, who said: ""Al-Sawād is partly through peace (ṣulḥ) and partly through force (ʿanwah). Whatever was acquired through force belongs to the Muslims, and whatever was acquired through peace, their wealth belongs to them."" [Weak]"
"18365","(18359) And with his chain of narration, Yaḥyā said: al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Manṣūr, from ʿUbayd Abī al-Ḥasan al-Muzanī, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Maʿqil, who said: ""No land below the mountain should be sold except the land of Banī Ṣalūbā and the land of al-Ḥīrah, for they have a covenant."" Al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ said: ""We used to hear that whatever is below the mountain and beyond it is through peace (ṣulḥ)."" [Sound]"
"18366","(18360) He said: and Yaḥyā narrated to us, Sharīk narrated from al-Ḥajjāj, from al-Ḥakam, from Ibn Maʿqil, who said: ""The people of al-Sawād have no covenant except the land of al-Ḥīrah, al-Lays, and Bāniqyā."" Sharīk said: ""The people of Bāniqyā guided Jarīr ibn ʿAbdullāh to a crossing point, and the people of al-Lays gave refuge to Abū ʿUbaydah and guided him to something."" Yaḥyā said: ""I think he meant treachery against the enemy."" [Weak]"
"18367","(18361) He said: and Yaḥyā narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Ashʿath, from al-Shaʿbī, who said: ""Khālid ibn al-Walīd made peace with the people of al-Ḥīrah and the people of ʿAyn al-Tamr, and he wrote about that to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and he approved it."" Yaḥyā said: ""I said to al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ: 'Are the people of ʿAyn al-Tamr like the people of al-Ḥīrah? There is something upon them but nothing upon their land.' He said: 'Yes.'"" [Weak]"
"18368","(18362) Yaḥyā narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from his father, who said: ""We came to the people of al-Ḥīrah and made peace with them for one thousand dirhams and a mount (raḥl)."" He said: ""I said to my father: 'What did you do with that mount?' He said: 'A companion of ours had no mount.'"" Thus it is in my book: one thousand dirhams. And others said: seventy thousand dirhams. [Sound]"
"18369","(18363) Yaḥyā narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥīm narrated from Ashʿath, from al-Ḥakam, who said: ""They used to permit the purchase of land from al-Ḥīrah on account of the fact that its people were under a peace treaty (ṣulḥ)."" [Weak]"
"18370","(18364) Yaḥyā narrated to us from al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ, from Mujālid ibn Saʿīd, who said: ""The people of al-Ḥīrah were only made to agree upon a sum of wealth which they would divide among themselves, and there was nothing upon the persons of the men."" [Sound]"
"18371","(18365) Yahyā narrated to us, Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, from Jābir, from al-Shaʿbī, who said: ""The people of al-Anbār have a covenant"" — or he said, ""a contract."" [Weak]"
